 None, 'lastname': 'Robbins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d10602d-b58f-5e16-a918-4f7481e52be4', 'word_count': 883, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d10602d-b58f-5e16-a918-4f7481e52be4'}, {'abstract': 'A new federal proposal on mass advertising would make stock investing even more dangerous.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/opinion/risky-and-getting-riskier-for-investors.html', 'snippet': 'A new federal proposal on mass advertising would make stock investing even more dangerous.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just what grandpa needed. Soon retirees and other investors will be barraged with advertisements for private stock offerings — via mail, cold calling, television, radio, billboards, the Internet and so on.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Risky and Getting Riskier', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Risky and Getting Riskier', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stocks and Bonds', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Private Placements (Non-Public Offerings) and Mini IPOs', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T00:55:40+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b461f99d-e13f-5ed5-9c2a-0bcacf761605', 'word_count': 498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b461f99d-e13f-5ed5-9c2a-0bcacf761605'}, {'abstract': 'Frank Gehry is an unsurprising choice to design Facebook’s new headquarters — and that’s the problem.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/facebook-plays-it-safe/', 'snippet': 'Frank Gehry is an unsurprising choice to design Facebook’s new headquarters — and that’s the problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I read with some amusement the news that Facebook had brought on the architect Frank Gehry to design an expansion to its Menlo Park, Calif., headquarters. Or more specifically, a new warehouse for engineers at its Menlo Park headquarters. Plans for the Silicon Valley site include a 420,000-square-foot, single-story warehouse topped with a garden that will span the entire roof. A tunnel will connect “Facebook East” to the company’s existing headquarters in the former space of Sun Microsystems, whose more hierarchical floor plan has been demolished to make way for Facebook’s open-plan ethos. (As one British blog so delicately put it, “Facebook moves into Sun Microsystems carcass.”) ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Facebook Plays It Safe', 'kicker': 'Allison Arieff', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gehry, Frank', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zuckerberg, Mark E', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Menlo Park (Calif)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:00:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allison Arieff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allison', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Arieff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e3835df1-9278-5d68-9e81-4d2dff70cd52', 'word_count': 1278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e3835df1-9278-5d68-9e81-4d2dff70cd52'}, {'abstract': 'Higher fuel efficiency standards will cut oil use and greenhouse gas emissions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/opinion/cleaner-cars-a-safer-planet.html', 'snippet': 'Higher fuel efficiency standards will cut oil use and greenhouse gas emissions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The federal automobile efficiency standards announced this week are an important step on America’s path to a lower-carbon and more-secure energy future. They are expected to yield multiple benefits: reduced dependence on foreign oil, fewer greenhouse gas emissions, consumer savings at the pump and a more competitive auto industry. They may also serve as proof that well-tailored government regulation can achieve positive results and that consensus among old enemies — in this case environmentalists and the car companies — is possible even at a time of partisan discord.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cleaner Cars, a Safer Planet', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Cleaner Cars, a Safer Planet', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fuel Efficiency', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oil (Petroleum) and Gasoline', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:04:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a6d803b-92dd-5903-bff6-0afd44da66f2', 'word_count': 403, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a6d803b-92dd-5903-bff6-0afd44da66f2'}, {'abstract': 'Recent events in the contest between President Obama and Mitt Romney have raised new questions about whether the political culture still holds any penalty for falsehood.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/us/politics/fact-checkers-howl-but-both-sides-cling-to-false-ads.html', 'snippet': 'Recent events in the contest between President Obama and Mitt Romney have raised new questions about whether the political culture still holds any penalty for falsehood.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In his very first television advertisement last year, Mitt Romney highlighted the nation’s dire unemployment crisis, its record number of home foreclosures and the rising national debt, and showed video of President Obama delivering this arresting remark: “If we keep talking about the economy, we’re going to lose.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/us/subFACT/subFACT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/01/us/subFACT/subFACT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/01/us/subFACT/subFACT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'j