 rulings could unnecessarily cost New York City and the Metropolitan Transportation Authority billions of dollars.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/opinion/a-fight-over-spending-in-new-york-state.html', 'snippet': 'Two court rulings could unnecessarily cost New York City and the Metropolitan Transportation Authority billions of dollars.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Twice in August, judges declared unconstitutional or otherwise rejected state laws because, they said, the laws ran afoul of a provision in the New York State Constitution known as home rule. Both decisions involved transportation, and both could cost the public a lot of money: New York City in one case; the Metropolitan Transportation Authority in the other.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fight Over Spending in New York State', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Fight Over Spending in New York State', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Transportation Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Car Services and Livery Cabs', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Engoron, Arthur F', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cozzens, R Bruce Jr', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:19:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7555b80-804a-5322-a844-6643e7a47f4e', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7555b80-804a-5322-a844-6643e7a47f4e'}, {'abstract': 'As part of the settlement, the biotechnology company Agios Pharmaceuticals entered licensing agreements covering certain intellectual property with the University of Pennsylvania.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/business/lawsuits-related-to-founding-of-agios-pharmaceuticals-are-settled.html', 'snippet': 'As part of the settlement, the biotechnology company Agios Pharmaceuticals entered licensing agreements covering certain intellectual property with the University of Pennsylvania.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two lawsuits that accused the president of the Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center of absconding with groundbreaking research from the University of Pennsylvania to start a biotechnology company have been settled. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Suits That Accused Sloan-Kettering Chief of Stealing Research Are Settled', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Suits That Accused Sloan-Kettering Chief of Stealing Research Are Settled', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Memorial Sloan-Kettering Cancer Center', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Copyrights and Copyright Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Pennsylvania', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Agios Pharmaceuticals', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Thompson, Craig B', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:20:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Pollack', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pollack', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bf8fd1e4-fa94-5f74-b8df-37c7369b8140', 'word_count': 474, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bf8fd1e4-fa94-5f74-b8df-37c7369b8140'}, {'abstract': 'A 20-year investigation into one of Mexico’s largest drug trafficking organizations came to a close Friday when Eduardo Arellano-Félix, a top operative of the gang, was extradited to San Diego.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/world/americas/mexican-drug-gang-suspect-extradited-to-the-us.html', 'snippet': 'A 20-year investigation into one of Mexico’s largest drug trafficking organizations came to a close Friday when Eduardo Arellano-Félix, a top operative of the gang, was extradited to San Diego.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A 20-year investigation into one of Mexico’s largest drug trafficking organizations came to a close Friday with the extradition of Eduardo Arellano-Félix, a top operative of the gang, to San Diego. After exhausting his legal appeals in Mexico, he will face a variety of charges, including racketeering and money laundering, in a United States court. The Mexican government sent Mr. Arellano-Félix, a capo in the Arellano-Félix organization led by his family members, to the United States more than four years after his arrest following a shootout with Mexican police. His brothers are already serving long sentences in the United States, and the Drug Enforcement Administration contends that the gang has largely been crippled, though several others have taken its place as top suppliers of cocaine and marijuana to the United States. Still, Lanny A. Breuer, a Justice Department official, called the extradition “a milestone in our fight against the Mexican drug cartels.” ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mexico: Drug Gang Suspect Extradited to the U.S.', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | The Americas', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mexico: Drug Gang Suspect Extradited to the U.S.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Arellano-Felix, Eduardo', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Extradition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drug Abuse and Traffic', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Racketeering and Racketeers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:29:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9e91e8d-3366-5b05-b5d9-796851cd7041', 'word_count': 155, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9e91e8d-3366-5b05-b5d9-796851cd7041'}, {'abstract': 'How the lowly minie ball made the Civil War an unrelenting gore fest.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/08/31/the-bullet-that-changed-history/', 'snippet': 'How the lowly minie ball made the Civil War an unrelenting gore fest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was late afternoon on Aug. 30, 1862, the concluding day of the Second Battle of Bull Run, and the largest simultaneous massed assault of the Civil War was about to be unleashed. The Confederate general James Longstreet’s corps was in position on the left flank of the Union general John Pope’s unsuspecting Army of Virginia, and when the signal was given 25,000 Rebels surged forward, catching the surprised Federals in an immense “hammer on anvil” movement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bullet That Changed History', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surgery and Surgeons', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:30:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pat Leonard', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pat', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leonard', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1eb23d92-df77-5bc2-8469-eb944a3dd090', 'word_count': 1585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1eb23d92-df77-5bc2-8469-eb944a3dd090'}, {'abstract': 'The city will pay $850,000 to settle a lawsuit stemming from the beating of an inmate at Rikers Island, a resolution similar to two other large settlements the city agreed to pay in June and July.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/nyregion/new-york-city-to-pay-settlement-in-another-prisoner-suit.html', 'snippet': 'The city will pay $850,000 to settle a lawsuit stemming from the beating of an inmate at Rikers Island, a resolution similar to two other large settlements the city agreed to pay in June and July.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York City will pay $850,000 to settle a lawsuit stemming from the beating of an inmate at Rikers Island that fit a rogue disciplinary pattern cited by law enforcement officials in which favored prisoners, in a system known as the Program, received tacit approval to keep order by assaulting and threatening other prisoners.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City to Pay in Another Prisoner Suit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York City to Pay In Another Prisoner Suit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prisons and Prisoners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rikers Island Prison Complex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'John, Kadeem', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-01T01:31:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Weiser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Weiser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0ee5664e-72fb-5103-bb48-83ae41a5bf2e', 'word_count': 752, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0ee5664e-72fb-5103-bb48-83ae41a5bf2e'}, {'abstract': 'Lew Ford, a veteran outfielder, has become a symbol for the Orioles, who have relied on contributions from every corner during their unexpected run.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/01/sports/baseball/lew-ford-is-a-symbol-for-the-surprising-orioles.html', 'snippet': 'Lew Ford, a veteran outfielder, has become a symbol for the Orioles, who have relied on contributions from every corner during their unexpected run.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the start of this season, Lew Ford, five years removed from his last stint in the majors, had a single goal: to bring a championship trophy to Central Islip, N.Y., the home of the Long Island Ducks of the independent Atlantic League of Professional Baseball. ', 'print_s