e-arab-spring-for-europe/', 'snippet': 'Whether Barack Obama is re-elected or Mitt Romney becomes the 45th president of the United States, the challenges of the Arab Spring will be there to confront either man. In addition, a President Romney might have some international fences to mend...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In our video piece above, David Kirkpatrick, the Cairo bureau chief of The New York Times, explains the challenges and contradictions of America’s Mideast policy — whoever wins the election in November. And the Bush administration’s last ambassador to the European Union, Kristen Silverberg, a foreign policy adviser to Mitt Romney, outlines to me how a Romney foreign policy would differ from that of President Barack Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Would a President Romney Mean for the Arab Spring? For Europe?', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-02T09:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marcus Mabry', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marcus', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mabry', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cfc0e03-41c1-5cb4-8684-efa864cc487f', 'word_count': 110, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cfc0e03-41c1-5cb4-8684-efa864cc487f'}, {'abstract': 'A company that built its brand by deftly copying and expanding on products developed by rivals, Samsung’s recent legal loss to Apple could serve as a bellwether for South Korea’s economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/03/technology/companies/south-korea-reassesses-samsung-after-battle-with-apple.html', 'snippet': 'A company that built its brand by deftly copying and expanding on products developed by rivals, Samsung’s recent legal loss to Apple could serve as a bellwether for South Korea’s economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEOUL, South Korea — When a jury in San Jose, Calif., ordered Samsung Electronics to pay $1.05 billion in damages for violating Apple’s patents for the iPhone and iPad, it did more than decide who had infringed upon whose intellectual property. To South Koreans, the legal battle highlighted the strengths and weaknesses of both Samsung and their economy in general.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/03/business/03Samsung/03Samsung-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/03/business/03Samsung/03Samsung-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Verdict, Assessing the Samsung Strategy in South Korea', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Verdict, Assessing the Samsung Strategy in South Korea', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Electronics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Telephones and Telecommunications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Samsung Group', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Korea', 'ra