', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-02T18:50:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By CRAIG O'SHANNESSY", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'SHANNESSY", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cd823107-78de-5dd7-982c-0519cbf67379', 'word_count': 1018, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cd823107-78de-5dd7-982c-0519cbf67379'}, {'abstract': 'Opera Slavica presented “Jenufa,” a dark tale that fits into the company’s Slavic-language repertory.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/03/arts/music/jenufa-by-opera-slavica-with-susan-marie-pierson.html', 'snippet': 'Opera Slavica presented “Jenufa,” a dark tale that fits into the company’s Slavic-language repertory.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Janacek’s “Jenufa,” which Opera Slavica presented in a wrenching performance on Thursday evening at the Bohemian National Hall, has always struck me as a thoroughly wintry work. That may be because of its almost ceaseless grimness, or because its final act includes the discovery of a murdered baby, buried in ice. But then, much of the music that Opera Slavica specializes in — the Russian and Czech repertory — is set in dark, chilly landscapes. So a challenge that faces the company’s young singers, along with bringing the music and characters to life, is evoking a suitably icy spirit for an audience that has just wandered in from the summer heat. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/03/arts/0903SLAV2/0903SLAV2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'cr