', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Allan Kozinn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Allan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kozinn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/b602f099-e231-5e6e-8449-d4e98650ce1d', 'word_count': 637, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b602f099-e231-5e6e-8449-d4e98650ce1d'}, {'abstract': 'Mothering a kindergartner and a high-schooler at the same time puts a nostalgic perspective on ‘back-to-school.’', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/seeing-one-child-off-to-preschool-another-to-high-school/', 'snippet': 'Mothering a kindergartner and a high-schooler at the same time puts a nostalgic perspective on ‘back-to-school.’', 'lead_paragraph': 'When my daughter, now 4, was born, I was 44 1/2. The half is important — or was, to me, at the time, as I imagined the late summer morning I would drop her off five and a half years hence to start kindergarten, when she’d be 5 1/2.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Seeing One Child Off to Preschool, Another to High School', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-02T19:02:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Buttenwieser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Buttenwieser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19e5e24c-0530-53b6-b10c-27e0e57d0824', 'word_count': 723, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19e5e24c-0530-53b6-b10c-27e0e57d0824'}, {'abstract': 'Ana Ivanovic raised her level of play late in the second set, then stayed on the attack to come from a set down to beat Sloane Stephens at Arthur Ashe Stadium on Saturday night to advance to the fourth round.', 'web_url': 'https://straightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/02/for-stephens-lessons-in-a-loss-to-ivanovic/', 'snippet': 'Ana Ivanovic raised her level of play late in the second set, then stayed on the attack to come from a set down to beat Sloane Stephens at Arthur Ashe Stadium on Saturday night to advance to the fourth round.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ana Ivanovic raised her level of play late in the second set, then stayed on the attack to come from a set down to beat Sloane Stephens at Arthur Ashe Stadium on Saturday night to advance to the fourth round of the United States Open. The match turned Ivanovic’s way when Stephens’s first-serve percentage plummeted and Ivanovic moved inside the baseline to attack the second serve. This led to four breaks of serve for Ivanovic, a pivotal break at 4 all in the second set followed by three service breaks in the final set. And as the pressure on her second serve mounted, Stephens began to visibly struggle with her serve, giving away six points on double faults in the third set as the tall, lithe Ivanovic moved even further inside the baseline.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Stephens, Lessons in a Loss to Ivanovic', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-02T19:15:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By GEOFF MACDONALD', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Geoff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MACDONALD', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organ