 Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Breaking It Down: Querrey vs. Berdych', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-03T04:05:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': "By CRAIG O' SHANNESSY", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Craig', 'middlename': "O'", 'lastname': 'SHANNESSY', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/61914a9e-22a4-5f6c-baa6-4a95087ba08f', 'word_count': 711, 'uri': 'nyt://article/61914a9e-22a4-5f6c-baa6-4a95087ba08f'}, {'abstract': 'The latest fitness gear broadcasts your health to the world. Motivational or mortifying?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/t-magazine/where-exercise-and-social-media-collide.html', 'snippet': 'The latest fitness gear broadcasts your health to the world. Motivational or mortifying?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Where was a time, not so long ago, when my girlfriend, Rose, and I had the sort of conversations couples across the globe have when reuniting at the end of a long day. We talked about work, about whether to eat in or go out, about trivial gossip regarding the lives of our friends and (more trivially) those of celebrities. All this, however, has come to be replaced by a peculiar, often snippy back-and-forth that begins the moment we see each other and that, to the uninitiated, would sound a whole lot like gibberish.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '74', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sporting News', 'kicker': 'Face', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sporting News', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Exercise', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'ran