ions at the Royal Society.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Royal Society’s early years, scientists wrote of research both bizarre and elegant, made seemingly uncanny predictions, and sought to reconcile their findings with prevailing beliefs about God. A few samples from the archives:', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Pursuit of Truth, Corporeal and Cosmic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Pursuit of Truth, Corporeal and Cosmic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Royal Society', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Newton, Isaac', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-03T19:05:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Quote', '_id': 'nyt://article/64ed645c-78fd-5acc-9bb1-4d36cf91c5c4', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64ed645c-78fd-5acc-9bb1-4d36cf91c5c4'}, {'abstract': 'The Royal Society in Great Britain, the world’s oldest continuous scientific society, is striving to stay relevant in the modern world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/04/science/royal-society-holds-firm-amid-political-challenges-to-science.html', 'snippet': 'The Royal Society in Great Britain, the world’s oldest continuous scientific society, is striving to stay relevant in the modern world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — To stroll out of Carlton Gardens into the elegant confines of the Royal Society is to find a trove of centuries-old wonders, from Sir Isaac Newton’s reflecting telescope to the first electric machine to fantastical illustrated catalogs of fish and birds.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/