, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/685a6bf4-2294-5b5b-aa7e-6a9d250aab79', 'word_count': 845, 'uri': 'nyt://article/685a6bf4-2294-5b5b-aa7e-6a9d250aab79'}, {'abstract': '“Telegraph Avenue” by Michael Chabon pictures a David-and-Goliath battle between a vinyl record shop and a proposed mall in California.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/04/books/telegraph-avenue-by-michael-chabon.html', 'snippet': '“Telegraph Avenue” by Michael Chabon pictures a David-and-Goliath battle between a vinyl record shop and a proposed mall in California.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The title of Michael Chabon’s pungent new novel, “Telegraph Avenue,” refers, of course, to the famous Telegraph Avenue that bridges Berkeley and Oakland, Calif., that frisky, clamorous thoroughfare so identified, since the 1960s, with the counterculture and community life. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/arts/0904BOOK/0904BOOK-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 663, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/arts/0904BOOK/0904BOOK-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1325, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/04/arts/0904BOOK/0904BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/04/arts/0904BOOK/0904BOOK-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Battling Progress and Other Demons', 'kicker': 'Books of The Times', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Battling Progress And Other Demons', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chabon, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-03T20:12:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michiko Kakutani', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michiko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kakutani', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc1e0ea4-52d9-5a20-a8ea-8d4b0eee0106', 'word_count': 1238, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc1e0ea4-52d9-5a20-a8ea-8d4b0eee0106'}, {'abstract': 'Keith Lee Grant directs an intimate chamber version of “Dreamgirls,” the 1981 R&B musical about the rise of a 1960s girl group.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/04/theater/reviews/dreamgirls-at-harlem-repertory-theater.html', 'snippet': 'Keith Lee Grant directs an intimate chamber version of “Dreamgirls,” the 1981 R&B musical about the rise of a 1960s girl group.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Whether you love or hate “Dreamgirls,” the vintage Michael Bennett musical about the rise and death of a 1960s singing group that strongly resembles the Supremes, you cannot deny the power of its signature number. When Jennifer Holliday opened her mouth to sing “And I Am Telling You I’m Not Going” at the Imperial Theater in 1981, Frank Rich wrote in The New York Times, Broadway history was made. And Dion Millington’s performance as Effie at the Harlem Repertory Theater’s inviting chamber production of the show is a thrilling reminder of how and why. The lyricist, Tom Eyen (who also wrote the book), and the composer, Henry Krieger, gave theater an undeniable moment of greatness. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'T