0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bloomberg News', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bloomberg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'News', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f694a897-8eb5-556d-8a48-3d5f698ab54f', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f694a897-8eb5-556d-8a48-3d5f698ab54f'}, {'abstract': 'Starting Wednesday, the Yankees have 27 games to make sure that a late-season swoon won’t cost them a playoff spot.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/sports/baseball/with-lead-all-but-gone-yankees-dwell-on-the-finish.html', 'snippet': 'Starting Wednesday, the Yankees have 27 games to make sure that a late-season swoon won’t cost them a playoff spot.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ST. PETERSBURG, Fla. — For days, if not weeks, the Yankees have been holding dear to one irrefutable fact of the American League East race. As poorly as they have played, and as well as the Baltimore Orioles and Tampa Bay Rays have performed, the Yankees have still held the lead.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'With Big Lead Gone, Yankees Dwell on Finishing', 'kicker': 'Rays 5, Yankees 2', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'With Big Lead Gone, Yankees Dwell on Finishing', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Rays', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T02:20:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Waldstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Waldstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d500a70b-5ebd-5cd3-8e16-6eb635e32bdc', 'word_count': 820, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d500a70b-5ebd-5cd3-8e16-6eb635e32bdc'}, {'abstract': 'With the start of the school year, we usher in a new season of Poetry Pairings. Here is our first one: Marilyn Taylor’s “Subject to Change” matched with photographs by teacher Will Okun.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/04/news-plus-verse-our-weekly-poetry-pairings/', 'snippet': 'With the start of the school year, we usher in a new season of Poetry Pairings. Here is our first one: Marilyn Taylor’s “Subject to Change” matched with photographs by teacher Will Okun.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the start of the school year, we usher in a new season of Poetry Pairings, a feature we first introduced in 2010. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'News Plus Verse: Our Weekly Poetry Pairings', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T02:26:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/646e398c-c707-59d1-aa80-295515aa7786', 'word_count': 581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/646e398c-c707-59d1-aa80-295515aa7786'}, {'abstract': 'The United States, the I.M.F. and the Egyptian government must work together to revive the country’s economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/opinion/egypts-economic-struggle.html', 'snippet': 'The United States, the I.M.F. and the Egyptian government must work together to revive the country’s economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Eighteen months after Egypt’s revolution, President Mohamed Morsi, the United States and the International Monetary Fund finally seem to be pulling in the same general direction on the question of how best to revive the country’s devastated economy. Their efforts are long overdue and will require a sustained commitment as Egypt struggles with its transition to democracy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Egypt’s Economic Struggle', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Egypt’s Economic Struggle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Egypt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States International Relations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Foreign Aid', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morsi, Mohamed', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T02:27:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/66446d07-9745-5181-93ad-0430d346eb76', 'word_count': 531, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66446d07-9745-5181-93ad-0430d346eb76'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney is hoping the question that helped Ronald Reagan turn around an election will be political kryptonite for President Obama. But it’s not so simple in 2012.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/us/politics/better-off-its-less-clear-than-in-1980-campaign.html', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney is hoping the question that helped Ronald Reagan turn around an election will be political kryptonite for President Obama. But it’s not so simple in 2012.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLOTTE, N.C. — When Ronald Reagan asked voters a week before the 1980 election whether they were better off than four years earlier, he turned a race that had been nip-and-tuck for months into a landslide victory — and showed how a pointed question can be a lethal political weapon. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/us/jpBETTER/jpBETTER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 790, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/us/jpBETTER/jpBETTER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1580, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/us/jpBETTER/jpBETTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/05/us/jpBETTER/jpBETTER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/us/jpBETTER/jpBETTER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Are You Better Off?’ The Answer Is Less Clear Than It Was in 1980', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Are You Better Off?’ The Answer Is Less Clear Than It Was in 1980', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subjec