nce’s secession. It’s the ideal outcome.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Quebec, Canada’s majority-French-speaking province, elected its first woman premier on Tuesday night. Pauline Marois, the leader of the separatist Parti Québécois (known as the PQ), will head a minority government, having fallen nine seats short of the 63 needed for a majority in the National Assembly, Quebec’s legislature. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quebec’s Near Win', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legislatures and Parliaments', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Referendums', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Charest, Jean', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marois, Pauline', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Liberal Party (Canada)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Parti Quebecois (Canada)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Quebec Province (Canada)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T11:58:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francisco Toro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francisco', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4aaaf88a-9ded-569b-9ebb-2dc34f2a7607', 'word_count': 626, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4aaaf88a-9ded-569b-9ebb-2dc34f2a7607'}, {'abstract': 'Dining reporters and critics describe the restaurants they wish would open. What are yours?', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/the-new-york-restaurant-wish-list/', 'snippet': 'Dining reporters