'value': "Hudson's Bay Co", 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T13:39:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b81e3a3c-1fb6-5f88-8278-c2abb65b6a37', 'word_count': 359, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b81e3a3c-1fb6-5f88-8278-c2abb65b6a37'}, {'abstract': 'Until now only one authenticated portrait of Dickinson as an adult was known to exist. But a second image of the poet, with a smile on her face and a friend by her side, may have been confirmed.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/still-no-new-pynchon-photo-but-heres-emily-dickinson/', 'snippet': 'Until now only one authenticated portrait of Dickinson as an adult was known to exist. But a second image of the poet, with a smile on her face and a friend by her side, may have been confirmed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her own words, Emily Dickinson said she was “small, like the wren; and my hair is bold, like the chestnut burr; and my eyes, like the sherry in the glass that the guest leaves.” (She also had a strange propensity for accepting rides from carriage drivers who are the physical embodiment of Death.) But what did that celebrated Massachusetts poet, who died in 1886 – long before the ubiquity of Instagram – actually look like? Until now only one authenticated portrait of Dickinson as an adult, a daguerreotype showing her sitting upright in a chair, was known to exist. But as The Guardian reports, a second image of Dickinson, with a smile on her face and a friend by her side, may have been confirmed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Still No New Pynchon Photo, but Here’s Emily Dickinson', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dickinson, Emily', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMHERST COLLEGE', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T13:41:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d928b3a-3009-5d68-b6af-2497d49bb48f', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d928b3a-3009-5d68-b6af-2497d49bb48f'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/what-were-reading-514/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Slate: A gravely obsessive Pepperidge Farm fan samples every variety of the label’s cookies and emerges with alarming insights like this: “I don’t quite understand how a Milano slice is really a Milano — it would seem an open-faced Milano would be a contradiction in terms, seeing as the sandwich structure of the original is the defining feature.” Willie Wonka, meet Jacques Derrida. — Patrick Farrell ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T13:44:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/56c01e9a-56c9-59b0-96cb-7ecc43092f24', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/56c01e9a-56c9-59b0-96cb-7ecc43092f24'}, {'abstract': 'Dogs adopted from humane shelters track the scent of an endangered salamander in New Mexico.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/canine-conservationists-on-the-move/', 'snippet': 'Dogs adopted from humane shelters track the scent of an endangered salamander in New Mexico.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The high energy levels of many dogs in animal shelters can be off-putting to some people seeking to adopt one as a family pet. When that happens, the dogs can end up as candidates for euthanasia. But wildlife conservationists have found a way to capitalize on that intensity, enlisting canines to track threatened species and thereby help protect ecosystems.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Canine Conservationists on the Move', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dogs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Salamanders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature Conservancy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Washington', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Mexico', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T13:45:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Glenn Swain', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Glenn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Swain', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/074f4e13-5eb9-52da-87e6-1d3182630a29', 'word_count': 524, 'uri': 'nyt://article/074f4e13-5eb9-52da-87e6-1d3182630a29'}, {'abstract': 'It’s a big fall season for the book industry, and there’s a new editor at an iconic brand as Joanna Coles takes over at Cosmopolitan magazine.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/the-breakfast-meeting-a-fall-book-bonanza-and-the-new-cosmo-girl/', 'snippet': 'It’s a big fall season for the book industry, and there’s a new editor at an iconic brand as Joanna Coles takes over at Cosmopolitan magazine.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The book industry is looking forward to some good news for a change. This fall, the big publishers are unleashing a murderer’s row of authors – including Salman Rushdie, Zadie Smith and Junot Díaz – in what it hopes will be a huge season. The two biggest swings: Tom Wolfe writing his first novel in eight years and a work of adult fiction by J.K. Rowling, the creator of Harry Potter.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: A Fall Book Bonanza and the New Cosmo Girl', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coles, Joanna', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hunt, Jeremy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murdoch, Rupert', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rowling, J K', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rushdie, Salman', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Zadie', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wang Jianlin', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wolfe, Tom', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':