 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 366, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 366}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/books/05childrens-two/05childrens-two-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Real Tea Party', 'kicker': "Children's Books", 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Freedman, Russell', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:00:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Books', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pamela Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pamela', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7357ad7d-1b2b-5762-99fa-12d1424be90d', 'word_count': 521, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7357ad7d-1b2b-5762-99fa-12d1424be90d'}, {'abstract': 'The government of Turkey used to say it had no Kurdish problem. Now it’s suggesting there’s no solution to it. Is that progress?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/kurds-and-the-turkish-way/', 'snippet': 'The government of Turkey used to say it had no Kurdish problem. Now it’s suggesting there’s no solution to it. Is that progress?', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — When I first started covering Turkey in the early 1990s, my foreign colleagues and I were sometimes mocked for referring to the brewing resentment in southeastern Turkey as “the Kurdish problem.” There was no sectarian or ethnic discrimination, we were told; the problem was terrorism. The Kurdistan Workers Party, better known as the PKK, resorted to violence and extortion, which no civilized society could abide. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Kurds and (the Turkish) Way', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kurds', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice and Development Party (Turkey)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Kurdistan Workers' Party", 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:06:20+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a3e0639-19c7-5b14-bd48-aa611835f923', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a3e0639-19c7-5b14-bd48-aa611835f923'}, {'abstract': 'An online guide may help you evaluate options if you are behind on student loan payments.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/a-tool-for-those-who-fall-behind-on-student-debt/', 'snippet': 'An online guide may help you evaluate options if you are behind on student loan payments.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have you fallen behind on your student loan payments? The Consumer Financial Protection Bureau this summer introduced an online tool to help you evaluate your options.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tool for Those Who Fall Behind on Student Debt', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Online Advice On Loans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Protection', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Credit and Debt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Consumer Financial Protection Bureau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:06:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a54e39ab-0bd9-5f94-a51b-0f2831d7028f', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a54e39ab-0bd9-5f94-a51b-0f2831d7028f'}, {'abstract': 'Mrs. Obama’s choice of designer sent a message. She has frequently worn dresses sent to her by Tracy Reese, a successful self-made businesswoman whose prices are also in the middle ground.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/fashion/michelle-obamas-dress-in-high-definition.html', 'snippet': 'Mrs. Obama’s choice of designer sent a message. She has frequently worn dresses sent to her by Tracy Reese, a successful self-made businesswoman whose prices are also in the middle ground.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT says a lot about politics today that it was possible, during a speech by Michelle Obama that was described as “genius” for its subtlety by both David Brooks and Mark Shields, that a parallel conversation was happening online, and in real time, about her shimmering brocade dress and matching fingernails. If you were following along on Twitter, where comments reportedly reached a peak of 28,000 a minute by the end of her speech, you might wonder how many people were really listening to what she was saying.', 'print_section': 'E', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/fashion/05FRONTROW/05FRONTROW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 707, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/fashion/05FRONTROW/05FRONTROW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1414, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/fashion/05FRONTROW/05FRONTROW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/06/fashion/05FRONTROW/05FRONTROW-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/fashion/05FRONTROW/05FRONTROW-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Michelle Obama’s Dress in High Definition', 'kicker': 'Front Row', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Delivering A Rebuttal In Brocade', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic National Committee', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democratic National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Michelle', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reese, Tracy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dresses', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:07:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7514b469-0b1e-5419-bbd7-0263490343cb', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7514b469-0b1e-5419-bbd7-0263490343cb'}, {'abstract': 'Responding to questions about science, Mitt Romney says he believes the world is getting warmer and “humans play a role in that warming.”', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/shifting-views-on-climate-change/', 'snippet': 'Responding to questions about science, Mitt Romney says he believes the world is getting warmer and “humans play a role in that warming.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama and his Republican challenger, Mitt Romney, have spelled out their views on climate change in a bit more detail than they usually do on the campaign trail in response to questions from a group of scientific organizations.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shifting Views on Climate Change', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Greenhouse Gas Emissions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN ASSN FOR THE ADVANCEMENT OF SCIENCE', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scientific American', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:11:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e0448c7-b373-5dbf-8a14-d32cb304abde', 'word_count': 797, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e0448c7-b373-5dbf-8a14-d32cb304abde'}, {'abstract': 'Lanford Wilson’s two-character romance will play the Roundabout’s Laura Pels Theater, one of its Off Broadway spaces, next spring.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/talleys-folly-revival-to-round-out-roundabout-season/', 'snippet': 'Lanford Wilson’s two-character romance will play the Roundabout’s Laura Pels Theater, one of its Off Broadway spaces, next spring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A revival of Lanford Wilson’s two-character romance “Talley’s Folly” will be part of the Roundabout Theater Company’s 2012-13 season, the theater announced on Wednesday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Roundabout to Revive ‘Talley’s Folly’ in February', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pulitzer Prizes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Lanford', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Michael (1964- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roundabout Theater Co', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:11:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d720065f-587d-50df-841e-dde0920694ff', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d720065f-587d-50df-841e-dde0920694ff'}, {'abstract': 'Though race-blind casting has been widespread in ballet for decades, clichéd and sometimes offensive views of race remain alive and well across the art form.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/arts/dance/ballet-clings-to-racial-ethnic-and-national-stereotypes.html', 'snippet': 'Though race-blind casting has been widespread in ballet for decades, clichéd and sometimes offensive views of race remain alive and well across the art form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Calendars for the coming ballet season have now been announced across much of America. As usual, story ballets abound. But what stories do they tell? Narrative ballet remains a heritage park: an Old World never-never land. How many of us would actually like to inhabit those onstage worlds? Which choreographers are telling stories for our time?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/arts/race/race-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/arts/race/race-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/arts/race/race-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/05/arts/race/race-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/05/arts/race/race-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Stereotypes in Toeshoes', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Stereotypes in Toeshoes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Firebird (Ballet)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Raymonda (Ballet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Le Corsaire (Ballet)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'AMERICAN BALLET THEATER', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bolshoi Ballet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:11:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ab8548c3-1227-59eb-a11c-7978e1e8e8c8', 'word_count': 1147, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ab8548c3-1227-59eb-a11c-7978e1e8e8c8'}, {'abstract': 'The stories in Ms. Watkins’s debut collection are all set in Nevada.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/desert-stories-claire-vaye-watkins-talks-about-battleborn/', 'snippet': 'The stories in Ms. Watkins’s debut collection are all set in Nevada.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Claire Vaye Watkins’s first book, “Battleborn,” a collection of short stories, arrives with notable provenance (the stories originally appeared in The Paris Review and elsewhere) and an unusually compelling author biography. Ms. Watkins’s father, Paul Watkins, was once a member of Charles Manson’s “family.” In Vincent Bugliosi’s “Helter Skelter,” he’s described as “a good-looking youth with a way with women” who “had been Manson’s chief procurer of young girls.” In a recent e-mail interview, Ms. Watkins discussed learning about her father’s life, the hold Nevada has on her imagination, her “obsessive and controlling” sense of play when she was a child and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Desert Stories: Claire Vaye Watkins Talks About ‘Battleborn’', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Word With: Claire Vaye Watkins: Inspired by the Pull of the Desert', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watkins, Claire Vaye', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watkins, Claire Vaye', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Nevada', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:11:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e6f2c2b-7939-5d3a-ae15-d26dac54ca02', 'word_count': 1208, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e6f2c2b-7939-5d3a-ae15-d26dac54ca02'}, {'abstract': 'Setting up a wireless printer on a home network usually takes a little time, software configuration and a network password.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/qa-setting-up-a-wireless-printer/', 'snippet': 'Setting up a wireless printer on a home network usually takes a little time, software configuration and a network password.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Q.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Q&A: Setting Up A Wireless Printer', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Setting Up A Wireless Printer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computer Printers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Microsoft Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T15:17:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f21203df-de78-5678-b183-40a928cd75f3', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f21203df-de78-5678-b183-40a928cd75f3'}, {'abstract': 'The former Chelsea star Didier Drogba has been a sensation for Shanghai Shenhua, but some say money would have been better spent nurturing domestic talent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/sports/soccer/drogba-bringing-excitement-and-controversy-to-chinese-soccer.html', 'snippet': 'The former Chelsea star Didier Drogba has been a sensation for Shanghai Shenhua, but some say money would have been better spent nurturing domestic talent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — With five defenders smothering him at the top of the penalty box, Didier Drogba, the former star player for Chelsea in England’s Premier League, calmly slid a lead pass to his new Shanghai Shenhua teammate Cao Yunding, who was dashing full-speed through the box. The nifty pass caught the defenders and the goalkeeper off guard. With a near-open net, Drogba’s teammate hesitated, perhaps perplexed by how easy it had seemed, then fired the ball past the dumbstruck goalkeeper. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/sports/06drogba3/06drogba3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/06/sports/06drogba3/06drogba3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight'