ization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/40f7e55a-6f79-5bb8-85a7-7c9de38fa5d4', 'word_count': 278, 'uri': 'nyt://article/40f7e55a-6f79-5bb8-85a7-7c9de38fa5d4'}, {'abstract': 'Photographs of bespoke tailors at work.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/how-hard-is-it-to-make-a-4000-suit/', 'snippet': 'Photographs of bespoke tailors at work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the rich get richer, luxury-goods proprietors often see their fortunes rise, too. (For evidence, check out the decidedly pre-Lehmanesque\xa0heft of the September issue of Vogue.) But as Adam Davidson notes in this week’s column, bespoke suit makers, maybe the world’s second-oldest profession, are struggling to survive. This is puzzling on a number of levels, the most obvious of which is that there are huge markets of wealthy guys who care about whether their jacket is double-breasted or single-vented, and for whom $4,000 is not an ungodly sum to spend on a garment. So why wouldn’t they want the best possible value for their dollar — a suit hand-sewn just for them? ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Hard Is It to Make a $4,000 Suit?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits (Apparel)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davidson, Adam', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Greenfield, Martin', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T16:45:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Kelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 't