': 'glocations', 'value': 'WASHINGTON HEIGHTS (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:04:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katherine Potter', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Potter', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/88b60392-2814-569b-bf4e-7ada98bbe468', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/88b60392-2814-569b-bf4e-7ada98bbe468'}, {'abstract': 'At least four million gene switches that reside in sections of the human genome once thought to be inactive turn out to have critical functions in the body, researchers reported.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/science/far-from-junk-dna-dark-matter-proves-crucial-to-health.html', 'snippet': 'At least four million gene switches that reside in sections of the human genome once thought to be inactive turn out to have critical functions in the body, researchers reported.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many mysteries of human biology is why complex diseases like diabetes, high blood pressure and psychiatric disorders are so difficult to predict and, often, to treat. An equally perplexing puzzle is why one individual gets a disease like cancer or depression, while an identical twin remains perfectly healthy.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/us/jp-GENE/jp-GENE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/us/jp-GENE/jp-GENE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/us/jp-GENE/jp-GENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/06/us/jp-GENE/jp-GENE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/us/jp-GENE/jp-GENE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bits of Mystery DNA, Far From ‘Junk,’ Play Crucial Role', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Study Discovers Road Map of DNA; A Key to Biology', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genome Research (Journal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Human Genome Research Institute', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Science (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Encode Project (National Human Genome Research Institute)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Genome Biology (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal of Biological Chemistry (Journal)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'DNA (Deoxyribonucleic Acid)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nature (Journal)', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:05:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Science', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gina Kolata', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kolata', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d53da50c-f49b-5253-abfa-a1aa352d8575', 'word_count': 1433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d53da50c-f49b-5253-abfa-a1aa352d8575'}, {'abstract': 'A roving ambassador for island conservation takes a side trip to see some primate relatives.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/close-encounters-with-homo-sapiens-closest-kin/', 'snippet': 'A roving ambassador for island conservation takes a side trip to see some primate relatives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent months I’ve become familiar with the work of the nonprofit group Seacology, which scours the world for opportunities to conserve island ecosystems and cultures. Investments are made in infrastructure — from solar panels to school equipment or medical clinics — in return for community commitments to protect environmental assets. Duane Silverstein, the group’s executive director since 1999, recently sent a Dot Earth “postcard” following a visit to two different projects in sub-Saharan Africa working to conserve Homo sapiens’ primate kin, chimpanzees and gorillas:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Close Encounters With Homo Sapiens’ Closest Kin', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Monkeys and Apes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodall, Jane', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFRICA', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lake Victoria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Uganda', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:13:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e7918b1-5f8a-533a-ac0d-2be512eaa6af', 'word_count': 617, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e7918b1-5f8a-533a-ac0d-2be512eaa6af'}, {'abstract': 'For nearly twenty years, McDonalds has been trying to win over Indian consumers, but the company’s most famous food gets in the way.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/mcdonalds-vegetarian-restaurants-latest-in-long-tough-battle-to-woo-india/', 'snippet': 'For nearly twenty years, McDonalds has been trying to win over Indian consumers, but the company’s most famous food gets in the way.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The announcement by McDonald’s on Tuesday that it would open a few vegetarian-only restaurants in India next year marks the fast-food chain’s latest attempt to appeal to consumers in an unlikely market.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'McDonald’s Vegetarian Restaurants: Latest in Long, Tough Battle to Woo India', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fast Food Industry', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pilgrimages', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "McDonald's Corporation", 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Delhi (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:14:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1687e069-a81d-580d-aeb8-3ecabeb80c70', 'word_count': 434, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1687e069-a81d-580d-aeb8-3ecabeb80c70'}, {'abstract': 'The company, which is part of Digital First Media and publishes The New Haven Register and The Trentonian, emerged from bankruptcy three years ago.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/newspaper-publisher-journal-register-files-for-bankruptcy-and-plans-for-a-sale/', 'snippet': 'The company, which is part of Digital First Media and publishes The New Haven Register and The Trentonian, emerged from bankruptcy three years ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Journal Register Company, whose newspapers include The New Haven Register and The Trentonian, has again filed for bankruptcy protection and hopes for a quick sale, said Digital First Media, which operates the company along with the MediaNews Group.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newspaper Publisher Journal Register Files for Bankruptcy and Plans for a Sale', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Journal Register Files for Bankruptcy for a Second Time', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paton, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal Register Co', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'MediaNews Group Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:20:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3e3542fe-8a17-5492-ae5f-953894ecd4c5', 'word_count': 992, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3e3542fe-8a17-5492-ae5f-953894ecd4c5'}, {'abstract': 'Representative Paul D. Ryan suggests that President Obama is more like Jimmy Carter than Bill Clinton.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/ryan-cites-policy-kinship-with-bill-clinton/', 'snippet': 'Representative Paul D. Ryan suggests that President Obama is more like Jimmy Carter than Bill Clinton.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ADEL, Iowa — From tying President Obama to the economic stagnation under one former president, Jimmy Carter, the Romney-Ryan campaign has moved on to offering rosy recollections of another Democrat in the White House, Bill Clinton. In both cases the aim is to paint an unflattering contrast with Mr. Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ryan Cites Policy Kinship With Bill Clinton', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicare', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Patient Protection and Affordable Care Act (2010)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2016', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carter, Jimmy', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clinton, Bill', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ryan, Paul D Jr', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'House of Representatives', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Republican Party', 'rank': 14, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Des Moines (IA)', 'rank': 15, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 16, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa City (Iowa)', 'rank': 17, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Wisconsin', 'rank': 18, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:20:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Trip Gabriel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Trip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gabriel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b69fdbf1-80b1-53d9-af4f-53eed719984f', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b69fdbf1-80b1-53d9-af4f-53eed719984f'}, {'abstract': 'We don’t want them buying our bonds.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/wicksell-goes-to-china/', 'snippet': 'We don’t want them buying our bonds.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The idea that we are at the mercy of the Chinese — that terrible things would happen if they stopped buying our bonds — is very influential. Yet it’s just wrong.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Wicksell Goes To China', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:20:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f6e6355-bb55-534e-9e84-0570d6e8602a', 'word_count': 224, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f6e6355-bb55-534e-9e84-0570d6e8602a'}, {'abstract': 'In a survey of some 3,500 Wall Street professionals, JPMorgan’s investment bank was selected as the best firm to work for in North America this year, judging by quality of life and overall prestige.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/survey-deems-jpmorgans-investment-bank-best-place-to-work/', 'snippet': 'In a survey of some 3,500 Wall Street professionals, JPMorgan’s investment bank was selected as the best firm to work for in North America this year, judging by quality of life and overall prestige.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A multibillion-dollar trading loss and multiple regulatory investigations apparently haven’t made life too miserable at JPMorgan Chase.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Survey Deems JPMorgan’s Investment Bank Best Place to Work', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blankfein, Lloyd C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dimon, James', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goldman Sachs Group Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Morgan Stanley', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'The Blackstone Group', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:23:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6984acb5-8a86-5bce-b8a6-8820aae4be6e', 'word_count': 310, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6984acb5-8a86-5bce-b8a6-8820aae4be6e'}, {'abstract': 'The seventh generation of Volkswagen’s compact hatchback bears a smoother, more upscale appearance and rides on the automaker’s new global vehicle architecture.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/volkswagen-previews-redesigned-golf-ahead-of-paris-motor-show/', 'snippet': 'The seventh generation of Volkswagen’s compact hatchback bears a smoother, more upscale appearance and rides on the automaker’s new global vehicle architecture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Golf, the backbone of Volkswagen’s global product lineup since its debut in 1974, with more than 29 million units sold, has received a complete redesign for the 2014 model year. The seventh generation of the Golf was unveiled in an elaborate ceremony on Monday in Berlin, at the city’s Neue Nationalgalerie.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Volkswagen Previews Redesigned Golf Ahead of Paris Motor Show', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Cars (Compact, Subcompact and Microcars)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Volkswagen AG', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Berlin (Germany)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Paris (France)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-05T17:27:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Kurczewski', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kurczewski', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cf4fc49-9400-507c-b5d7-a5671e8f0c28', 'word_count': 414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cf4fc49-9400-507c-b5d7-a5671e8f0c28'}, {'abstract': 'Wednesday begins the hard sell of President Obama to the middle class. And for this task, the campaign has juxtaposed two prime-time speakers – Elizabeth Warren and Bill Clinton, one right after the other – who in their core philosophies represent contradictory, even irreconcilable strains of American liberalism.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/05/in-clinton-and-warren-competing-messages-for-the-middle-class/', 'snippet': 'Wednesday begins the hard sell of President Obama to the middle class. And for this task, the campaign has juxtaposed two prime-time speakers – Elizabeth Warren and Bill Clinton, one right after the other – who in their core philosophies represent...', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re a candidate for president, the convention affords you a fleeting window of opportunity, about three hours total of network airtime, to present speakers who will get across your one central message to the wide section of America. In the case of this Democratic convention, though, Wednesday’s lineup reveals the essential dilemma that has often made President Obama’s re-election campaign, and much of his presidency, seem as muddled as a Clint Eastwood monologue.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Clinton and Warren, Competing Messages for the Middle Class', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'na