ons', 'value': 'Nature (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oxfam', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T11:15:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Justin Gillis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Justin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gillis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1d53e23a-a8f7-516d-987e-48fa589395cf', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1d53e23a-a8f7-516d-987e-48fa589395cf'}, {'abstract': 'One Giants blog is restructuring to take advantage of the new insights available from film that only N.F.L. coaches were once privy to.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/grading-the-giants-with-all-22-film/', 'snippet': 'One Giants blog is restructuring to take advantage of the new insights available from film that only N.F.L. coaches were once privy to.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a fee, fans this season can see full-length game film that was once exclusive to N.F.L. personnel. The film, known as the All-22, shows the whole field and all 22 players on it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Grading the Giants With All-22 Film', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T11:19:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d11e8e0f-6183-51dc-8816-d30f88b26958', 'word_count': 1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d11e8e0f-6183-51dc-8816-d30f88b26958'}, {'abstract': 'If you are not sure Linux is for you, you can give it a test drive from a USB stick or a CD before you install it.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/tip-of-the-week-linux-on-a-stick/', 'snippet': 'If you are not sure Linux is for you, you can give it a test drive from a USB stick or a CD before you install it.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An aging computer that cannot quite meet the requirements for a Windows 7 upgrade can sometimes find a few more years of usefulness with a switch to the Linux operating system. Ubuntu, which makes a free and easy-to-use version of Linux, has traditionally made it easy to try out the system by posting instructions for making a compact disc that can boot up and run Linux for a test drive before installing it. But for old computers with broken disc drives, Ubuntu Linux can also be run from a USB flash drive. The Ubuntu site has detailed instructions here for making a bootable USB stick (or CD) with the current version of Ubuntu from either a Windows or Mac computer, and an online demo here. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Tip of the Week: Linux on a Stick', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Linux (Operating System)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Software', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T11:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By J.d. Biersdorfer', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/523adef2-bf39-5d0a-9204-82dc78b98227', 'word_count': 127, 'uri': 'nyt://article/523adef2-bf39-5d0a-9204-82dc78b98227'}, {'abstract': 'The European Central Bank took its broadest step yet toward easing the euro zone crisis, throwing its financial clout behind an effort to fend off financial collapse for Spain and Italy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/business/global/european-central-bank-leaves-interest-rates-unchanged-at-0-75-percent.html', 'snippet': 'The European Central Bank took its broadest step yet toward easing the euro zone crisis, throwing its financial clout behind an effort to fend off financial collapse for Spain and Italy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FRANKFURT — The European Central Bank on Thursday took its most ambitious step yet toward easing the euro zone crisis, throwing its unlimited financial clout behind an effort to protect Spain and Italy from financial collapse.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/business/Euro/Euro-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/business/Euro/Euro-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1287, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/business/Euro/Euro-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name