le', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0bc3be3-f654-5ba8-994e-31df8d202428', 'word_count': 362, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0bc3be3-f654-5ba8-994e-31df8d202428'}, {'abstract': 'Jeffrey Katzenberg and D.A. Pennebaker are among those honored.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/its-only-september-but-motion-picture-academy-is-naming-some-awards-winners/', 'snippet': 'Jeffrey Katzenberg and D.A. Pennebaker are among those honored.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — As much of Hollywood moved to Toronto for its annual film festival, which begins on Thursday, some of the rest convened on Wednesday night to select four film veterans — Jeffrey Katzenberg, George Stevens Jr., D.A. Pennebaker and Hal Needham — as recipients of the next Governors Awards from the Academy of Motion Picture Arts and Sciences.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Only September, but Motion Picture Academy Is Naming Some Awards Winners', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Motion Picture Academy Honors Film Veterans', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Festivals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Katzenberg, Jeffrey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pennebaker, D A', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevens, George Jr', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACADEMY OF MOTION PICTURE ARTS AND SCIENCES', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Toronto (Ontario)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:24:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Cieply', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cieply', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41cdbbc1-fb16-576b-be05-e7fdf35cfd59', 'word_count': 209, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41cdbbc1-fb16-576b-be05-e7fdf35cfd59'}, {'abstract': 'How to succeed in college, clothing retailers open stores just for men, a phone that can take a dunking and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/thursday-reading-tips-for-succeeding-in-college/', 'snippet': 'How to succeed in college, clothing retailers open stores just for men, a phone that can take a dunking and other consumer-focused news from The New York Times.', 'lead_paragraph': ' A variety of consumer-focused articles appears daily in The New York Times and on our blogs. Each weekday morning, we gather them together here so you can quickly scan the news that could hit you in your wallet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Thursday Reading: Tips for Succeeding in College', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:25:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b748a289-5bee-5240-a4b5-0f81c0ef28c3', 'word_count': 134, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b748a289-5bee-5240-a4b5-0f81c0ef28c3'}, {'abstract': 'A London book party for two of Mumbai’s most-loved designers draws an A-list crowd.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/at-india-fantastique-silk-stars-and-high-fashion/', 'snippet': 'A London book party for two of Mumbai’s most-loved designers draws an A-list crowd.', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON – It is not often that stilt walkers wearing columns of silk sway and totter on the pavement on New Bond Street. But on Wednesday evening, towering models in a fanfare of silk outfits embroidered with tiny mirrors greeted guests, including the steel baron Lakshmi Mittal and film director Gurinder Chadha, at a book launch and auction for the celebrated Indian designers Abu Jani and Sandeep Khosla.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At ‘India Fantastique,’ Silk, Stars and High Fashion', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bachchan, Amitabh', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dench, Judi', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mittal, Lakshmi N', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rukh Khan, Shah', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:27:22+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monisha Rajesh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monisha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rajesh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/46d0a1ba-4ec6-5a33-b238-ea343f90ac0e', 'word_count': 855, 'uri': 'nyt://article/46d0a1ba-4ec6-5a33-b238-ea343f90ac0e'}, {'abstract': 'Julian Castro’s grandmother was an orphan. Michelle Obama’s father was a pump operator.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/meet-the-parents-part-ii/', 'snippet': 'Julian Castro’s grandmother was an orphan. Michelle Obama’s father was a pump operator.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHARLOTTE, N.C.–The G.O.P. convention last week, an opportunity to introduce “the real” Mitt Romney to America, doubled as a genealogy of the Republican leadership. Bob McDonnell said his grandfather, a poor farm boy, left Ireland 100 years ago and came to Ellis Island; Ted Cruz said his dad was imprisoned and tortured in Cuba; and Chris Christie noted that his Irish father worked at the Breyers Ice Cream plant.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Meet the Parents, Part II', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Castro, Julian', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Democratic Party', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:27:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/97bc307a-8d8b-5e9d-b686-506c5dbf31bf', 'word_count': 1229, 'uri': 'nyt://article/97bc307a-8d8b-5e9d-b686-506c5dbf31bf'}, {'abstract': 'Metropolitan Diary: There’s bad timing, and colossally bad timing. This mishap, in Central Park, was the latter, but it ended happily.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/a-collision-on-the-lake/', 'snippet': 'Metropolitan Diary: There’s bad timing, and colossally bad timing. This mishap, in Central Park, was the latter, but it ended happily.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dear Diary:', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Collision on the Lake', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Metropolitan Diary', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boats and Boating', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Weddings and Engagements', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'CENTRAL PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Hochhaus', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hochhaus', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3541b589-862b-573d-bc06-8bab9185422c', 'word_count': 288, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3541b589-862b-573d-bc06-8bab9185422c'}, {'abstract': 'Concerned groups said they would shut down a nearby port over fears about radioactive byproducts from the Lynas plant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/business/global/protests-promised-over-license-for-malaysia-rare-earth-plant.html', 'snippet': 'Concerned groups said they would shut down a nearby port over fears about radioactive byproducts from the Lynas plant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KUALA LUMPUR, Malaysia — Opponents of a rare earth refinery in Malaysia refused to back down Thursday after the authorities gave the Australian company behind the project the approval to proceed.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Opponents of Malaysia Rare Earth Plant Promise to Protest', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Opponents of Malaysia Rare Earth Plant Promise to Protest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refineries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Lynas Corp Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Malaysia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rare Earths', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:31:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liz Gooch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liz', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gooch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b49b78cb-0664-545f-8c0f-abf2dcfe451b', 'word_count': 743, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b49b78cb-0664-545f-8c0f-abf2dcfe451b'}, {'abstract': 'One thing he got wrong.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/bill-clinton-and-structural-unemployment/', 'snippet': 'One thing he got wrong.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m pretty annoyed with Bill Clinton right now; he’s just spoiled one of my standard joke lines. You see, when giving talks about the economy, I’ve often said that I have a different take from other pundits, because I know some stuff most political reporters don’t, like … arithmetic. I’ll never be able to use that line again without being accused of stealing it from the Big Dog.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bill Clinton and Structural Unemployment', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:41:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0024919-cc4b-5a8e-bf99-3fbc3039dce3', 'word_count': 234, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0024919-cc4b-5a8e-bf99-3fbc3039dce3'}, {'abstract': 'City Room will be providing posts from public schools around the city on Thursday as children return to class.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/summers-more-or-less-out-schools-in/', 'snippet': 'City Room will be providing posts from public schools around the city on Thursday as children return to class.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Today is the first day of school for New York City’s 1.1 million public school students, virtually all of whom will encounter at least one significant change this year. Teachers are beginning to roll out the new common core curriculum, a set of standards that emphasize more critical thinking and nonfiction reading. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Summer’s (More or Less) Out. School’s In.', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T12:42:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/929f2f0f-eb22-5d46-ba63-09f3012444de', 'word_count': 109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/929f2f0f-eb22-5d46-ba63-09f3012444de'}, {'abstract': 'The revised vans are the clearest indication of the models to be offered when Ford begins to phase out its E-Series in North America.', 'web_url': 'https://wheels.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/ford-transit-and-transit-connect-make-landfall-in-amsterdam/', 'snippet': 'The revised vans are the clearest indication of the models to be offered when Ford begins to phase out its E-Series in North America.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of a lavish event held to announce its European product plan, Ford introduced refreshed versions of its van fleet on Thursday in Amsterdam: the Transit, Transit Connect commercial van and Transit Connect Wagon. And in keeping with the automaker’s strategy of uniting its global vehicle lines, the vans are the clearest indication of the models to be offered in North America.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ford Transit and Transit Connect Make Landfall in Amsterdam', 'kicker': 'Wheels', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Stenquist', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stenquist', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9f1ad00d-f1fe-5653-8587-7f1c0fb61cbe', 'word_count': 582, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9f1ad00d-f1fe-5653-8587-7f1c0fb61cbe'}, {'abstract': 'The Tasmanian actress opens up about her beauty routine and life in New York City.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/face-time-rachael-taylor/', 'snippet': 'The Tasmanian actress opens up about her beauty routine and life in New York City.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I met with the actress Rachael Taylor in her new Manhattan apartment, where the gorgeous Tasmanian transplant made me feel instantly at home with her warm, welcoming personality and candid conversation. We chatted a bit about her new television series, “666 Park Avenue” (which airs Sept. 30 on ABC at 10 p.m.), a bit about her upcoming movie, “The Loft,” which co-stars the sexy James Marsden, and a whole lot about her beauty regimen.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Face Time | Rachael Taylor', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:00:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Turnbow', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Turnbow', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86ed684d-c989-5e4a-a806-7bdf4f6054ed', 'word_count': 1196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86ed684d-c989-5e4a-a806-7bdf4f6054ed'}, {'abstract': 'Owners of three uninhabited islands in the East China Sea have made a verbal agreement to sell, according to a source close to the talks.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/world/asia/japan-agrees-to-buy-islands-at-center-of-dispute-with-china.html', 'snippet': 'Owners of three uninhabited islands in the East China Sea have made a verbal agreement to sell, according to a source close to the talks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KYOTO, Japan — The Japanese government has struck a tentative agreement to buy three uninhabited islands that are part of a chain at the center of a heated territorial dispute with China, a person close to the talks said Thursday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Japan Said to Have Tentative Deal to Buy 3 Disputed Islands From Private Owners', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Japan Said to Have Tentative Deal to Buy 3 Disputed Islands From Private Owners', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Senkaku Islands', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Taiwan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'East China Sea', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Martin Fackler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64ce6e1c-28b4-587c-be1f-4bd87b4b0c69', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64ce6e1c-28b4-587c-be1f-4bd87b4b0c69'}, {'abstract': 'Also, hackers briefly take over Al Jazeera’s Arabic and English Web sites.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/the-breakfast-meeting-bill-clinton-grabs-spotlight-and-new-tablets-and-phones/', 'snippet': 'Also, hackers briefly take over Al Jazeera’s Arabic and English Web sites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bill Clinton addressed the Democratic National Convention for 45 minutes Wednesday night, giving a detailed and impassioned endorsement of President Obama, Jeff Zeleny and Mark Landler reported. He frequently spoke off the cuff — according to NPR, which paired the written text to a tape of Mr. Clinton’s delivery, he added 2,300 words to a speech that started with 3,200 words of prepared text. Mr. Clinton pushed well past the start of 11 o’clock local news on the East Coast.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Bill Clinton Grabs Spotlight, and New Tablets and Phones', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:07:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/abdbcc0c-b3d5-57c3-937f-0bc9c2d7174c', 'word_count': 381, 'uri': 'nyt://article/abdbcc0c-b3d5-57c3-937f-0bc9c2d7174c'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: A new app may help taxi seekers hail cabs, but it is incapable of making the ride a more pleasant enterprise than the tribulation it all too often is.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/hailing-a-cab-may-be-easier-but-the-ride-remains-unchanged/', 'snippet': 'The Day: A new app may help taxi seekers hail cabs, but it is incapable of making the ride a more pleasant enterprise than the tribulation it all too often is.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Can you see Holly Golightly stepping outside her East Side apartment and using a smartphone app to call a cab?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hailing a Cab May Be Easier, but the Ride Remains Unchanged', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Smartphones', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Taxicabs and Taxicab Drivers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:23:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b08c652-f736-59ae-87db-d7984ebda61f', 'word_count': 713, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b08c652-f736-59ae-87db-d7984ebda61f'}, {'abstract': 'The show, based on a 2003 film, will be directed and choreographed by Susan Stroman.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/big-fish-musical-to-open-in-chicago/', 'snippet': 'The show, based on a 2003 film, will be directed and choreographed by Susan Stroman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A stage musical adaptation of the 2003 film “Big Fish” will play a five-week engagement in Chicago next spring, the producers announced on Thursday. The two-time Tony Award-winner Norbert Leo Butz (“Catch Me if You Can”) will star in the show, which has music and lyrics by Andrew Lippa (“The Addams Family”) and a book by the film’s screenwriter, John August. Susan Stroman (“The Producers”) will be the director and choreographer.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Big Fish’ Musical to Open in Chicago', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Big Fish’ Musical To Open in Chicago', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Butz, Norbert Leo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lippa, Andrew', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stroman, Susan', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:30:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37deea09-3d54-5eac-bdfd-f119b6c48bab', 'word_count': 194, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37deea09-3d54-5eac-bdfd-f119b6c48bab'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/what-were-reading-515/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Bon Appetit: For your consideration, a rant against the most iconic of all cocktail vessels, the martini glass.—Robert Simonson', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:38:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a240a6d6-4ff3-537f-a69c-d9e3152d864a', 'word_count': 382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a240a6d6-4ff3-537f-a69c-d9e3152d864a'}, {'abstract': 'The Realty Income Corporation agreed to acquire American Realty Capital Trust on Thursday, a $2.9 billion deal involving hundreds of retail and commercial properties.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/real-estate-companies-strike-2-9-billion-deal/', 'snippet': 'The Realty Income Corporation agreed to acquire American Realty Capital Trust on Thursday, a $2.9 billion deal involving hundreds of retail and commercial properties.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Realty Income Corporation agreed to acquire American Realty Capital Trust on Thursday, striking a $2.9 billion deal involving hundreds of retail and commercial properties. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '2 Real Estate Firms Strike $2.9 Billion Deal', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Realty Income Corporation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:39:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/77474a15-7292-5e05-b3df-895ed5f507ce', 'word_count': 246, 'uri': 'nyt://article/77474a15-7292-5e05-b3df-895ed5f507ce'}, {'abstract': 'What Bill Clinton did for Democrats (and to Republicans) last night.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/the-clinton-magic/', 'snippet': 'What Bill Clinton did for Democrats (and to Republicans) last night.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a sweeping, sprawling, drawling 48-minute stemwinder of a speech last night, the former President of the United States drove a rhetorical truck through the biggest opening that Mitt Romney and the Republicans created for Democrats last week. The G.O.P. convention promised to make difficult choices and hard calls and be bracingly honest with the public, but the Republican speakers discussed policy in only the broadest and loosest terms, eschewing detail in favor of generalizations and creativity in favor of boilerplate. In response, Bill Clinton did everything they didn’t, diving deep on policy detail, covering issues ranging from Medicaid to welfare to prescription drugs to student loans, and effectively defining the Republicans\xa0— defining them negatively, it need hardly be said\xa0— on a range of issues where they declined to define themselves. Writing on Paul Ryan last week, I said that we shouldn’t be surprised that his speech was less wonky than his reputation, since a VP address is “hardly the time to talk about block grants and premium support and whether to peg spending rates to inflation or G.D.P. plus one percent.” But Clinton reminded us that this need not be true — that it’s possible to get wonky without losing your audience, and to embrace policy detail as a weapon against your opponents. Journalists tend to assume that voters tune policy substance out, and when the substance is delivered droningly or too abstractly no doubt they often do. But for the most effective rhetoricians, the mere act of talking in detail about the work of government can be a more effective way to persuade the public that you’re serious than a hundred vague promises to “make the hard choices” and “level with the American people.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Clinton Magic', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:44:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9612b7c9-1445-523c-b820-cf3a2c2f6e49', 'word_count': 750, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9612b7c9-1445-523c-b820-cf3a2c2f6e49'}, {'abstract': 'Narendra Modi, the poster boy for growth in India, is a scaremongering sectarian. He shouldn’t hold any public position — let alone that of prime minister of India.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/are-narendra-modis-political-fortunes-turning/', 'snippet': 'Narendra Modi, the poster boy for growth in India, is a scaremongering sectarian. He shouldn’t hold any public position — let alone that of prime minister of India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DELHI — Narendra Modi, the chief minister of Gujarat and the poster boy for growth in India, may be in trouble finally. At least he should be.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Unfit for Any Post', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Corruption (Institutional)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Modi, Narendra', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Gujarat State (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:48:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hartosh Singh Bal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hartosh', 'middlename': 'Singh', 'lastname': 'Bal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e208852e-e1af-5190-a0ec-39932167fa4e', 'word_count': 692, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e208852e-e1af-5190-a0ec-39932167fa4e'}, {'abstract': 'A federal agency says that 9 percent of the wells it tested in New York State had high enough levels of naturally occurring methane to warrant future monitoring.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/a-base-line-for-methane-in-water-supplies/', 'snippet': 'A federal agency says that 9 percent of the wells it tested in New York State had high enough levels of naturally occurring methane to warrant future monitoring.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Measurements in recent years of naturally occurring methane in New York State wells could serve as a base line for assuring the quality of water supplies if and when fracking for natural gas gets under way in the state, the United States Geological Survey reported this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Base Line for Methane in Water Supplies', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hydraulic Fracturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Methane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Natural Gas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water Pollution', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wells', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNutt, Marcia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Geological Survey', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T13:51:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02d84489-9179-532f-a7ea-10d540a24d2d', 'word_count': 466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02d84489-9179-532f-a7ea-10d540a24d2d'}, {'abstract': 'The move into Tal Shehab is the latest step in a sweeping military campaign seeking to wipe out the opposition and raises fears that tens of thousands of refugees who have been fleeing across the border may be cut off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/Syria.html', 'snippet': 'The move into Tal Shehab is the latest step in a sweeping military campaign seeking to wipe out the opposition and raises fears that tens of thousands of refugees who have been fleeing across the border may be cut off.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — The Syrian Army stormed the village of Tal Shehab near the Jordanian border, opposition groups and activists said on Thursday, raising fears that the forces of President Bashar al-Assad may seek to cut off the torrent of tens of thousands of refugees who have been fleeing across the border.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/07syria/07syria-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 687, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/07syria/07syria-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1374, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/07syria/07syria-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/07syria/07syria-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/world/middleeast/07syria/07syria-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'S