Lattman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lattman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a40d1dab-71f7-541b-8f33-78ee7b74f935', 'word_count': 756, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a40d1dab-71f7-541b-8f33-78ee7b74f935'}, {'abstract': 'Family iTrips created apps that allow users to nimbly explore listings, including shops, hotels and restaurants.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/traveling-with-children-with-help-from-apps/', 'snippet': 'Family iTrips created apps that allow users to nimbly explore listings, including shops, hotels and restaurants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Figuring out an itinerary for children can feel like a game of Whac-A-Mole. That puppet show in Boston that your niece loved during her last stay? She’s aged out of it. Your 17-year-old son is happy to crash lectures at Georgetown during a trip to Washington? His 10-year-old brother is not so into it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Traveling With Children, With Help From Apps', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Washington (DC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-06T17:00:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Drake', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Drake', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/139ccbbf-60ce-53fb-a275-ace7d8ec41b3', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/139ccbbf-60ce-53fb-a275-ace7d8ec41b3'}, {'abstract': 'At an event in Los Angeles, Amazon.com showed off new products including the Kindle Paperwhite, with a new whiter screen than its earlier black-and-white Kindles, and a new Kindle Fire HD that is a more direct competitor to the iPad than the old Kindle Fire. Here are live updates from the event.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/06/live-updates-from-amazon-press-event/', 'snippet': 'At an event in Los Angeles, Amazon.com showed off new products including the Kindle Paperwhite, with a new whiter screen than its earlier black-and-white Kindles, and a new Kindle Fire HD that is a more direct competitor to the iPad than the old K...', 'lead_paragraph': ' Amazon.com invited journalists to Santa Monica, Calif., for a briefing on new Kindle products. It showed off the Kindle Paperwhite with a new whiter screen, and a new Kindle Fire HD that is a more direct competitor to the iPad than the old Kindle Fire. The company gave a sneak preview of some new Kindle devices in a TV commercial that was broadcast Wednesday night.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/technology/06bits-amazon2/06bits-amazon2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 670, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/technology/06bits-amazon2/06bits-amazon2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1341, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/technology/06bits-amazon2/06bits-amazon2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/06/technology/06bits-amazon2/06bits-amaz