ltimedia', 'news_desk': 'New York', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/b6cf63cb-1c05-563a-ab4d-2a56a23c503c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/b6cf63cb-1c05-563a-ab4d-2a56a23c503c'}, {'abstract': 'Even though Yasuo Matsui lived in New York for four decades and designed several New York buildings, the architect was among the Japanese detained during World War II.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/realestate/streetscapes-yasuo-matsui-a-career-collides-with-war.html', 'snippet': 'Even though Yasuo Matsui lived in New York for four decades and designed several New York buildings, the architect was among the Japanese detained during World War II.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE mass imprisonment of Japanese in World War II remains a painful blot on our history. There were no internment camps for the Japanese on the East Coast, but you didn’t need to live in the West to be touched by the hand of fear, as Yasuo Matsui found out. Even though he had lived here for four decades, designed one of New York’s tallest buildings and ran a major construction company, the Japanese-born architect was rousted out of bed on the night of the Pearl Harbor attack, held at Ellis Island for two months and spent the war under house arrest.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09STREETS2_SPAN/09STREETS2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09STREETS2_SPAN/09STREETS2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09STREETS2_SPAN/streets-2-jumbo.