21c-59fb-96e5-00481ddcce51', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://audio/13ed51f2-421c-59fb-96e5-00481ddcce51'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times critics share their thoughts on “Bachelorette,” “The Inbetweeners” and “Keep the Lights On.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/this-weeks-movies-sept-7/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times critics share their thoughts on “Bachelorette,” “The Inbetweeners” and “Keep the Lights On.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In This Week’s Movies, the New York Times critics share their thoughts on “Bachelorette,” “The Inbetweeners” and “Keep the Lights On.” Full reviews can be found here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: Sept. 7', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T15:56:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5bc4eab-55c8-54a6-ada9-afcfad1d35ab', 'word_count': 27, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5bc4eab-55c8-54a6-ada9-afcfad1d35ab'}, {'abstract': 'A week after Mitt Romney ridiculed concern about rising seas, President Obama declared that climate change was real and a threat to future generations.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/obama-counterpunches-on-climate-change/', 'snippet': 'A week after Mitt Romney ridiculed concern about rising seas, President Obama declared that climate change was real and a threat to future generations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney, the Republican presidential nominee, took a not-too-subtle jab at President Obama in his convention speech last week, mocking Mr. Obama’s soaring 2008 campaign language about rolling back the rising seas and healing the planet. Mr. Romney’s gibe drew thunderous applause from the Republican delegates, many of whom express doubt about the existence of climate change.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Counterpunches on Climate Change', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Air Pollution', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Carbon Dioxide', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Democratic National Convention', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Speeches and Statements', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T15:59:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By John M. Broder', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c48d321-0e9c-50ae-9fe9-05c4b1ce0ad4', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c48d321-0e9c-50ae-9fe9-05c4b1ce0ad4'}, {'abstract': 'A start-up that uses social media to develop product has raised $68 million in a venture capital fund-raising round.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/quirky-gets-backing-from-andreessen-horowitz-and-kleiner-perkins/', 'snippet': 'A start-up that uses social media to develop product has raised $68 million in a venture capital fund-raising round.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Social media has transformed many areas, including real estate and online communications. Now, venture capitalists are betting the sector can even tackle product manufacturing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Quirky Gets Backing From Andreessen Horowitz and Kleiner Perkins', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Inventions and Patents', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Andreessen Horowitz', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Quirky Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Donna Fenn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Donna', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fenn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0685d60e-6e0b-56a6-a5c6-a879e0ab8b0f', 'word_count': 490, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0685d60e-6e0b-56a6-a5c6-a879e0ab8b0f'}, {'abstract': 'On Sept. 17, 2001, President George W. Bush visited a mosque and spoke eloquently about respect for Islam — an act of leadership that should be celebrated instead of overlooked.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/08/us/on-religion-six-days-after-9-11-another-anniversary-worth-honoring.html', 'snippet': 'On Sept. 17, 2001, President George W. Bush visited a mosque and spoke eloquently about respect for Islam — an act of leadership that should be celebrated instead of overlooked.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the coming days, the calendar will bring the anniversaries of two signal events. One, of course, is Sept. 11, a Tuesday this year, as it was in 2001, when Al Qaeda terrorists in four hijacked planes killed more than 3,000 Americans. With public memorial services and private tears, those deaths will be recalled and mourned.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Six Days After 9/11, Another Anniversary Worth Honoring', 'kicker': 'On Religion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Six Days After 9/11, Another Date Worth Honoring', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bush, George W', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:03:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Samuel G. Freedman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Samuel', 'middlename': 'G.', 'lastname': 'Freedman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/90896b1b-3691-5fec-a170-9fdc2d7a740b', 'word_count': 1035, 'uri': 'nyt://article/90896b1b-3691-5fec-a170-9fdc2d7a740b'}, {'abstract': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/weekend-fare-108/', 'snippet': 'Food-related events around New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To celebrate the start of the new school year, free soft serve yogurt will be handed out from 2 to 6 p.m. Friday at Chelsea’s Table, located at Pier 62 (just west of the 23rd Street entrance to Chelsea Piers). One free small cup of the yogurt with one topping will be available to each customer at the café or the Stonyfield Organic Dairy Bar.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weekend Fare', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:08:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bao Ong', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bao', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ong', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/55dcc6c4-000d-5412-8ff1-55df90794cc9', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/55dcc6c4-000d-5412-8ff1-55df90794cc9'}, {'abstract': 'A gift of property is one way to take advantage of a $5.12 million tax exemption that will soon expire. But shared property raises the question of its management.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/08/your-money/estate-planning/family-property-means-managing-for-a-legacy-wealth-matters.html', 'snippet': 'A gift of property is one way to take advantage of a $5.12 million tax exemption that will soon expire. But shared property raises the question of its management.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOME wealthy people will have to make a decision soon if they are to take advantage of the $5.12 million gift tax exemption that is due to expire at the end of the year. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/08/your-money/08wealth/08wealth-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 693, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/08/your-money/08wealth/08wealth-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1386, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Family Property Means Managing for a Legacy', 'kicker': 'Wealth Matters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Family Property Means Managing for a Legacy', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate (Commercial)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'High Net Worth Individuals', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Personal Finances', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:10:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'subsection_name': 'Estate Planning', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2d2882b9-6d1a-5a78-a23a-b83f4a1af191', 'word_count': 1414, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2d2882b9-6d1a-5a78-a23a-b83f4a1af191'}, {'abstract': 'The fallen rock star gets an encore in contemporary fiction.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/fallen-rock-stars-in-contemporary-fiction.html', 'snippet': 'The fallen rock star gets an encore in contemporary fiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the onset of last summer, the magazine that employed me as a rock critic decided to continue publishing without me, no longer supplying a salary or respite from the office water cooler. My dismissal came via a private equity group situation — “No hard feelings, right?” — yet I couldn’t help taking offense. For the next several months, I steered clear of the music world that for years I had cherished.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 661, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1323, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub/0909-Ruttenberg-Sub-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Charts', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Off the Charts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Spiotta, Dana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hornby, Nick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Franzen, Jonathan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Egan, Jennifer', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:12:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Ruttenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ruttenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f880b914-7e29-56ae-9d9c-5c32c6d75c31', 'word_count': 1236, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f880b914-7e29-56ae-9d9c-5c32c6d75c31'}, {'abstract': 'Just 69.8 percent of all men over age 16 were either working or looking for work in August.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/share-of-men-in-labor-force-at-all-time-low/', 'snippet': 'Just 69.8 percent of all men over age 16 were either working or looking for work in August.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Friday’s jobs report for August was chock full of all sorts of bad news. Among the most distressing: The share of men actively participating in the labor force — that is, working or looking for work — was at an all-time low.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Share of Men in Labor Force at All-Time Low', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Labor Statistics', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Labor Department (US)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:13:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f4047eac-67ba-590e-a875-b1e194fb6fe8', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f4047eac-67ba-590e-a875-b1e194fb6fe8'}, {'abstract': 'After losing a leg because of an injury in Afghanistan, Ryan McIntosh was determined to remain physically active. For the past two weeks, he has been working as a ball boy on the United States Open tennis courts.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/a-soldier-on-the-tennis-court/', 'snippet': 'After losing a leg because of an injury in Afghanistan, Ryan McIntosh was determined to remain physically active. For the past two weeks, he has been working as a ball boy on the United States Open tennis courts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The players aren’t the only ones breaking a sweat on the courts at the United States Open.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 308, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 308}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 526, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/health/07well_ryan/07well_ryan-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Soldier on the Tennis Court', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:14:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Science', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': "By Anahad O'Connor", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anahad', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Connor", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9202f801-e123-5769-97d1-ef62e10bead8', 'word_count': 1054, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9202f801-e123-5769-97d1-ef62e10bead8'}, {'abstract': 'Jennifer Gilmore says her new novel was motivated in part by her observations of how motherhood is discussed in our culture.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Jennifer Gilmore says her new novel was motivated in part by her observations of how motherhood is discussed in our culture.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week, Jennifer Gilmore reviews “The Revised Fundamentals of Caregiving,” a novel by Jonathan Evison, in which a former stay-at-home dad who lost his children in a grisly accident becomes a caregiver for hire. “Caretaking is often stereotyped as women’s work,” Gilmore wrote in an e-mail, but in Evison’s book, “caring deeply, dealing with grief, transcends gender.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09upfront/09upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 516, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09upfront/09upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 787, 'width': 516, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilmore, Jennifer', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:16:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bcee657-7151-57a5-83a9-eaaf684b92f3', 'word_count': 308, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bcee657-7151-57a5-83a9-eaaf684b92f3'}, {'abstract': 'The novelist Tess Gerritsen — whose mystery “Last to Die” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3 — was long reluctant to include elements of her Chinese-American heritage in her books.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/inside-the-list.html', 'snippet': 'The novelist Tess Gerritsen — whose mystery “Last to Die” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3 — was long reluctant to include elements of her Chinese-American heritage in her books.', 'lead_paragraph': 'DOCTOR, LAWYER, WRITER? The suspense novelist Tess Gerritsen — her latest Rizzoli and Isles mystery, “Last to Die,” enters the hardcover fiction list at No. 3 — told The New Zealand Herald last year that she had been reluctant for more than 20 novels to introduce elements of her Chinese-American heritage into her fiction. That’s because early in her career, when she had just quit medicine to write romantic thrillers, editors warned her there was no market for Asian characters, and the advice stayed with her even as she moved away from the romance genre.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09list-img/09list-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 218, 'width': 216, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09list-img/09list-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 218, 'width': 216, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09list-img/09list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09list-img/09list-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09list-img/09list-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Inside the List', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'TBR: Inside the List', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:17:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gregory Cowles', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gregory', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cowles', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0c712e6c-994e-5182-b132-951d32b6e15e', 'word_count': 579, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0c712e6c-994e-5182-b132-951d32b6e15e'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MORTALITY,  by Christopher Hitchens. (Twelve, $22.99.) Like virtually everything Hitchens wrote over his long career, these dispatches from “Tumorville,” written after he learned of his ultimately fatal esophageal cancer, are diamond-hard and brilliant.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:17:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/cfa7b8b0-1b5f-511e-a9bc-c5b92e7a15e6', 'word_count': 268, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cfa7b8b0-1b5f-511e-a9bc-c5b92e7a15e6'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BUSY MONSTERS,  by William Giraldi. (Norton, $15.95.) Speaking in a pastiche of idioms (Southern, Victorian, Western), Charles Homar, the cuckolded hero of Giraldi’s first novel, wrestles with notions of manliness. Pledging to win back his errant fiancée, who has run off with a squid hunter, Charles chronicles his haywire schemes — involving Bigfoot, U.F.O.’s and an oversexed Italian bodybuilder — in installments for a weekly magazine.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:18:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/5937f6f9-d4cf-5098-ba47-b180178250f8', 'word_count': 497, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5937f6f9-d4cf-5098-ba47-b180178250f8'}, {'abstract': 'New fiction by Amanda Coplin, Bridget Siegel, Vincent Lam, Erik Orsenna and David R. Gillham.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/the-orchardist-by-amanda-coplin-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New fiction by Amanda Coplin, Bridget Siegel, Vincent Lam, Erik Orsenna and David R. Gillham.', 'lead_paragraph': ' THE ORCHARDIST  By Amanda Coplin.  Harper/HarperCollins, $26.99.  ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Stuart/0909-Stuart-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 717, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Stuart/0909-Stuart-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1433, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Stuart/0909-Stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Stuart/0909-Stuart-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Stuart/0909-Stuart-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Fiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lam, Vincent', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Siegel, Bridget', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bell, Anthea', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Orsenna, Erik', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coplin, Amanda', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gillham, David R', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:19:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jan Stuart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stuart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/5dc1da5a-d598-5bbe-9161-db2ed41f5af9', 'word_count': 997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5dc1da5a-d598-5bbe-9161-db2ed41f5af9'}, {'abstract': 'Garret Keizer considers privacy and the economic and class dimensions of its erosion.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/privacy-by-garret-keizer.html', 'snippet': 'Garret Keizer considers privacy and the economic and class dimensions of its erosion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Privacy is a topic that tends to inspire discursive essays rather than philosophical treatises. In “A Bachelor’s Complaint,” in 1823, Charles Lamb deplored the exhibitionism of married couples who “perk up” their public displays of affection when in the company of single friends. In 1890, Samuel Warren and Louis Brandeis lamented that American law, which protects privacy indirectly through laws safeguarding property, contains no remedies for the mental suffering that results from an offense against honor. Rather than attempting a comprehensive treatment of the subject, the privacy essayist identifies a distinctive value that privacy protects (ranging from dignity and autonomy to intimacy and friendship) and defends its value by using concrete examples and anecdotes from daily life.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 981, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1961, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-Rosen/0909-Rosen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Indecent Exposure', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Indecent Exposure', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keizer, Garret', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privacy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:19:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeffrey Rosen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeffrey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ea97abef-1b43-5aa6-9c6f-5da4a9ac8674', 'word_count': 1285, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ea97abef-1b43-5aa6-9c6f-5da4a9ac8674'}, {'abstract': 'In this historical novel, Kate Grenville explores modern Australia’s origins through the story of one family.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/sarah-thornhill-by-kate-grenville.html', 'snippet': 'In this historical novel, Kate Grenville explores modern Australia’s origins through the story of one family.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kate Grenville’s latest novel, “Sarah Thornhill,” provides a wrenching conclusion to a tough-hearted trilogy about the colonizing of Australia. In her previous excursions into this rough, unforgiving land, shiploads of transported convicts, indentured servants and down-on-their-luck gentry were seen carving out English-style estates and tangling with aboriginals who somehow — to the newcomers’ astonishment — couldn’t accept the fact that they’d been conquered. By the time the heroine of this new book is born, in 1816, the country’s future is almost settled.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 692, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 990, 'width': 1466, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09cokal-img/09cokal-img-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Outback Outcasts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Outback Outcasts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Grenville, Kate', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:20:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susann Cokal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cokal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/0f07e829-073a-5d56-a721-957f49b0ea28', 'word_count': 982, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0f07e829-073a-5d56-a721-957f49b0ea28'}, {'abstract': 'A historian examines the end of French rule in Vietnam and the beginning of American involvement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/embers-of-war-by-fredrik-logevall.html', 'snippet': 'A historian examines the end of French rule in Vietnam and the beginning of American involvement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'During the Casablanca conference in January 1943, Franklin Roosevelt spoke bitterly to his son about European imperialism: “Don’t think for a moment, Elliott, that Americans would be dying in the Pacific tonight if it hadn’t been for the shortsighted greed of the French and the British and the Dutch.” Earlier, he had spoken openly to the White House correspondents: “There has never been, there isn’t now and there never will be, any race of people on earth fit to serve as masters over their fellow men. . . . We believe that any nationality, no matter how small, has the inherent right to its own nationhood.” That was the core of the Atlantic Charter in 1941, which both Churchill and Roosevelt signed. It called on “the right of all peoples to choose the form of government under which they will live.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BRINKLEY/09BRINKLEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 963, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BRINKLEY/09BRINKLEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BRINKLEY/09BRINKLEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BRINKLEY/09BRINKLEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BRINKLEY/09BRINKLEY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Were We in Vietnam?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Were We in Vietnam?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Vietnam War', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Logevall, Fredrik', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {