op_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/07/dining/video-brisket/video-brisket-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/dining/video-brisket/video-brisket-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/07/dining/video-brisket/video-brisket-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Two Cuts of Brisket', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Clark, Melissa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Food', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:22:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'Gabe Johnson and David Frank', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gabe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Frank', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/224a97ca-44b9-5698-bb9b-92b34999d903', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/224a97ca-44b9-5698-bb9b-92b34999d903'}, {'abstract': 'Rajiv Chandrasekaran reports on America’s involvement in Afghanistan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/little-america-by-rajiv-chandrasekaran.html', 'snippet': 'Rajiv Chandrasekaran reports on America’s involvement in Afghanistan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rajiv\xa0Chandrasekaran has done it again. Like “Imperial Life in the Emerald City,” his chronicle of Washington’s hapless management of the Iraq war in its early days, “Little America” is a beautifully written and deeply reported account of how a divided United States government and its dysfunctional bureaucracy have foiled American efforts abroad, this time to suppress the Taliban insurgency and bring stability to Afghanistan. It tells a story of political foibles, overly ambitious goals and feckless Afghans and Americans. The United States seems condemned to lurch between disastrous quick fixes and unrealistic visions of remaking countries overnight in its own image, never finding a middle road. No doubt most readers of this book will come away with the conclusion that our principal enemy in all this is ourselves.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 365, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 365}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 796, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1593, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09ROBINSON/09ROBINSON-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Long Divisions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Long Divisions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chandrasekaran, Rajiv', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:23:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Linda Robinson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Linda', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Robinson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/119abf23-0ab3-515a-a124-d44b7670c2e8', 'word_count': 1133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/119abf23-0ab3-515a-a124-d44b7670c2e8'}, {'abstract': 'Antony Beevor’s survey of World War II reconstructs the battles at sea, on land and in the air.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/the-second-world-war-by-antony-beevor.html', 'snippet': 'Antony Beevor’s survey of World War II reconstructs the battles at sea, on land and in the air.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Toward the end of this powerful narrative of World War II, Antony Beevor quotes a report, by the Australian war correspondent Godfrey Blunden, of an encounter with some American troops who had just been released from German P.O.W. camps. They had been in Europe only a few months, thrown into combat and almost instantly captured during the Ardennes offensive, Hitler’s last big throw of the dice. The men now had “xylophone ribs” and “gangling arms.” Some of their fellow prisoners had been beaten to death by their guards for attempting to take sugar beets from fields. Blunden wrote: “They were more pitiful because they were only boys drafted from nice homes in a nice country knowing nothing about Europe, not tough like Australians, or shrewd like the French or irreducibly stubborn like the English. They just didn’t know what it was all about.”', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09TOYE/09TOYE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 875, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09TOYE/09TOYE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1750, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09TOYE/09TOYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09TOYE/09TOYE-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09TOYE/09TOYE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Many Wars in One', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Many Wars in One', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World War II (1939-45)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beevor, Antony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:23:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Toye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8afc6821-6733-5863-bd64-c95754974173', 'word_count': 1151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8afc6821-6733-5863-bd64-c95754974173'}, {'abstract': 'Paul Auster’s memoir includes an elegy for his mother, reflections on aging and an examination of the body’s frailties and desires.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/winter-journal-by-paul-auster.html', 'snippet': 'Paul Auster’s memoir includes an elegy for his mother, reflections on aging and an examination of the body’s frailties and desires.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paul Auster was a novelist in Brooklyn before there were novelists in Brooklyn. (When I was growing up there in the 1980s, my father used to point him out to me as we did the grocery shopping in Park Slope — this was a rare kind of sighting.) Back in those days, his novels — the New York Trilogy, “Leviathan,” “Moon Palace” — seemed postmodern and fresh. They mingled the tropes of detective fiction with a kind of European quasi-philosophical ambiguity. “Kafka goes gumshoe,” as one of his editors described it. The novels comprised stories nested within stories; there were characters named Paul Auster, and there were doppelgängers even for the \xaddoppelgängers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-ORourke/0909-ORourke-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1003, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-ORourke/0909-ORourke-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2006, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-ORourke/0909-ORourke-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-ORourke/0909-ORourke-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/0909-ORourke/0909-ORourke-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'These Wild Solitudes', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'These Wild Solitudes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auster, Paul', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:24:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': "By Meghan O'Rourke", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Meghan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "O'Rourke", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c95928f6-6b1d-5bcc-9f0a-fa49513a5937', 'word_count': 1388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c95928f6-6b1d-5bcc-9f0a-fa49513a5937'}, {'abstract': 'As chancellor, Thomas Becket was Henry II’s confidant; as archbishop, he became a bitter enemy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/books/review/thomas-becket-by-john-guy.html', 'snippet': 'As chancellor, Thomas Becket was Henry II’s confidant; as archbishop, he became a bitter enemy.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The biographer’s trap,” John Guy remarks in “Thomas Becket,” his portrait of that foremost friend turned foremost foe of Henry II, “is to look for a decisive moment of change.” But, he adds, “to do that is to write the history of the saint without his shadow.” With \xadBecket, this temptation often seems to have been irresistible, from the very night of his murder, Dec. 29, 1170, near the high altar of Canterbury Cathedral. As a crowd swooped down on the battered corpse of the archbishop, tearing off pieces of clothing to dip in the gruesome puddle of his blood and brains, the outlines of the story of Becket’s sudden conversion from \xadluxury-loving chancellor to ascetic defender of the church were already being rehearsed, soon to be followed by tales of his miraculous powers.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BECKER/09BECKER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 762, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BECKER/09BECKER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1525, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BECKER/09BECKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BECKER/09BECKER-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/books/review/09BECKER/09BECKER-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Murder in the Cathedral', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Murder in the Cathedral', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Becket, Thomas (1118-1170)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guy, John', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alida Becker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alida', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Becker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc4c0a13-a73a-5045-bb6c-8c82ef22bddf', 'word_count': 959, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc4c0a13-a73a-5045-bb6c-8c82ef22bddf'}, {'abstract': 'Can a student use an audio book for a school assignment? It depends on the reason the book or reading was assigned.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/when-audio-books-do-and-dont-count-for-school/', 'snippet': 'Can a student use an audio book for a school assignment? It depends on the reason the book or reading was assigned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Does listening to an audio book count for school reading, and if it does, when? I ran across this quandary when helping my rising third grader fill out her “summer reading” list before going back to school. She wanted to include the books she’s listened to (I’d like to say it was to show her teacher what she enjoyed, but I know full well it was to make her list look longer), and I hesitated. Did a book listened to, I asked here last week, really count as a book “read?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/style/motherlode-audio-book/motherlode-audio-book-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/style/motherlode-audio-book/motherlode-audio-book-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1536, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/style/motherlode-audio-book/motherlode-audio-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/07/style/motherlode-audio-book/motherlode-audio-book-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/07/style/motherlode-audio-book/motherlode-audio-book-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Audio Books Do and Don’t Count for School', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Audiobooks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:29:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d8dacc2d-bb10-551a-8ed8-357456067684', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d8dacc2d-bb10-551a-8ed8-357456067684'}, {'abstract': 'How card playing shaped the Civil War, and how the war shaped cards.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/killing-time/', 'snippet': 'How card playing shaped the Civil War, and how the war shaped cards.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The temptations that will beset you will be very great,” a Mississippi man, already a veteran in the Civil War, warned his newly enlisted younger brother. The evil he warned of wasn’t treason or desertion or theft. It was cards. “Of all the evil practices that abound in Camp, gambling is the most pernicious and fraught with the most direful consequences.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Killing Time', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cards and Card Games', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:30:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jean Huets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jean', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Huets', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/32aa749b-913f-5360-b23f-8899b44f536d', 'word_count': 1494, 'uri': 'nyt://article/32aa749b-913f-5360-b23f-8899b44f536d'}, {'abstract': 'My experience at a Bruce Springsteen concert.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/bonus-tracks-bruuuuuuuuce/', 'snippet': 'My experience at a Bruce Springsteen concert.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This summer, Lizzy Goodman wrote about the New Jersey band the Gaslight Anthem. Her article highlighted the Gaslight Anthem’s anxiety — if you can call it that — of being seen as the heirs to Bruce Springsteen’s legacy. Last weekend, she got to see the real thing. Her dispatch from the floor seats follows. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bonus Tracks: Bruuuuuuuuce!', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Springsteen, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T16:30:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lizzy Goodman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lizzy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Goodman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2b118e31-e6b0-5255-b470-881726107978', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2b118e31-e6b0-5255-b470-881726107978'}, {'abstract': 'You have to ask butchers for a second-cut brisket, but it’s worth the effort.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/dining/the-tender-second-cut-of-brisket-or-deckle.html', 'snippet': 'You have to ask butchers for a second-cut brisket, but it’s worth the effort.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR years, I thought brisket was by nature a somewhat stringy, dry and chewy cut of beef. No matter how exacting the recipe or how careful the cook, the leanness of the meat dictated the texture. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/dining/12APPE_SPAN/12APPE_SPAN-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 383, 'width': 600, 'su