': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Azarenka, Victoria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Errani, Sara', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sharapova, Maria', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Serena', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T18:45:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/52ff7a26-3a68-5ab6-9b9b-e41bf4d60dec', 'word_count': 2816, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52ff7a26-3a68-5ab6-9b9b-e41bf4d60dec'}, {'abstract': 'One veteran, in the waning years of his life, gives another, fresh from war, comfort and perspective that couldn’t be found anywhere else.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/a-soldiers-friendship-shared-across-generations-ends-with-more-gained-than-lost/', 'snippet': 'One veteran, in the waning years of his life, gives another, fresh from war, comfort and perspective that couldn’t be found anywhere else.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My friend Bob Kotlowitz died two weekends ago. He was 87 — lucky enough to live a long, rich life — but he just as easily could’ve been killed before the age of 19. Death is an integral part of an infantryman’s life, floating around us in a thin, grim fog. We’re trained and conditioned to kill, we go to war to kill: that’s the heart of the life. There’s a cosmic balancing act to all this: death is pretty impartial. Our friends are killed in the war — whether they’re shot, blown up or snuffed out in accidents — or during training. It doesn’t get easier, all this dying, even after leaving the Army. After all, we’re still at war and friends still die overseas. At the same time, some, faced with the cumulative weight of all this, seek their own quietus. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Soldiers’ Friendship, Shared Across Generations, Ends With More Gained Than Lost', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Friendship', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fussell, Paul', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kotlowitz, Robert', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Harper's Magazine", 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United States Army', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T18:47:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Farwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Farwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/259997f1-4d2d-5fbe-8fee-60d003d4f8ce', 'word_count': 1411, 'uri': 'nyt://article/259997f1-4d2d-5fbe-8fee-60d003d4f8ce'}, {'abstract': 'The first apartment in New York is the proverbial needle in the haystack.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/realestate/landing-a-first-apartment-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': 'The first apartment in New York is the proverbial needle in the haystack.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOR countless newly minted college graduates, it’s a rite of passage. After the mortarboards have been tossed into the air, they head for New York City, ready to embark on the next chapter of their lives. In 2010, some 40,000 graduates age 22 to 28 moved to the city in search of jobs, according to the latest figures from the Census Bureau’s American Community Survey.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/09COVER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/09COVER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/cover-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/cover-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/09COVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/realestate/09COVER2/09COVER2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type'