print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SoHo (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'MURRAY HILL (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Heights (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:08:25+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Real Estate', 'section_name': 'Real Estate', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/c844ed37-204b-5b01-9395-06f2057459c9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/c844ed37-204b-5b01-9395-06f2057459c9'}, {'abstract': 'Late last month, the still windmill that had served primarily as a symbol in Israel began to turn its sails once again, becoming the second working windmill in the country.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/a-windmill-comes-to-life-in-israel/', 'snippet': 'Late last month, the still windmill that had served primarily as a symbol in Israel began to turn its sails once again, becoming the second working windmill in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With high hopes, the wooden sails of a new white stone windmill in Jerusalem began to turn in 1858. It had been commissioned by a Jewish financier in London who hoped to entice Jews to move from inside the walls of the old city and, once there, to grind their own grain. But the mill soon fell into disuse, probably because newer steam models rendered it obsolete, a historian said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Windmill Comes to Life in Israel', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:10:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joshua Prager', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joshua', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Prager', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/01285f08-d17d-56ee-9335-ef0b026730fe', 'word_count': 565, 'uri': 'nyt://article/01285f08-d17d-56ee-9335-ef0b026730fe'}, {'abstract': 'The first winners of annual awards celebrating Broadway include Chita Rivera and Tommy Tune.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/mayors-office-shines-spotlight-on-broadway/', 'snippet': 'The first winners of annual awards celebrating Broadway include Chita Rivera and Tommy Tune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As part of an effort to celebrate Broadway, which contributes $11 billion to the New York economy, the mayor’s Office of Media and Entertainment has named its first annual Spotlight Award winners. Among them are  Chita Rivera, the actress and dancer; Charles Strouse, the composer of “Bye Bye Birdie” and “Annie”; Arlene Dahl, the actress and author; and Tommy Tune, the actor, director and choreographer.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mayor’s Office Shines Spotlight on Broadway', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dahl, Arlene', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera, Chita', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Strouse, Charles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tune, Tommy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'ACTORS EQUITY ASSN', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Broadway League', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Alliance of Theatrical Stage Employees', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Nederlander Organization', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Times Square and 42nd Street (NYC)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:14:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Heller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89d5aebc-9621-5778-b87a-a391037e9653', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89d5aebc-9621-5778-b87a-a391037e9653'}, {'abstract': 'All wheat-eating cultures have for centuries devised delicious ways to use stale bread, and not only as a means of frugal sustenance.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/stale-bread-a-vehicle-for-a-myriad-of-recipes/', 'snippet': 'All wheat-eating cultures have for centuries devised delicious ways to use stale bread, and not only as a means of frugal sustenance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s City Kitchen column features the Middle Eastern bread salad called fattoush, which utilizes stale pita. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stale Bread, That Versatile Vehicle', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bread', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cooking and Cookbooks', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Tanis, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:15:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Tanis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tanis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3aabae1b-ee16-5e7a-91e2-650dbfdd3319', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3aabae1b-ee16-5e7a-91e2-650dbfdd3319'}, {'abstract': 'A survey of traders suggests that many reptiles and amphibians captured in Indonesia were illegally seized and wind up as Western collectors’ pets.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/indonesias-furtive-snake-trade/', 'snippet': 'A survey of traders suggests that many reptiles and amphibians captured in Indonesia were illegally seized and wind up as Western collectors’ pets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rhinos may be poached for their precious horns and tigers for their skin and bones, but with reptiles it’s often about the allure of possessing live exotic animals. Illegal trading of reptiles and amphibians in Southeast Asia is aimed mainly at stocking the international pet trade. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Indonesia’s Furtive Snake Trade', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'AMPHIBIANS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANIMALS', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reptiles', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Snakes', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Conservation Biology (Journal)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indonesia', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:15:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/756e1267-34e1-5e02-993a-391f78f85adb', 'word_count': 556, 'uri': 'nyt://article/756e1267-34e1-5e02-993a-391f78f85adb'}, {'abstract': 'Save the bully pulpit for later.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/07/not-the-time-to-be-demanding/', 'snippet': 'Save the bully pulpit for later.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve been pounding the drum for Keynesian policies ever since the financial crisis struck; I was one of the few people to talk negatively about Obama’s inaugural address, because it seemed to miss the point that we were suffering from inadequate demand; and I was frantic about the inadequate size of the stimulus.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Not The Time To Be Demanding', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-07T21:18:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f23d420a-c96e-5a4b-aa04-ba7e85f8357d', 'word_count': 196, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f23d420a-c96e-5a4b-aa04-ba7e85f8357d'}, {'abstract': 'What to get the social-media savvy couple who have everything? A wedding hashtag that trends locally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/fashion/on-twitter-making-a-wedding-hashtag-trend.html', 'snippet': 'What to get the social-media savvy couple who have everything? A wedding hashtag that trends locally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT started, as many crazy ideas do, with too much whiskey at the bachelor party. That’s when a groomsman, Ed Dale Jr., decided to create the Twitter hashtag #massewedding and enlist the wedding party, plus as many guests and vendors as possible, to tweet, he said, “about every two minutes” on the day his childhood friend Bryan Masse married Jill Solilak in Winnipeg, Manitoba. ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN/09TWITTERWEDDINGS1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/fashion/09TWITTERWEDDING