aightsets.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/08/live-analysis-u-s-open-mens-semifinals/', 'snippet': 'No. 3 seed Andy Murray faces sixth-seeded Tomas Berdych, then No. 2 seed Novak Djokovic, the defending champion, takes on fourth-seeded David Ferrer.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s hard to imagine that a final four that consists of the Nos. 2, 3, 4, and 6 seeds would be considered a surprise, but that’s the state of the men’s semifinals at the 2012 United States Open. It was the first time in 33 Grand Slam events in which neither Roger Federer nor Rafael Nadal reached the semifinal round, though the cast of remaining characters was hardly an obscure one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Murray Reaches Final; Second Semifinal Is Suspended', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Open (Tennis)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berdych, Tomas', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Djokovic, Novak', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferrer, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Murray, Andy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T14:27:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Rothenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rothenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c531852-1b02-590f-b1c8-abef835536bb', 'word_count': 3766, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c531852-1b02-590f-b1c8-abef835536bb'}, {'abstract': 'A TV ad for Senator Scott P. Brown of Massachusetts, who is in a tight race with his Democratic rival, Elizabeth Warren, features a compliment from President Obama.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/us/politics/senator-scott-p-brown-turns-to-obama-for-campaign-help.html', 'snippet': 'A TV ad for Senator Scott P. Brown of Massachusetts, who is in a tight race with his Democratic rival, Elizabeth Warren, features a compliment from President Obama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAMBRIDGE, Mass. — President Obama is appearing in yet another television commercial in the Massachusetts Senate race. This time, it was not produced by the Democratic candidate, Elizabeth Warren, but by the Republican, Senator Scott P. Brown.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/us/MASSACHUSETTS-1/MASSACHUSETTS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/09/us/MASSACHUSETTS-1/MASSACHUSETTS-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/us/MASSACHUSETTS-1/MASSACHUSETTS-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Hitching His Wagon to Obama’s Star: a Republican Senator', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'G.O.P. Senator Uses President for Help in Ad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Political Advertising', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Scott P', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warren, Elizabeth', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T15:06:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Katharine Q. Seelye', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Katharine', 'middlename': 'Q.', 'lastname': 'Seelye', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/89480ea2-955c-57ff-895e-adf90dd38c53', 'word_count': 746, 'uri': 'nyt://article/89480ea2-955c-57ff-895e-adf90dd38c53'}, {'abstract': 'Battles between the Syrian military and rebels burst a main pipe that delivered drinking water to hundreds of thousands of residents of Aleppo, opposition groups said Saturday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/world/middleeast/syria.html', 'snippet': 'Battles between the Syrian military and rebels burst a main pipe that delivered drinking water to hundreds of thousands of residents of Aleppo, opposition groups said Saturday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIRUT, Lebanon — Clashes between the Syrian military and rebel fighters burst a main pipe that delivered drinking water to hundreds of thousands of residents of Aleppo, opposition groups said Saturday, as the United Nations refugee agency said more than 1.2 million Syrians still inside the country, half of them children, had been displaced from their homes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Clashes Worsen Misery in Syria’s Biggest Cities', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Clashes Worsen Misery In Syria’s Biggest Cities', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T15:08:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick and Hala Droubi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcc5680c-abad-587e-924f-31da0a665929', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcc5680c-abad-587e-924f-31da0a665929'}, {'abstract': 'Times’ reporters provided highlights and analysis of Saturday’s college football action.', 'web_url': 'https://thequad.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/08/live-updates-second-weekend/', 'snippet': 'Times’ reporters provided highlights and analysis of Saturday’s college football action.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The stars remain aligned for the second Saturday of the college football season. Paul Myerberg is in Manhattan. Tim Rohan, Tom Spousta, Zach Schonbrun and Hunter Atkins are scattered across the country, from New Jersey to SEC country, whether in Baton Rouge or College Station, Tex. Keep tabs throughout the day for updates and analysis.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Highlights and Analysis: Second Weekend', 'kicker': 'The Quad', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'College Athletics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T15:18:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Myerberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 