': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Education', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T16:17:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Lichtenstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lichtenstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/5178f152-e347-5f5e-a95e-f55099ce8814', 'word_count': 1015, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5178f152-e347-5f5e-a95e-f55099ce8814'}, {'abstract': 'The producers of one of the most expensive, and troubled, musicals of the fall season are in talks with the investor’s estate to secure the promised money.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/08/rebecca-delayed-again-after-major-investor-dies/', 'snippet': 'The producers of one of the most expensive, and troubled, musicals of the fall season are in talks with the investor’s estate to secure the promised money.', 'lead_paragraph': '3:30 p.m. | Updated One of the most expensive — and troubled — musicals of the Broadway fall season, “Rebecca,” is once again in limbo, with producers announcing on Saturday that a major investor in the show has died and they are in protracted talks with the estate to secure the promised money. The investor was responsible for an investment pool of $4.5 million for the musical, more than one third of its $12 million capitalization costs for Broadway, according to the producers, who declined to divulge the investor’s name.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Rebecca’ Delayed, Again, After Major Investor Dies', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Rebecca’ Is Delayed After Investor Dies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': Non