al': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/3f35b498-c200-51d5-86b7-08c6963b6db5', 'word_count': 1679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3f35b498-c200-51d5-86b7-08c6963b6db5'}, {'abstract': 'The State Department said Ernesto Zedillo should be immune from a federal civil lawsuit filed over a 1997 massacre during his term.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/world/americas/us-moves-to-grant-former-mexican-president-immunity-in-suit.html', 'snippet': 'The State Department said Ernesto Zedillo should be immune from a federal civil lawsuit filed over a 1997 massacre during his term.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEXICO CITY — A former Mexican president who is now a scholar at Yale University should be immune from a civil lawsuit brought against him in the United States in connection with a 1997 massacre during his term, the State Department said Friday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.S. Moves to Grant Former Mexican President Immunity in Suit', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.S. Moves to Grant Former Mexican President Immunity in Suit', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zedillo, Ernesto', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Immunity from Prosecution', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State Department', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T16:41:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Americas', 'byline': {'original': 'By Randal C. Archibold', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Randal', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Archibold', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f78fd26-90fd-5d2e-8916-b074621576b6', 'word_count': 669, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f78fd26-90fd-5d2e-8916-b074621576b6'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s selective use of black speaking styles gives him a linguistic advantage over Mitt Romney.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/opinion/sunday/obama-and-the-racial-politics-of-american-english.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s selective use of black speaking styles gives him a linguistic advantage over Mitt Romney.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TWO aspects of President Obama’s acceptance speech to the Democratic National Convention on Thursday night were of linguistic interest. The first was “signifying” — the use of indirect humor as critique, and a much discussed feature of black speech. “My opponent and his running mate are ... new ... to foreign policy,” he said, adding the two pauses for great comedic effect. The second, and more familiar, was the soaring crescendo, beginning with “in the words of Scripture, ours is a future filled with hope,” in which Mr. Obama demonstrated his strongest mode of linguistic performance — the black preacher style — to end his remarks (“knowing that providence is with us and that we are surely blessed”).', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/sunday-review/09ENGLISH/09ENGLISH-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 372, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/09/sunday-review/09ENGLISH/09ENGLISH-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 372}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/sunday-review/09ENGLISH/09ENGLISH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 631, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': No