ject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sperm', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Men and Boys', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diet and Nutrition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Psychology and Psychologists', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hazardous and Toxic Substances', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Genetics and Heredity', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T17:03:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Shulevitz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shulevitz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9745ac29-e73c-5cd1-b575-e0cf3ddc214d', 'word_count': 2435, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9745ac29-e73c-5cd1-b575-e0cf3ddc214d'}, {'abstract': 'Count on candidates to reference “the issues,” but not explore them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/opinion/sunday/the-issues-yawn.html', 'snippet': 'Count on candidates to reference “the issues,” but not explore them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this or any election year the candidates can be counted on to make regular references in their campaign speeches to “the issues.” It’s important to realize that in doing so they are not actually alluding to the real-world challenges facing Americans. They are alluding to “the issues” — just that, that actual two-word phrase.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Issues? Yawn.', 'kicker': 'Loose Ends', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Issues? Yawn.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-08T17:12:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Javerbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Javerbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/c8b0c6ab-4788-575d-8831-4cd1f331eef1', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c8b0c6ab-4788-575d-8831-4cd1f331eef1'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama’s advisers have indicated that they are eager to re-engage their opponents on their Medicare plan, while the Romney camp would prefer to talk about the economy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/09/us/politics/obama-and-romney-battle-for-votes-in-2-swing-states.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama’s advisers have indicated that they are eager to re-engage their opponents on their Medicare plan, while the Romney camp would prefer to talk about the economy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEMINOLE, Fla. — President Obama on Saturday began hammering away at the Republican ticket’s plans for Medicare, using a campaign swing through Florida, with its large number of retired and elderly voters, to try to turn the page from anemic employment growth, his biggest weakness, to entitlements, a Democratic strength.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/09/us/JP-CANDIDATE-1/JP-CANDIDATE-1-articleLarge.