ty', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Davenport, Steve', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football (College)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Oklahoma State University', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T01:28:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tim Rohan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tim', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rohan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1aca1214-7d4f-53f2-a4d9-c9519c96f83d', 'word_count': 689, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1aca1214-7d4f-53f2-a4d9-c9519c96f83d'}, {'abstract': 'Nations are developing disruptive offensive software — at great potential risk.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/opinion/a-new-kind-of-warfare.html', 'snippet': 'Nations are developing disruptive offensive software — at great potential risk.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Cybersecurity efforts in the United States have largely centered on defending computer networks against attacks by hackers, criminals and foreign governments, mainly China. Increasingly, however, the focus is on developing offensive capabilities, on figuring out how and when the United States might unleash its own malware to disrupt an adversary’s networks. That is potentially dangerous territory. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A New Kind of Warfare', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A New Kind of Warfare', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cyberwarfare', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T01:36:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e59fbe08-b6fd-5f3f-9974-da667d2d2296', 'word_count': 476, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e59fbe08-b6fd-5f3f-9974-da667d2d2296'}, {'abstract': 'With its balanced offense, led by quarterback Alex Smith, and an opportunistic defense, San Francisco contained Green Bay and Aaron Rodgers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/sports/football/49ers-offense-wears-down-packers.html', 'snippet': 'With its balanced offense, led by quarterback Alex Smith, and an opportunistic defense, San Francisco contained Green Bay and Aaron Rodgers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GREEN BAY, Wis. — Even after quarterback Alex Smith directed five fourth-quarter comebacks in last year’s regular season and one more in the playoffs, the San Francisco 49ers emerged with a reputation as a dynamic defensive team with a one-dimensional, functional offense. Smith threw only five interceptions and managed the offense decently, but he certainly was no Aaron Rodgers, the electrifying Green Bay Packers star with whom he shares draft history.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/10/sports/packers/packers-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/10/sports/packers/packers-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/10/sports/packe