: '2012-09-10T14:13:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth A. Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/862f5cec-aafe-5d78-bc60-a90c260dfe1c', 'word_count': 833, 'uri': 'nyt://article/862f5cec-aafe-5d78-bc60-a90c260dfe1c'}, {'abstract': 'At least one other worker was critically injured when a roof or ceiling fell on workers at a building under construction in Fort Greene.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/at-least-2-injured-in-brooklyn-house-collapse/', 'snippet': 'At least one other worker was critically injured when a roof or ceiling fell on workers at a building under construction in Fort Greene.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Updated, 3:51 p.m. | A worker was killed and another seriously injured on Monday when a third-story construction floor overloaded with cinder blocks collapsed beneath the men and they fell 30 feet at an apartment building under construction in Brooklyn, officials said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Worker Killed in Brooklyn Building Collapse', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Fort Greene (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T14:16:17+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Schweber and Andy Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweber', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bba0240c-915e-523d-9d25-c9308d5ea3ea', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bba0240c-915e-523d-9d25-c9308d5ea3ea'}, {'abstract': 'Peyton Manning, making his first start for Denver, had a better Week 1 than Jim Irsay, the Colts’ owner who allowed Manning to leave Indianapolis.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/leading-off-manning-wins-big-irsay-loses-bigger/', 'snippet': 'Peyton Manning, making his first start for Denver, had a better Week 1 than Jim Irsay, the Colts’ owner who allowed Manning to leave Indianapolis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The resounding conclusion of the circus that was Indianapolis’s jettisoning of quarterback Peyton Manning after last season was that the Colts’ owner, Jim Irsay, had better be right about this. He had better be right that Stanford quarterback Andrew Luck would be franchise savior No. 2, as polished and poised as the original. And he’d better be right that Manning was finished because, even if Luck is all that, Colts fans were always going to fume every time Manning engineered a winning drive for the Broncos, were always going to blame Irsay for shipping their beloved hero out of town, were always going to cringe when they thought of the man who cut Peyton Manning.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: Manning Wins Big; Irsay Loses Bigger', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Irsay, Jim', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Manning, Peyton', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Broncos', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Indianapolis Colts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T14:27:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bcdfc1fb-3362-5b8e-bef6-a9afe18d7ce9', 'word_count': 699, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bcdfc1fb-3362-5b8e-bef6-a9afe18d7ce9'}, {'abstract': 'The causes are many, from overfishing to the warming of the seas, and damage is far more dire in some places than others.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/in-the-caribbean-coral-die-off-myriad-wrinkles/', 'snippet': 'The causes are many, from overfishing to the warming of the seas, and damage is far more dire in some places than others.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At its ongoing conference in South Korea, the International Union for Conservation of Nature released a report on Friday indicating that live coral coverage on reefs in the Caribbean has plummeted from nearly 50 percent in the 1970s to less than 10 percent today. Yet describing the entire Caribbean as a region where reefs are in a state of general collapse tends to cloud the problem’s complexity, the study suggests.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In the Caribbean Coral Die-Off, Myriad Wrinkles', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Caribbean Coral, a Complex Picture', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coral', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reefs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Science Foundation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T14:27:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma Bryce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bryce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d30f9c9-665b-569b-8927-2028d5cfe486', 'word_count': 463, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d30f9c9-665b-569b-8927-2028d5cfe486'}, {'abstract': 'Amazon.com and Google are waging a war to become the pre-eminent online mall, Claire Cain Miller and Stephanie Clifford report in Monday’s New York Times. And e-commerce sites large and small are caught in the cross-fire.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/daily-report-google-fights-amazon-for-shoppers-clicks/', 'snippet': 'Amazon.com and Google are waging a war to become the pre-eminent online mall, Claire Cain Miller and Stephanie Clifford report in Monday’s New York Times. And e-commerce sites large and small are caught in the cross-fire.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amazon.com and Google are waging a war to become the pre-eminent online mall, Claire Cain Miller and Stephanie Clifford report in Monday’s New York Times. And e-commerce sites large and small are caught in the cross-fire.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlarge