ind the puzzle, and what he liked about it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Numberplay: Catbird Seat', 'kicker': 'Wordplay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Puzzles', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:00:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Crosswords & Games', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary Antonick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Antonick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ab80844-d34f-5908-a9cd-ea0419bbd7f1', 'word_count': 697, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ab80844-d34f-5908-a9cd-ea0419bbd7f1'}, {'abstract': 'Iranian designers who were born around the time of the Islamic Revolution in 1979 have produced some mesmerizing work.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/designed-in-iran/', 'snippet': 'Iranian designers who were born around the time of the Islamic Revolution in 1979 have produced some mesmerizing work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Nothing has captured my attention as much lately as the graphic design coming out of Iran. While I’ve been familiar with the incredible work of more established designers like Reza Abedini, recently I’ve been mesmerized by the generation of designers who were born around the time of the Islamic Revolution of 1979 — those who grew up at a time when\xa0Iranians were grappling with an Islamic society. You can see this most notably in the work of Homa Delvaray, Iman Raad and Farhad Fozouni, who combine disparate cultural influences as well as technology and traditional applied arts. They utilize Farsi script as imagery, rather than simply as type.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Designed in Iran', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Design', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:01:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By tonya douraghy', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f05f5eb-a586-513e-a021-0af37a7f9cd1', 'word_count': 164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f05f5eb-a586-513e-a021-0af37a7f9cd1'}, {'abstract': 'Top American commanders were absent from the ceremony marking the hand-over of Bagram prison to Afghan control, and even agreement on a crucial point of the signed prison deal itself was elusive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/world/asia/parwan-prison-at-bagram-transferred-to-afghans-at-least-formally.html', 'snippet': 'Top American commanders were absent from the ceremony marking the hand-over of Bagram prison to Afghan control, and even agreement on a crucial point of the signed prison deal itself was elusive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BAGRAM, Afghanistan — It was supposed to be a ceremony to celebrate the full transfer of Afghan detainees and prison operations to Afghan control. But top American commanders were absent on Monday, hundreds of Afghan prisoners remained under American detention, and details of the deal remained in dispute.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/11/world/asia/11bagram-span/11bagram-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 395, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/11/world/asia/11bagram-span/11bagram-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeh