ppet': 'Filip Peliwo of Canada beat Liam Broady of Britain to win his second juniors singles Grand Slam title of the year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Filip Peliwo, the son of Polish immigrants to Canada, capped an outstanding year by winning the 2012 United States Open junior singles title on Sunday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Junior Champion Is First to Reach 4 Slam Finals Since 1984', 'kicker': 'Straight Sets', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:24:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Tennis', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/15f9f8e0-c827-5b38-bb80-26566d1d5e48', 'word_count': 301, 'uri': 'nyt://article/15f9f8e0-c827-5b38-bb80-26566d1d5e48'}, {'abstract': 'A recent appeals court decision may result in a test of whether the S.E.C. can win an insider trading verdict by putting on a circumstantial case built around a well-timed trade and contacts with an insider.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/can-s-e-c-win-an-old-fashioned-insider-case/', 'snippet': 'A recent appeals court decision may result in a test of whether the S.E.C. can win an insider trading verdict by putting on a circumstantial case built around a well-timed trade and contacts with an insider.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over thirty years ago, the actor John Houseman intoned about brokerage firm Smith Barney: “They make money the old-fashioned way. They earn it.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can the S.E.C. Win an Insider Case the Old-Fashioned Way?', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insider Trading', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'GE Capital Corp', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Securities and Exchange Commission', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'SunSource International Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:25:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d6b732ea-77e7-579d-8f16-360144359372', 'word_count': 954, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d6b732ea-77e7-579d-8f16-360144359372'}, {'abstract': 'Eleven years after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks, a whole generation has grown up with a defining moment — and a reason to serve.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/millennials-in-uniform-or-not-come-of-age-in-the-911-decade/', 'snippet': 'Eleven years after the Sept. 11 terrorist attacks, a whole generation has grown up with a defining moment — and a reason to serve.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Raise your hand if you know someone serving in the military,” my political anthropology professor asked one day during a senior seminar. I looked around the room and saw a few scattered hands in the air, then down at my own, firmly planted in my lap. It was the fall of 2007, and the United States military had been engaged in two theaters of war for the better part of a decade. I was about to graduate with a degree in global security, and I could not think of a single person I knew who was serving — nor had the thought even crossed my mind until the question was posed so directly. While public service was featured prominently in my schools and community growing up, and certainly among my peers in college, the thought of service was always relegated to the civilian sphere — Peace Corps, Teach for America, infrastructure projects in South America. Military service, though not portrayed in a negative way, was simply absent from discussions of post-graduation endeavors.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Millennials, in Uniform or Not, Come of Age in the 9/11 Decade', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'World Trade Center (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:26:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Julia L. Stern', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Julia', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Stern', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4d1e3e76-67e3-5635-bb56-704be3f7cfd9', 'word_count': 1056, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4d1e3e76-67e3-5635-bb56-704be3f7cfd9'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Ioulex.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/photos-of-the-moment-diane-von-furstenburg/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Ioulex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of The Moment | Diane Von Furstenburg', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T16:30:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By ioulex', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/74f3195e-c6e9-57c5-a96d-5fb16d109339', 'word_count': 41, 'uri': 'nyt://article/74f3195e-c6e9-57c5-a96d-5fb16d109339'}, {'abstract': 'Mitt Romney suggests that President Obama has taken sides in the Chicago labor dispute.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/romney-criticizes-teachers-union-and-obama/', 'snippet': 'Mitt Romney suggests that President Obama has taken sides in the Chicago labor dispute.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney issued a statement Monday criticizing the teachers’ union in Chicago for going on strike, and faulted President Obama and Vice President Joseph R. Biden Jr. for their words in support of labor, suggesting that the president has already taken sides in the fight.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Criticizes Teachers’ Union and Obama', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Biden, Joseph R Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Teachers Union', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name':