desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Global Business', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew E. Kramer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'E.', 'lastname': 'Kramer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2bf66b6-83cf-578b-935d-3167bd3c24e1', 'word_count': 1331, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2bf66b6-83cf-578b-935d-3167bd3c24e1'}, {'abstract': 'A round-up of superior dry shampoos that do double duty as waterless cleaning machines and styling aids.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/samurai-shopper-high-and-dry/', 'snippet': 'A round-up of superior dry shampoos that do double duty as waterless cleaning machines and styling aids.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Samurai Shopper takes her skin care very seriously, but hair care is barely a blip on the radar. I’m more focused on fighting follicles that bloom outside the scalp zone; when fuzz attacks, I’m already in the lounge chair at Browhaus, 56 Spring Street. The hair on my head simply requires occasional pruning, regular washing and daily brushing. Every so often, though, a hair product comes along that proves noteworthy. Take dry shampoo: apparently I’m the last earthling to discover that dry shampoo does double duty as a waterless cleaning machine and a styling aid. Best of all, the cheap ones are pretty good, even if the good ones are better.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Samurai Shopper | High and Dry', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T18:14:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By S.S. Fair', 'person': [{'firstname': 'S.', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Fair', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f5b5eb6-b826-54e7-9870-a85cdac6244d', 'word_count': 609, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f5b5eb6-b826-54e7-9870-a85cdac6244d'}, {'abstract': 'For Emilio Morenatti, who lost his foot in Afghanistan in 2009, photographing the Paralympics for The Associated Press was a special opportunity to work, honoring disabled athletes whose challenges resemble his own.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/an-empathetic-eye-on-the-paralympics/', 'snippet': 'For Emilio Morenatti, who lost his foot in Afghanistan in 2009, photographing the Paralympics for The Associated Press was a special opportunity to work, honoring disabled athletes whose challenges resemble his own.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first Paralympic Games since 2009, when Emilio Morenatti lost his foot to a roadside bomb in Afghanistan, ended in London on Sunday night. For Mr. Morenatti, who does not consider himself a sports photographer, the Paralympics were a priority, a more personal assignment that better connected him with a community he recently joined.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Empathetic Eye on the Paralympics', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Olympic Games', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Paralympic Games', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostheses', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morenatti, Emilio', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Associated Press', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T18:15:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multi