ew architecture prize by the University of California at Berkeley.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The architect Deborah Berke has been selected as the first recipient of a new $100,000 prize that honors the advancement of women in architecture. The inaugural 2012 Berkeley-Rupp Architecture Professorship and Prize, awarded by the University of California, Berkeley’s College of Environmental Design, was announced Monday. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York Architect Selected by Berkeley for Prize', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'New York Architect Selected For New $100,000 Prize', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARCHITECTURE', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Contests and Prizes', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of California, Berkeley', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T20:47:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Pogrebin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pogrebin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5a35d7c8-489a-5d3c-9437-60c57bb6e733', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5a35d7c8-489a-5d3c-9437-60c57bb6e733'}, {'abstract': 'Some Ohio Republicans say he does.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/does-mitt-romney-deserve-credit-for-bin-ladens-death/', 'snippet': 'Some Ohio Republicans say he does.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The obvious answer to the question in the headline is no, he doesn’t. But Public Policy Polling has a new survey suggesting that six percent of Ohioans, and 15 percent of Ohio Republicans, think Mitt Romney “deserves more credit” for the killing of Osama bin Laden than Barack Obama.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Does Mitt Romney Deserve Credit for Bin Laden’s Death?', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'bin Laden, Osama', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T20:56:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/362a0fb6-4dda-564a-b935-f20595afa618', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/362a0fb6-4dda-564a-b935-f20595afa618'}, {'abstract': 'Trailing after the convention, the Republican nominee needs to emulate George W. Bush in 2004.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/romneys-post-convention-predicament/', 'snippet': 'Trailing after the convention, the Republican nominee needs to emulate George W. Bush in 2004.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To my admittedly non-expert eye, today’s 7-day rolling averages from Gallup and Rasmussen suggests that President Obama’s polling numbers leaped upward after Bill Clinton’s epic speech (and maybe Michelle Obama’s as well) at last week’s Democratic convention and have held pretty much steady since. If the Democratic ticket had received extra boost from the president’s own remarks last Thursday, one would have expected his numbers to keep climbing over the weekend. Instead, his Monday advantage looks a lot like his Saturday advantage, which suggests either that 1) viewers were as underwhelmed as I was by Obama’s performance, 2) there’s a ceiling on the president’s poll numbers that no bounce can break through, or 3) Friday’s mediocre jobs number prevented the bounce from bouncing higher.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney’s Post-Convention Predicament', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T21:03:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/66c07420-fda8-5456-9089-f5ab482e1575', 'word_count': 625, 'uri': 'nyt://article/66c07420-fda8-5456-9089-f5ab482e1575'}, {'abstract': 'Oh noes — we’ve been found out.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/10/stalinists-on-the-scrimmage-line/', 'snippet': 'Oh noes — we’ve been found out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I gotta say, it’s hard being an enemy of American values when George Will is on the case. Last year he uncovered our plot to undermine individualism by making people ride trains; now he’s uncovered our long-term strategy of inculcating collectivist values through, um, college football.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stalinists on the Scrimmage Line', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-10T21:04:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_