ey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yaccino', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8bc6ecbd-a630-5e66-a56e-0b1e46a6adef', 'word_count': 1122, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8bc6ecbd-a630-5e66-a56e-0b1e46a6adef'}, {'abstract': 'People magazine is addressing a slump in single copy sales with what executives are calling their most comprehensive advertising campaign to date.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/ad-campaign-will-encourage-people-to-love-people/', 'snippet': 'People magazine is addressing a slump in single copy sales with what executives are calling their most comprehensive advertising campaign to date.', 'lead_paragraph': 'People magazine is addressing a slump in single copy sales – issues sold one by one, in venues like newsstands and supermarkets, rather than by annual subscription — with what executives are calling their most comprehensive advertising campaign to date.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ad Campaign Will Encourage People to ‘Love People’', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ADVERTISING AND MARKETING', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Magazines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'People (Magazine)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Time Inc', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T13:00:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stuart Elliott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stuart', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Elliott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0e2023aa-e6fe-5c7e-bc0f-8a7db8126f72', 'word_count': 914, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0e2023aa-e6fe-5c7e-bc0f-8a7db8126f72'}, {'abstract': 'The centuries-old scientific and engineering idea of progress through observing, modeling, testing and modifying is under attack. Now it is better to collect and examine lots of data, looking for patterns, and follow up on the most promising. The latest example: Autodesk says designers should generate a thousand product versions.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/the-brute-force-computing-revolution/', 'snippet': 'The centuries-old scientific and engineering idea of progress through observing, modeling, testing and modifying is under attack. Now it is better to collect and examine lots of data, looking for patterns, and follow up on the most promising. The ...', 'lead_paragraph': ' Autodesk has announced that it is selling design simulation software that works online, via Amazon’s AWS cloud service. This continues the company’s move into a lower-paying, hopefully higher-volume, world of mobile devices and cloud computing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/11/technology/11bits-bass/11bits-bass-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/11/technology/11bits-bass/11bits-bass-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Brute Force Computing Revolution', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cloud