'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mumbai (India)', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'United States', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T13:02:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b2ff4c42-5b23-5aea-9b99-58dacf01c785', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2ff4c42-5b23-5aea-9b99-58dacf01c785'}, {'abstract': 'Also, YouTube has a new app for the iPhone, but you have to download it; and as the curtain comes up on Katie Couric’s new daytime talk show, the battle for inheriting Oprah Winfrey’s mantle begins in earnest.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/the-breakfast-meeting-foxconns-use-of-interns-and-good-nipples-bad-nipples/', 'snippet': 'Also, YouTube has a new app for the iPhone, but you have to download it; and as the curtain comes up on Katie Couric’s new daytime talk show, the battle for inheriting Oprah Winfrey’s mantle begins in earnest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apple’s Chinese manufacturing partner, Foxconn Technology, has come under criticism recently after reports of students being forced to “intern” at the company’s factories that make iPhones and their components, David Barboza and Charles Duhigg report. The reliance on so-called interns — including those studying law or English, worker advocates say — is particularly high as Foxconn attempts to meet demand for the new iPhone 5. “They don’t want to work there — they want to learn,” one advocate said. “But if they don’t work, they are told they will not graduate, because it is a very busy time with the new iPhone coming, and Foxconn does not have enough workers without the students.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Foxconn’s Use of ‘Interns,’ and Good Nipples, Bad Nipples', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T13:03:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/740ac28b-7030-597e-a5e6-55585e713491', 'word_count': 336, 'uri': 'nyt://article/740ac28b-7030-597e-a5e6-55585e713491'}, {'abstract': 'The charge comes after Japan bought the islands in the East China Sea for nearly $30 million from private owners.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/world/asia/china-accuses-japan-of-stealing-disputed-islands.html', 'snippet': 'The charge comes after Japan bought the islands in the East China Sea for nearly $30 million from private owners.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TIANJIN, China — The Chinese government accused Japan on Tuesday of stealing a group of disputed islands in the East China Sea, hours after the Japanese government announced that it had bought them from their private Japanese owners for nearly $30 million.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/world/ISLANDS/ISLANDS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 826, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/world/ISLANDS/ISLANDS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1653, 'width': 2048, 'subTyp