st across Maryland’s Pleasant Valley on Sept. 11, 1862, Polly Yarrow could see men in Confederate gray coming through Brownsville and Crampton’s Gaps in South Mountain and just a few miles from the 10,000 Union troops garrisoned in and around Harpers Ferry. If the Confederates attacked, which seemed likely, they would take the road that ran by her front door. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ms. Yarrow and the Road to Antietam', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blacks', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Slavery', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Stonewall', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T16:30:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By James H. Johnston', 'person': [{'firstname': 'James', 'middlename': 'H.', 'lastname': 'Johnston', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/729aff66-96f7-57a8-b8c4-2be77dc0e098', 'word_count': 1771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/729aff66-96f7-57a8-b8c4-2be77dc0e098'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Samantha Casolari.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/photos-of-the-moment-marc-jacobs-8/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Samantha Casolari.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLi