b24d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/73705b68-5f5c-5d1c-bce7-dca9385cb24d'}, {'abstract': 'The Midnight Madness program at the Toronto International Film Festival includes a mixture of blood, action and laughs.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/what-happens-after-midnight-in-toronto/', 'snippet': 'The Midnight Madness program at the Toronto International Film Festival includes a mixture of blood, action and laughs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TORONTO — “Would you like to see a movie with lots of naked witches worshiping goats?” asked Rob Zombie, the musician and filmmaker, as he introduced his latest movie, “The Lords of Salem,” on Monday night. For the amped-up audience at that midnight screening, there was only one answer to that question.Carnage, mayhem and rowdy crowds are the order of the evening during the Toronto International Film Festival’s Midnight Madness program. Those willing to stay up until the wee hours will have the pleasure (or displeasure) of seeing a stream of blood-soaked genre films unspool each night of the festival.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Happens After Midnight in Toronto …', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toronto International Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McDonagh, Martin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zombie, Rob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T18:27:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mekado Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mekado', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ae74bd7-d1a4-52a4-a218-d4c41ff1f3a9', 'word_c