_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'College Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matt Krupnick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matt', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krupnick', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e8527b08-b218-55c2-882e-afaa480a179b', 'word_count': 1149, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e8527b08-b218-55c2-882e-afaa480a179b'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Ioulex.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/photos-of-the-moment-zero-maria-cornejo-5/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the New York fashion week photo diary of Ioulex.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of The Moment | Zero + Maria Cornejo', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cornejo, Maria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:01:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By ioulex', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d7b09ed1-a924-5ffd-b5f5-7937b5a95578', 'word_count': 40, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d7b09ed1-a924-5ffd-b5f5-7937b5a95578'}, {'abstract': '“Step Up to the Plate,” about the chef Michel Bras and his son, Sébastien, and “Three Stars,” about the highest Michelin rating, are opening in New York this month.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/two-new-films-peer-into-celebrated-kitchens/', 'snippet': '“Step Up to the Plate,” about the chef Michel Bras and his son, Sébastien, and “Three Stars,” about the highest Michelin rating, are opening in New York this month.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the opening scenes in “Step Up to the Plate,” an 89-minute documentary by Paul Lacoste about the Michelin three-star restaurant Bras Michel et Sébastien, is a display of Mr. Bras and his son, Sébastien, arranging dozens of herbs and pieces of vegetables in a dish, seemingly random but ever so carefully composed, called gargouillou (pronounced gar-goo-YOU). As each leaf and strip is placed, they name the item. (Sorrel, celery leaf, pea shoots, red amaranth, valerian, broom, sesame powder and bear’s garlic were among those used in the month of May.) The salad has become a signature of the restaurant.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Two New Films Peer Into Celebrated Kitchens', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bras, Michel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Michelin Group', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:04:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ccb40d1-4be8-5cc5-af58-4b5200de3198', 'word_count': 544, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ccb40d1-4be8-5cc5-af58-4b5200de3198'}, {'abstract': 'Included in the former secretary of defense’s possessions are the flag from his office and original drawings of editorial cartoons about him, many of them unfavorable.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/sothebys-to-auction-robert-mcnamara-memorabilia/', 'snippet': 'Included in the former secretary of defense’s possessions are the flag from his office and original drawings of editorial cartoons about him, many of them unfavorable.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Robert S. McNamara, secretary of defense during both the Kennedy and Johnson administrations, turns out to have been a meticulous pack rat. Next month Sotheby’s is auctioning off his personal memorabilia, including letters, many of them annotated in McNamara’s own hand; photographs; the flag and globe from his office at the Defense Department; a collection of North Vietnamese propaganda; crib notes he made before appearing in “The Fog of War,” the Errol Morris documentary; and three albums containing the original drawings of editorial cartoons about him, a great many of them unfavorable.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sotheby’s to Auction Robert McNamara Memorabilia', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sotheby’s to Auction Memorabilia Collected by Robert McNamara', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Auctions', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memorabilia and Souvenirs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McNamara, Robert S', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Morris, Errol', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Onassis, Jacqueline Kennedy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Defense Department', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': "Sotheby's", 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:09:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charles McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charles', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a1e3c3b-282c-5820-bcb6-b36adb211954', 'word_count': 252, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a1e3c3b-282c-5820-bcb6-b36adb211954'}, {'abstract': 'Buildings under construction at a recycling site in Brooklyn are being raised four feet beyond legal requirements so they can withstand flooding in decades to come.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/an-ounce-of-prevention-in-new-york-city/', 'snippet': 'Buildings under construction at a recycling site in Brooklyn are being raised four feet beyond legal requirements so they can withstand flooding in decades to come.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As I note in my article about climate change, rising seas and the risks for New York City, many local governments in the United States are drafting contingency plans to address the prospect of more severe flooding in coming decades.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Ounce of Prevention in New York City', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Global Warming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Levees and Dams', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recycling of Waste Materials', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Outerbridge, Thomas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Brooklyn (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'SUNSET PARK (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:12:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mireya Navarro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mireya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Navarro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a30b31b7-8d38-56a0-9acc-404139a87c8e', 'word_count': 576, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a30b31b7-8d38-56a0-9acc-404139a87c8e'}, {'abstract': 'If the Kurt Eichenwald Op-Ed does not break any new ground, why did The Times publish it all?', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/911-coverage-part-2-with-little-new-to-say-should-media-say-it-anyway/', 'snippet': 'If the Kurt Eichenwald Op-Ed does not break any new ground, why did The Times publish it all?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Kurt Eichenwald’s Op-Ed piece on Tuesday about the Bush administration’s response to warnings about Al Qaeda’s plans generated response among some readers, who thought that it was important enough to be treated as news, not opinion. “Why is NYT op-ed on 9-11 warnings an op-ed and not a news article?” the ProPublica editor Eric Umansky asked on Twitter.\xa0 Its author, a former New York Times reporter, has written “500 Days: Secrets and Lies in the Terror Wars,” and is a contributing editor at Vanity Fair.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '9/11 Coverage, Part 2: With Little New to Say, Should Media Say It, Anyway?', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:14:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5189a2f7-f9a1-5119-8d2d-052362fc2d67', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5189a2f7-f9a1-5119-8d2d-052362fc2d67'}, {'abstract': 'Speaking to National Guard members in Reno, Nev., Mitt Romney expressed gratitude for troops serving overseas and said the defense budget should not be cut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/us/politics/after-criticism-romney-thanks-armed-forces.html', 'snippet': 'Speaking to National Guard members in Reno, Nev., Mitt Romney expressed gratitude for troops serving overseas and said the defense budget should not be cut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'RENO, Nev. — Facing criticism for failing to mention American troops or the Afghan war effort in his convention speech, Mitt Romney spoke before National Guard members on Tuesday and called for robust support of the nation’s armed forces, saying that “the return of our troops cannot and must not be used as an excuse to hollow out our military through devastating defense budget cuts.”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1424, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/us/12romney-3/12romney-3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'After Criticism of His Convention Speech, Romney Thanks Nation’s Armed Forces', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'After Criticism of His Convention Speech, Romney Thanks Nation’s Armed Forces', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Republican National Convention', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Budget (US)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:16:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f41952b-44bd-53bf-9536-32c17da2f7eb', 'word_count': 847, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f41952b-44bd-53bf-9536-32c17da2f7eb'}, {'abstract': 'On an occasion when many Americans remember that terrible day in 2001, one Afghan asks that we remember his country too.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/eleven-years-after-911-afghanistan-still-matters/', 'snippet': 'On an occasion when many Americans remember that terrible day in 2001, one Afghan asks that we remember his country too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My family and I were refugees in Pakistan on Sept. 11, 2001. Like most Afghan refugees, we did not pay particular attention to world news. In fact, many refugees did not even own a television so they could watch what was happening in the world. Little did we know that the tragic events of that day would drastically change the lives of millions of Afghans.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Eleven Years After 9/11, Afghanistan Still Matters', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Afghanistan War (2001- )', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'September 11 (2001)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Terrorism', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:18:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Javid Ahmad', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Javid', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ahmad', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dc88e482-b6d9-54db-863d-d91ac98fec73', 'word_count': 949, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dc88e482-b6d9-54db-863d-d91ac98fec73'}, {'abstract': 'The sisters Kate and Laura Mulleavy of Rodarte packed a lot of energy into collages of guipure and jacquard with metal cages, built-in jewelry and panels of silk printed with an ivy trellis, on evening gowns and short strapless dresses.', 'web_url': 'https://runway.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/rodarte-from-ivy-league-to-leather-pants/', 'snippet': 'The sisters Kate and Laura Mulleavy of Rodarte packed a lot of energy into collages of guipure and jacquard with metal cages, built-in jewelry and panels of silk printed with an ivy trellis, on evening gowns and short strapless dresses.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Minutes before the Rodarte show was to begin this afternoon, Jennifer Graylock, one of the veteran front-row photographers, stopped by to hand out business cards for one of her side ventures, what she describes as a line of art furniture. Ms. Graylock takes portraits of celebrities and prints them on the seats of chairs, you see. The concept is called “Sit on My Face,” and you can imagine that a designer might adapt it to a runway show, so that editors could find their places by looking for their mugs in the front row. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rodarte: From Ivy League to Leather Pants', 'kicker': 'On the Runway', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Fashion Week', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dunst, Kirsten', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulleavy, Kate', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mulleavy, Laura', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rodarte', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:20:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Wilson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wilson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4567f12f-d946-5c3b-8209-85537f7c2af4', 'word_count': 219, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4567f12f-d946-5c3b-8209-85537f7c2af4'}, {'abstract': 'Adnan Farhan Abdul Latif, a Yemeni man, was ordered freed in 2010 by a Federal District Court judge but remained in captivity after the ruling was overturned.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/us/politics/detainee-who-died-at-guantanamo-had-release-blocked-by-court.html', 'snippet': 'Adnan Farhan Abdul Latif, a Yemeni man, was ordered freed in 2010 by a Federal District Court judge but remained in captivity after the ruling was overturned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The detainee who died on Saturday at the American military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba, was a Yemeni man who had been ordered freed in 2010 by a Federal District Court judge but remained in captivity after the ruling was overturned by the Court of Appeals for the District of Columbia Circuit last year. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Military Identifies Guantánamo Detainee Who Died', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Military Identifies Guantánamo Detainee Who Died', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Guantanamo Bay Naval Base (Cuba)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Detainees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Latif, Adnan Farhan Abdul', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Fatalities)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:20:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Charlie Savage', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Charlie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Savage', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acd4f5e6-0df2-5e65-b6af-65cfbf1e89b2', 'word_count': 838, 'uri': 'nyt://article/acd4f5e6-0df2-5e65-b6af-65cfbf1e89b2'}, {'abstract': 'Paula Creamer traveled all night Monday to arrive at the Women’s British Open, distancing herself from her recent loss to Jiyai Shin in the longest playoff between two goflers in L.P.G.A. history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/sports/golf/after-long-playoff-paula-creamer-travels-to-womens-british-open.html', 'snippet': 'Paula Creamer traveled all night Monday to arrive at the Women’s British Open, distancing herself from her recent loss to Jiyai Shin in the longest playoff between two goflers in L.P.G.A. history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'HOYLAKE, England — Roughly 23 hours after her par putt spun out of the cup at Kingsmill Resort in Williamsburg, Va., Paula Creamer stuck her tee in the ground on the fourth hole at Royal Liverpool Golf Club on the Wirral Peninsula. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 712, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1424, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/sports/golf/12golf-pic/12golf-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Late Arrival to British Open Has Good Excuse', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Late Arrival to Women’s British Open Has Good Excuse', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Women's British Open (Golf)", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Creamer, Paula', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shin, Jiyai', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:20:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4235c4d7-5aa7-5cf4-a609-b0412e48bf91', 'word_count': 410, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4235c4d7-5aa7-5cf4-a609-b0412e48bf91'}, {'abstract': 'In this week’s links: a science of ethics; a yearning for Rome and reality; and the importance of parody.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/stone-links-answerable-questions/', 'snippet': 'In this week’s links: a science of ethics; a yearning for Rome and reality; and the importance of parody.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At The Guardian, a lengthy exchange between Julian Baggini and Lawrence Krauss on how philosophy relates to science. Krauss, a physicist, takes the view that all answerable questions are empirical; any philosophical question that turns out to be answerable, then, is in fact a disguised (frequently nascent) scientific one. Baggini contends that ethical questions are the great counterexample to Krauss’s reductive view. They cannot be settled by any simple appeal to empirical facts (concerning biology or evolutionary psychology, say); yet we do not consider them unanswerable or fit merely for idle philosophical debate. (One also wonders what Krauss would say about mathematical knowledge, which is, on most views, nonempirical.) Krauss, however, digs his heels in, arguing that ethical questions can indeed be settled empirically: “Ultimately, I think our understanding of neurobiology and evolutionary biology and psychology will reduce our understanding of morality to some well-defined biological constructs.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Stone Links: Answerable Questions', 'kicker': 'The Stone', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Science and Technology', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scalia, Antonin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rome (Italy)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:24:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark de Silva', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'de Silva', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0bd2071b-3343-5ddf-8839-c96ee720973f', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0bd2071b-3343-5ddf-8839-c96ee720973f'}, {'abstract': 'The head of Digital First Media Group has been heavily criticized since his Journal Register Company filed for bankruptcy last week, but he asserted that it was “the absolute right thing to do.”', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/for-paton-bankruptcy-for-journal-register-is-embarrassing-but-necessary/', 'snippet': 'The head of Digital First Media Group has been heavily criticized since his Journal Register Company filed for bankruptcy last week, but he asserted that it was “the absolute right thing to do.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'The last few days have provided a lesson in humility, John Paton acknowledged.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Paton, Bankruptcy for Journal Register Is ‘Embarrassing’ but Necessary', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bankruptcies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'News and News Media', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Paton, John', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Columbia Journalism Review', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Denver Post', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Journal Register Co', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New Haven Register', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pension Benefit Guaranty Corp', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:25:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christine Haughney', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haughney', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d852f3dd-e79c-51c6-8ee9-eb293077d028', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d852f3dd-e79c-51c6-8ee9-eb293077d028'}, {'abstract': 'Or maybe not.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/exploding-government/', 'snippet': 'Or maybe not.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I gather that Rand Paul has made a lame effort to defend his claim that we’ve seen “exploding” government under Obama by claiming that he only meant the federal workforce, which has gone up slightly even as state and local employment has fallen. But as I’ve pointed out, the vast majority of government workers actually are at the state and local level. (And yes, the feds have an influence: the Recovery Act contained substantial aid to lower levels of government, which should have been continued). ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Exploding Government', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:38:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/86b8b825-18b9-5181-b2b4-7d649642fd9a', 'word_count': 161, 'uri': 'nyt://article/86b8b825-18b9-5181-b2b4-7d649642fd9a'}, {'abstract': 'The death of Juanita Hawkins, who watched over the writer’s mother for more than 35 years, conjures memories and gratitude.', 'web_url': 'https://newoldage.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/the-caregiver/', 'snippet': 'The death of Juanita Hawkins, who watched over the writer’s mother for more than 35 years, conjures memories and gratitude.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I was driving through Kansas, taking the kids back to college, when my cellphone buzzed. It was my sister-in-law letting me know that Juanita Hawkins had died.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Caregiver', 'kicker': 'The New Old Age', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elder Care', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T19:38:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Judith Graham', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Judith', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Graham', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceed74ad-94e6-5b64-a337-2db97d0c6cd0', 'word_count': 1057, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceed74ad-94e6-5b64-a337-2db97d0c6cd0'}, {'abstract': 'Made commercially since the 1920s, Japanese whiskeys are gaining ground in the United States.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/dining/japanese-whiskeys-gain-ground-in-america.html', 'snippet': 'Made commercially since the 1920s, Japanese whiskeys are gaining ground in the United States.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ONE of the most expensive cocktails in New York can be found at Ryu, the Japanese-inspired restaurant that opened last spring in the meatpacking district. It’s a Sazerac variation called Shogun’s Grip and it’s ticketed like a four-star entree: $35. Adam Schuman, then the beverage director, had a good excuse for the stiff tariff. Its base is 18-year-old Yamazaki, the Japanese single malt made by Suntory that can cost $140 a bottle.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multim