organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cf173f1d-18ed-5cef-b05b-b3efc988904e', 'word_count': 311, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cf173f1d-18ed-5cef-b05b-b3efc988904e'}, {'abstract': 'American Spoon, in northern Michigan, makes its jams, syrups and sauces from local fruit, some of it gathered in the wild.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/dining/producing-preserves-from-fruit-in-northern-michigan.html', 'snippet': 'American Spoon, in northern Michigan, makes its jams, syrups and sauces from local fruit, some of it gathered in the wild.', 'lead_paragraph': 'American Spoon was ahead of the curve when it comes to the artisanal movement. Justin Rashid founded the company in northern Michigan in 1982, in partnership with the chef Larry Forgione, and started making preserves from local fruit, some of it gathered in the wild. Over the years the company prospered and expanded its line to include lightly sweetened spoon fruits, conserves, fruit butters, savory jams, syrups, sauces and mustards. This year, some new items include rich triple berry conserve (strawberries, black raspberries and blueberries), which will prolong the taste of summer. Also notable is rare tart-sweet wild thimbleberry jam. Whole-seed mustard will enhance a plate of braised pork shank once fall sets in. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/dining/12STUFF1_JAMSPAN/12STUFF1_JAMSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/12/dining/12STUFF1_JAMSPAN/12STUFF1_JAMSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/dining/12STUFF1_JAMSPAN/12STUFF1-jumbo.jpg', 'heigh