cing', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-11T21:54:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian Seibert', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Seibert', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/986b72ba-0250-52f2-b0ca-73357ae053d4', 'word_count': 1024, 'uri': 'nyt://article/986b72ba-0250-52f2-b0ca-73357ae053d4'}, {'abstract': '“I’m Carolyn Parker” is an account of a woman’s five-year effort to rebuild her house in the Lower Ninth Ward of New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/movies/im-carolyn-parker-a-documentary-by-jonathan-demme.html', 'snippet': '“I’m Carolyn Parker” is an account of a woman’s five-year effort to rebuild her house in the Lower Ninth Ward of New Orleans after Hurricane Katrina.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jane Jacobs would have loved Carolyn Parker. Honestly, it’s hard to imagine anyone not liking Ms. Parker, though there are those who might be intimidated by her toughness or overwhelmed by her energy. But Jacobs, the great 20th-century philosopher-evangelist of urban life, would surely recognize this retired restaurant cook, a resident of the Lower Ninth Ward in New Orleans and the subject of Jonathan Demme’s marvelous new documentary, as an indispensable “public character.” With her stubborn attachment to place, her vigilant, sympathetic “eyes on the street” and her unsentimental civic-mindedness, Ms. Parker is the exactly the kind of person Jacobs identified as crucial to the life of great American cities.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/arts/12carolyn/12carolyn-articleLarge.jpg', 