rs and officials need to cool off and become more cooperative, or can needed change come only through confrontation? What approach would retain the best teachers?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/roomfordebate/2012/09/11/must-teachers-and-school-officials-be-foes/', 'snippet': 'Do relations between teachers and officials need to cool off and become more cooperative, or can needed change come only through confrontation? What approach would retain the best teachers?', 'lead_paragraph': '', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Must Teachers and School Officials Be Foes?', 'kicker': 'Room for Debate', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T02:23:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f1ed22c-edf6-5f7c-91ca-59fc9db96b7c', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f1ed22c-edf6-5f7c-91ca-59fc9db96b7c'}, {'abstract': 'Television ads for the president will start Thursday, a sign that the state is more competitive than his campaign had once expected.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/11/obama-plans-wisconsin-advertising-signaling-the-state-is-now-in-play/', 'snippet': 'Television ads for the president will start Thursday, a sign that the state is more competitive than his campaign had once expected.', 'lead_paragraph': '“On, Wisconsin!”', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Obama Plans Wisconsin Advertising, Signaling the State Is Now in Play', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Obama Ad Campaign: In Wisconsin, Acting to Hold On', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T02:24:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Zeleny', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zeleny', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dee70b3d-414c-5cc0-902a-f46983d70436', 'word_count': 251, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dee70b3d-414c-5cc0-902a-f46983d70436'}, {'abstract': 'Questions have arisen as to why two prominent agents were not mentioned in the Mitchell report on steroid use in baseball, even when players they represented were implicated.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/sports/baseball/questions-surround-agents-absent-from-steroid-report.html', 'snippet': 'Questions have arisen as to why two prominent agents were not mentioned in the Mitchell report on steroid use in baseball, even when players they represented were implicated.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Since George J. Mitchell released his report nearly five years ago on the use of steroids in baseball, he has been repeatedly praised by members of Congress, Commissioner Bud Selig and others for conducting a robust and independent investigation that produced the most comprehensive account of how steroids soiled the sport.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Questions Surround Agents Absent From Steroids Report', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Questions Surround Two Agents Absent From Report on Steroid Use', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mitchell, George J', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Levinson, Seth (Sports Agent)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doping (Sports)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Steroids', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value'