said was a major disability fraud scheme at the Long Island Rail Road.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ten more retired Long Island Rail Road workers have been charged with federal crimes, accused of falsely claiming to be disabled so they could obtain federal disability pensions in addition to their railroad pensions in what prosecutors have called a huge fraud scheme, the authorities announced on Wednesday.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '26', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'More Arrests of Retirees in Fraud Case at L.I.R.R.', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'More Arrests Of Retirees In Fraud Case At L.I.R.R.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Long Island Rail Road Co', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Railroad Retirement Board', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T16:21:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By William K. Rashbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Rashbaum', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/be92ce0a-fcd7-570c-849a-ec2fe45f420b', 'word_count': 526, 'uri': 'nyt://article/be92ce0a-fcd7-570c-849a-ec2fe45f420b'}, {'abstract': 'He responded to the riots in Libya and Egypt like he responds to everything: By attacking the president, regardless of the facts.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/the-romney-routine/', 'snippet': 'He responded to the riots in Libya and Egypt like he responds to everything: By attacking the president, regardless of the facts.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney’s fallen into quite the campaign routine – attack President Obama over anything, at any time, regardless of the facts. He followed this script to a T in responding to yesterday’s riots in North Africa.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Romney Routine', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Terry (Pastor)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T16:28:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a9f9458-17aa-5f56-805a-04619bc8b4e4', 'word_count': 592, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a9f9458-17aa-5f56-805a-04619bc8b4e4'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Eberts, after trying engineering and banking without success, turned to film and produced gems like “Chariots of Fire,” “Gandhi,” “Dances With Wolves,” “The Killing Fields” and “Watership Down.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/movies/jake-eberts-movie-producer-dies-at-71.html', 'snippet': 'Mr. Eberts, after trying engineering and banking without success, turned to film and produced gems like “Chariots of Fire,” “Gandhi,” “Dances With Wolves,” “The Killing Fields” and “Watership Down.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jake Eberts, who trained to be a chemical engineer but instead built a career producing and financing acclaimed and successful films like “Chariots of Fire,” “Gandhi,” “Dances With Wolves” and “The Killing Fields,” died on Sept. 6 in Montreal. He was 71. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/sports/Eberts1/Eberts1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 656, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/sports/Eberts1/Eberts1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1313, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/sports/Eberts1/Eberts1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/13/sports/Eberts1/Eberts1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/sports/Eberts1/Eberts1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jake Eberts, Accidental Film Producer of Oscar Winners, Dies at 71', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Jake Eberts, Accidental Film Producer, Dies at 71', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T16:29:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Yardley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Yardley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/38ac0282-ddb2-5d2f-a0e2-5b003c3aa49d', 'word_count': 899, 'uri': 'nyt://article/38ac0282-ddb2-5d2f-a0e2-5b003c3aa49d'}, {'abstract': 'Mr. Max said he found the subject of his new book “entirely absorbing,” adding, “He consumed me.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/god-mary-karr-and-ronald-reagan-d-t-max-on-david-foster-wallace/', 'snippet': 'Mr. Max said he found the subject of his new book “entirely absorbing,” adding, “He consumed me.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Less than six months after David Foster Wallace committed suicide in 2008 at the age of 46, D. T. Max published a long article in The New Yorker about Wallace, his depression and his attempts to write a new novel. That essay\xa0 spurred Mr. Max’s new book, “Every Love Story Is a Ghost Story,” the first biography of Wallace. Michiko Kakutani called it a “revealing” book that “gives us a sympathetic appraisal of Wallace’s life and work, tracing the connections between the two, while mapping the wellsprings of his philosophical vision.” In a recent e-mail interview, Mr. Max discussed Wallace’s literary influences, his sometimes tumultuous relationships with women, the role of God in his life and more. Below are edited excerpts from the conversation:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/books/12max-cover/12max-cover-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 588, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/books/12max-cover/12max-cover-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 888, 'width': 588, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/books/12max-cover/12max-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/12/books/12max-cover/12max-cover-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/books/12max-cover/12max-cover-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'God, Mary Karr and Ronald Reagan: D.T. Max on David Foster Wallace', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Max, D T', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wallace, David Foster', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T16:30:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Williams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Williams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5201be22-d685-5479-936b-d6d282c7ae3b', 'word_count': 1498, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5201be22-d685-5479-936b-d6d282c7ae3b'}, {'abstract': 'Who was to blame for the Union discovery of secret Confederate documents before the Battle of Antietam?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/12/lees-lost-order/', 'snippet': 'Who was to blame for the Union discovery of secret Confederate documents before the Battle of Antietam?', 'lead_paragraph': 'By early September 1862, having culminated his triumphant Second Manassas campaign with Gen. Thomas J. “Stonewall” Jackson’s postscript victory at Chantilly, Va. — within an easy commute of present-day Washington — Robert E. Lee was laying plans to win the war. He had concentrated his army at Frederick, Md., about 40 miles northwest of Lincoln’s capital. A few months before, the Union had been on the verge of taking the Southern capital of Richmond, Va. Now Lee was a victory away from, if not capturing Washington, demonstrating the South’s military and political viability on the doorstep of the Union. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lee’s Lost Order', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Stonewall', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Robert E', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McClellan, George B', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Virginia', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-12T16:30:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Phil Leigh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Phil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leigh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edf167b7-72ea-59b0-89be-1e8895ef7bc9', 'word_count': 1751, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edf167b7-72ea-59b0-89be-1e8895ef7bc9'}, {'abstract': 'New York', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/09/12/fashion/runway-womens/holmes-yang-spring-2013-rtw.html', 'snippet': 'New York', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/12/fashion/runway-womens/holmesyang_spring13app-slide-FCBT/holmesyang_spring13app-slide-FCBT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType'