': {'original': 'By Hilarie M. Sheets', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hilarie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/089cd366-9211-5f85-802b-2cf1b6890917', 'word_count': 1263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/089cd366-9211-5f85-802b-2cf1b6890917'}, {'abstract': 'Some architects and interior designers are using technology and sleight of hand to hide annoying details like switches, grates and knobs to create a streamlined space.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/garden/the-cult-of-disappearing-design.html', 'snippet': 'Some architects and interior designers are using technology and sleight of hand to hide annoying details like switches, grates and knobs to create a streamlined space.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE first thing you see when you walk into Patrick McInerney’s living room is that there’s nothing to see. The walls are bare, and ditto for the ceiling. You try to switch on the lights, but there doesn’t appear to be a switch. There’s music playing, but where is it coming from? The lamp is obviously working — the bulb is lighted, after all — but it seems to be plugged into ... the plaster?', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/garden/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 224, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/13/garden/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 224}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/garden/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN/13DISAPPEAR_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 382, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank