, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T04:03:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Learning Network', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Learning Network'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1bfa8611-fcb8-515b-a726-aa9364b81c53', 'word_count': 143, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1bfa8611-fcb8-515b-a726-aa9364b81c53'}, {'abstract': 'Even as it actively promotes its democratic values around the world, the United States has also been building massive, expensive, fortress-like embassies that are often seen as anything but open and welcoming. In these troubled times, can a safe embassy also be an accessible one? Can tight security and elegant design co-exist?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/must-u-s-embassies-now-be-fortresses/', 'snippet': 'Even as it actively promotes its democratic values around the world, the United States has also been building massive, expensive, fortress-like embassies that are often seen as anything but open and welcoming. In these troubled times, can a safe e...', 'lead_paragraph': 'HONG KONG — There is certain to be a demand — any minute now, wait for it — for more rigorous security at American embassies and consulates around the world. The attacks this week on the consulate in Benghazi, Libya, and the embassy in Cairo almost require a review of how U.S. diplomatic facilities are sited, designed, built and guarded.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/blogs/bagghdad-rdv/bagghdad-rdv-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/blogs/bagghdad-rdv/bagghdad-rdv-supe