ver the casting for the role of Nina Simone in “Nina.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Anti-Muslim Trailer’s Murky Past, and Casting Nina Simone', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T14:29:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cdf24c45-e4b8-53a3-8ea1-57dc13f9efdc', 'word_count': 555, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cdf24c45-e4b8-53a3-8ea1-57dc13f9efdc'}, {'abstract': 'Decades after a youth spent on New York stages, Diane Lane is in Chicago, taking on the lead in the Tennessee Williams classic “Sweet Bird of Youth.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/theater/diane-lane-back-onstage-in-sweet-bird-of-youth.html', 'snippet': 'Decades after a youth spent on New York stages, Diane Lane is in Chicago, taking on the lead in the Tennessee Williams classic “Sweet Bird of Youth.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16LANE1/16LANE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16LANE1/16LANE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16LANE1/16LANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16LANE1/16LANE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16LANE1/16LANE1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Child of the Stage Returns to Play a Faded Princess', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Child of the Stage Returns to Play A Faded Princess', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Tennessee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lane, Diane', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Goodman Theater', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cromer, David', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T14:30:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick Healy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Healy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6ca70f87-ab6b-59f4-985f-7bbb92139686', 'word_count': 1827, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6ca70f87-ab6b-59f4-985f-7bbb92139686'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/arts/sept-16-22.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The weekend-long festivals by the London promoter All Tomorrow’s Parties have often been in faded vacation spots — English holiday camps and their counterparts in the Catskills and Atlantic City. As musical experiences the American events have been blissful but not boffo; costs ran high and attendance ran low. So the company is trying a new model with ALL TOMORROW’S PARTIES AMERICA PRESENTS I’LL BE YOUR MIRROR  — nearly 40 acts across three days at Pier 36, an entertainment complex on the Lower East Side. Frank Ocean, the R&B man of the hour, headlines on Friday, after a day including the comedian Hannibal Buress, a collaboration between Tyondai Braxton and Philip Glass, and Lee Ranaldo’s “Hanging Guitar” project, involving suspended, swinging Fender Jaguars. The reunited Afghan Whigs headline next Saturday, and other bands that day include the Roots, José González, Dirty Three and Chavez; for next Sunday the organizers have a slate including Godspeed You! Black Emperor, along with the Make-Up, the Washington punk-soul band, in reunited form. 5 p.m. to 1 a.m. Friday; noon to 1 a.m. next Saturday and Sunday; South and Montgomery Streets; atpfestival.com; $60 to $199. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sept. 16 — 22', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T14:32:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8d7cb47a-49c7-5c25-a206-6847bae1f85a', 'word_count': 1327, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8d7cb47a-49c7-5c25-a206-6847bae1f85a'}, {'abstract': 'Internet pirates and disgraced politicians, gunrunners and pedophiles, terrorists and mob bosses — why do so many criminals drift to Cambodia?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/cambodia-is-a-sunny-country-for-shady-characters/', 'snippet': 'Internet pirates and disgraced politicians, gunrunners and pedophiles, terrorists and mob bosses — why do so many criminals drift to Cambodia?', 'lead_paragraph': 'PHNOM PENH — The municipal council recently hung a neon sign across Sisowath Quay, on the capital’s popular riverfront. “Phnom Penh — The Charming City,” it boasted.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Cambodia’s Most Unwanted', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deportation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Crime', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Phnom Penh (Cambodia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T14:33:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Luke Hunt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Luke', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hunt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d2b537e9-c4cf-52bf-9ae4-c02ef07e4295', 'word_count': 611, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d2b537e9-c4cf-52bf-9ae4-c02ef07e4295'}, {'abstract': 'The escape and capture of Kevin, a peacock kept by John Bowne High School in Queens, is the subject of drawings submitted by children.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/09/11/nyregion/cityroom-KDTN-peacock.html', 'snippet': 'The escape and capture of Kevin, a peacock kept by John Bowne High School in Queens, is the subject of drawings submitted by children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The escape and capture of Kevin, a peacock kept by John Bowne High School in Queens, is the subject of drawings submitted by children.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'image