'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Rosenthal', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenthal', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/44e3a7f6-974b-5274-b944-5a298b66d534', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/44e3a7f6-974b-5274-b944-5a298b66d534'}, {'abstract': 'A marine conservationist describes efforts to control a lionfish invasion by catching and eating the invaders.', 'web_url': 'https://dotearth.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/lionfish-for-lunch-but-also-here-for-the-long-haul/', 'snippet': 'A marine conservationist describes efforts to control a lionfish invasion by catching and eating the invaders.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Just in case you missed it, my friend and occasional fishing tutor Carl Safina has a string of posts on Mark Bittman’s blog on the continuing invasion of American waters by lionfish, a reef denizen, studded with toxin-tipped spines, that was most likely brought here from Asian waters by the aquarium trade.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lionfish for Lunch, But Also Here For the Long Haul', 'kicker': 'Dot Earth', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Coral', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fish and Other Marine Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Invasive Species', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bittman, Mark', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Safina, Carl', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T17:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew C. Revkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Revkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bd87806d-4a43-5234-99c8-95ee2914a8f7', 'word_count': 175, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bd87806d-4a43-5234-99c8-95ee2914a8f7'}, {'abstract': 'This city may lack conventional addresses, but determined visitors can find everything from boiled-cheese snacks to a swimmable volcanic lake in Nicaragua’s capital.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/travel/36-hours-in-managua-nicaragua.html', 'snippet': 'This city may lack conventional addresses, but determined visitors can find everything from boiled-cheese snacks to a swimmable volcanic lake in Nicaragua’s capital.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FOUNDED just before the Spanish left the country in the early 19th century, Managua, the capital of Nicaragua, is a city without a center, and, bizarrely, without conventional addresses; instead there are locations, many referring to Lake Managua, which the city, otherwise, doesn’t much celebrate. The 1972 earthquake shattered Managua’s once glorious boulevards, and today building styles vary wildly, from the squat residential buildings that popped up after the tremors, to older, more majestic neo-Classical and modern edifices. But despite an initial impression of chaos, you will find smooth roads and a full range of hotels and restaurants serving hearty local fare. The years of political upheaval marked by the 1979 revolution and then the brutal civil war of the 1980s have finally given way to stability. Yet travelers on their way to the coasts or quaint colonial towns still rush away from the capital upon arrival. That’s too bad. There is a blossoming of culture in this city that is the key to understanding modern Nicaragua.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16-HOURS3/16-HOURS3-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Managua, Nicaragua', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '36 Hours: Managua, Nicaragua', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Managua (Nicaragua)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T17:40:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sarah Wildman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sarah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Wildman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/355e0b53-e5ab-599d-9338-f61b5ba4ab53', 'word_count': 1466, 'uri': 'nyt://article/355e0b53-e5ab-599d-9338-f61b5ba4ab53'}, {'abstract': 'The Spring 2013 women’s collection show from Calvin Klein at New York Fashion Week.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/now-streaming-calvin-klein-spring-2013-womens-collection/', 'snippet': 'The Spring 2013 women’s collection show from Calvin Klein at New York Fashion Week.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Spring 2013 women’s collection show from Calvin Klein at New York Fashion Week. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Now Streaming | Calvin Klein Spring 2013 Women’s Collection', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T17:50:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By T Magazine', 'person': [{'firstname': '', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e9221de7-d523-55f7-a466-f8f957e1ee74', 'word_count': 20, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e9221de7-d523-55f7-a466-f8f957e1ee74'}, {'abstract': 'The white French Renaissance palace, which ended up as little more than a symbol of the stalled recovery from the 2010 earthquake, will be ripped apart over the coming months and carted off.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/world/americas/haitis-national-palace-being-demolished.html', 'snippet': 'The white French Renaissance palace, which ended up as 