13T21:12:06+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/18ed0fa4-9b49-5339-915e-17ad1fab1676', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/18ed0fa4-9b49-5339-915e-17ad1fab1676'}, {'abstract': 'Afghanistan officials asked Internet companies to block access to YouTube, where an anti-Islamic video can be viewed, and asked local officials to urge calm in the country.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/world/asia/afghanistan-tries-to-block-video-and-head-off-rioting.html', 'snippet': 'Afghanistan officials asked Internet companies to block access to YouTube, where an anti-Islamic video can be viewed, and asked local officials to urge calm in the country.', 'lead_paragraph': 'KABUL, Afghanistan — Racing to head off potentially violent demonstrations over an anti-Islamic video, the Afghan government on Thursday pushed Internet providers to shut down access to Web sites hosting the clip, and asked political and religious leaders to urge calm, senior Afghan officials said.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Afghanistan Tries to Block Video and Head Off Rioting', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Afghanistan Tries to Block Video and Head Off Rioting', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Censorship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'AFGHANISTAN', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:18:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alissa J. Rubin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alissa', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Rubin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1af60d88-afd3-5667-8f27-211e3688ae0d', 'word_count': 880, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1af60d88-afd3-5667-8f27-211e3688ae0d'}, {'abstract': 'Haunting photography of supercell and monsoon storms in the United States, and the alter ego behind them.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/erik-hijweege-a-k-a-kevin-erskine-the-storm-chasing-photographer/', 'snippet': 'Haunting photography of supercell and monsoon storms in the United States, and the alter ego behind them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To illustrate Nate Silver’s recent article on the state of weather forecasting, we turned to the work of the storm photographer Kevin Erskine, whose haunting pictures of atmospheric disturbances are the subject of his book “Supercell,” which came out last year. “Erskine” is the professional pseudonym used by Erik Hijweege, a\xa0Dutch photographer who was inspired to combine his longstanding interests in storms and landscape photography when he visited the United States seven years ago\xa0and met the meteorologist and well-known storm chaser Tim Marshall.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Erik Hijweege, a k a Kevin Erskine, the Storm-Chasing Photographer', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Erskine, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:19:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marvin Orellana', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marvin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Orellana', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/70e032bc-8f43-54a7-8862-b1dc90c90f65', 'word_count': 182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/70e032bc-8f43-54a7-8862-b1dc90c90f65'}, {'abstract': '“Echoes of the Past” brings to the Institute for the Study of the Ancient World sixth-century treasures from Chinese Buddhist cave temples and showcases modern efforts to save them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/arts/design/echoes-of-the-past-chinese-buddhist-cave-art-in-new-york.html', 'snippet': '“Echoes of the Past” brings to the Institute for the Study of the Ancient World sixth-century treasures from Chinese Buddhist cave temples and showcases modern efforts to save them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the Buddhist way of thinking, all things must pass, though it seems a shame when they do, especially if they’re out-of-this-world beautiful, as so much Chinese Buddhist art is. But could that vision of transience be changing in the 21st century, as technology lets all kinds of things, including art, live on forever out there in digital clouds?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14TEMPLES1_SPAN/14TEMPLES1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14TEMPLES1_SPAN/14TEMPLES1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14TEMPLES1_SPAN/JPTEMPLES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14TEMPLES1_SPAN/JPTEMPLES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'su