onstitutional Court and results from a Dutch election may mean that the worst is over for the euro, or so European Union leaders hope.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/world/europe/european-union-celebrates-german-and-dutch-decisions.html', 'snippet': 'A decision by the German Constitutional Court and results from a Dutch election may mean that the worst is over for the euro, or so European Union leaders hope.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — There was a general sigh of relief in the European Union this week. The cause was not better performance in the troubled and highly indebted southern countries of the euro zone, but crucial decisions made in the rich northern nations with perfect credit ratings, where skepticism about the common currency is running high.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 665, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1329, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/world/european/european-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dutch and Germans Give European Union Reasons to Cheer', 'kicker': 'News Analysis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dutch and Germans Give European Union Reasons to Cheer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Germany', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Merkel, Angela', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Netherlands', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Union', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Spain', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'European Sovereign Debt Crisis (2010- )', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Commission', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'European Stability Mechanism', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:24:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Erlanger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Erlanger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News Analysis', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a09f727-f247-5556-bf05-92d79c830023', 'word_count': 1071, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a09f727-f247-5556-bf05-92d79c830023'}, {'abstract': 'In Nicholas Jarecki’s “Arbitrage” Richard Gere plays a hedge-fund magnate whose glittering, rarefied world may be coming undone.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/movies/richard-gere-in-arbitrage.html', 'snippet': 'In Nicholas Jarecki’s “Arbitrage” Richard Gere plays a hedge-fund magnate whose glittering, rarefied world may be coming undone.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Arbitrage,” a sleek entertainment about how very good greed can be, is a fairy tale masquerading as a tragedy. It pivots on Robert Miller (Richard Gere), a shifty hedge-fund manager and 21st-century robber baron who’s foxed his way to the top of New York’s moneyed classes. Charmed and charming, with a quicksilver stride and the self-possession of a man accustomed to hearing the word yes, Robert lives in a rarefied world of private jets, live-in help and seven-figure checks made out to favorite charities. Masterworks adorn his Gramercy Park mansion where a multistory crystal chandelier cascades in the foyer like a waterfall of diamonds. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/14ARBITAGE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/14ARBITAGE_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/ARBITAGE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/ARBITAGE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/14ARBITAGE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/14ARBITAGE_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14ARBITAGE_SPAN/14ARBITAGE_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'High Flier, Poised for Steep Descent', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'High Flier, Poised For Steep Descent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gere, Richard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sarandon, Susan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Arbitrage (Movie)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jarecki, Nicholas', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:29:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/31a4c066-eb71-58f1-b150-fa50114fdc8c', 'word_count': 644, 'uri': 'nyt://article/31a4c066-eb71-58f1-b150-fa50114fdc8c'}, {'abstract': 'In “Regarding Warhol: Sixty Artists, Fifty Years,” the Metropolitan Museum makes a foray into showing contemporary art that is relentlessly au courant and sometimes baffling.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/arts/design/regarding-warhol-at-the-metropolitan-museum.html', 'snippet': 'In “Regarding Warhol: Sixty Artists, Fifty Years,” the Metropolitan Museum makes a foray into showing contemporary art that is relentlessly au courant and sometimes baffling.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Metropolitan Museum of Art has rushed in where museums more experienced in the new and the now might have feared to tread. It is highlighting its growing commitment to contemporary art with an examination of the immense, amorphous influence of one of the most important artists of the 20th century: Andy Warhol, the visionary Pop Art painter, innovative filmmaker, trenchant social observer, image thief, deadpan quotation machine, boho magazine publisher and forthright gay artist about town. This effort is not an encouraging sight, but then the Met has a long way to go where contemporary art is concerned.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 605, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1209, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WARHOL_SPAN/14WARHOL_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The In-Crowd Is All Here', 'kicker': 'Art Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The In-Crowd Is All Here', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Warhol, Andy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Museum of Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:38:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roberta Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roberta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3ca6278-385a-5022-9d11-790dd28ed143', 'word_count': 1707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3ca6278-385a-5022-9d11-790dd28ed143'}, {'abstract': 'T plucks the best shoes right off the runway. Today’s: a bottom-heavy black satin platform with an elaborate gold heel.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/the-daily-shoe-calvin-klein-2/', 'snippet': 'T plucks the best shoes right off the runway. Today’s: a bottom-heavy black satin platform with an elaborate gold heel.', 'lead_paragraph': 'T plucks the best shoes right off the runway.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Daily Shoe | Calvin Klein', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Shoes and Boots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Costa, Francisco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Calvin Klein Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:38:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Herman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Herman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/67556faa-b4d2-564f-ad40-20d6ebf62b6d', 'word_count': 87, 'uri': 'nyt://article/67556faa-b4d2-564f-ad40-20d6ebf62b6d'}, {'abstract': 'Less than you might think.', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/how-much-would-free-college-cost-for-all-new-yorkers/', 'snippet': 'Less than you might think.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the magazine’s Education Issue, I write about the Kalamazoo Promise, a scholarship program that gives a full ride to all students who graduate from Kalamazoo public schools and go to college in Michigan. To replicate the program in New York City, to cover the college tuition of all 52,000 students who graduate from the nation’s largest school system every year, an endowment of something like $20 billion would be needed, according to Keith Luke, at the Connecticut-based Commonfund, which manages $25 billion in assets for nonprofit institutions. “Such an endowment,” he says, “would provide for annual tuition payments of $5,000 for each student for four years, growing annually at the rate of inflation for higher education.” Forbes magazine estimates that 57 billionaires live in the city. Mayor Bloomberg himself has donated more than $2.4 billion to favorite causes, and in 2013 Columbia University will finish a $5 billion fund-raising campaign, bringing in $1 billion more than its initial goal. So the money for a New York Promise is probably out there, but it would require one of the world’s biggest philanthropic efforts.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/magazine/13-graduates/13-graduates-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 716, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/magazine/13-graduates/13-graduates-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1432, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/magazine/13-graduates/13-graduates-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/13/magazine/13-graduates/13-graduates-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/magazine/13-graduates/13-graduates-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How Much Would Free College Cost for All New Yorkers?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tuition', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:40:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ted C. Fishman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ted', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Fishman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37691066-28d8-5553-b652-72915eb63913', 'word_count': 183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37691066-28d8-5553-b652-72915eb63913'}, {'abstract': 'The presidential campaigns got creative with their pleas for cash, trying to grab supporters’ attention after a summer filled with increasingly desperate fundraising e-mails.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/13/a-trip-with-beyonce-or-on-hair-force-one-you-decide/', 'snippet': 'The presidential campaigns got creative with their pleas for cash, trying to grab supporters’ attention after a summer filled with increasingly desperate fundraising e-mails.', 'lead_paragraph': 'First came the e-mail from Beyoncé Knowles: want to fly to New York to hang out with her and President Obama?', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Trip With Beyonce or on Hair Force One? You Decide.', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': "'Love, Beyonce' A Creative Turn in Pleas for Cash", 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Campaign Finance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Mail', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Knowles, Beyonce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-13T21:44:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Emma G. Fitzsimmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Emma', 'middlename'