 {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Schedule', '_id': 'nyt://article/dcf27b31-a008-54c2-ae39-9e1576eb7b87', 'word_count': 3341, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dcf27b31-a008-54c2-ae39-9e1576eb7b87'}, {'abstract': 'A round-the-island architectural cruise, with commentary on Manhattan landmarks and sites, yields views of new waterfront greenery worth a closer look on shore.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/nyregion/an-architectural-cruise-around-manhattan.html', 'snippet': 'A round-the-island architectural cruise, with commentary on Manhattan landmarks and sites, yields views of new waterfront greenery worth a closer look on shore.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Manhattan has one of the most recognizable faces in the world. Yet it can be strangely elusive, even Garboesque. The buildings are too tall and too close together to see in their entirety from the ground, so New Yorkers who want to get a good look at the skyline have to go to the movies, visit a prime viewing spot like the Brooklyn Heights Promenade or look out an airplane window.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WATERFRONT_SPAN/14WATERFRONT-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WATERFRONT_SPAN/14WATERFRONT-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WATERFRONT_SPAN/14WATERFRONT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/arts/14WATERFRONT_SPAN/14WATERFRONT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048,