86, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 386}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/world/15pope-span/15pope-span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Benedict Takes Message of Tolerance to Lebanon', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Benedict Takes Message Of Tolerance to Lebanon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Lebanon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Benedict XVI', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Christians and Christianity', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Discrimination', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T10:43:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kareem Fahim and Rachel Donadio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kareem', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fahim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Donadio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/267809e3-fbee-53d6-8e29-edc8d3fded43', 'word_count': 881, 'uri': 'nyt://article/267809e3-fbee-53d6-8e29-edc8d3fded43'}, {'abstract': 'A new study suggests that distortions in the wholesale electricity market are getting bigger as more wind power is added. But wind proponents say that consumers are benefiting from lower prices.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/an-argument-over-wind/', 'snippet': 'A new study suggests that distortions in the wholesale electricity market are getting bigger as more wind power is added. But wind proponents say that consumers are benefiting from lower prices.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the wind industry facing the expiration of a production tax credit at the end of the year, the sector’s main trade association is facing off against Exelon, the big power generation company, over whether the tax break should be renewed. Last week, the American Wind Energy Association expelled Exelon as a member because the company opposed a renewal of the credit.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Argument Over Wind', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prices (Fares, Fees and Rates)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tax Credits, Deductions and Exemptions', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wind Power', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Wind Energy Assn', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Exelon Corporation', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T10:57:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Matthew L. Wald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Matthew', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Wald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/942135c6-f7e0-503f-9aa0-0ae33cd8c007', 'word_count': 1121, 'uri': 'nyt://article/942135c6-f7e0-503f-9aa0-0ae33cd8c007'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen Burt makes a passionate, quirky case for why we need poetry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/stephen-burt-poetrys-cross-dressing-kingmaker.html', 'snippet': 'Stephen Burt makes a passionate, quirky case for why we need poetry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When I asked Stephen Burt’s parents if they worried about him when he was young, Burt’s father, Jeff, replied by asking me if I was Jewish. I told him I was. “Well,” he said, “to ask Jewish parents if they were worried about their children — it is a statement of fact! But were we more worried about Stephen than about the other children? The answer is yes.” As a boy, Stephen, the eldest of their four sons, was, in his dad’s words, “extremely precocious”; his parents’ reminiscences make him sound lovable but very intense, with interests out of step with other boys his age. His mother, Sandra, told me that in third grade he wrote an essay about “a little boy whose mother forced him to go out and play.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '46', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 741, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 741}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 826, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1210, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16burt/16burt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry’s Cross-Dressing Kingmaker', 'kicker': 'The Education Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Needs Poetry?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Harvard University', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Burt, Stephen', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poetry and Poets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark Oppenheimer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oppenheimer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/865c5dfb-4f97-5f85-90e6-7b12c75eb92d', 'word_count': 2552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/865c5dfb-4f97-5f85-90e6-7b12c75eb92d'}, {'abstract': 'Advances in computerized tutoring are testing the faith that human contact makes for better learning.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/how-computerized-tutors-are-learning-to-teach-humans.html', 'snippet': 'Advances in computerized tutoring are testing the faith that human contact makes for better learning.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Neil Heffernan was listening to his fiancée, Cristina Lindquist, tutor one of her students in mathematics when he had an idea. Heffernan was a graduate student in computer science, and by this point — the summer of 1997 — he had been working for two years with researchers at Carnegie Mellon University on developing computer software to help students improve their skills. But he had come to believe that the programs did little to assist their users. They were built on elaborate theories of the student mind — attempts to simulate the learning brain. Then it dawned on him: what was missing from the programs was the interventions teachers made to promote and accelerate learning. Why not model a computer program on a human tutor like Lindquist?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/16tutor-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 655, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/16tutor-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 655}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/mag-16Tutor-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 932, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/mag-16Tutor-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/16tutor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/16tutor-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16tutor/16tutor-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Machines Are Taking Over', 'kicker': 'The Education Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Machines Are Taking Over', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Heffernan, Neil T', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Koedinger, Kenneth R', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'E-Learning', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tutors and Tutoring', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Worcester Polytechnic Institute', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Carnegie Learning', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T11:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Murphy Paul', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': 'Murphy', 'lastname': 'Paul', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/747add86-6086-5a6d-b8df-fd031c681b88', 'word_count': 3606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/747add86-6086-5a6d-b8df-fd031c681b88'}, {'abstract': 'How do you strike a balance between making sure you reach all of your followers and making sure you don’t annoy all of your followers?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/how-many-times-can-you-tweet-the-same-tweet/', 'snippet': 'How do you strike a balance between making sure you reach all of your followers and making sure you don’t annoy all of your followers?', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a heavy Twitter user. I love how fast it moves. If you are looking for instant feedback on anything, Twitter is the place to test your content or messaging. It’s also the place to get answers to your questions. And it’s the best listening device on the Internet. You can search for keywords, customers, competition, and industry thought leaders and find out what everyone is thinking. And no one even knows you are listening.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How Many Times Can You Tweet the Same Tweet?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kawasaki, Guy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T11:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melinda F. Emerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melinda', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Emerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/28808eac-a0fb-5003-aded-a0af7d0771b1', 'word_count': 771, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28808eac-a0fb-5003-aded-a0af7d0771b1'}, {'abstract': 'The latest New York Times/CBS news poll found that the presidential candidates remained essentially tied among likely voters.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/poll-obama-holds-narrow-edge-over-romney/', 'snippet': 'The latest New York Times/CBS news poll found that the presidential candidates remained essentially tied among likely voters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'President Obama holds a narrow three-point advantage over Mitt Romney among Americans most likely to vote in November, according to the latest New York Times/CBS News poll.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poll: Obama Holds Narrow Edge Over Romney', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T11:01:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsecti