 new way to search for how many people stand between a famous person and Kevin Bacon. But it’s more than a game. Google says it demonstrates the potential of its Knowledge Graph, which it says is the next step in search, showing how people, places and things are related.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/need-ryan-goslings-bacon-number-ask-google/', 'snippet': 'Google has a new way to search for how many people stand between a famous person and Kevin Bacon. But it’s more than a game. Google says it demonstrates the potential of its Knowledge Graph, which it says is the next step in search, showing how pe...', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a sign of how committed Google is to helping people answer their most pressing and urgent questions, the search engine has unveiled a way to find famous people’s Bacon number. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/blogs/kevin-bacon/kevin-bacon-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/blogs/kevin-bacon/kevin-bacon-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1080, 'width': 1920, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/blogs/kevin-bacon/kevin-bacon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/14/blogs/kevin-bacon/kevin-bacon-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/blogs/kevin-bacon/kevin-bacon-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Need Ryan Gosling’s Bacon Number? Ask Google', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Google Can Find A ‘Bacon Number’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bacon, Kevin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Claire Cain Miller', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Claire', 'middlename': 'Cain', 'lastname': 'Miller', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/73354985-1c86-56e5-97ea-f1e77fe66216', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/73354985-1c86-56e5-97ea-f1e77fe66216'}, {'abstract': 'A hundred years after “Rite of Spring” sparked an uproar in Paris, shock has grown mainstream, raising a question: Can art still shock today?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/arts/shock-me-if-you-can.html', 'snippet': 'A hundred years after “Rite of Spring” sparked an uproar in Paris, shock has grown mainstream, raising a question: Can art still shock today?', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE morning of “The Rite of Spring” premiere, on May 29, 1913, at the Théâtre des Champs-Élysées in Paris, Le Figaro predicted that ballet would deliver “a new thrill which will surely raise passionate discussion” and “leave all true artists with an unforgettable impression.” That turned out to be one of the greatest understatements of the new artistic century. The passionate discussion began during the first few bars of the music, as derisive laughter rose from the seats, and soon grew into an uproar that sent Stravinsky fleeing the hall in disgust.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 656, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 801, 'width': 1251, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16shock-span-new/16shock-span-new-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 149, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shock Me if You Can', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shock Me If You Can: After a Century of Shaking and Rattling Audiences, Artists Still Push the Boundaries of Confrontation', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waters, John', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Finley, Karen', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Culture (Arts)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bradshaw, Thomas', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stravinsky, Igor', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:21:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jennifer Schuessler', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jennifer', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schuessler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/14213142-9d18-51b2-b6d0-5dcda827bada', 'word_count': 1772, 'uri': 'nyt://article/14213142-9d18-51b2-b6d0-5dcda827bada'}, {'abstract': '“The Rite of Spring,” Igor Stravinsky’s ballet that celebrates human sacrifice, is widely praised today but was a flop when it was first produced, in Paris in 1913.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/arts/music/rite-of-spring-cools-into-a-rite-of-passage.html', 'snippet': '“The Rite of Spring,” Igor Stravinsky’s ballet that celebrates human sacrifice, is widely praised today but was a flop when it was first produced, in Paris in 1913.', 'lead_paragraph': '“THE RITE OF SPRING,” or “Le Sacre du Printemps,” Igor Stravinsky’s historic shocker, a ballet that shows and celebrates a remorseless human sacrifice, will be 100 years old next May. The bandwagon of centennial commemorations is getting an early start on Thursday with the  New York Philharmonic. But in stark contrast to its present-day lionization, it was a spectacular flop at its first showing, by Sergei Diaghilev’s Ballets Russes, at the Théâtre des Champs-Élysées in Paris.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16RITE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 396, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16RITE2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 396}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16JPRITE2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 676, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16JPRITE2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1351, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16JPRITE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/arts/16RITE2_SPAN/16JPRITE2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shocker Cools Into a ‘Rite’ of Passage', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shock Me If You Can: Stravinsky’s ‘Rite of Spring’ Once Ignited Passions. Time Has Cooled It Into a Musical Rite of Passage.', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Nijinsky, Vaslav', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stravinsky, Igor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Rite of Spring (Ballet)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:21:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard Taruskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taruskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a1657477-2f8f-5505-bfda-8413a8a9fe33', 'word_count': 1997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a1657477-2f8f-5505-bfda-8413a8a9fe33'}, {'abstract': 'After Jay Cutler wished Packers defensive backs good luck trying to stop him, Green Bay intercepted him four times.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/15/sports/it-was-just-bears-quarterback-jay-cutlers-luck.html', 'snippet': 'After Jay Cutler wished Packers defensive backs good luck trying to stop him, Green Bay intercepted him four times.', 'lead_paragraph': "There are more than a few things that make almost no sense in sports. Normally, we’d get right to the most outrageous one, but we need to build up to the proper level of incredulous eye roll for the N.H.L.'s impending lockout. So we’ll start with something simpler, a forehead slapper with universal appeal: that Chicago quarterback Jay Cutler was actually trash-talking in the leadup to Thursday night’s game against the Packers.", 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/sports/15leading1/15leading1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 962, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/15/sports/15leading1/15leading1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1924, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 