nd Black Sea ecosystems a millennium ago.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Humans were tampering with nature long before the Industrial Revolution’s steam and internal combustion engines arrived on the scene. The invention of agriculture around 8,000 years ago, some argue, significantly changed ecosystems as it spread around the globe. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'From Ancient Deforestation, a Delta Is Born', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Agriculture and Farming', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forests and Forestry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lakes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rivers', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Scientific Reports (Journal)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Woods Hole Oceanographic Institution', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Black Sea', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Danube River', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:33:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Rachel Nuwer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Rachel', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nuwer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13838add-70d4-5aaa-af82-ea7281b9da0b', 'word_count': 718, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13838add-70d4-5aaa-af82-ea7281b9da0b'}, {'abstract': 'The after-party.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/on-the-house.html', 'snippet': 'The after-party.', 'lead_paragraph': ' I recently had a party and afterward had quite a few large bottles of leftover wine (they were opened and wouldn’t keep). There is a particular corner in my neighborhood where benign “drunkards” hang out and drink. They have done so for years, and everyone accepts this as part of our neighborhood. My question is, Should I drop this mother lode of wine off on their perch for them (because who am I to judge their choices?), or pour it down the drain (which would be a “waste”)? DEAN D., LOS ANGELES', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16ethicist/16ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16ethicist/16ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16ethicist/16ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16ethicist/16ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16ethicist/16ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'On the House', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On The House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ALCOHOLIC BEVERAGES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/18aa2fe7-a715-5aae-b984-24f63432a012', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/18aa2fe7-a715-5aae-b984-24f63432a012'}, {'abstract': 'The hard-labor makings of the fluffy treat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/who-made-that-marshmallow.html', 'snippet': 'The hard-labor makings of the fluffy treat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A century ago, confectioners made marshmallows from a froth of sugar, starch and gelatin, in a laborious process that involved lots of primping. Each candy had to “sweat” for several hours to form its delicate skin, and then it was sprinkled with starch. In the 1950s, Alex Doumak — an executive at the Doumak candy company — dreamed up an efficient solution to the problem of the slow marshmallow: he treated the candy goo as an industrial material. Machines forced the marshmallow slurry through tubes, subjected it to blasts of gas at 200 pounds per square inch, extruded it into long tails and then cut it into bite-size chunks. Doumak filled the candy with air, like a tire, trapping nothingness in an architecture of sugar. “Extrusion allowed for the marshmallow we’re used to today — with that springy, fluffy texture,” Sue Watson, manager of the company archives at Kraft Foods, says. Kraft began extruding its own marshmallows in the late 1950s, but its most creative touch might have been the space-age name it gave to the factory process — jet-puffed — as if the marshmallows had exploded out of a rocket-ship engine. As part of a 1959 promotion, children sent in empty Kraft marshmallow bags for a chance to own a 29-foot spaceship equipped with astronaut suits, countdown lights and an “astro projector for space observation.” The modern marshmallow may be mostly air, but it’s a sweet lesson in less is more.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/16wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 482, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/16wmt-articleLarge-v3.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 482}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/mag-16WMT-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 739, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/mag-16WMT-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1082, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/16wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/16wmt-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16wmt/16wmt-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Marshmallow?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Marshmallow)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Candy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/91cd6142-83d3-57cd-a8ab-d82d031a0fd5', 'word_count': 392, 'uri': 'nyt://article/91cd6142-83d3-57cd-a8ab-d82d031a0fd5'}, {'abstract': 'The Oscar-winning actress on self-promotion, imaginary boyfriends and the right way to lose a job.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/melissa-leo-is-a-fighter.html', 'snippet': 'The Oscar-winning actress on self-promotion, imaginary boyfriends and the right way to lose a job.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Your new film, “Francine,” is about a woman, fresh out of jail, who seems destined to become one of those people on “Hoarders,” with a home piled with animal carcasses and feces. What is your own biggest eccentricity?  What you see is what you get.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16talk/mag-16Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16talk/mag-16Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16talk/16talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16talk/16talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16talk/16talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Melissa Leo Is a Fighter', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Truth Will Get You Fired', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leo, Melissa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ACTORS AND ACTRESSES', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:35:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/9e28dae4-788d-5bc3-a6a2-7006ba2d9da4', 'word_count': 707, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9e28dae4-788d-5bc3-a6a2-7006ba2d9da4'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/the-9-212-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHO WEARS THE PANTS IN THIS ECONOMY?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 9.2.12 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 9.2.12 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:35:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/050d180e-7a15-585c-9b3f-ec27a5e4f713', 'word_count': 668, 'uri': 'nyt://article/050d180e-7a15-585c-9b3f-ec27a5e4f713'}, {'abstract': 'The pleasures of learning to tap-dance (badly) in middle age.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/im-with-stupid.html', 'snippet': 'The pleasures of learning to tap-dance (badly) in middle age.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There they were, my mother and my father, smiling up at me from the third row at my first-ever tap-dance recital. My husband, my teenage daughters and my daughter’s friend were also in the audience, but it was my parents I was most aware of as I sashayed through my shuffle-ball-changes and cramp rolls. Especially my father, who sat there beaming as if I’d just won a Tony Award.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '78', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16lives/mag-16Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16lives/mag-16Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/magazine/16lives/16lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'I’m With Stupid', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I’m With Stupid', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:35:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robin Marantz Henig', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robin', 'middlename': 'Marantz', 'lastname': 'Henig', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/346d2b76-8387-5a3e-bc6b-50f33868fd9f', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/346d2b76-8387-5a3e-bc6b-50f33868fd9f'}, {'abstract': 'A young woman turns a strange hue.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/magazine/feeling-blue.html', 'snippet': 'A young woman turns a strange hue.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The 17-year-old girl was eating lunch in her high-school cafeteriain the Netherlands when one of her friends gave her a funny look.“Your face is turning blue,” the friend told her. The others at the table stared. They saw it, too. The young woman hurried to the bathroom, where she saw a faint bluish tinge across her cheeks.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Feeling Blue', 'kicker': 'Diagnosis', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Feeling Blue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sanders, Lisa', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Skin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Heart', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name'