, '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/468cc2ba-3dd6-54bd-a943-c3ce818de6ba', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/468cc2ba-3dd6-54bd-a943-c3ce818de6ba'}, {'abstract': 'Before père and fils, there was the first Alexandre Dumas, a mixed-race general of the French Revolution.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/the-black-count-by-tom-reiss.html', 'snippet': 'Before père and fils, there was the first Alexandre Dumas, a mixed-race general of the French Revolution.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In the 1790s, the son of an aristocratic white father and a black slave woman became a charismatic French general who for a time rivaled Napoleon himself, and afterward languished in an Italian dungeon. His story inspired the novel “The Count of Monte Cristo,” written by his son, Alexandre Dumas, who also drew upon his father’s adventures in “The Three Musketeers.” Posterity remembers this son as Dumas père, to distinguish him from Alexandre Dumas fils, also a writer, whose novel “La Dame aux Camélias” was the source for Verdi’s “La Traviata.” But the general was the first of the three Alexandres (he preferred to be known as Alex), and in “The Black Count,” Tom \xadReiss, the author of “The Orientalist,” has recovered this fascinating story with a richly imaginative biography.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16DAMROSH/16DAMROSH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 882, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16DAMROSH/16DAMROSH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1763, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16DAMROSH/16DAMROSH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16DAMROSH/16DAMROSH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16DAMROSH/16DAMROSH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 151, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Third Musketeer', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Third Musketeer', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dumas, Alexandre', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Reiss, Tom', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:46:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Leo Damrosch', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Leo', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Damrosch', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/bfbbcf6b-65f5-5fbf-a9f0-40ea4ee9c000', 'word_count': 1204, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bfbbcf6b-65f5-5fbf-a9f0-40ea4ee9c000'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography of Rachel Carson, who warned against the indiscriminate use of pesticides.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/on-a-farther-shore-by-william-souder.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography of Rachel Carson, who warned against the indiscriminate use of pesticides.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the bookshelves of many a contemporary environmental journalist looms at least one canonical text she’s hesitant to read. For this reviewer, it was Rachel Carson’s “Silent Spring,” among the gloomiest books ever written, an unrelenting catalog of crimes committed by man against nature. But after reading William Souder’s engrossing new biography of Carson, “On a Farther Shore,” I returned to the book and discovered its central message to be — depressingly — timeless. Substitute organic pesticides and herbicides with the endocrine-\xaddisrupting compounds found in everyday household items or the creep of chemicals used in hydrof\xadracking, and you may experience the same hair-prickling alarm felt by Carson’s readers 50 years ago. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16ROYTE/16ROYTE-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 946, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16ROYTE/16ROYTE-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1892, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16ROYTE/16ROYTE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16ROYTE/16ROYTE-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16ROYTE/16ROYTE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Poisoned Earth', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Poisoned Earth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pesticides', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Souder, William', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Carson, Rachel', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:47:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elizabeth Royte', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elizabeth', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Royte', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/f50aada3-8acb-5e03-a0d9-bd90f32c46fc', 'word_count': 1195, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f50aada3-8acb-5e03-a0d9-bd90f32c46fc'}, {'abstract': 'Grave robbers and serial killers populate this novel set in 19th-century England.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/the-thing-about-thugs-by-tabish-khair.html', 'snippet': 'Grave robbers and serial killers populate this novel set in 19th-century England.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Thing About Thugs” is an odd confection of a novel, set mostly in what looks like late-Victorian London. The streets are gaslit. The underworld teems with the flotsam of empire: lascars, Irishmen and so on, the undesirables of many nations. The city is overwhelmed with crime and prostitution and an influx of immigrants. Opium dens abound. And a serial killer is on the loose. Known as the “head cannibal,” he decapitates his victims after murdering them, but the heads are never found. The Metropolitan Police are baffled, as they were by Jack the Ripper, active in 1888, who also desecrated the bodies of his victims. Meanwhile, characters with names like One-Eyed Jack make shady deals in low taverns, and at the dinner tables of the upper classes Darwinian ideas are hotly debated. It feels as if we’re in the disillusioned twilight of the 19th century, but our narrator — or one of our narrators — sitting in his grandfather’s library “surrounded by Dickens and Collins,” claims that his story is set in 1837, the year Victoria ascended to the throne.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SUBMCGRATH/16SUBMCGRATH-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 873, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SUBMCGRATH/16SUBMCGRATH-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1746, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SUBMCGRATH/16SUBMCGRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SUBMCGRATH/16SUBMCGRATH-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SUBMCGRATH/16SUBMCGRATH-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Vile Bodies', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Vile Bodies', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Khair, Tabish', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:48:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patrick McGrath', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patrick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McGrath', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/30bde4da-2a67-55b5-ba16-0e9af778e0c1', 'word_count': 891, 'uri': 'nyt://article/30bde4da-2a67-55b5-ba16-0e9af778e0c1'}, {'abstract': 'In Tatjana Soli’s second novel, a cancer patient is cared for by an enigmatic Caribbean woman.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/the-forgetting-tree-by-tatjana-soli.html', 'snippet': 'In Tatjana Soli’s second novel, a cancer patient is cared for by an enigmatic Caribbean woman.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her ambitious and much admired 2010 novel “The Lotus Eaters” (which won the James Tait Black Prize, one of the most prestigious in Britain), Tatjana Soli explored the experiences of a female war photographer who spent nearly a dozen years in Vietnam, and connected her themes and subject matter not only to Alfred Tennyson but to the “Odyssey.” In her second novel, “The Forgetting Tree,” she is no less daring, and no less haunting. Soli refuses to do the easy thing — she demands of the reader both patience and trust.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SMILEY/16SMILEY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 782, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SMILEY/16SMILEY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1564, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SMILEY/16SMILEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SMILEY/16SMILEY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/16SMILEY/16SMILEY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'California Gothic', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California Gothic', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Soli, Tatjana', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:49:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jane Smiley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Smiley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1daee14f-6e9f-5504-b2c5-ed6469037ed7', 'word_count': 1152, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1daee14f-6e9f-5504-b2c5-ed6469037ed7'}, {'abstract': 'In Linwood Barclay’s “Trust Your Eyes,” a schizophrenic savant who witnesses a murder suffers from a credibility problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/trust-your-eyes-by-linwood-barclay-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'In Linwood Barclay’s “Trust Your Eyes,” a schizophrenic savant who witnesses a murder suffers from a credibility problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Some days, all you really want is for someone to tell you a \xadwicked-good story. Linwood Barclay answers the reader’s perpetual prayer with TRUST YOUR EYES (New American Library, $25.95), an offbeat thriller about a schizophrenic savant who witnesses a murder but is so clearly nuts that no one will take him seriously. In a neat twist on Hitchcock’s “Rear Window,” Thomas Kilbride, who has been crazy about maps since he was 6 years old, is obsessively poring over Whirl360, a computer program rather like Google Street View, when he fixates on the image of someone being suffocated while standing at a window in an apartment building on the Lower East Side of Manhattan. But because he believes the C.I.A. has assigned him the job of committing to photographic memory all the streets in all the major cities of the world, Thomas has a bit of a credibility problem.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bks-crime/0916-bks-crime-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 987, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bks-crime/0916-bks-crime-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1975, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bks-crime/0916-bks-crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bks-crime/0916-bks-crime-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bks-crime/0916-bks-crime-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Peeping Savant', 'kicker': 'Crime', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Peeping Savant', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Child, Lee', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Read, Cornelia', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barclay, Linwood', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kardos, Michael', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T14:55:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Marilyn Stasio', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Marilyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Stasio', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/667fd615-8150-5dae-bdef-33ac713b1925', 'word_count': 939, 'uri': 'nyt://article/667fd615-8150-5dae-bdef-33ac713b1925'}, {'abstract': 'Acting on a suggestion Faulkner once made, the Folio Society is publishing “The Sound and the Fury” with 14 colors of ink.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/books/review/the-sound-and-the-fury-in-14-colors.html', 'snippet': 'Acting on a suggestion Faulkner once made, the Folio Society is publishing “The Sound and the Fury” with 14 colors of ink.', 'lead_paragraph': 'What happens when you add colored ink to the previous black-and-white type of a William Faulkner classic? Could the technology somehow compromise the reader’s experience, or do multiple inks actually make for a greater novel? Is this what Faulkner had in mind — and does it matter?', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/books/review/0916-bkis-essay/0916-bkis-essay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 777, 'subType': 'jumb