6:02+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Books', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/10840a30-c648-514b-ba41-e021b389eabe', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/10840a30-c648-514b-ba41-e021b389eabe'}, {'abstract': 'In a beachfront area near Chile’s wine country, long waves roll, ceviche is served by a fire and the landscape resists comparison.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/travel/drawn-to-the-magnet-of-chiles-surf-coast.html', 'snippet': 'In a beachfront area near Chile’s wine country, long waves roll, ceviche is served by a fire and the landscape resists comparison.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I FOUND Pichilemu through a local’s tip. In planning an itinerary that would start in Santiago and end a couple of hours south in the wine country, I was looking for a low-key weekend spot near the two destinations where I could stop for a few days and recharge with my family. “Well, there is a new hotel in Pichilemu that opened a few weeks back,” said a new friend who was born in Chile and travels all over the country. I figured if anyone would know, he would. One look at the Web site and I booked in.\xa0', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 661, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1319, 'width': 2042, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16NEXT1/16NEXT1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Drawn to the Magnet of Chile’s Surf Coast', 'kicker': 'Next Stop', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Drawn to the Magnet of Chile’s Surf Coast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chile', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Surfing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T15:46:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ondine Cohane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ondine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/023834b1-3b53-534a-beb2-5a0a8b6fa51a', 'word_count': 1409, 'uri': 'nyt://article/023834b1-3b53-534a-beb2-5a0a8b6fa51a'}, {'abstract': 'Apple’s iPhone 5 got bigger than its predecessor, and some wonder why the company didn’t make room for a technology called near-field communications (N.F.C.), which enables devices to exchange information wirelessly over very short distances. It appears that Apple chose beauty over added functions with its aluminum-and-glass phone.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/iphone-5-nfc/', 'snippet': 'Apple’s iPhone 5 got bigger than its predecessor, and some wonder why the company didn’t make room for a technology called near-field communications (N.F.C.), which enables devices to exchange information wirelessly over very short distances. It a...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Apple’s iPhone 5 got bigger than its predecessor, and some wonder why the company didn’t make room inside for a technology called near-field communications (N.F.C.), which enables devices to exchange information wirelessly over very short distances. This feature could make paying for things with a phone less of a hassle. (You’d swipe the phone over a device at the checkout counter.) It turns out that getting N.F.C. to work right with the new design of the iPhone wasn’t even possible.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/technology/14bits-iphone5/14bits-iphone5-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 686, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/technology/14bits-iphone5/14bits-iphone5-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1346, 'width': 2010, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/technology/14bits-iphone5/14bits-iphone5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/14/technology/14bits-iphone5/14bits-iphone5-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/14/technology/14bits-iphone5/14bits-iphone5-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Why the iPhone 5 Does Not Have N.F.C. — It Can’t', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'IPhone 5 Omits One Technology', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-14T15:46:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Brian X. Chen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Brian', 'middlename': 'X.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0d525c1f-d508-52ea-b023-ced4f0bb71ef', 'word_count': 302, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0d525c1f-d508-52ea-b023-ced4f0bb71ef'}, {'abstract': 'As conflict cools in Colombia’s capital, a centuries-old quarter of pastel-colored homes and cultural attractions is drawing a flood of visitors.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/travel/a-tourist-revival-in-the-heart-of-bogota.html', 'snippet': 'As conflict cools in Colombia’s capital, a centuries-old quarter of pastel-colored homes and cultural attractions is drawing a flood of visitors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was a windswept Saturday in the Candelaria neighborhood of Bogotá, and the Plaza de Bolívar was packed. Troubadours, jugglers, balloon vendors, pineapple sellers and Amazonian fruit-juice pressers vied for the attention of tourists. Grizzled Andean Indians led children around on llamas. Pilgrims gathered at the Bogotá Cathedral, a soaring Gothic structure that contains the remains of the city’s 16th-century founder, the conquistador Gonzalo Jiménez de Quesada. Beside a statue of the South American liberator Simon Bolívar, a tattooed comedian held an audience of hundreds rapt.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1_SPAN-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1_SPAN-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2043, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/travel/16CULTURED1_SPAN/16CULTURED1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tourist Revival in the Heart of Bogotá', 'kicker': 'Cultured Traveler', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In the Heart of Bogotá, a Pueblo', '