iddlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/71225655-d759-556a-9a6d-8af96e6c7b25', 'word_count': 585, 'uri': 'nyt://article/71225655-d759-556a-9a6d-8af96e6c7b25'}, {'abstract': 'The Nissan Altima is now the second-best-selling passenger car in the country. The company has redesigned the sedan for 2013, and although the new car drives beautifully, Nissan has played it safe.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/automobiles/autoreviews/altima-sailing-into-the-mainstream.html', 'snippet': 'The Nissan Altima is now the second-best-selling passenger car in the country. The company has redesigned the sedan for 2013, and although the new car drives beautifully, Nissan has played it safe.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN Nissan unveiled a new Altima for 2002, it heralded the company’s redesign as much as the car’s. Nissan’s workaholic new leader, Carlos Ghosn, was engineering a remarkably fast turnaround that made him a global auto-industry superstar. And the great-looking Altima — formerly an obscure runt among family sedans — was the model that, more than any other, transformed Nissan from a debt-ridden basket case into one of the most profitable automakers. ', 'print_section': 'AU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/automobiles/16ALTI1/16ALTI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/16/automobiles/16ALTI1/16ALTI1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/16/automobiles/16ALTI1/16ALTI1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 542, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo'