tario)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Toronto International Film Festival', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T00:30:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d5fe53f-d599-5031-a09c-4c00575ca65a', 'word_count': 1316, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d5fe53f-d599-5031-a09c-4c00575ca65a'}, {'abstract': 'The American Civil Liberties Union filed a lawsuit against Georgia on Thursday for denying the Ku Klux Klan’s application to participate in an “adopt a highway” program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/15/us/georgia-in-suing-state-aclu-defends-klan.html', 'snippet': 'The American Civil Liberties Union filed a lawsuit against Georgia on Thursday for denying the Ku Klux Klan’s application to participate in an “adopt a highway” program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The American Civil Liberties Union filed a lawsuit against Georgia on Thursday for denying the Ku Klux Klan’s application to participate in an “adopt a highway” program. The lawsuit says the state violated the freedom of speech of a chapter of the Klan in Union County, near the North Carolina border. State transportation officials in June denied the chapter’s application to clean trash along a stretch of Route 515 in the Appalachian Mountains, saying the program was for only “civic-minded organizations in good standing.” Debbie Seagraves, the executive director of the A.C.L.U. Foundation of Georgia, said: “There will always be speech and groups conveying hateful messages that are distasteful to some. That is why the First Amendment protects free speech for all.” The State Transportation Department said it had expected the lawsuit and stood by its decision to deny the application. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Georgia: In Suing State, A.C.L.U. Defends Klan', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | South', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Georgia: In Suing State, A.C.L.U. Defends Klan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ku Klux Klan', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'American Civil Liberties Union', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T00:31:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robbie Brown', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robbie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c40d4d6-d14c-555e-acde-441385c5aa38', 'word_count': 146, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c40d4d6-d14c-555e-acde-441385c5aa38'}, {'abstract': 'False alarms forced students and staff members to flee at the University of Texas, Austin; North Dakota State University, Fargo; and Hiram College in northeast Ohio.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/15/us/colleges-in-three-states-report-bomb-threats.html', 'snippet': 'False alarms forced students and staff members to flee at the University of Texas, Austin; North Dakota State University, Fargo; and Hiram College in northeast Ohio.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Three college campuses in North Dakota, Ohio and Texas were evacuated on Friday after receiving bomb threats. The false alarms forced students and staff members to flee in the middle of classes at the University of Texas, Austin; North Dakota State University, Fargo; and Hiram College in northeast Ohio. In Austin, Rhonda Weldon, a spokeswoman, said a man called claiming “to have placed bombs all over campus” that would detonate in 90 minutes, and officials decided to order the university’s 51,000 students and 24,000 staff and faculty members to evacuate “out of an abundance of caution.” Classes were canceled for the day. At North Dakota State, which has 14,000 students, officials ordered an evacuation after receiving a bomb threat, but the campus was reopened by 1 p.m., officials said. At Hiram, with 1,300 students, crews with bomb-sniffing dogs checked all of the buildings. It was not clear who made the threats or whether they were connected, officials said. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '17', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Colleges in Three States Report Bomb Threats', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Education', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Colleges In Three States Report Bomb Threats', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Threats and Threatening Messages', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Evacuations and Evacuees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T00:33:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manny Fernandez', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fernandez', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/78430b43-749b-58cb-b167-361983c2578b', 'word_count': 162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/78430b43-749b-58cb-b167-361983c2578b'}, {'abstract': 'A man wants to know whether, when he dies, he should be buried with his first wife, who died at 26, or his current wife.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/fashion/take-it-to-the-grave.html', 'snippet': 'A man wants to know whether, when he dies, he should be buried with his first wife, who died at 26, or his current wife.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I married the love of my life at a young age. We were high school sweethearts, together for 12 years and married for 6. Sadly, she passed away when we were 26. Five years later, I met a wonderful woman to whom I am now married, and we have two beautiful children. My second wife understands the deep love I shared with my first wife and respects our bond. When I die, should I be buried with my true love or in a family plot with my second family? Brian, New York ', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail