e': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Liesl Schillinger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Liesl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schillinger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8f105388-0ae0-5cea-a35d-782ebdf90782', 'word_count': 759, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8f105388-0ae0-5cea-a35d-782ebdf90782'}, {'abstract': 'Some of the best baseball stories right now involve unknown players on also-ran teams.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/14/when-good-things-happen-to-bad-seasons/', 'snippet': 'Some of the best baseball stories right now involve unknown players on also-ran teams.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you live in a baseball market where your team is already out of it, it’s likely that for a while there has been talk about next year, about “rebuilding.” I played for my share of those teams, and I know that this is the time of year when you can get a sense of what that rebuilt team might look like once they stop rebuilding. Because, as team rosters expand from 25 to 40 in September, this is when many young players get a true shot. They are penned into the lineup and allowed to play, to make mistakes, to compete against the best, every single night. The team is playing for the future, but the future gets to play, now.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Good Things Happen to Bad Seasons', 'kicker': 'Doug Glanville', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T01:00:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doug Glanville', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doug', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glanville', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 