ctor-in-new-york-democratic-legislative-primary-upsets.html', 'snippet': 'Two state legislators facing inquiries into financial wrongdoing lost Democratic primary elections this week, and a third who is accused in a bribery case won by an unexpectedly narrow margin.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a state with a scant history of electoral upsets, it was not in any job description, but it might as well have been: Once an incumbent, always an incumbent. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Legislators Tainted by Scandal Find Defeat at the Polls', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Legislators Tainted by Scandal Find Defeat at the Polls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Voting and Voters', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rivera, Naomi', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Boyland, William F Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Legislature', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Huntley, Shirley L', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T01:20:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu and Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a86b2ef-75af-518f-9a94-6a956db81693', 'word_count': 724, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a86b2ef-75af-518f-9a94-6a956db81693'}, {'abstract': 'Regulators, led by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, are said to be close to taking action against JPMorgan Chase for insufficient safeguards.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/15/business/money-laundering-inquiry-said-to-target-us-banks.html', 'snippet': 'Regulators, led by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency, are said to be close to taking action against JPMorgan Chase for insufficient safeguards.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Federal and state authorities are investigating a handful of major American banks for failing to monitor cash transactions in and out of their branches, a lapse that may have enabled drug dealers and terrorists to launder tainted money, according to officials who spoke on the condition of anonymity. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Money-Laundering Inquiry Is Said to Aim at U.S. Banks', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Money-Laundering Inquiry Said to Be Looking Into U.S. Banks', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'JPMorgan Chase & Company', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Office of the Comptroller of the Currency', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bank of America Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Banking and Financial Institutions', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-15T01:21:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Silver-Greenberg and Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/548ce6f9-a320-5e28-ae7f-3cf914d2cdc2', 'word_count': 1073, 'uri': 'nyt://article/548ce6f9-a320-5e28-ae7f-3cf914d2cdc2'}, {'abstract': 'Lehman Brothers fell four years ago, and Occupy Wall Street rose last year. Where are we now?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/15/opinion/nocera-two-days-in-september.html', 'snippet': 'Lehman Brothers fell four years ago, and Occupy Wall Street rose last year. Where are we now?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Anniversary No. 1: Exactly four years ago, Lehman Brothers filed for bankruptcy protection. In the days and weeks that followed, we saw the unfolding of the dramatic — and ultimately successful — effort by the federal government to rescue the banking system. But, once the dust settled, we began to see something else: like the stock market crash of 1929, Lehman wasn’t an isolated event. Rather, it was a signal that deep problems had enveloped our economy — problems that wouldn’t be solved quickly or easily. That is why the Lehman bankruptcy still resonates. It was the moment everything changed.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '23', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-jumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-superJumbo-v2.png', 'height': 228, 'width': 228, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2014/11/01/opinion/nocera-circular/nocera-circular-