e': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Bilton', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bilton', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe6a423b-38fa-50d1-af20-a128680aa05b', 'word_count': 628, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe6a423b-38fa-50d1-af20-a128680aa05b'}, {'abstract': 'Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel, appearing on two American Sunday talk shows, reiterated that the best way to prevent Iran from acquiring a nuclear weapon was to draw a “red line” that, if crossed, would trigger military intervention. He also denied that he was injecting himself into the American presidential contest.', 'web_url': 'https://thecaucus.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/16/israeli-leader-says-iran-is-20-yards-from-nuclear-bomb/', 'snippet': 'Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel, appearing on two American Sunday talk shows, reiterated that the best way to prevent Iran from acquiring a nuclear weapon was to draw a “red line” that, if crossed, would trigger military intervention. ...', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel reiterated on Sunday that the best way to prevent Iran from acquiring a nuclear weapon was to draw a “red line” that, if crossed, would trigger military intervention. He also denied that he was injecting himself into one of the most divisive foreign policy issues of the American presidential campaign.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Israeli Leader Says Iran Is Closer to Building a Nuclear Bomb', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuclear Weapons', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', '