dia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sept. 16: Watching the Clock and Awaiting the Unknown', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T12:43:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Silver', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9d285d90-f35b-5365-bfeb-5dd6d44365a2', 'word_count': 1979, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d285d90-f35b-5365-bfeb-5dd6d44365a2'}, {'abstract': 'Giants Coach Tom Coughlin scolded the rookie Bucs coach Greg Schiano for breaking an unwritten rule by having his team try to tackle Eli Manning on a kneel-down at the end of the game.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/schiano-coughlin-and-the-unwritten-rules/', 'snippet': 'Giants Coach Tom Coughlin scolded the rookie Bucs coach Greg Schiano for breaking an unwritten rule by having his team try to tackle Eli Manning on a kneel-down at the end of the game.', 'lead_paragraph': 'O.K., it’s time to get out the unwritten rule book. You know, the one that you can’t see because it doesn’t exist.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/sports/football/17coughlin-pic/17coughlin-pic-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/sports/football/17coughlin-pic/17coughlin-pic-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1367, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/sports/football/17coughlin-pic/17coughlin-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/17/sports/football/17coughlin-pic/17coughlin-pic-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/sports/football/17coughlin-pic/17coughlin-pic-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Schiano, Coughlin and the Unwritten Rules', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coughlin, Tom', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schiano, Greg', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Giants', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tampa Bay Buccaneers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T12:49:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/716e7cc1-2e6a-5910-a0f3-c1681a92b6f9', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/716e7cc1-2e6a-5910-a0f3-c1681a92b6f9'}, {'abstract': 'The Socialist International’s latest congress in Cape Town turned into a good old tussle between North and South.', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/workers-of-the-world-re-unite/', 'snippet': 'The Socialist International’s latest congress in Cape Town turned into a good old tussle between North and South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAPE TOWN, South Africa —', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Workers of the World Reunite!', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Socialist International', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Cape Town (South Africa)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Africa', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T12:52:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de03c634-d6cf-5855-a53d-be040a43dbe7', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de03c634-d6cf-5855-a53d-be040a43dbe7'}, {'abstract': 'From encroaching ice to problems with a spill containment vessel, a series of problems has derailed Shell’s hopes of harvesting oil this year.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/shell-stymied-on-this-years-arctic-oil-quest/', 'snippet': 'From encroaching ice to problems with a spill containment vessel, a series of problems has derailed Shell’s hopes of harvesting oil this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Saying that a spill response dome aboard a vessel has been damaged, Shell announced Monday that it would defer its plan to drill for oil in the Arctic off the Alaskan coast this year. In seeking approval to drill, the company fought a long battle against environmentalists and others who say that doing so in the pristine icy region is too risky. In a statement, Greenpeace claimed vindication and called Shell’s high-stakes program a “monumentally reckless gamble.” But the company says it will press on and seek a safety permit for the vessel next year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Shell Stymied in 2012 Arctic Oil Quest', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T12:55:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e747c458-6041-5efd-99bc-2d77c9694ac7', 'word_count': 96, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e747c458-6041-5efd-99bc-2d77c9694ac7'}, {'abstract': 'Can Panama City’s Old Town survive its own success?', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/t-magazine/panama-city-mission-viejo.html', 'snippet': 'Can Panama City’s Old Town survive its own success?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In Panama City’s oldest standing neighborhood, the rooftops are bright red. Some are made of tile, in the style of the conquistadors who established the quarter in 1673, others of corrugated metal — temporary patches rusted crimson during decades of neglect. Together, they represent the storied past and seedy present of Casco Viejo, a neighborhood Panamanians call Casco and view as both a shrine and slum. These days, empty lots once home to squatters and stray dogs are giving way to valet parking, part of a process that may make the neighborhood more popular, if less compelling. Even hotel developers are torn.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '53', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 809, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1619, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O/23talk-panama-slide-PJ9O-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mission Viejo', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mission Viejo', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Panama City (Panama)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Fred A. Bernstein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Fred', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Bernstein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/cb738c50-fa9e-530f-a61b-f7a569e97db0', 'word_count': 1117, 'uri': 'nyt://article/cb738c50-fa9e-530f-a61b-f7a569e97db0'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: “Charging Bull,” the 7,000-pound bronze statue on Broadway in the financial district, has been a protective captive of the police since the Occupy protests began.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/the-bull-tied-to-occupy-wall-street/', 'snippet': 'The Day: “Charging Bull,” the 7,000-pound bronze statue on Broadway in the financial district, has been a protective captive of the police since the Occupy protests began.', 'lead_paragraph': ' With a full year having passed since it came into being, Occupy Wall Street is noting the moment in a manner that reflects one of the things it does best. It is celebrating itself.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Bull Tied to Occupy Wall Street', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Financial District (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:00:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd9970f5-9bee-50a9-85e2-52926aee50ad', 'word_count': 720, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd9970f5-9bee-50a9-85e2-52926aee50ad'}, {'abstract': 'The strange history of the Ryan delusion.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/concord-conned-etc/', 'snippet': 'The strange history of the Ryan delusion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Alec MacGillis has a long, interesting piece on the Paul Ryan phenomenon — on how Washington managed to convince itself that he was a Serious, Honest Conservative when he was nothing of the sort. There’s a lot of useful information in the piece. But I’d question MacGillis’s central thesis — namely, that Ryan was mainly exploiting the Beltway’s innumeracy.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Concord Conned, Etc.', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:01:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/65fee93f-ff8e-5a10-9026-73435fb4a609', 'word_count': 357, 'uri': 'nyt://article/65fee93f-ff8e-5a10-9026-73435fb4a609'}, {'abstract': 'If union delegates do not accept a proposed contract at a scheduled vote on Tuesday, then a court will hear the city’s case for a preliminary injunction on Wednesday.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/education/chicago-teachers-strike-enters-second-week.html', 'snippet': 'If union delegates do not accept a proposed contract at a scheduled vote on Tuesday, then a court will hear the city’s case for a preliminary injunction on Wednesday.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CHICAGO — As the president of the Chicago Teachers Union emerged from a meeting of her union delegates on Sunday night to announce that, no, school would not be starting up again and that, no, the delegates were not ready to sign off on what had seemed like a deal, Mayor Rahm Emanuel watched her on a television in City Hall. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 617, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1234, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/us/18strike_cnd1/18strike_cnd1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Chicago Strike Goes On, the Mayor Digs In', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Chicago Strike Goes On, the Mayor Digs In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Teachers Union', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Rahm', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:04:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Davey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Davey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/eadc601a-6b2d-51e3-b62a-ccd79a675a44', 'word_count': 1376, 'uri': 'nyt://article/eadc601a-6b2d-51e3-b62a-ccd79a675a44'}, {'abstract': 'While several large venture capital firms have led Square’s past financing rounds, this deal featured less traditional backers, like Rizvi Traverse Management, Citi Ventures and Starbucks.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/square-closes-financing-round/', 'snippet': 'While several large venture capital firms have led Square’s past financing rounds, this deal featured less traditional backers, like Rizvi Traverse Management, Citi Ventures and Starbucks.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Square, the San Francisco-based mobile payments company led by the Twitter co-founder Jack Dorsey, announced on Monday that it had closed its latest financing round. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Square Closes Financing Round', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Commerce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Venture Capital', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorsey, Jack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kleiner Perkins Caufield & Byers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sequoia Capital', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Square Inc', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Starbucks Corporation', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:08:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Evelyn M. Rusli', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Evelyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f3e6bd5d-10d7-52c1-81f4-7ce586bb0b8f', 'word_count': 239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f3e6bd5d-10d7-52c1-81f4-7ce586bb0b8f'}, {'abstract': 'Members of the Sherlock Holmes Society celebrated the 125th anniversary of the story in which Arthur Conan Doyle introduced his famous fictional consulting detective by traveling — in costume — to the waterfall in Switzerland where Holmes was believed, for a time, to have met his demise.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/a-fight-to-the-death-to-commemorate-sherlocks-birth/', 'snippet': 'Members of the Sherlock Holmes Society celebrated the 125th anniversary of the story in which Arthur Conan Doyle introduced his famous fictional consulting detective by traveling — in costume — to the waterfall in Switzerland where Holmes was beli...', 'lead_paragraph': 'You “Downton Abbey” enthusiasts who express your dedication to that show by gathering at parties, enjoying finger sandwiches and engaging in drinking rituals will have to step up your game, because the Sherlock Holmes fans have got you beat. BBC News caught up  with 70 members of the Sherlock Holmes Society (a group that claims more than 1,200 members worldwide) who celebrated the 125th anniversary of the novel in which Arthur Conan Doyle introduced his famous fictional consulting detective by traveling — in costume — to the waterfall in Switzerland where Holmes was believed, for a time, to have met his demise. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Fight to the Death to Commemorate Sherlock’s Birth', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Anniversary Trip For Sherlock Holmes', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holmes, Sherlock (Fictional Character)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Doyle, Arthur Conan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:16:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0de54491-2ffd-59c0-9c18-b4a04e7b368a', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0de54491-2ffd-59c0-9c18-b4a04e7b368a'}, {'abstract': 'An engineer accuses federal regulators of falsely invoking security concerns in redacting parts of a report on the potential for dangerous flooding from dam failures upstream from nuclear plants.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/on-our-radar-nuclear-plants-and-flood-risks/', 'snippet': 'An engineer accuses federal regulators of falsely invoking security concerns in redacting parts of a report on the potential for dangerous flooding from dam failures upstream from nuclear plants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'An engineer within the Nuclear Regulatory Commission accuses its regulators of deliberately covering up information related to the vulnerability of nuclear power plants that sit downstream from large dams and reservoirs. [The Huffington Post]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: Nuclear Plants and Flood Risks', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:30:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d44e5b8d-d453-597d-97c8-67504fe5c10e', 'word_count': 151, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d44e5b8d-d453-597d-97c8-67504fe5c10e'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/what-were-reading-523/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Smithsonian: A celebration of the oft-neglected fondue pot. “If you still have yours kicking around in the closet, I encourage you to crack it out,” because “it’s a wonderfully social way to enjoy food.” — Glenn CollinsThe Washington Post: To ease that arduous transition from summer to fall, Jason Wilson offers the McClelland Cocktail, made with real sloe gin. Why is it called the McClelland? If you know, tell us. — Eric Asimov ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:37:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9538a72-3f46-53ca-b143-9f7cd8f91291', 'word_count': 399, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9538a72-3f46-53ca-b143-9f7cd8f91291'}, {'abstract': 'Also, a film based on a video game wins the weekend’s box office contest; and the practice of letting sources approve their quotes results in a palpable loss to journalism.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/the-breakfast-meeting-youtubes-balancing-act-and-an-app-aims-for-stardom/', 'snippet': 'Also, a film based on a video game wins the weekend’s box office contest; and the practice of letting sources approve their quotes results in a palpable loss to journalism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Google rejected a request from the White House last week to pull “The Innocence of Muslims” from its YouTube service in light of the spreading protests of the video in the Muslim world. At the same time, it did temporarily block the video from Egypt and Libya, where the protests have been the most fierce – and that, in a nutshell, is the tricky situation Google faces in trying to fashion a policy on the acceptability of content viewed around the world, Somini Sengupta writes. Google quickly determined that the video — which depicts the Prophet Muhammad as a child molester and thug, among other things — did not violate its hate speech rules or any law, but nonetheless took it down in Egypt and Libya “given the very sensitive situation” there.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: YouTube’s Balancing Act, and an App Aims for Stardom', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T13:45:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2ebaa2b7-86b1-50fc-870e-a54cfda2f143', 'word_count': 560, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2ebaa2b7-86b1-50fc-870e-a54cfda2f143'}, {'abstract': 'The more income you earn, the more likely you are to have gone to college.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/college-still-worth-it/', 'snippet': 'The more income you earn, the more likely you are to have gone to college.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Adam Looney and Michael Greenstone at the Hamilton Project have put together a beautiful chart illustrating once again why college is worth it, despite the current fad of claiming otherwise.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'College, Still Worth It', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T14:00:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/266b70f2-68af-523e-85a3-916bcd2f7cd3', 'word_count': 217, 'uri': 'nyt://article/266b70f2-68af-523e-85a3-916bcd2f7cd3'}, {'abstract': 'The case against China on cars and auto parts will have little immediate impact, but it is one of the few legal options available.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/business/global/us-files-wto-case-against-china-over-cars.html', 'snippet': 'The case against China on cars and auto parts will have little immediate impact, but it is one of the few legal options available.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BEIJING — President Obama’s trade case against China on cars and auto parts will have little immediate impact on jobs and companies in the United States, but it is one of the few legal options available to the United States as China’s auto industry faces overcapacity pro