None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sustainable Living', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T14:53:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Christopher F. Schuetze', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Christopher', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Schuetze', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/153c048d-3964-544b-8342-63f216e0cf30', 'word_count': 841, 'uri': 'nyt://article/153c048d-3964-544b-8342-63f216e0cf30'}, {'abstract': 'It’s time to reset the bar on the unfortunate practice of quotation approval by news sources.', 'web_url': 'https://publiceditor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/the-times-needs-a-policy-on-quotation-approval-and-soon/', 'snippet': 'It’s time to reset the bar on the unfortunate practice of quotation approval by news sources.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Carr wrote a great column in Monday’s New York Times on the topic of news sources demanding that they approve quotations in stories. Mr. Carr’s “The Media Equation” had the headline “The Puppetry of Quotation Approval,” and it laid out just what an unfortunate practice has developed. He wrote: “Inch by inch, story by story, deal by deal, we are giving away our right to ask a simple question and expect a simple answer. It may seem obvious but it is still worth stating: The first draft of history should not be rewritten by the people who make it.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Times Needs a Policy on Quotation Approval, and Soon', 'kicker': 'The Public Editor&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T14:53:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Margaret Sullivan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Margaret', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sullivan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3bfecbb9-5246-53ca-a129-8da4c9e0ce69', 'word_count': 485, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3bfecbb9-5246-53ca-a129-8da4c9e0ce69'}, {'abstract': 'And why do so many companies crash and burn after roaring starts?', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/introducing-the-next-level-can-you-teach-entrepreneurship/', 'snippet': 'And why do so many companies crash and burn after roaring starts?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Talk about needing an exit strategy. I needed one from the humidity, heat and mosquitoes of the Okefenokee Swamp. But how does one go from Swamp Road in Waycross, Ga., to running a global company? I’m sure there are other routes, but for me the fastest roads out were entrepreneurship and education.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Introducing The Next Level: Can You Teach Entrepreneurship?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Business Schools', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T15:00:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cliff Oxford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cliff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oxford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/acf