ocations', 'value': 'Kolkata (India)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T23:48:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gardiner Harris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gardiner', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Harris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/42677402-01ef-53d8-bfdb-345a2b1096fa', 'word_count': 1324, 'uri': 'nyt://article/42677402-01ef-53d8-bfdb-345a2b1096fa'}, {'abstract': 'Seizing on Newsweek’s invitation to discuss its provocative cover story under the Twitter hashtag #MuslimRage, thousands of Muslim users of the social network mocked the premise', 'web_url': 'https://thelede.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/newsweeks-muslim-rage-cover-mocked-online/', 'snippet': 'Seizing on Newsweek’s invitation to discuss its provocative cover story under the Twitter hashtag #MuslimRage, thousands of Muslim users of the social network mocked the premise', 'lead_paragraph': 'CAIRO — After a week of violent protests over an online video demeaning the Prophet Muhammad, the American news media has conducted a searching psychoanalysis of the Muslim mind to ask why such an offense should trigger such wrath. Essayists have generalized about resentments dating back to the 8th century, an anachronistic discomfort with modernity, or the excesses of Islamist politics, among other familiar themes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newsweek’s ‘Muslim Rage’ Cover Mocked Online', 'kicker': 'The Lede', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Newsweek', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-17T23:52:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Blogs', 'byline': {'original': 'By David D. Kirkpatrick', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fac7453d-4fb6-5f1d-8b3b-449a5f99a98a', 'word_count': 187, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fac7453d-4fb6-5f1d-8b3b-449a5f99a98a'}, {'abstract': 'The Czech Republic has banned the sale of liquor containing 20 percent or higher alcohol content after at least 20 people were killed from consuming methanol-tainted spirits.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/world/europe/czechs-ban-hard-liquor-sales-after-methanol-poisonings.html', 'snippet': 'The Czech Republic has banned the sale of liquor containing 20 percent or higher alcohol content after at least 20 people were killed from consuming methanol-tainted spirits.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PRAGUE — The Czech Republic has banned the sale of liquor containing 20 percent or higher alcohol content after at least 20 people were killed and dozens of others were seriously injured from consuming methanol-tainted spirits.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/world/CZECH-1/CZECH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/18/world/CZECH-1/CZECH-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/world/CZECH-1/CZECH-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 632, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'c