article/95c05b2b-8bdb-5e36-871a-a06ce457e307'}, {'abstract': 'Pennsylvania’s Board of Pardons denied on Monday an application for clemency by Terrance Williams, a convicted murderer who is scheduled to be executed on Oct. 3.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/us/pennsylvania-board-denies-clemency-for-terrance-williams.html', 'snippet': 'Pennsylvania’s Board of Pardons denied on Monday an application for clemency by Terrance Williams, a convicted murderer who is scheduled to be executed on Oct. 3.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Pennsylvania’s Board of Pardons in Harrisburg denied on Monday an application for clemency by Terrance Williams, a convicted murderer who is scheduled to be executed on Oct. 3. Clemency requires unanimous approval by the five-member board, and the vote was only 3 to 2 in favor of the application. Shawn Nolan, Mr. Williams’s attorney, argued that Mr. Williams had been beaten and sexually abused during childhood, and had killed Amos Norwood in Philadelphia in 1984 after the victim sexually abused him from the age of 13. Tom Dolgenos of the Philadelphia district attorney’s office said the allegations of sexual abuse were “hearsay” raised by Mr. Williams years after his 1986 trial in an attempt to prevent the execution. Mr. Williams is now seeking a stay of execution in a state court. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pennsylvania: Board Denies Clemency ', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Mid-Atlantic', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pennsylvania: Board Denies Clemency', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pennsylvania', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Murders and Attempted Murders', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Williams, Terrance', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sex Crimes', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Capital Punishment', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T00:55:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Hurdle', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hurdle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/5cb43a11-d9ca-53d7-af9b-a6d884d2f740', 'word_count': 133, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5cb43a11-d9ca-53d7-af9b-a6d884d2f740'}, {'abstract': 'Ambassador Michael B. Oren, who happens to have been born and bred in New Jersey, is representing a prime minister who has infuriated the White House.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/world/middleeast/israeli-diplomat-michael-oren-is-man-in-middle.html', 'snippet': 'Ambassador Michael B. Oren, who happens to have been born and bred in New Jersey, is representing a prime minister who has infuriated the White House.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — With Prime Minister Benjamin Netanyahu of Israel engaged in an unusually public dispute with the Obama administration over Iran, Mr. Netanyahu’s man in Washington, Michael B. Oren, has been working rooms all over town.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/world/JP-OREN/JP-OREN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/18/world/JP-OREN/JP-OREN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/world/JP-OREN/JP-OREN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit'