_id': 'nyt://article/9d65012a-e653-5149-97e9-f320fd9cc5ca', 'word_count': 1382, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9d65012a-e653-5149-97e9-f320fd9cc5ca'}, {'abstract': 'Why your friends seem to have more friends than you do: a mathematical explanation.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/friends-you-can-count-on/', 'snippet': 'Why your friends seem to have more friends than you do: a mathematical explanation.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You spend your time tweeting, friending, liking, poking, and in the few minutes left, cultivating friends in the flesh. Yet sadly, despite all your efforts, you probably have fewer friends than most of your friends have. But don’t despair — the same is true for almost all of us. Our friends are typically more popular than we are.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Friends You Can Count On', 'kicker': 'Me, Myself and Math', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mathematics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Facebook Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:00:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Strogatz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strogatz', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/19347082-62f8-56ab-85be-7e20059b292f', 'word_count': 2197, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19347082-62f8-56ab-85be-7e20059b292f'}, {'abstract': 'After paying nearly $120 million for it at auction, the new owner of Edvard Munch’s “Scream” is lending it to the Museum of Modern Art.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/arts/design/munchs-scream-to-hang-for-six-months-at-moma.html', 'snippet': 'After paying nearly $120 million for it at auction, the new owner of Edvard Munch’s “Scream” is lending it to the Museum of Modern Art.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Edvard Munch’s 1895 version of “The Scream” — which became the most expensive artwork ever sold at auction when it brought nearly $120 million at Sotheby’s in May — will go on view at the Museum of Modern Art, courtesy of its new mystery owner, for six months, starting on Oct. 24. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/arts/MUSEUM/MUSEUM-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/arts/MUSEUM/MUSEUM-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/arts/MUSEUM/MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/18/arts/MUSEUM/MUSEUM-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/arts/MUSEUM/MUSEUM-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Scream’ to Go on View at MoMA', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘Scream’ To Go On View At MoMA', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Museum of Modern Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Munch, Edvard', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Black, Leon D', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carol Vogel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carol', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vogel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e6d19e9-a660-58fc-bb63-36cfa0d199ce', 'word_count': 671, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e6d19e9-a660-58fc-bb63-36cfa0d199ce'}, {'abstract': 'It shouldn’t come as a surprise to us that not everyone in the world worships the First Amendment.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/17/libya-violence-and-free-speech/', 'snippet': 'It shouldn’t come as a surprise to us that not everyone in the world worships the First Amendment.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Back when Salman Rushdie was made the object of a fatwa because his book “The Satanic Verses” was regarded by many Iranians as a blasphemy against the prophet, I went to a conference where a panel discussion was devoted to Rushdie’s situation. A member of the audience raised his hand and, without a trace of irony, asked, “What’s the matter with those Iranians? Haven’t they ever heard of the First Amendment?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Libya, Violence and Free Speech', 'kicker': 'Stanley Fish', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Freedom of Speech and Expression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stanley Fish', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stanley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fish', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a3dd398-af87-545e-8d0a-985814096a7e', 'word_count': 1162, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a3dd398-af87-545e-8d0a-985814096a7e'}, {'abstract': 'The commissioner of the Massachusetts Department of Public Health resigned on Monday amid allegations that a chemist at a state drug laboratory mishandled evidence samples.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/us/massachusetts-health-official-resigns.html', 'snippet': 'The commissioner of the Massachusetts Department of Public Health resigned on Monday amid allegations that a chemist at a state drug laboratory mishandled evidence samples.', 'lead_paragraph': ' The commissioner of the Massachusetts Department of Public Health, John Auerbach, resigned on Monday amid allegations that a chemist at a state drug laboratory mishandled evidence samples, potentially affecting 34,000 criminal cases. Gov. Deval Patrick ordered the laboratory shut down late last month as prosecutors, the state police and the state attorney general’s office investigate whether Annie Dookhan, a chemist, breached protocol, potentially causing wrongful convictions in state cases. Mr. Auerbach is the most senior official to step down in the scandal; last week, the laboratory’s director resigned and another official there was fired. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Massachusetts: Health Official Resigns', 'kicker': 'National Briefing | Northeast', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Massachusetts: Health Official Resigns', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Auerbach, John', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dookhan, Annie', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Forensic Science', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Massachusetts', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:02:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jess Bidgood', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jess', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bidgood', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/35421765-5b0f-5fb0-9fae-8d1a2634921c', 'word_count': 94, 'uri': 'nyt://article/35421765-5b0f-5fb0-9fae-8d1a2634921c'}, {'abstract': 'The Vatican could stop worrying about what Roman Catholic nuns are up to if it just looked at the work being done at Providence House in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/opinion/speaking-the-truth-to-the-vatican.html', 'snippet': 'The Vatican could stop worrying about what Roman Catholic nuns are up to if it just looked at the work being done at Providence House in New York.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After years of drug addiction, serial theft and hard prison time, a 54-year-old mother named Renee is entering a revolutionary phase of her life — transition from a rehabilitation sanctuary in Coney Island to the possibility of self-reliance in the outside world. The sanctuary is one of several outposts of Providence House, a recovery program run by the Sisters of St. Joseph, a society of street-tough, adaptive New York nuns. The program has nurtured more than 12,000 women parolees and their children back from the brink.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Speaking the Truth to the Vatican', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Speaking the Truth to the Vatican', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Nuns', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Roman Catholic Church', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Leadership Conference of Women Religious', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:07:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/52f072a8-15c0-5b0d-853c-3f9442474fdf', 'word_count': 344, 'uri': 'nyt://article/52f072a8-15c0-5b0d-853c-3f9442474fdf'}, {'abstract': 'The winning lottery numbers for Sept. 17, 2012, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/nyregion/lottery-numbers-for-new-york-new-jersey-and-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'The winning lottery numbers for Sept. 17, 2012, for New York, New Jersey and Connecticut.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sept. 17, 2012', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Lottery Numbers', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Lottery Numbers', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lotteries', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Connecticut', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:08:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/1f538202-4d84-5294-8dc5-abc7a78859e4', 'word_count': 211, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1f538202-4d84-5294-8dc5-abc7a78859e4'}, {'abstract': 'Legislators need to find a way to save the deteriorating Pier 40, one of the largest sources of revenue of the cherished Hudson River Park.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/opinion/save-pier-40-and-save-the-hudson-river-park.html', 'snippet': 'Legislators need to find a way to save the deteriorating Pier 40, one of the largest sources of revenue of the cherished Hudson River Park.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New Yorkers cherish their access to the piers and green spaces that make up the Hudson River Park, which stretches from Pier 99 near 59th St. to Battery Place. Established by state law in 1998, the park gives urbanites a chance to stroll along one the nation’s great rivers. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Pier to Pay for a Park', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Pier to Pay for a Park', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parks and Other Recreation Areas', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hudson River Park Trust', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hudson River Park (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:09:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/1e37ba2d-8035-5006-979b-87a67735f642', 'word_count': 379, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1e37ba2d-8035-5006-979b-87a67735f642'}, {'abstract': 'Voters in last Thursday’s primary were handed ballots with candidates’ names printed in an eye-straining 7-point type, akin to the ingredient list on the side of a cereal box.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/nyregion/n