g and Voters', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Primaries and Caucuses', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:11:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael M. Grynbaum', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b3d7ec8e-91f0-5ab5-934f-0dc3bccff55f', 'word_count': 749, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b3d7ec8e-91f0-5ab5-934f-0dc3bccff55f'}, {'abstract': 'An unnecessary strike is brought on by distrust and provocations from both the city and the teachers’ union.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/opinion/chicago-standoff.html', 'snippet': 'An unnecessary strike is brought on by distrust and provocations from both the city and the teachers’ union.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Chicago teachers’ strike should serve as an object lesson about the dangers of incendiary language in labor negotiations. The strike, which appeared to be near settlement over the weekend, has continued after angry union delegates failed to vote to return to work on Sunday. The dispute need never have reached this point, with 350,000 students starting a second week out of classes.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Chicago Standoff', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Chicago Standoff', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Chicago Teachers Union', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teachers and School Employees', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Strikes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Emanuel, Rahm', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Chicago (Ill)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lewis, Karen (1953- )', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T01:11:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/4cdc1e85-5c65-526a-8031-7c6ee4f7b7d6', 'word_count': 415, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4cdc1e85-5c65-526a-8031-7c6ee4f7b7d6'}, {'abstract': 'Iran’s top atomic energy official said that explosions were intended to cut power to two uranium enrichment facilities and claimed saboteurs had infiltrated an international inspection agency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/world/middleeast/iran-scientist-says-blasts-targeted-nuclear-sites.html', 'snippet': 'Iran’s top atomic energy official said that explosions were intended to cut power to two uranium enrichment facilities and claimed saboteurs had infiltrated an international inspection agency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Iran’s most senior atomic energy official revealed on Monday that separate explosions, which he attributed to sabotage, had targeted power supplies to the country’s two main uranium enrichment facilities, including the deep underground site that American and Israeli officials say is the most invulnerable to bombing.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Iranian Official Says Blasts Targeted Nuclear Sites', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Iranian Says Nuclear Sites Were Targets Of Explosions', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'n