Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T10:00:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bruce Bartlett', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bruce', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bartlett', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bdf316e-780b-5fd7-b519-9049e8c762f1', 'word_count': 1183, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bdf316e-780b-5fd7-b519-9049e8c762f1'}, {'abstract': 'A Dartmouth computer scientist and a former Adobe executive have created a start-up company in digital image forensics, Fourandsix Technologies. Its product determines whether photographic images have been altered. The core market is law enforcement and news organizations. The open question, the company’s president said, is how large a business there may prove to be for its technology in fields like health care, mobile payments and social media.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/taking-digital-image-forensics-out-of-the-lab-and-into-the-marketplace/', 'snippet': 'A Dartmouth computer scientist and a former Adobe executive have created a start-up company in digital image forensics, Fourandsix Technologies. Its product determines whether photographic images have been altered. The core market is law enforceme...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Hany Farid, a professor at Dartmouth College, has built a career and a reputation as a leading researcher in digital image forensics. He has made software tools for a number of impressive projects in recent years. One was a pixel-sleuthing program to detect how much fashion photographs have been burnished with Adobe’s Photoshop editing program to remove wrinkles and flab, while plumping up lips and breasts. Another was software for the automated detection of child pornography on the Web to help law enforcement agencies.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 264, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 264}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 1855, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/18/technology/18bits-photoshop/18bits-photoshop-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Start-Up Figures Out Photoshop Abuses', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject'