': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e50717ab-5788-5f91-b620-63edd80cb829', 'word_count': 11, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e50717ab-5788-5f91-b620-63edd80cb829'}, {'abstract': 'And scantily dressed Russians, too.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/fire-that-writer/', 'snippet': 'And scantily dressed Russians, too.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Clearly, we’re living in a bad political novel written by some kind of liberal. I mean, things like this don’t happen in real life:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fire That Writer!', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T12:39:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5fab26f6-371d-5c99-a6b5-fd0b2792592f', 'word_count': 163, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5fab26f6-371d-5c99-a6b5-fd0b2792592f'}, {'abstract': 'They’re how the thing is supposed to work.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/inflation-expectations-a-feature-not-a-bug/', 'snippet': 'They’re how the thing is supposed to work.', 'lead_paragraph': 'And now for something completely different: I’m a bit puzzled by the tone of this FT report on how QE3 is doing so far, US inflation fears rise after QE3, which seems to imply that a rise in breakeven rates — the difference between the interest rate on ordinary bonds and inflation-protected bonds — is a danger sign. (Breakeven rates are a simple gauge of expected inflation).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Inflation Expectations: A Feature, Not A Bug', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T12:46:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9004990-379c-5594-801e-3e930b79b7b3', 'word_count': 138, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9004990-379c-5594-801e-3e930b79b7b3'}, {'abstract': 'What you need to know today about Wall Street, deals, hedge funds, private equity and the law.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/morning-agenda-is-occupy-a-fad/', 'snippet': 'What you need to know today about Wall Street, deals, hedge funds, private equity and the law.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Occupy Wall Street: From Frenzy to Fad? \xa0|\xa0 Occupy Wall Street celebrated its one-year anniversary on Monday as protesters filled the Financial District. But the movement — which initially struck a chord, or a nerve, depending on your view — doesn’t seem to be generating the same buzz anymore. As Andrew Ross Sorkin writes, Occupy Wall Street may now be more of a footnote in financial history:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Occupy a Fad?', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T12:54:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By William Alden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'William', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a16d5775-3ff1-5ca9-99e3-f0552b1e8ac3', 'word_count': 1918, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a16d5775-3ff1-5ca9-99e3-f0552b1e8ac3'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: The Jewish New Year began 11 days spread over a month and a half when alternate-side street-cleaning regulations are suspended.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/welcome-all-the-days-of-forgiveness-to-car-owners/', 'snippet': 'The Day: The Jewish New Year began 11 days spread over a month and a half when alternate-side street-cleaning regulations are suspended.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Monday was the first day of Rosh Hashana, the Jewish New Year, and for observant Jews it ushered in a solemn 10-day period of reflection and repentance. For some of them and for others of different religions, it also ushered in a longer period of gratification and joy. These are New Yorkers, nonbelievers included, who share a bond that transcends faith. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Welcome, All, the Days of Forgiveness to Car Owners', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parking', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rosh Hashanah', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traffic and Parking Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T12:56:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b74a22de-625c-5331-b786-4179e752d667', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b74a22de-625c-5331-b786-4179e752d667'}, {'abstract': 'Facing criticism for saying 47 percent of voters believe they are “victims,” Mitt Romney sought to steer the conversation toward a debate about government’s role in people’s lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/us/politics/in-leaked-video-romney-says-middle-east-peace-process-likely-to-remain-unsolved-problem.html', 'snippet': 'Facing criticism for saying 47 percent of voters believe they are “victims,” Mitt Romney sought to steer the conversation toward a debate about government’s role in people’s lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney on Tuesday fully embraced the substance of his secretly recorded comments that 47 percent of Americans are too dependent on government, saying that his views helped define the philosophical choice for voters in his campaign against President Obama.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/us/jp-19romney1/jp-19romney1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/us/jp-19romney1/jp-19romney1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/us/jp-19romney1/jp-19romney1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 623, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/us/jp-19romney1/jp-19romney1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1246, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/us/jp-19romney1/jp-19romney1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/19/us/j