mise, a program announced in November 2005 that pays for the city’s high-school graduates to go to public colleges in Michigan, is accompanied by a sidebar that imagines what it would take to do the same for students in New York City. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/magazine/17-harlem-students/17-harlem-students-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/magazine/17-harlem-students/17-harlem-students-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/magazine/17-harlem-students/17-harlem-students-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/17/magazine/17-harlem-students/17-harlem-students-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/magazine/17-harlem-students/17-harlem-students-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Skepticism Too High for the Kalamazoo Promise to Work in New York?', 'kicker': 'The 6th Floor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Philanthropy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Scholarships and Fellowships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kalamazoo Promise', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T14:49:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bill Vourvoulias', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bill', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vourvoulias', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/656c26aa-34c8-5b82-b4cf-2e355b3a33cc', 'word_count': 433, 'uri': 'nyt://article/656c26aa-34c8-5b82-b4cf-2e355b3a33cc'}, {'abstract': 'Don’t let okra’s slimy reputation put you off: Whole small pods add great texture, flavor and nutrition to this dish, without the slime.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/health/nutrition/couscous-with-tomatoes-okra-and-chickpeas-recipes-for-health.html', 'snippet': 'Don’t let okra’s slimy reputation put you off: Whole small pods add great texture, flavor and nutrition to this dish, without the slime.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Okra is popular in the North African cuisines of Tunisia and Algeria, where it is also dried. Because you don’t cut it up, the okra doesn’t become slimy. It contributes great flavor to the stew. Very high in dietary fiber, okra is a great source of vitamins A, C, B complex and the phytonutrients glutathione, xanthin, lutein and beta carotene, all believed to have antioxidant properties. For the best texture and flavor, look for the smallest pods you can find.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/health/nutrition/19recipehealth-pic/19recipehealth-pic-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/health/nutrition/19recipehealth-pic/19recipehealth-pic-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth':