stname': 'Stefania', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rousselle', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/5ca4d400-772b-50db-88bd-dc81eded7780', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/5ca4d400-772b-50db-88bd-dc81eded7780'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the London fashion week photo diary of Kasia Bobula.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/photos-of-the-moment-j-w-anderson-5/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the London fashion week photo diary of Kasia Bobula.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of The Moment | J.W. Anderson', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T17:15:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kasia Bobula', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kasia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bobula', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3fc526db-0a73-5b4c-823d-d14b2d5b071b', 'word_count': 36, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3fc526db-0a73-5b4c-823d-d14b2d5b071b'}, {'abstract': 'Both Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring” and Debussy’s “Jeux” were radical works that still have the power to shock.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/arts/music/radical-music-sometimes-shocking-sometimes-subtle.html', 'snippet': 'Both Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring” and Debussy’s “Jeux” were radical works that still have the power to shock.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On May 15, 1913, at the Théâtre des Champs-Élysées in Paris, just two weeks before the premiere of Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring” provoked a riot among outraged audience members, the Ballets Russes presented the premiere of another daring ballet, also choreographed by Vaslav Nijinsky, also conducted by Pierre Monteux. It was Debussy’s “Jeux,” the composer’s last work for orchestra, commissioned by the company director, Sergei Diaghilev. Though not well received, the ballet was much discussed in Parisian cultural circles, until, that is, it was eclipsed by the scandal of the “Rite of Spring” (“Le Sacre du Printemps”).', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 424, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 424}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 821, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1420, 'width': 1772, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radical-1/19radical-1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Shocking or Subtle, Still Radical', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Shocking Or Subtle, Still Radical', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'The Rite of Spring (Ballet)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stravinsky, Igor', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Debussy, Claude', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T17:23:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/662219ff-aaaa-5a89-b698-d946704b2f00', 'word_count': 1276, 'uri': 'nyt://article/662219ff-aaaa-5a89-b698-d946704b2f00'}, {'abstract': 'The Republican nominee has failed to distance himself from his predecessor’s failures, but he’s also failed to imitate his successes.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/what-bush-knew-and-romney-doesnt/', 'snippet': 'The Republican nominee has failed to distance himself from his predecessor’s failures, but he’s also failed to imitate his successes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I used my Campaign Stops column last week to make the case (not for the first time) that Mitt Romney is struggling to defeat Barack Obama in large part because the public’s memory of how the Bush era ended remains a major drag on the Republican Party’s political prospects, and Romney hasn’t done nearly enough to define himself as a post-Bush figure. But with the whole political world talking about the leaked fundraiser tape in which Romney channels an inaccurate and self-defeating right-wing talking point to basically write off 47 percent of America as irresponsible, dependent, guaranteed-to-vote Obama moochers,\xa0it’s worth pointing out that the current Republican nominee has also conspicuously failing to learn from George W. Bush’s substantial political successes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Bush Knew, and Romney Doesn’t', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T17:35:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/927d403f-c7c7-50b3-bea6-da88f2c975ba', 'word_count': 1007, 'uri': 'nyt://article/927d403f-c7c7-50b3-bea6-da88f2c975ba'}, {'abstract': 'Only 3 of the 28 players on the Israeli team in the World Baseball Classic are from Israel, the rest being Americans with professional baseball experience and Jewish roots.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/09/19/sports/baseball/19israeli_ss.html', 'snippet': 'Only 3 of the 28 players on the Israeli team in the World Baseball Classic are from Israel, the rest being Americans with professional baseball experience and Jewish roots.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Only 3 of the 28 players on the Israeli team in the World Baseball Classic are from Israel, the rest being Americans with professional baseball experience and Jewish roo