ocument_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/adc7032b-a160-5316-8d69-c3850e3f3add', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/adc7032b-a160-5316-8d69-c3850e3f3add'}, {'abstract': 'Just two decades ago, the beautiful and otherworldly red lionfish started showing up in south Florida and the Caribbean. Now they’re a plague.', 'web_url': 'https://bittman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/18/scourge-of-the-lionfish-part-4-from-beautiful-novelty-to-marine-invader/', 'snippet': 'Just two decades ago, the beautiful and otherworldly red lionfish started showing up in south Florida and the Caribbean. Now they’re a plague.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Carl Safina is founder of Blue Ocean Institute at Stony Brook University and a MacArthur Fellow. His books include “A Sea in Flames: The Deepwater Horizon Oil Blowout,” and “The View From Lazy Point; A Natural Year in an Unnatural World,” which won the \xa02012 Orion Award. His series “Saving the Ocean” will be premiering this fall on PBS television. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Scourge of the Lionfish, Part 4: From Beautiful Novelty to Marine Invader', 'kicker': 'Mark Bittman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fishing, Commercial', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Invasive Species', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oceans and Seas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Safina, Carl', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Blue Ocean Institute', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Public Broadcasting Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'State University of New York at Stony Brook', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Atlantic Ocean', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Bahama Islands', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Caribbean Area', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Florida', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Indian Ocean', 'rank': 12, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mexico', 'rank': 13, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T17:42:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Carl Safina', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Carl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Safina', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7527a8fa-ed52-59bd-a4e8-1b83dabaa15a', 'word_count': 388, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7527a8fa-ed52-59bd-a4e8-1b83dabaa15a'}, {'abstract': 'The Times asked readers for their family’s favorite traditional recipes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/09/17/dining/traditional-reader-recipes.html', 'snippet': 'The Times asked readers for their family’s favorite traditional recipes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times asked readers for their family’s favorite traditional recipes.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Reader Recipes: Family Traditions', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recipes', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T17:46:42+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Food', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/ec77dfdf-7bc1-5c49-a139-d38e41fb6527', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/ec77dfdf-7bc1-5c49-a139-d38e41fb6527'}, {'abstract': 'Family recipes, handed down by immigrants, link generations and keep a heritage alive.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/dining/in-the-kitchen-holding-on-to-heritage-before-it-slips-away.html', 'snippet': 'Family recipes, handed down by immigrants, link generations and keep a heritage alive.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MENTION the Bahamas, and most people naturally envision idyllic seascapes with pearly beaches and turquoise waters. For me, though, any talk of my mother’s homeland always brings to mind the tang of sour limes and the sweetness of freshly cut coconut, the tastes that flavored my childhood.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/19/dining/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN/19TRADITIONS3_SPAN-th