lled as fumbles, but the N.F.L. said current officials were acting “admirably.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a normal season, a group of N.F.L. referees taking a few minutes under the lights of “Monday Night Football” to figure out where a ball should be placed after a defensive holding penalty would be amusing. But with replacement officials making scenes like that one too common and sometimes struggling with the rules of the game, the amusement has begun to fade.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 690, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1379, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/sports/JPREFEREES/JPREFEREES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Replacement Officials Face Growing Criticism', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Replacement Officials Face Growing Criticism', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T19:51:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Benjamin Hoffman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Benjamin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hoffman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b4bc813-b6b9-599c-a8c9-3f6cb25e2ec8', 'word_count': 870, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b4bc813-b6b9-599c-a8c9-3f6cb25e2ec8'}, {'abstract': 'The documentary “Radio Unnameable” pays tribute both to Bob Fass, an influential voice in broadcasting since the 1960s, and to the times whose ideals and follies he helped articulate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/movies/radio-unnameable-a-documentary-about-bob-fass-of-wbai.html', 'snippet': 'The documentary “Radio Unnameable” pays tribute both to Bob Fass, an influential voice in broadcasting since the 1960s, and to the times whose ideals and follies he helped articulate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On the night of Feb. 11, 1967, hundreds — maybe thousands — of people congregated in the international terminal of Kennedy Airport, not to embark on flights to far-flung places but rather, well, it isn’t entirely clear or relevant. The gathering was an impromptu party, a nonpolitical demonstration, a happening named, in the spirit of the times, a fly-in. Now we might be inclined to see it as a prehistoric flash mob, an example of the power of communication technology to create instantaneous, ephemeral but nonetheless meaningful communities.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radio-inline/19radio-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 934, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radio-inline/19radio-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1868, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radio-inline/19radio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radio-inline/19radio-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19radio-inline/19radio-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rekindling the Spirit of the ’60s, Even for Those Who Can’t Remember', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rekindling the Spirit of the ’60s, Even for Those Who Can’t Remember', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Radio', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fass, Bob', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lovelace, Paul', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-18T19:52:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/96938809-e02c-5d76-9ee0-596bb867ba91', 'word_count': 704, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96938809-e02c-5d76-9ee0-596bb867ba91'}, {'abstract': 'Wrenching footage of missile attacks and their civilian victims in Vibeke Lokkeberg’s film transcends the politics of the conflict that underlies the bloodshed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/movies/tears-of-gaza-a-documentary-of-human-misery.html', 'snippet': 'Wrenching footage of missile attacks and their civilian victims in Vibeke Lokkeberg’s film transcends the politics of the conflict that underlies the bloodshed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A brutally uncompromising blast of outrage, Vibeke Lokkeberg’s “Tears of Gaza” is less a documentary than a collage of suffering. Dropping us smack in the middle of the Israeli attacks on Gaza in the winter of 2008-9, the film tramples politics beneath the raw weight of civilian testimony. Woven together, these monologues of bereavement and confusion, illustrated with images so terrible they repel rational explanation, form a tapestry of human misery that’s impossible to shake off. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19tears/19tears-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/19tears/19tears-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url':