terial': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/194eec1a-4514-5fd9-9f97-ccbc2fcd1801', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/194eec1a-4514-5fd9-9f97-ccbc2fcd1801'}, {'abstract': 'Bel Borba, an artist from Salvador da Bahia, Brazil, is spending a month making public art in New York neighborhoods.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/arts/design/bel-borba-brings-contagious-creativity-to-new-york-streets.html', 'snippet': 'Bel Borba, an artist from Salvador da Bahia, Brazil, is spending a month making public art in New York neighborhoods.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It was an odd sight for an industrial street in the Ridgewood section of Queens, so of course the delivery-truck drivers, the workers from nearby manufacturing plants and other curious passers-by felt compelled to stop, look and ask questions. On the maroon-colored external wall of a furniture factory, the Brazilian artist Bel Borba was busy making a large mosaic of white tile, portraying a globe surrounded by objects that looked like a cross between sunflowers and mechanical fans.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/BORBA/BORBA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 384, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/BORBA/BORBA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 384}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/BORBA/BORBA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 684, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/19/arts/BORBA/BORBA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1368, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',