inding part-time work, starting a new business, overcoming age discrimination and more.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Readers recently submitted questions about finding a job in middle age to Tory Johnson, founder of Women for Hire, which produces career expos, and Spark & Hustle which guides people in beginning and expanding small businesses. Here are her responses. [This feature is now closed for comment.]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/booming/13expert/13expert-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/booming/13expert/13expert-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 581, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/booming/13expert/13expert-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/13/booming/13expert/13expert-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/13/booming/13expert/13expert-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Job Opportunities in Middle Age', 'kicker': 'Ask the Expert', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T16:54:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c900818-682c-5c0f-adfd-7bb5109a0936', 'word_count': 2239, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c900818-682c-5c0f-adfd-7bb5109a0936'}, {'abstract': 'The development of local arms-producing industries in Syria provides a good indicator of the stubborn determination and evolving organization of the fighting groups trying to unseat President Bashar al-Assad.', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/syrias-dark-horses-with-lathes-makeshift-arms-production-in-aleppo-governorate-part-i/', 'snippet': 'The development of local arms-producing industries in Syria provides a good indicator of the stubborn determination and evolving organization of the fighting groups trying to unseat President Bashar al-Assad.', 'lead_paragraph': 'There are many indicators of the stubborn determination and evolving organization of the fighting groups in Syria seeking to unseat President Bashar al-Assad. One of them is this: the development of local arms-producing industries in Syrian cities and the countryside. In a war in which the antigovernment fighters have sought outside support and been disappointed by what has been offered, especially from the West, the society that produced the fighters has also undertaken the production of matériel for guerrilla war, a social project of such a scale that it has contributed to the armed opposition’s momentum and confidence this year. The progression is both intriguing and relevant to those seeking to understand the Free Syrian Army, as the loosely organized antigovernment fighters are called. This is not just for the view that local production provides of latter-day warfare with do-it-yourself arms. It offers insights into something else — the social forces within an uprising that is reordering the Middle East.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syria’s Dark Horses, With Lathes: Makeshift Arms Production in Aleppo Governorate, Part I', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bombs and Explosives', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Firearms', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Improvised Explosive Devices', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Free Syrian Army', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'ALEPPO (SYRIA)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T16:58:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By C. J. Chivers', 'person': [{'firstname': 'C.', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Chivers', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de8f1042-96c5-507d-bb0e-892f338f6c1a', 'word_count': 2581, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de8f1042-96c5-507d-bb0e-892f338f6c1a'}, {'abstract': 'It’s misleading to suggest that the country splits evenly into income-based voting blocs.', 'web_url': 'https://takingnote.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/class-politics-and-the-47-percent/', 'snippet': 'It’s misleading to suggest that the country splits evenly into income-based voting blocs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Among the many fallacies in Mitt Romney’s now-infamous “47 percent” speech is that “people who pay no income tax” will “vote for the president no matter what.” More than one-fifth of non-filers are elderly, a constituency likely to favor Mr. Romney.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Class, Politics, and the 47 Percent', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Is That Class Divide?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Tax', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T16:59:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Juliet Lapidos', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Juliet', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lapidos', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6607a99f-8386-57bd-a379-822580f62d82', 'word_count': 230, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6607a99f-8386-57bd-a379-822580f62d82'}, {'abstract': 'The new all-organic, prix fixe menu was designed by the celebrated chef Alice Waters.', 'web_url': 'https://intransit.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/new-hyatt-organic-menu-aimed-at-smallest-guests/', 'snippet': 'The new all-organic, prix fixe menu was designed by the celebrated chef Alice Waters.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Children’s menus have come a long way since the Happy Meal. The latest in refined dining for little ones: a new all-organic, prix fixe menu designed by Alice Waters and prepared at Hyatt Hotels across North America.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New Hyatt Organic Menu Aimed at Smallest Guests', 'kicker': 'In Transit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'HOTELS AND MOTELS', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waters, Alice', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Hyatt Hotels Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T17:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byline': {'original': '