e home, the Danish company Georg Jensen took a different approach to jewelry making with its latest offering. The Magic showpiece earrings shown here have a familiar baroque look, and make beautiful use of yellow gold, white pearls and diamonds, but it’s their ability to break down into a daintier drop size that really amazes. Abracadabra, indeed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Get | Georg Jensen’s Magic Showpiece Earrings', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Georg Jensen A/S', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T17:33:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4f344fbb-8358-5697-b0b2-1df55f26155d', 'word_count': 71, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4f344fbb-8358-5697-b0b2-1df55f26155d'}, {'abstract': 'Kansas has been a reliably conservative state for years, but until recently, Kansans had a strong tradition of centrist Republicanism.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/19/the-end-of-a-kansas-tradition-moderation/', 'snippet': 'Kansas has been a reliably conservative state for years, but until recently, Kansans had a strong tradition of centrist Republicanism.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We continue our Presidential Geography series, a one-by-one examination of the peculiarities that drive the politics in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Here is a look at Kansas, the Sunflower State. FiveThirtyEight spoke with Joseph A. Aistrup, a professor of political science at Kansas State University; Burdett A. Loomis, a professor of political science at Kansas University; H. Edward Flentje, a professor in Wichita State University’s Hugo Wall School of Urban and Public Affairs; and David Kensinger, president of Roadmap Solutions, Gov. Sam Brownback’s policy organization.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The End of a Kansas Tradition: Moderation', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T17:33:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2e90b5e2-1080-55a0-b180-703ee34cd685', 'word_count': 953, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2e90b5e2-1080-55a0-b180-703ee34cd685'}, {'abstract': 'From art to crabs, dive bars to “The Wire,” Natty Boh to the home of Poe, this is a textured town with multiple identities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/travel/36-hours-in-baltimore.html', 'snippet': 'From art to crabs, dive bars to “The Wire,” Natty Boh to the home of Poe, this is a textured town with multiple identities.', 'lead_paragraph': '“YOU can look far and wide, but you’ll never discover a stranger city with such extreme style,” John Waters once noted about his hometown. Maybe that’s why Baltimore’s trumpeted glass-and-steel Inner Harbor development, with its chain restaurants, neon-loud amusements and brand-name shopping, feels so counterintuitive as a symbol for the city. But walk in any direction and the city’s charm reasserts itself. Indeed, Baltimore’s best draws tend to be left-of-center: offbeat theater, grandly decrepit neighborhoods on the cusp of gentrification, a world-class museum devoted to outsider art and a dive-bar culture that must be one of the nation’s finest.', 'print_section': 'TR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 456, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 456}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 778, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1139, 'width': 1500, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/travel/23HOURS1_SPAN/23HOURS1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': '36 Hours in Baltimore', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '36 Hours: Baltimore', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Baltimore (Md)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Poe, Edgar Allan, House and Museum', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Maryland', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-19T17:34:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Travel', 'section_name': 'Travel', 'byl