ne, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d3ba0d2c-b1d3-53ae-b2de-9703bc111887', 'word_count': 584, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d3ba0d2c-b1d3-53ae-b2de-9703bc111887'}, {'abstract': 'To find the original cuisine of Mexico, head to the heart of the Yucatán Peninsula. Then ask for directions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/the-yucatan-peninsulas-food-of-the-ancients.html', 'snippet': 'To find the original cuisine of Mexico, head to the heart of the Yucatán Peninsula. Then ask for directions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’ve traveled to the Yucatán Peninsula a dozen times looking for “real” Mayan cuisine, driving through the center and up and down both (or is it all three?) coasts, but the food that’s at the soul of the Yucatán is not easily discovered. What you get as an outsider is feast food like cochinita pibil — a sometimes-wonderful chili-marinated pork buried and cooked in an earthen pit — which is not enough to have you rush down there and start eating.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '42', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD/23eat_ss-slide-SIPD-superJumb