of cancer patients said they were in pain or receiving pain medications, and roughly a third felt they needed more painkillers to fully treat their symptoms. Why aren’t doctors helping more?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Recounting her father’s struggle with cancer was difficult for the young woman, even several years after his death. He’d endured first surgery and then chemotherapy and radiation, she told me, and the cancer had gone into remission. He was thrilled, but the aggressive treatment left him with chronic, debilitating pain. Once active, he struggled to get around in his own home.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/health/20Chen/20Chen-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Poor Pain Control for Cancer Patients', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cancer', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Doctors', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pain', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pain-Relieving Drugs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T14:17:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pauline W. Chen, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pauline', 'middlename': 'W.', 'lastname': 'Chen', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/99417be2-e53c-516f-9320-18cf068d08e1', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/99417be2-e53c-516f-9320-18cf068d08e1'}, {'abstract': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches Walt Whitman’s poem “I Hear America Singing” with “Occupy Wall Street: A Frenzy That Fizzled,” a DealBook column that assessed the impact of the Occupy movement one year after it started.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/poetry-pairing-i-hear-america-singing/', 'snippet': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches Walt Whitman’s poem “I Hear America Singing” with “Occupy Wall Street: A Frenzy That Fizzled,” a DealBook column that assessed the impact of the Occupy movement one year after it started.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This week’s Poetry Pairing matches Walt Whitman’s timeless poem “I Hear America Singing” with “Occupy Wall Street: A Frenzy That Fizzled,” a DealBook column that assessed the impact of the Occupy Wall Street movement on its first anniversary.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Poetry Pairing | ‘I Hear America Singing’', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T14:25:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shannon Doyne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shannon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Doyne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fdbfe251-dd21-5507-b537-f0e5133918bc', 'word_count': 616, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fdbfe251-dd21-5507-b537-f0e5133918bc'}, {'abstract': 'In an era of drought, the water-conservation public service announcement is becoming an art form.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/via-you-tube-a-new-conservation-genre/', 'snippet': 'In an era of drought, the water-conservation public service announcement is becoming an art form.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The drought of 2012, which continues to spread westward, is making its mark on the national consciousness in many ways. Rising food prices. Interrupted livelihoods. Fields of stunted, desiccated crops.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Via YouTube, a New Conservation Genre', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Cartoons and Cartoonists', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conservation of Resources', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drought', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Water', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'YouTube.com', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T14:26:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Felicity Barringer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Felicity', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barringer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/efb44c58-d1a9-59e2-82ef-d7641531bb82', 'word_count': 303, 'uri': 'nyt://article/efb44c58-d1a9-59e2-82ef-d7641531bb82'}, {'abstract': 'David Wright is chasing Ed Kranepool for most hits in Mets history, but the team’s 50th anniversary season has become a comic tribute to its inaugural season, in 1962.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/mets-know-how-to-mark-an-anniversary/', 'snippet': 'David Wright is chasing Ed Kranepool for most hits in Mets history, but the team’s 50th anniversary season has become a comic tribute to its inaugural season, in 1962.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ed Kranepool got his 1,418th, and final, hit for the Mets in the last game of the 1979 regular season. The team was terrible, finishing with a 63-99 record. The Mets were six years removed from their last postseason appearance and seven years away from their next. They were, to put it bluntly, in the middle of nowhere, which is where the Mets often reside.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Mets Know How to Mark an Anniversary', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kranepool, Ed', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wright, David', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T14:30:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jay Schreiber', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jay', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schreiber', 'qualifi