e': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Patricia Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Patricia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4948900b-1108-5f51-88b8-0cf51de54dd0', 'word_count': 858, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4948900b-1108-5f51-88b8-0cf51de54dd0'}, {'abstract': 'To port or not to port? Cancer patients often must decide whether to brave constant needle pricks or consent to a surgically implanted port.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/needles-or-port-a-cancer-patient-decides/', 'snippet': 'To port or not to port? Cancer patients often must decide whether to brave constant needle pricks or consent to a surgically implanted port.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first question many cancer patients are asked at a doctor’s office or an infusion center is “Do you have a port?” I shake my head no, but add the words “not yet.” To port or not to port: That is the question facing me when I arrive on the hospital hall dedicated to clinical trials. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/health/06well_cancer/06well_cancer-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 681, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/health/06well_cancer/06well_cancer-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1362, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/health/06well_cancer/06well_cancer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/06/health/06well_cancer/06well_cancer-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/06/health/06well_cancer/06well_cancer-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Needles or Port? A Cancer Patient Decides', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Clinical Trials', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ovarian Cancer', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gubar, Susan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:08:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Susan Gubar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Susan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gubar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8b3e861d-670e-541b-950a-27244bc2c8f1', 'word_count': 927, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8b3e861d-670e-541b-950a-27244bc2c8f1'}, {'abstract': 'Visitors to the 26th edition of the Biennale des Antiquaires in Paris can indulge in object envy amid art and objects from five millenniums. If you can’t afford the art, take home a poster.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/art-at-the-palais-dcor-by-lagerfeld/', 'snippet': 'Visitors to the 26th edition of the Biennale des Antiquaires in Paris can indulge in object envy amid art and objects from five millenniums. If you can’t afford the art, take home a poster.', 'lead_paragraph': 'PARIS — Through Sunday, visitors to the 26th edition of the Biennale des Antiquaires can indulge in object envy at a fair that Souren Melikian of The International Herald Tribune says has “shed its fussy appearance of yore.” The art for sale by the 122 dealers goes as far back as the 3rd millennium B.C. and is as recent as the 21st century — a first for this biennale. And then there is the jewelry. Look out for rose gold, Suzy Menkes writes. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/rdv-biennaleA/rdv-biennaleA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 697, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/rdv-biennaleA/rdv-biennaleA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1394, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art at the Palais, Décor by Lagerfeld', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Conventions, Fairs and Trade Shows', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:17:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e685288-a903-5a9f-b68c-2e39badedb02', 'word_count': 198, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e685288-a903-5a9f-b68c-2e39badedb02'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Philharmonic opened its season with works for piano and orchestra by Gyorgy Kurtag and Beethoven and Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/arts/music/philharmonic-opens-season-at-avery-fisher-hall.html', 'snippet': 'The New York Philharmonic opened its season with works for piano and orchestra by Gyorgy Kurtag and Beethoven and Stravinsky’s “Rite of Spring.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'Typically the New York Philharmonic opens its season with a gala concert that is broadcast in public television’s “Live From Lincoln Center” series. But in a significant departure Alan Gilbert opened his fourth season as the Philharmonic’s music director on Wednesday night at Avery Fisher Hall with a substantial subscription program, offering works for piano and orchestra by Gyorgy Kurtag and Beethoven, and ending with Stravinsky’s formidable “Rite of Spring.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/20/arts/phil2/phil2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Restrained Leap Into the Stravinsky-thon', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Restrained Leap Into the Stravinsky-thon', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Philharmonic', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gilbert, Alan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Classical Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:18:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anthony Tommasini', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anthony', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tommasini', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ec922916-e477-52e0-9496-c54b812fbd7b', 'word_count': 895, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ec922916-e477-52e0-9496-c54b812fbd7b'}, {'abstract': 'Every so often – and more frequently on stage than in film – the interiority of artistic creation is translated into a visible, physical language that raises goose bumps.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/theater-talkback-lives-of-the-artists/', 'snippet': 'Every so often – and more frequently on stage than in film – the interiority of artistic creation is translated into a visible, physical language that raises goose bumps.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most gratifying scene in the generally less-than-gratifying “Chaplin: The Musical,” which opened last week at the Ethel Barrymore Theater, comes when the show’s title character turns into Charlie Chaplin. Of course this young man, portrayed by the winsome Rob McClure, had always been someone who bore that name (or that of the less familiar-sounding Charles Chaplin).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Theater Talkback: Lives of the Artists', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, John', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lapine, James', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Logan, John', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McClure, Rob', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rothko, Mark', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Schiele, Egon', 'rank': 10, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sondheim, Stephen', 'rank': 11, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:23:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3d9aad0c-a48a-5974-838c-f0a0a955677d', 'word_count': 857, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3d9aad0c-a48a-5974-838c-f0a0a955677d'}, {'abstract': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former Republican governor of Minnesota, was named chief executive of the Financial Services Roundtable on Thursday, replacing Steve Bartlett.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/pawlenty-to-become-wall-street-lobbyist/', 'snippet': 'Tim Pawlenty, the former Republican governor of Minnesota, was named chief executive of the Financial Services Roundtable on Thursday, replacing Steve Bartlett.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Tim Pawlenty was hoping to spend plenty of time in the White House next year. Now, rather than living there, the onetime presidential contender will visit regularly as a top Wall Street lobbyist. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Pawlenty to Become Wall Street Lobbyist', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Appointments and Executive Changes', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pawlenty, Tim', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Financial Services Roundtable', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:25:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Protess', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Protess', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/378ebf09-2640-5750-bfd5-72ec94d4169a', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/378ebf09-2640-5750-bfd5-72ec94d4169a'}, {'abstract': 'The iPhone is capable of making remarkably good recordings, but its built-in microphone is not. One solution is the Mikey Digital, from Blue Microphones.', 'web_url': 'https://gadgetwise.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/a-recording-studio-that-fits-in-a-pocket/', 'snippet': 'The iPhone is capable of making remarkably good recordings, but its built-in microphone is not. One solution is the Mikey Digital, from Blue Microphones.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The iPhone is capable of making remarkably good recordings, but its built-in microphone is not. One solution is the Mikey Digital, from Blue Microphones.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A  Recording Studio That Fits in a Pocket', 'kicker': 'Gadgetwise', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:26:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Roy Furchgott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Roy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Furchgott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/de7eb032-a578-552e-a59f-b3868199c41e', 'word_count': 277, 'uri': 'nyt://article/de7eb032-a578-552e-a59f-b3868199c41e'}, {'abstract': 'I had bangs and a publishing job. And I had been caught red-handed doing something ironic with a cupcake.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/jersey-city-a-love-story/', 'snippet': 'I had bangs and a publishing job. And I had been caught red-handed doing something ironic with a cupcake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2005, a new bar opened around the corner from my apartment, near Graham Avenue and Metropolitan in Brooklyn. I was there for a birthday party when a photographer snapped a photo of a friend faux-feeding me a cupcake. A few days later, the picture appeared alongside a review of the bar in The Village Voice. The headline called it “Aging Hipster Heaven.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Jersey City, a Love Story', 'kicker': 'Townies', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New Jersey', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T16:31:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessanne Collins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessanne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Collins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3ec5b8c5-5c49-5c7a-a342-28f7b659dff6', 'word_count': 1233, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3ec5b8c5-5c49-5c7a-a342-28f7b659dff6'},