ng Post-Law School Prospects', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law Schools', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wages and Salaries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'NerdWallet LLC', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T18:21:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ann Carrns', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ann', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Carrns', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a7d3a93-57b6-5d0e-9c11-9aa6ef69245e', 'word_count': 304, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a7d3a93-57b6-5d0e-9c11-9aa6ef69245e'}, {'abstract': 'Recent Times features, including a new series on the art of writing, inspire us to create this list of 10 “rules” for writing well. In it you’ll find links to advice on everything from using punctuation to reading the way writers do.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/writing-rules-advice-from-the-new-york-times-on-writing-well/', 'snippet': 'Recent Times features, including a new series on the art of writing, inspire us to create this list of 10 “rules” for writing well. In it you’ll find links to advice on everything from using punctuation to reading the way writers do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Times has recently published a few features that we consider gifts to English teachers everywhere, including a summer “How To” section of the Sunday Book Review, and a new series, called “Draft,” on the art of writing, which features essays by grammarians, historians, linguists, journalists, novelists and others. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Writing Rules! Advice From The Times on Writing Well', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T18:22:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Amanda Christy Brown and Katherine Schulten', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Amanda', 'middlename': 'Christy', 'lastname': 'Brown', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Katherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schulten', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a4db9f64-2139-5100-a036-9a8e722c258a', 'word_count': 2128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a4db9f64-2139-5100-a036-9a8e722c258a'}, {'abstract': 'It’s been 80 years since someone took that iconic photo of men having a vertiginous lunch high above Manhattan, and it’s still not certain who that someone was.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/revisiting-a-lunch-at-perilous-heights/', 'snippet': 'It’s been 80 years since someone took that iconic photo of men having a vertiginous lunch high above Manhattan, and it’s still not certain who that someone was.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is a defining image of New York City’s history, one that captured the essence of an era, even if it was, in part, a staged scene. On a warm autumn day in 1932, 11 workmen sat perched on a steel girder at Rockefeller Center, smoking, eating sandwiches and reading the newspaper some 800 feet up in the air. Nearby, a camera’s shutter clicked: think men in overalls and flat caps, and boots dangling above the rooftops of Midtown Manhattan. The moment captured in black and white was both heroic and mundane. It was a triumph for the city in the throes of economic crisis and has become one of the most famous images in the world.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Revisiting a Lunch at Perilous Heights', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Archives and Records', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ebbets, Charles C', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Corbis Corp', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Herald Tribune', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Manhattan (NYC)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Rockefeller Center (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T18:30:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jesse Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jesse', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3e414b7-30d5-540d-9e9b-96c8b6c72c5e', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3e414b7-30d5-540d-9e9b-96c8b6c72c5e'}, {'abstract': 'One of the best things about the festival is that while you see new things by established names, you also see work — much of it interesting, and some of it excellent — by young designers.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/london-design-festival-part-ii/', 'snippet': 'One of the best things about the festival is that while you see new things by established names, you also see work — much of it interesting, and some of it excellent — by young designers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog