ajor': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:16:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/516d6dc0-0898-583d-bf98-9033b43e66f9', 'word_count': 380, 'uri': 'nyt://article/516d6dc0-0898-583d-bf98-9033b43e66f9'}, {'abstract': 'There’s a reason a school gives medals to kids who do well on standardized tests, and it probably isn’t to recognize academic achievement.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/rewarding-success-on-standardized-tests/', 'snippet': 'There’s a reason a school gives medals to kids who do well on standardized tests, and it probably isn’t to recognize academic achievement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the young son of a parent in California came home with a medal for his success on last year’s standardized tests, she wrote asking for advice on how to respond — not to his test-taking ability, but to the celebration of that ability in front of his peers. The school awarded “gold” medals for advanced achievement in language arts and/or math, and “silver” to children whose score was “proficient.” (Yes, a medal for “proficiency.”) Children who weren’t given a medal were given a certificate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rewarding Success on Standardized Tests', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Tests and Examinations', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:19:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f426ded0-9154-59d0-a734-0d498bd3891e', 'word_count': 839, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f426ded0-9154-59d0-a734-0d498bd3891e'}, {'abstract': 'The graying mustache will be shaved by a barber in the plaza outside the Jackie Robinson Rotunda at Citi Field on Sept. 27.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/keith-hernandez-to-shave-mustache-at-mets-final-home-game/', 'snippet': 'The graying mustache will be shaved by a barber in the plaza outside the Jackie Robinson Rotunda at Citi Field on Sept. 27.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The removal of Keith Hernandez’s mustache is becoming an event, befitting the renowned facial hair that in 2007 was voted the best ever in sports by the American Mustache Institute. The graying mustache will be shaved by a barber in the plaza outside the Jackie Robinson Rotunda at Citi Field on Sept. 27 before the Mets’ final home game of the season. With the Mets 4-23 at home since the All-Star break, the shave could be more exciting than the afternoon game against the Pittsburgh Pirates.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Hernandez to Shave Mustache at Mets’ Home Finale', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hernandez, Keith', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:23:11+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'n