eek at the age of 92. He served Richard Nixon as the first chairman of the White House Council on Environmental Quality, and later as administrator of the fledgling Environmental Protection Agency – helping shape landmark statutes like the Endangered Species Act, the Clean Air Act and the Clean Water Act. His death serves as a reminder of the G.O.P.’s historic tradition of environmental stewardship, a tradition stretching as far back as Teddy Roosevelt, which the party has now repudiated.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Stop the War on Coal’ Act', 'kicker': 'Taking Note', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Calling Teddy Roosevelts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Environment', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Lisa P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Waxman, Henry A', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Environmental Protection Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:52:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Robert B. Semple Jr', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Robert', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/414fc600-f5a8-561e-8c0d-1102024da7cd', 'word_count': 417, 'uri': 'nyt://article/414fc600-f5a8-561e-8c0d-1102024da7cd'}, {'abstract': 'In “Electoral Dysfunction” the comic Mo Rocca tries to explain how voting works (or doesn’t) in the United States’ presidential elections.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/movies/electoral-dysfunction-with-mo-rocca.html', 'snippet': 'In “Electoral Dysfunction” the comic Mo Rocca tries to explain how voting works (or doesn’t) in the United States’ presidential elections.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a film that takes on a serious concern of United States democracy — voting and the effective, if not always overt, encouragement and discouragement of that act as practiced across the nation — “Electoral Dysfunction” pulls off an admirable trick: It’s pleasant. It treats Democrats and Republicans respectfully, and its humor, with the comic Mo Rocca as guide, is closer to Garrison Keillor than to Michael Moore.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/movies/electoral2-span/electoral2-span-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/movies/electoral2-span/electoral2-span-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 480, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/movies/electoral2-span/electoral2-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/21/movies/electoral2-span/electoral2-span-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/movies/electoral2-span/electoral2-span-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Goofy Guide to the Strange Democratic Process', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Electoral Dysfunction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:56:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By David DeWitt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'DeWitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3dab6d66-baf9-58d5-9be7-6f814051afe7', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3dab6d66-baf9-58d5-9be7-6f814051afe7'}, {'abstract': 'Wyoming has the most evenly distributed income, according to a new report from the Census Bureau.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/20/new-york-state-leads-in-income-inequality/', 'snippet': 'Wyoming has the most evenly distributed income, according to a new report from the Census Bureau.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Of all American states, New York again has the most unequal income distribution, according to a new report from the Census Bureau. Wyoming has the most equitably distributed income.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York State Leads in Income Inequality', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Income Inequality', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'States (US)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Census Bureau', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-20T20:59:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None,