 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fad20aef-b0ca-5138-9dde-c956c2725dee', 'word_count': 192, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fad20aef-b0ca-5138-9dde-c956c2725dee'}, {'abstract': 'In the presidential campaign, China has become a focal point encompassing both security and economic concerns and highlighting the nations’ complex, tense relationship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/us/politics/obamas-evolution-to-a-tougher-line-on-china.html', 'snippet': 'In the presidential campaign, China has become a focal point encompassing both security and economic concerns and highlighting the nations’ complex, tense relationship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — President Obama’s patience with China had been fraying for months, and by November 2010 he was fed up. Meeting with President Hu Jintao in Seoul, South Korea, Mr. Obama warned that if China did not do more to curb North Korea’s bellicose behavior, he would have to take steps to shield the United States from the threat of a nuclear missile attack from the North. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/world/sub-jp21policy1/sub-jp21policy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/world/sub-jp21policy1/sub-jp21policy1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/world/sub-jp21policy1/sub-jp21policy1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/world/sub-jp21policy1/sub-jp21policy1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType':