fier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ba71d4b6-9fa2-558b-9da6-b65c5d76573d', 'word_count': 2282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ba71d4b6-9fa2-558b-9da6-b65c5d76573d'}, {'abstract': 'A touching exhibition at the Neue Galerie examines the optimism of the last decades of Ferdinand Hodler, the Swiss Symbolist.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/arts/design/ferdinand-hodler-view-to-infinity-at-neue-galerie.html', 'snippet': 'A touching exhibition at the Neue Galerie examines the optimism of the last decades of Ferdinand Hodler, the Swiss Symbolist.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Near the end of the Neue Galerie’s exceptionally touching exhibition of late works by the Swiss Symbolist painter Ferdinand Hodler (1853-1918), you come to a small, chapel-like room with walls draped in purple velvet and ecclesiastical music playing quietly in the background. Facing you on entering is a long, horizontal painting from 1915 of an emaciated woman on her deathbed, a composition inspired by Hans Holbein the Younger’s “Body of the Dead Christ in the Tomb” (1521). To the right hang three small landscapes, all views of Lake Geneva, distant mountains and skies painted in vivid, Impressionistic colors. To the left is a self-portrait from 15 years earlier, the image of a robust, genial man with a direct, wide-eyed gaze and a generous beard. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/arts/21HODLER1_SPAN/21HODLER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/arts/21HODLER1_SPAN/21HODLER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image'