: {'original': 'By Martin Fackler and Ian Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Martin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fackler', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d68b2eb0-1480-5518-bad1-42ef1cdf1eca', 'word_count': 1351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d68b2eb0-1480-5518-bad1-42ef1cdf1eca'}, {'abstract': 'With products like frozen, sunny-side-up eggs made of soy and whey protein, May Wah Vegetarian Market in Chinatown is a beacon for vegans, vegetarians and, yes, those who eat real meat.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/nyregion/may-wah-vegetarian-market-a-beacon-for-lovers-of-imitation-meat.html', 'snippet': 'With products like frozen, sunny-side-up eggs made of soy and whey protein, May Wah Vegetarian Market in Chinatown is a beacon for vegans, vegetarians and, yes, those who eat real meat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'IT was the vegan “beef chunks” that lured Chana Widawski to May Wah Vegetarian Market in Chinatown on a recent evening. “I’m an outdoors person and a big camper,” she explained. The chewy dried brown nuggets had been a hit with friends on a recent canoe trip — “Everyone loved them,” she said — and she was back for more.', 'print_section': 'MB', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/nyregion/23JOINT2/23JOINT2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 931, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/nyregion/23JOINT2/23JOINT2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1861, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'leg