as been meeting together since 2005?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Military’s Repeal of ‘Don’t Ask, Don’t Tell’ Seems to Be a Success', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T08:23:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/10421934-0da0-59e5-99f8-35b700af0281', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/10421934-0da0-59e5-99f8-35b700af0281'}, {'abstract': 'The author Tom Wolfe eviscerated baby boomers but also took the 19th century utopians to task for failing to anticipate Levittown.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/booming/21excerpt-booming.html', 'snippet': 'The author Tom Wolfe eviscerated baby boomers but also took the 19th century utopians to task for failing to anticipate Levittown.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Aug. 23, 1976, New York Magazine published “The Me Decade,” a cover story by Tom Wolfe that eviscerated baby boomers as the most ludicrous, self-absorbed and spoiled generation in the history of mankind. (The article begins with a 1,500-word description of an unnamed boomer at a spiritual gathering at the Ambassador Hotel in Los Angeles trying to will away her hemorrhoids). As I’ve written elsewhere, this wasn’t entirely fair. At the time, there were 29 million boomers averaging about 20 years old — awfully young to be judged so harshly. Mr. Wolfe, on the other hand, was 45 and at the top of his game. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/boomi