 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winerip', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e500ff53-f5f9-5845-8d32-dfc54078174b', 'word_count': 964, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e500ff53-f5f9-5845-8d32-dfc54078174b'}, {'abstract': '“To Kill a Mockingbird” by Harper Lee is about race, and the story of this country cannot be told without race as a centerpiece.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/booming/21friday-booming.html', 'snippet': '“To Kill a Mockingbird” by Harper Lee is about race, and the story of this country cannot be told without race as a centerpiece.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Last week, for the Friday Question, we asked you to name your favorite song of the 1960s and ‘70s, and did you ever. We received more than 500 responses — thoughtful, passionate and surprisingly varied. More on that in just a minute. But first, this week’s question: What do you think is the greatest American novel since World War II?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What’s the Greatest American Novel Since World War II?', 'kicker': 'The Friday Question', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lee, Harper', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T09:05:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Winerip', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Winerip', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f9c30886-cf7c-5a87-826c-d7fe218e0787', 'word_count': 355, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f9c30886-cf7c-5a87-826c-d7fe218e0787'}, {'abstract': 'Tell us what Times articles, Opinion pieces, videos, graphics, slide shows, blogs or podcasts got your attention this week and why.', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/what-interested-you-most-in-the-times-this-week-17/', 'snippet': 'Tell us what Times articles, Opinion pieces, videos, graphics, slide shows, blogs or podcasts got your attention this week and why.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Mondays through Thursdays we ask you to read a Times article that we’ve chosen and answer our questions. On Fridays, we ask you to make the pick, and tell us why you chose it.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Interested You Most in The Times This Week?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T09:09:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a9cc0ca5-d56c-5f64-a599-3e1872f95b1b', 'word_count': 167, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a9cc0ca5-d56c-5f64-a599-3e1872f95b1b'}, {'abstract': 'News, fashion, arts and sports coverage in today’s International Herald Tribune.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/iht-quick-read-friday-sept-21/', 'snippet': 'News, fashion, arts and sports coverage in today’s International Herald Tribune.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEWS The Conservative prime minister of Britain, David Cameron, has hitched his political fortunes to the coalition government’s ability to cut the national budget. Despite his efforts to curb public outlays and reduce one of Europe’s biggest budget deficits, government spending is higher than when he took office two years ago. And the cuts — a public sector pay freeze, a reduction in outlays for local governments and staff cuts at government ministries, to name a few — have drawn howls of protest. Landon Thomas Jr. reports from London. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'IHT Quick Read: Friday, Sept. 21', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T09:39:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f7e718f8-9610-5984-abd5-abb0ad45538b', 'word_count': 405, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f7e718f8-9610-5984-abd5-abb0ad45538b'}, {'abstract': 'Amy Hutton, the director of admissions for the department of music at Virginia Commonwealth University, offers tips to help students choose a performing arts school and prepare for auditions.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/tip-sheet-how-to-apply-to-a-performing-arts-school/', 'snippet': 'Amy Hutton, the director of admissions for the department of music at Virginia Commonwealth University, offers tips to help students choose a performing arts school and prepare for auditions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Amy Hutton is the director of admissions for the department of music at Virginia Commonwealth University, as well as a former professor in its theater department.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/17/education/choice-tipsheet-75/choice-tipsheet-75-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtyp