': 'Milan', 'lead_paragraph': 'Milan', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/fashion/runway-womens/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/fashion/runway-womens/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/fashion/runway-womens/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/fashion/runway-womens/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9/etro_spring13app-slide-GGZ9-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Etro: Spring 2013 RTW', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T13:18:49+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Fashion & Style', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'subsection_name': "Women's Runway", 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/dab311e6-b842-5933-bd84-51cbcbd7ea8d', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/dab311e6-b842-5933-bd84-51cbcbd7ea8d'}, {'abstract': 'Universal Music Group won Europe’s approval for its $1.9 billion acquisition of EMI Music, Wal-Mart said it would stop carrying Amazon’s Kindle, and PBS has a chance to win an Emmy for best drama.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/the-breakfast-meeting-universal-emi-deal-passes-in-europe-and-relief-at-news-corp/', 'snippet': 'Universal Music Group won Europe’s approval for its $1.9 billion acquisition of EMI Music, Wal-Mart said it would stop carrying Amazon’s Kindle, and PBS has a chance to win an Emmy for best drama.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Universal Music Group won Europe’s approval on Friday for its $1.9 billion acquisition of the British-based EMI Music, Ben Sisario and James Kanter report. The approval came after Universal agreed to a series of concessions meant to foster competition even as the sale itself will reduce the number of major music labels to three from four. The concessions include the selling off about a third of EMI’s assets including most of Parlophone, its flagship label in Europe, with acts like Coldplay, Pink Floyd and Kylie Minogue. A consolation for the newly enlarged Universal is that it will keep the Beatles.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: Universal-EMI Deal Passes in Europe, and Relief at News Corp.', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T13:35:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/81261d8b-9cce-594d-9cd4-e96b8b3c8de6', 'word_count': 514, 'uri': 'nyt://article/81261d8b-9cce-594d-9cd4-e96b8b3c8de6'}, {'abstract': 'Anti-bullfighting campaigners suffered their second setback in a month when French judges ruled today that the activity did not contravene the Constitution. As aficionados celebrate, what’s the next step for the animal rights lobby?', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/bullfighting-hallowed-tradition-or-animal-torture/', 'snippet': 'Anti-bullfighting campaigners suffered their second setback in a month when French judges ruled today that the activity did not contravene the Constitution. As aficionados celebrate, what’s the next step for the animal rights lobby?', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — In a setback for animal rights campaigners, France’s top judges ruled on Friday that bullfighting does not contravene the Constitution.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/blogs/rdv-bullfight/rdv-bullfight-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 718, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/blogs/rdv-bullfight/rdv-bullfight-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1436, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Bullfighting: Hallowed Tradition or Animal Torture? France Rules', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bullfighting', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Constitutional Council (France)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T13:40:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a6a02ff-6288-56a5-b30e-3ec29ff4749d', 'word_count': 666, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a6a02ff-6288-56a5-b30e-3ec29ff4749d'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/what-were-reading-527/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Gothamist: The $1 pizza slice has become the gold standard for bargain eating in New York. But here’s another contender: the $1 oyster. — Patrick Farrell', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T13:41:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/448fd44a-67d0-53b1-a3bd-eb37bd83f03b', 'word_count': 482, 'uri': 'nyt://article/448fd44a-67d0-53b1-a3bd-eb37bd83f03b'}, {'abstract': 'This weekend: a three-day, word-of-mouth event in upstate New York promises to let attendees channel their inner Peter Pan.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/the-escape-artists/', 'snippet': 'This weekend: a three-day, word-of-mouth event in upstate New York promises to let attendees channel their inner Peter Pan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Escape Artists', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Coffin, Peter', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Monica Khemsurov', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Monica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khemsurov', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/258c0563-443b-5c1e-b44e-598703ed12b1', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/258c0563-443b-5c1e-b44e-598703ed12b1'}, {'abstract': 'Research in Motion said it had restored BlackBerry e-mail and other services in Europe, the Middle East and Africa after a technical problem.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/technology/outage-affects-european-rim-customers.html', 'snippet': 'Research in Motion said it had restored BlackBerry e-mail and other services in Europe, the Middle East and Africa after a technical problem.', 'lead_paragraph': 'OTTAWA — Research in Motion said that an unidentified technical problem cut off BlackBerry e-mail and other services to some of its customers in Europe, the Middle East and Africa for several hours on Friday.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'BlackBerrys Fail Overseas', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'BlackBerrys Fail Overseas', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'BlackBerry (Handheld Device)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Research In Motion Ltd', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Wireless Communications', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Power Outages and Blackouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:05:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ian Austen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ian', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Austen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7c0a6b6f-5eb4-5f88-b92a-6b76372042f9', 'word_count': 322, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7c0a6b6f-5eb4-5f88-b92a-6b76372042f9'}, {'abstract': 'The median is the message.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/notes-on-the-political-economy-of-redistribution/', 'snippet': 'The median is the message.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney is getting beaten up, and rightly so, for claiming that redistribution is un-American. Of course we redistribute, and we’ve been doing it on a substantial scale for generations. Medicare, for example, is in effect a strongly redistributive program: it’s supported by a payroll tax (and other revenue) in which the amount you pay in depends on your income, but it supplies a benefit that depends only on your medical costs. From each according to his ability, to each according to his needs!', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes on the Political Economy of Redistribution', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:09:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/999d6fde-7291-5740-8bd6-d0a87ff02a0c', 'word_count': 679, 'uri': 'nyt://article/999d6fde-7291-5740-8bd6-d0a87ff02a0c'}, {'abstract': 'The New York Times critics on “Trouble With the Curve,” “End of Watch” and “How to Survive a Plague.”', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/this-weeks-movies-sept-21/', 'snippet': 'The New York Times critics on “Trouble With the Curve,” “End of Watch” and “How to Survive a Plague.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'In This Week’s Movies, the New York Times critics offer their thoughts on “Trouble With the Curve,” “End of Watch” and “How to Survive a Plague.” View all of this week’s reviews here.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'This Week’s Movies: Sept. 21', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:13:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b5f4a02b-bd0b-58ae-b364-cec88d6f173a', 'word_count': 33, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b5f4a02b-bd0b-58ae-b364-cec88d6f173a'}, {'abstract': 'This is not going as planned.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/polls-and-money/', 'snippet': 'This is not going as planned.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A couple of weeks ago I speculated that Obama’s strengthened position after the conventions could turn into a self-fulfilling prophecy via the money channel, as donors scramble to realign themselves with the probable winner. Sure enough, it seems to be happening:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Polls And Money', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4ae5f6ba-e5d6-5a0a-9fda-b575eca5f93b', 'word_count': 107, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4ae5f6ba-e5d6-5a0a-9fda-b575eca5f93b'}, {'abstract': 'Plus: The danger of a political debate at 60 m.p.h.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/who-made-that-bumper-sticker.html', 'snippet': 'Plus: The danger of a political debate at 60 m.p.h.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One day in the mid-1940s, Forest Gill, the owner of a print shop, knelt down in a Kansas City parking lot and measured a car’s bumper. Ever since the automobile age began, drivers had advertised their opinions with handmade placards tied to their cars with bits of twine. Gill realized that he could make “bumper signs” forever obsolete.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 393, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 393}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 699, 'width': 1053, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23wmt/23wmt-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Who Made That Bumper Sticker?', 'kicker': 'Design', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Who Made That? (Bumpersticker)', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bumper Stickers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Pagan Kennedy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Pagan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kennedy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/16c922dc-f2e6-5696-a042-55fe73af64c7', 'word_count': 442, 'uri': 'nyt://article/16c922dc-f2e6-5696-a042-55fe73af64c7'}, {'abstract': 'Big Government and Big Capital, meet Big Peer Network.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/the-internet-we-built-that.html', 'snippet': 'Big Government and Big Capital, meet Big Peer Network.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Who created the Internet and why should we care? These questions, so often raised during the Bush-Gore election in 2000, have found their way back into the political debate this season — starting with one of the most cited texts of the preconvention campaign, Obama’s so-called “you didn’t build that” speech. “The Internet didn’t get invented on its own,” Obama argued, in the lines that followed his supposed gaffe. “Government research created the Internet so that all the companies could make money off the Internet.” In other words: business uses the Internet, but government made it happen.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '48', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption':