'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Johnson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Johnson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/514f2e4b-8569-511b-b122-2faa51ccfc63', 'word_count': 1489, 'uri': 'nyt://article/514f2e4b-8569-511b-b122-2faa51ccfc63'}, {'abstract': 'Acting dreams dashed by prostate problems? It could be worse.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/my-hollywood-affliction.html', 'snippet': 'Acting dreams dashed by prostate problems? It could be worse.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I came to Los Angeles when I was 25 to be an actor. I thought I was ready for anything: unemployment, rejection, acting classes. I didn’t expect illness. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '54', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23lives/mag-23Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23lives/mag-23Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 450, 'width': 450, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23lives/23lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23lives/23lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23lives/23lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'My Hollywood Affliction', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Hollywood Affliction', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Prostate Gland', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Hollywood (Calif)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Acquired Immune Deficiency Syndrome', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Tobolowsky', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tobolowsky', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5e915d55-d25f-5f8b-9145-647ce7c1c47e', 'word_count': 808, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5e915d55-d25f-5f8b-9145-647ce7c1c47e'}, {'abstract': 'Making hits with Michael and peace with Tupac.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/the-pop-diplomacy-of-quincy-jones.html', 'snippet': 'Making hits with Michael and peace with Tupac.', 'lead_paragraph': 'You once wrote that Michael Jackson stopped working with you because he felt threatened by the credit you were getting for his music. Considering he was never able to repeat the success he had with “Off the Wall,” “Thriller” and “Bad,” how much credit do you deserve?  Well, what do you think?', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23talk/mag-23Talk-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23talk/mag-23Talk-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1000, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23talk/23talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23talk/23talk-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23talk/23talk-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Pop Diplomacy of Quincy Jones', 'kicker': 'Talk', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Statesman of Pop', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Shakur, Tupac', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jackson, Michael', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jones, Quincy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Andrew Goldman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Andrew', 'lastname': 'Goldman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interview', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d5549fe-94c4-56a0-9d63-9fbadb13c440', 'word_count': 761, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d5549fe-94c4-56a0-9d63-9fbadb13c440'}, {'abstract': 'Readers respond.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/the-9-912-issue.html', 'snippet': 'Readers respond.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A Rust-Belt Miracle', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The 9.9.12 Issue', 'kicker': 'Reply All | Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The 9.9.12 Issue', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/825e512d-b304-5426-8aa6-7493bc829a77', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/825e512d-b304-5426-8aa6-7493bc829a77'}, {'abstract': 'Cheap labor; free cake.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/magazine/a-tidy-sum.html', 'snippet': 'Cheap labor; free cake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I’m a math teacher at an international school in Mombasa, Kenya, and a woman who cleans some of the teachers’ apartments offered me her services. Here’s the problem: She is asking for only 300 Kenyan shillings, or about $3.50, for two hours of work. She will be cleaning my bathroom and my kitchen, as well as the floor and my clothing. To me, her work is worth much more than $3.50, and it feels wrong to accept without providing what I believe to be fair payment. If I pay her more, however, she might raise her prices for other customers or pressure my co-workers to pay more than they are willing. What should I do: accept the price she has offered or pay her adequately for her services? NAME WITHHELD, MOMBASA, KENYA', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 402, 'width': 402, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/magazine/23ethicist/23ethicist-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Tidy Sum', 'kicker': 'The Ethicist', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Tidy Sum', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lying', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethics (Personal)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Mombasa (Kenya)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ethicist, The (Times Column)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Service Industries', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chuck Klosterman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chuck', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klosterman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/4a76fa0b-a449-560d-a9e7-f02db8c7ca46', 'word_count': 479, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4a76fa0b-a449-560d-a9e7-f02db8c7ca46'}, {'abstract': 'Slightly below the radar, the Betsy-Tacy children’s book series has captivated generations of fans.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/maud-hart-lovelaces-betsy-tacy-books.html', 'snippet': 'Slightly below the radar, the Betsy-Tacy children’s book series has captivated generations of fans.', 'lead_paragraph': 'At the Betsy-Tacy Convention in Mankato, Minn., in 1997, I disgraced myself at the trivia contest. In front of my daughter, Josephine, then 7 years old, and several hundred similarly dedicated “Betsy-Tacy” fans, I answered the question “What was the decoration on Betsy Ray’s wedding cake?” with the very wrong answer: a bell. The cake was in fact decorated with a dove; the bell, it will be recalled, was hanging decoratively in the living room.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '27', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 385, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 385}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 659, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1318, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-essay/0923-bks-essay-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Growing Up Together', 'kicker': 'Essay', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Growing Up Together', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:19:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Perri Klass, M.D', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Perri', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klass', 'qualifier': 'M.D', 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0a25c2e8-d342-57d5-a7e0-262ff0943a1f', 'word_count': 1287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0a25c2e8-d342-57d5-a7e0-262ff0943a1f'}, {'abstract': 'Claire Vaye Watkins’s stories show a concern for knowing the past and surviving the present.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/battleborn-by-claire-vaye-watkins.html', 'snippet': 'Claire Vaye Watkins’s stories show a concern for knowing the past and surviving the present.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The most notable feature of “Battleborn,” the first story collection by Claire Vaye Watkins, is its physical landscape, especially as it affects the people who stake their claims on its inhospitable terrain. The reader is introduced to Reno’s founders, both the notorious and the anonymous; to figures who are insiders as well as outsiders; to personalities with historical pedigrees and to ones concocted to perfectly fit the crime. The point of view roams, but the Nevada setting provides a hard ground on which the reader counts for stability. Although the individual stories stand alone, together they tell the tale of a place, and of the population that thrives and perishes therein.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-nelson/0923-bks-nelson-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 847, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-nelson/0923-bks-nelson-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1694, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-nelson/0923-bks-nelson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-nelson/0923-bks-nelson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-nelson/0923-bks-nelson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When the Dust Settles', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When the Dust Settles', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watkins, Claire Vaye', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:20:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Antonya Nelson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Antonya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Nelson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/d215ad54-47ae-5d87-b342-3f0f093a3cfe', 'word_count': 917, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d215ad54-47ae-5d87-b342-3f0f093a3cfe'}, {'abstract': 'Zadie Smith’s novel presents characters of varying (and sometimes mixed) races, ethnic groups and classes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/nw-by-zadie-smith.html', 'snippet': 'Zadie Smith’s novel presents characters of varying (and sometimes mixed) races, ethnic groups and classes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Zadie Smith’s latest novel, “NW,” introduces four characters: Leah, Felix, Keisha (renamed Natalie) and Nathan, all of whom grew up in the same impoverished part of northwest London. The postal code for the area is the NW of the title, and Caldwell — the housing project where the characters were raised — is the only fictional place on a very real map. The project consists of five tower blocks, each named for a giant of English philosophy: Smith, Hobbes, Bentham, Locke, Russell. Given the grimness of the locale, the names are only slightly less amusing than the titles of real tower blocks in Kilburn, which are named for Austen, Dickens and Fielding. There are times when the ironies of fiction cannot match those that reality provides.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ENRIGHT/23ENRIGHT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 980, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ENRIGHT/23ENRIGHT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1960, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ENRIGHT/23ENRIGHT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ENRIGHT/23ENRIGHT-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ENRIGHT/23ENRIGHT-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Mind the Gap', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Mind the Gap', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Smith, Zadie', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'NW (Book)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:21:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anne Enright', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anne', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Enright', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/84ad39b2-ba8c-5781-874a-b7e810439e83', 'word_count': 1283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/84ad39b2-ba8c-5781-874a-b7e810439e83'}, {'abstract': 'Pankaj Mishra’s group portrait of the thinkers whose ideas shaped contemporary China, India and the Muslim world.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/from-the-ruins-of-empire-by-pankaj-mishra.html', 'snippet': 'Pankaj Mishra’s group portrait of the thinkers whose ideas shaped contemporary China, India and the Muslim world.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 2011, the Chinese dissident artist Ai Weiwei exhibited 12 bronze animal heads representing the signs of the Chinese zodiac outside the Plaza Hotel in Manhattan. The heads were enlarged replicas of a set designed in the 18th century by two European Jesuits for the emperor Qianlong and displayed in the gardens of the Yuanmingyuan, the emperor’s Old Summer Palace. At the time of the exhibition, Ai had disappeared into detention in China. The political controversy overshadowed the work itself, which posed its most searching questions not to the Chinese government, but to the West.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-kunzru/0923-bks-kunzru-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1022, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-kunzru/0923-bks-kunzru-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2043, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-kunzru/0923-bks-kunzru-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-kunzru/0923-bks-kunzru-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-kunzru/0923-bks-kunzru-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Greater Asia', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Greater Asia', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mishra, Pankaj', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Far East, South and Southeast Asia and Pacific Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:21:16+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hari Kunzru', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hari', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kunzru', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/c9e01acb-87bb-5eaf-930d-6dd234b87174', 'word_count': 1164, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c9e01acb-87bb-5eaf-930d-6dd234b87174'}, {'abstract': 'Alex Witchel describes how her vibrant mother’s personality vanished despite her robust body.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/all-gone-a-memoir-by-alex-witchel.html', 'snippet': 'Alex Witchel describes how her vibrant mother’s personality vanished despite her robust body.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In this haunting, unflinching and at times unexpectedly hilarious memoir, Alex Witchel offers up a fiercely honest account of how her adored mother slowly began “disappearing in plain sight.” “All Gone” is a potluck dish containing a variety of ingredients: childhood stories, complete with a cast of vibrantly portrayed relatives; practical knowledge gleaned from Witchel’s desperate battle against the relentless advance of her parent’s small-stroke-induced dementia; and, yes, real recipes passed down by her mother and grandmother. (I’ve tried a few, and they’re very good. I particularly recommend the chicken with prunes.)', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23SCARF/23SCARF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 723, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23SCARF/23SCARF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1445, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23SCARF/23SCARF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23SCARF/23SCARF-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23SCARF/23SCARF-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Way She Was', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Way She Was', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dementia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witchel, Alex', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:22:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maggie Scarf', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maggie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Scarf', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/7f18bf20-2328-59dd-9a7c-b77123ef0771', 'word_count': 995, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7f18bf20-2328-59dd-9a7c-b77123ef0771'}, {'abstract': 'A “lost” novel by James M. Cain, the author of “The Postman Always Rings Twice” and “Double Indemnity.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/the-cocktail-waitress-by-james-m-cain.html', 'snippet': 'A “lost” novel by James M. Cain, the author of “The Postman Always Rings Twice” and “Double Indemnity.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you strike a match the flame burns blue closest to the source of combustion. For most of his prolific career James M. Cain had the blue flame burning in his explorations of greed, passion and murder. Novels like “Double Indemnity” and “The Postman Always Rings Twice” sizzled with it. And Hollywood always came calling. His work drew the best and brightest talents, and the results were classic. As recently as last year “Mildred Pierce” was produced for the second time in 60-plus years, in an Emmy-winning television run. Cain expertly mined the fine line between desire and lust and the consequences incurred by crossing it. And that skill is timeless, whether on the page or on-screen.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23CONNELLY/23CONNELLY-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 802, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23CONNELLY/23CONNELLY-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1604, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23CONNELLY/23CONNELLY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23CONNELLY/23CONNELLY-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23CONNELLY/23CONNELLY-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Call', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Call', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Cain, James', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:22:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Connelly', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Connelly', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/a2b622e6-7dd4-590b-9108-a66c7c478aa8', 'word_count': 1159, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a2b622e6-7dd4-590b-9108-a66c7c478aa8'}, {'abstract': 'Sweden’s top league, the Elitserien, has been ordered to lift its ban against short-term contracts, clearing the way for locked-out N.H.L. players to play there.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/ruling-opens-door-for-lundqvist-to-join-frolunda/', 'snippet': 'Sweden’s top league, the Elitserien, has been ordered to lift its ban against short-term contracts, clearing the way for locked-out N.H.L. players to play there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Sweden’s top league, the Elitserien, has been ordered by the government to lift its ban against short-term contracts, opening the way for locked-out N.H.L. players to play in the only European league that had blocked their entry.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ruling Opens Door for Lundqvist to Play in Sweden', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Sweden League Ends Ban', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Karlsson, Erik', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kopitar, Anze', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lundqvist, Henrik', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Elitserien (Swedish Elite League)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:23:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f59786dd-c3b6-55d2-a629-dc67d9348dfc', 'word_count': 250, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f59786dd-c3b6-55d2-a629-dc67d9348dfc'}, {'abstract': 'The shower’s handmade panels are held together by a stainless-steel frame that takes less than 30 minutes to put together.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/garden/an-outdoor-shower-that-disassembles-for-winter.html', 'snippet': 'The shower’s handmade panels are held together by a stainless-steel frame that takes less than 30 minutes to put together.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The winters can be harsh where Jim Lobley lives in western Massachusetts, but he still wanted to shower outdoors. “I thought it would be great to have something I could set up wherever, and not have to dig trenches and put up a permanent installation,” said Mr. Lobley, who had his own design-build firm. “And then in the fall, I could just roll up the hoses and bring them inside and wouldn’t have to worry about things freezing.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 399, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 399}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1360, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/garden/21bath/21bath-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'An Outdoor Shower That Only Looks Permanent', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'An Outdoor Shower That Only Looks Permanent', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathing and Showering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:23:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Home', 'section_name': 'Home & Garden', 'byline': {'original': 'By Maria Newman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Maria', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Newman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2bd9f522-73e4-566e-87b8-e905ba60105b', 'word_count': 205, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2bd9f522-73e4-566e-87b8-e905ba60105b'}, {'abstract': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/paperback-row.html', 'snippet': 'Paperback books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE SIBLING EFFECT: What the Bonds Among Brothers and Sisters Reveal About Us,  by Jeffrey Kluger. (Riverhead, $16.) Kluger, a science writer and senior editor at Time magazine, weaves his own experiences of growing up in a contentious, at times dysfunctional family into a fascinating analysis of scientific research on birth order, twins, blended families and parental divorce.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '24', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Paperback Row', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Paperback Row', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:23:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ihsan Taylor', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ihsan', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Taylor', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/6a8cd327-c7f4-52f2-8f80-2e3268cf81c8', 'word_count': 536, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6a8cd327-c7f4-52f2-8f80-2e3268cf81c8'}, {'abstract': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/editors-choice.html', 'snippet': 'Recently reviewed books of particular interest.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE END OF MEN: And the Rise of Women,  by Hanna Rosin. (Riverhead, $27.95.) Women around the world are increasingly dominant in work, education, households, and even love and marriage, Rosin argues in this optimistic narrative.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Editors’ Choice', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Editors’ Choice | Recent Books of Particular Interest', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:23:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'List', '_id': 'nyt://article/69e38ce2-0d13-52dd-a405-37741b54505e', 'word_count': 273, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69e38ce2-0d13-52dd-a405-37741b54505e'}, {'abstract': 'Michael Connelly on the work ethic of writing that he learned as a newspaper reporter.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/up-front.html', 'snippet': 'Michael Connelly on the work ethic of writing that he learned as a newspaper reporter.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the years, Michael Connelly has made hundreds of appearances in the Book Review — most of them, of course, on the best-seller list, where his Harry Bosch mysteries are in regular rotation. (“The Black Box,” his latest, will be published in November.) But this week Connelly gets his first byline in our pages, writing about “The Cocktail Waitress,” a posthumously published novel by James M. Cain. In some ways the review completes a circle for Connelly, who started his career writing for newspapers and who took early inspiration as a novelist from hard-boiled authors like Cain.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-upfront/0923-bks-upfront-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 647, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-upfront/0923-bks-upfront-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1293, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Up Front', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Up Front', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Connelly, Michael', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:24:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b128527c-2779-5a22-94f0-9fb31c3d6b25', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b128527c-2779-5a22-94f0-9fb31c3d6b25'}, {'abstract': 'New books by Guy Gugliotta, Janet Groth, Siri Hustvedt and Bryan Sykes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/freedoms-cap-by-guy-gugliotta-and-more.html', 'snippet': 'New books by Guy Gugliotta, Janet Groth, Siri Hustvedt and Bryan Sykes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FREEDOM’S CAP  The United States Capitol and the Coming of the Civil War.  By Guy Gugliotta.  Hill & Wang, $35. ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-chronicle/0923-bks-chronicle-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-chronicle/0923-bks-chronicle-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1534, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-chronicle/0923-bks-chronicle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-chronicle/0923-bks-chronicle-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/0923-bks-chronicle/0923-bks-chronicle-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Nonfiction Chronicle', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hustvedt, Siri', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Sykes, Bryan', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gugliotta, Guy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Groth, Janet', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:25:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Abigail Meisel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Abigail', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Meisel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/ff03acb0-1b9d-53a6-95c2-98bb23119c20', 'word_count': 1045, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ff03acb0-1b9d-53a6-95c2-98bb23119c20'}, {'abstract': 'Though not strictly a memoir, David Byrne’s book about music explores his own personal and professional experiences.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/david-byrnes-how-music-works.html', 'snippet': 'Though not strictly a memoir, David Byrne’s book about music explores his own personal and professional experiences.', 'lead_paragraph': 'David Byrne is a brilliantly original, eccentric rock star, and he has written a book to match his protean talents. It is not exactly a memoir — “the ‘aging rocker bio’ is a crowded shelf,” as he puts it in his acknowledgments. It is not exactly a series of essays about music, either, though it is some of that. It’s a little bit of both, proudly or unashamedly exposing Byrne’s biases and aimed at a particular audience: his own fans. Since he appeals to people generously diverse in age and interests, however, that’s a pretty big potential readership.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ROCKWELL/23ROCKWELL-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 931, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ROCKWELL/23ROCKWELL-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1862, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ROCKWELL/23ROCKWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ROCKWELL/23ROCKWELL-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23ROCKWELL/23ROCKWELL-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Making Sense', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Making Sense', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Byrne, David', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rock Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Talking Heads', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:25:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By John Rockwell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'John', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rockwell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/45dc5d77-a0a9-53d1-b165-702520c29e88', 'word_count': 1189, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45dc5d77-a0a9-53d1-b165-702520c29e88'}, {'abstract': 'The actor and travel writer Andrew McCarthy takes a deeply personal journey amid some of the world’s most far-flung places.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/books/review/the-longest-way-home-by-andrew-mccarthy.html', 'snippet': 'The actor and travel writer Andrew McCarthy takes a deeply personal journey amid some of the world’s most far-flung places.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Early on in “The Longest Way Home,” Andrew McCarthy recounts a vivid childhood memory of venturing alone into the yard one winter night. Intent on making a snow angel, he instead finds himself on his back mesmerized by the stars. It isn’t their beauty that transports him; it’s the unspeakable vastness of their glorious existence. In this moment, McCarthy is no longer a boy “prone to worry and fret,” but rather a wise young soul who knows to his very bones what it is to be alive, his entire being flooded with the exquisite perception that we are at once profoundly connected to the wide and cluttered world and utterly, inexorably alone in it. If I had to cut out the heart of this book, that’s where I’d put the knife, on that boy in the snow on that dark night long ago. It’s an intimate realization that glimmers with universality and sets us up for the question McCarthy grapples with mightily in the course of this engaging debut: How do we manage to nurture our deepest bonds at the same time that we honor our most necessary solitude? ', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23STRAYED/23STRAYED-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1002, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23STRAYED/23STRAYED-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1954, 'width': 1997, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23STRAYED/23STRAYED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23STRAYED/23STRAYED-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/books/review/23STRAYED/23STRAYED-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where Is He Now?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where Is He Now?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'McCarthy, Andrew', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:25:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cheryl Strayed', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cheryl', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strayed', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbdcb957-b49e-559f-8648-cb3de5fb7082', 'word_count': 1377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbdcb957-b49e-559f-8648-cb3de5fb7082'}, {'abstract': 'Ben Stiller and colleageues Janeane Garafolo, Bob Odenkirk, Judd Apatow and Andy Dick will mark the 20th anniversary of their short-lived Fox sketch series at the Paley Center for Media on Nov. 10.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/ben-stiller-show-reunion-is-set-for-new-york-comedy-festival/', 'snippet': 'Ben Stiller and colleageues Janeane Garafolo, Bob Odenkirk, Judd Apatow and Andy Dick will mark the 20th anniversary of their short-lived Fox sketch series at the Paley Center for Media on Nov. 10.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a different universe television audiences would be preparing for Fox’s 21st season of “The Ben Stiller Show,” the freely associative sketch show that mashed up Woody Allen comedies and monster movies and wondered what structure Bruce Willis would get trapped in for “Die Hard 12,” instead of morosely noting that it was canceled after half a season in 1992 (and an earlier MTV incarnation in 1990). ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Ben Stiller Show’ Reunion Is Set for New York Comedy Festival', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Reunion at Festival For ‘Ben Stiller Show’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Comedy and Humor', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'APATOW, JUDD', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Leary, Denis', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Odenkirk, Bob', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Quinn, Colin', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stiller, Ben', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:26:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2aeba430-7bf7-5a10-9fbe-fe9dbfdcce1a', 'word_count': 263, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2aeba430-7bf7-5a10-9fbe-fe9dbfdcce1a'}, {'abstract': 'Six days into the National Hockey League lockout, the prospects of no season and the loss of money and publicity from the Winter Classic could induce owners to settle for a deal.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/sports/hockey/as-lockout-drags-on-owners-resolve-could-be-tested.html', 'snippet': 'Six days into the National Hockey League lockout, the prospects of no season and the loss of money and publicity from the Winter Classic could induce owners to settle for a deal.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Six days into the N.H.L. lockout, the first week of preseason games has been canceled, some teams have announced temporary layoffs and the league office has reduced its employees’ workweeks and salaries.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/sports/22hockey1/22hockey1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 773, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/sports/22hockey1/22hockey1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1545, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/sports/22hockey1/22hockey1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/22/sports/22hockey1/22hockey1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/sports/22hockey1/22hockey1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As Lockout Drags On, Owners’ Resolve Could Be Tested', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Lockout Continues, and Money Is Left on Table, Owners May Be Tested', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Hockey League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bettman, Gary', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Lockouts', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organized Labor', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fehr, Donald', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:35:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Z. Klein', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': 'Z.', 'lastname': 'Klein', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/661e4534-2589-5834-80e4-de2891c9127b', 'word_count': 1177, 'uri': 'nyt://article/661e4534-2589-5834-80e4-de2891c9127b'}, {'abstract': 'Executives of the British bank agreed last month to pay $340 million to settle claims that it moved hundreds of billions of dollars in tainted money and lied to regulators. Until Friday, though, the final details were not hashed out.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/standard-chartered-set-to-sign-pact-with-new-york-regulator/', 'snippet': 'Executives of the British bank agreed last month to pay $340 million to settle claims that it moved hundreds of billions of dollars in tainted money and lied to regulators. Until Friday, though, the final details were not hashed out.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:51 p.m. | Updated ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Standard Chartered Signs Pact With New York Regulator', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'British Bank Solidifies Deal With State Regulator', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Money Laundering', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Regulation and Deregulation of Industry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lawsky, Benjamin M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Department of Financial Services (NYS)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Justice Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Standard Chartered Bank PLC', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:38:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jessica Silver-Greenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jessica', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Silver-Greenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c18efc9-82d3-587a-8566-509f0b5b84cc', 'word_count': 552, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c18efc9-82d3-587a-8566-509f0b5b84cc'}, {'abstract': 'Rutgers University has rolled out more specialized housing options and other resources for its gay, lesbian, bisexual and transgender students in the wake of Tyler Clementi’s death.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/nyregion/after-clementis-suicide-rutgers-embraces-its-gay-and-transgender-students.html', 'snippet': 'Rutgers University has rolled out more specialized housing options and other resources for its gay, lesbian, bisexual and transgender students in the wake of Tyler Clementi’s death.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW BRUNSWICK, N.J. — It has been two years since Tyler Clementi, a gay freshman at Rutgers University, committed suicide after learning that his roommate had ridiculed his sexuality and invited friends to spy on him and another man through a webcam. That terrible episode brought the school national attention, none of it welcome: previously known as a large and diverse state school, Rutgers became associated with homophobia and cruelty.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '18', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multime