ments of a Beethoven string quartet are the dancers’ bodies in “Danza Permanente,” a piece by D D Dorrvillier that is coming to the Kitchen.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MUSICIANS play, and dancers dance. But the choreographer D D Dorvillier is flipping that notion upside down in a work that transposes a late Beethoven string quartet into a dance for four.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 680, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1359, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN/23DORVILLIER2_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In a Footstep, Making a Cello for Beethoven', 'kicker': 'Dance', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In a Footstep, Making a Cello For Beethoven', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Danspace Project', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorvillier, D D', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Beethoven, Ludwig van', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:49:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts&Leisure', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gia Kourlas', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kourlas', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fd3e78db-6f44-5fcb-9ef8-2778d447ad4a', 'word_count': 1049, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fd3e78db-6f44-5fcb-9ef8-2778d447ad4a'}, {'abstract': 'In an interview, Mr. Almunia, the driving figure in the negotiations in Brussels over the future of EMI, explains why Europe insisted that Universal sell its assets globally, not just in Europe.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/joaqun-almunia-on-europes-tough-terms-for-approving-universal-emi-deal/', 'snippet': 'In an interview, Mr. Almunia, the driving figure in the negotiations in Brussels over the future of EMI, explains why Europe insisted that Universal sell its assets globally, not just in Europe.', 'lead_paragraph': '12:39 p.m. | Updated The most important man in music at the moment is not a hit-spotting executive in New York or London, but, rather, a diminutive Spanish bureaucrat whose decisions about the breakup of EMI will largely determine the shape of the industry for years to come.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedi