6c', 'word_count': 618, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3892502-9030-5177-a73a-7d60b17a986c'}, {'abstract': 'A luxury faucet-maker in Brooklyn finds a steady market in China. | Shrinking life expectancy for the least-educated whites.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/100000001798036/business-day-live-september-21-2012.html', 'snippet': 'A luxury faucet-maker in Brooklyn finds a steady market in China. | Shrinking life expectancy for the least-educated whites.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A luxury faucet-maker in Brooklyn finds a steady market in China. | Shrinking life expectancy for the least-educated whites.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/business/video-bdl-120921/video-bdl-120921-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/business/video-bdl-120921/video-bdl-120921-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/business/video-bdl-120921/video-bdl-120921-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/21/business/video-bdl-120921/video-bdl-120921-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Made in Brooklyn, Exported to China', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T14:59:22+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Mac William Bishop, Vijai Singh and Channon Hodge', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mac', 'middlename': 'William', 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Vijai', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Channon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hodge', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/8550f428-2380-5de3-8112-9a27fe57a44b', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/8550f428-2380-5de3-8112-9a27fe57a44b'}, {'abstract': 'A selection of events.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/arts/sept-23-29.html', 'snippet': 'A selection of events.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WHEN abstraction in art was still new and confounding, sophisticates sometimes referred to music as an illuminating comparison. Harmony, dissonance, rhythm, melody, counterpoint and other musical features were seen to have parallels in seemingly nonrepresentational visual compositions. Musical composers, on the other hand, had a longstanding tradition of evoking mental imagery by sonic means. So it came about in 1928 that VASILY KANDINSKY, the artist often credited for creating the first purely abstract paintings, designed movable sets and changing lighting to accompany a performance of Modest Mussorgsky’s “Pictures at an Exhibition.” Presented in Dessau, Germany, the show was a great success.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 146, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 146}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 250, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 500, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/arts/23GUIDE1_SPAN/23GUIDE1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Sept. 23 — 29', 'kicker': 'The Week Ahead', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T15:03:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/37ca17cd-d53a-5ad7-aaf0-b9012e1fb191', 'word_count': 1354, 'uri': 'nyt://article/37ca17cd-d53a-5ad7-aaf0-b9012e1fb191'}, {'abstract': 'The Louvre shines a light on a vast collection of Islamic art, including scores of important works that have been out of sight for decades.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/art-tells-of-upheavals-as-louvre-opens-a-new-wing/', 'snippet': 'The Louvre shines a light on a vast collection of Islamic art, including scores of important works that have been out of sight for decades.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Paris—This weekend the Louvre is introducing “the most radical architectural intervention since the pyramid in 1989,” Carol Vogel writes. “Designed to house new galleries for Islamic art, it consists of ground- and lower-ground-level interior spaces topped by a golden, undulating roof that seems to float within the neo-Classical Visconti Courtyard in the middle of the Louvre’s south wing, right below the museum’s most popular galleries, where the Mona Lisa and Veronese’s ‘Wedding Feast of Cana’ are hung.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/arts/rdv-louvre21/rdv-louvre21-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/arts/rdv-louvre21/rdv-louvre21-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Art Tells of Upheavals as Louvre Opens a New Wing', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Louvre', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T15:03:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By The International Herald Tribune', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The International Herald Tribune'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/402cb942-6b26-56a9-9909-914bfa18997c', 'word_count': 323, 'uri': 'nyt://article/402cb942-6b26-56a9-9909-914bfa18997c'}, {'abstract': 'The grumbling over a new Apple maps service didn’t deter customers from once again jamming stores to get the latest iPhone. The latest Apple device went on sale at 8 a.m. local time Friday in Apple retail stores and those of its wireless carrier partners. Crowds were heavy, especially at flagship Apple stores like the one on Fifth Avenue in New York.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/the-iphone-5-now-on-the-loose/', 'snippet': 'The gr