blic Art Fund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'COLUMBUS CIRCLE (NYC)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T18:02:10+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Arts', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/72c5b180-50e1-545b-9be8-ccc9b52aa2c9', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/72c5b180-50e1-545b-9be8-ccc9b52aa2c9'}, {'abstract': 'Bucks readers discuss whether the potential financial reward of becoming a whistle-blower would outweigh other concerns.', 'web_url': 'https://bucks.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/weighing-the-right-thing-to-do/', 'snippet': 'Bucks readers discuss whether the potential financial reward of becoming a whistle-blower would outweigh other concerns.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Paul Sullivan recounts in his Wealth Matters column this week, anyone considering the financial rewards of becoming a whistle-blower should fully consider all the possible fallout. A lawyer who has represented whistle-blowers, John Phillips, explained it this way: “You may find yourself unemployable. Home foreclosures, divorce, suicide and depression all go with this territory.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Weighing the Right Thing to Do', 'kicker': 'Bucks', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Federal Taxes (US)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Whistle-Blowers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Internal Revenue Service', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T18:03:21+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Your Money', 'byline': {'original': 'By Bucks Editors', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Bucks', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Editors', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d498e55-50c8-50a7-aa73-23414b377425', 'word_count': 156, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d498e55-50c8-50a7-aa73-23414b377425'}, {'abstract': 'At a sailing school in Ireland, Verlyn Klinkenborg relearns the ropes.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/t-magazine/irelands-charted-waters.html', 'snippet': 'At a sailing school in Ireland, Verlyn Klinkenborg relearns the ropes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We troop down to the harbor at the once industrial end of town, nautical mousquetaires wearing sea boots and knit caps, salopettes and foul-weather jackets, bundled in life vests with crotch straps and tethers. We pass black boot prints tarred with waste oil and a great heap of wharfish refuse, tangles of long-discarded fishing nets, thick manila hawsers. And then we come to the jetty, a wide concrete ramp leading down to the broad water of Baltimore Harbor in the parish of Rath and the Islands, County Cork, Ireland. Two aluminum skiffs are waiting, rafted together. We climb across to the outer skiff, then we’re off to the sailboats. There is a light breeze. The boats rest on their moorings like half-depleted helium balloons.', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '116', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23well-ireland/23well-ireland-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 960, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23well-ireland/23well-ireland-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1919, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23well-ireland/23well-ireland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23well-ireland/23well-ireland-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/t-magazine/23well-ireland/23well-ireland-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Charted Waters', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Charted Waters', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Travel and Vacations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sailboats and Sailing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Ireland', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T18:06:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7638a6b8-4987-540f-b784-f4dc6d486472', 'word_count': 2046, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7638a6b8-4987-540f-b784-f4dc6d486472'}, {'abstract': 'With “Discovering Columbus,” an apartment built 60 feet over Columbus Circle in New York City, Tatzu Nishi has achieved a nifty bit of Surrealist displacement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/arts/design/tatzu-nishis-discovering-columbus-installation.html', 'snippet': 'With “Discovering Columbus,” an apartment built 60 feet over Columbus Circle in New York City, Tatzu Nishi has achieved a nifty bit of Surrealist displacement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’ve ever wanted to see what the city’s pre-eminent statue of Christopher Columbus looks like standing on a large coffee table in an upscale New York living room with killer views, now is your chance. Under the auspices of the Public Art Fund, the Japanese artist Tatzu Nishi has built a convincingly appointed penthouse-worthy space around the 13-foot-high marble sculpture of Columbus that has presided over Columbus Circle from a height of 60 feet since it was completed by Gaetano Russo in 1892. In doing so, Mr. Nishi has achieved a nifty bit of Surrealist displacement without moving the sculpture an inch — albeit not quite as nifty as I’d hoped. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 374, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 374}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 405, 'width': 650, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/22/arts/22NISHI/22NISHI-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype':