t_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Automobiles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Collectors and Collections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Ford Motor Company', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T18:12:30+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Automobiles', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/454cdaf2-c99f-542a-b501-3e9a112184e6', 'word_count': 283, 'uri': 'nyt://article/454cdaf2-c99f-542a-b501-3e9a112184e6'}, {'abstract': 'The Gospel of Judas, Jesus’s wife, and the paranoid style in Jesus scholarship.', 'web_url': 'https://douthat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/21/the-jesus-conspiracy/', 'snippet': 'The Gospel of Judas, Jesus’s wife, and the paranoid style in Jesus scholarship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It’s been six years since National Geographic revealed, amid much fanfare and discussion, the existence of a heretofore-unknown document that seemed to retell the New Testament narrative from the point of view of Judas Iscariot. That experience should have been a cautionary tale about the intersection of Biblical archaeology and media sensationalism: The first wave of coverage suggested that the document painted Judas as a misunderstood hero who was “only obeying his master’s wishes when he betrayed Jesus with a kiss,” but the evidence soon mounted that this sensationalistic claim relied on dubious translation decisions, and that the Judas in the fragmentary gospel might well actually be the embodiment of a Gnostic “king of demons” rather than Jesus’s most loyal friend.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Jesus Conspiracy', 'kicker': 'Ross Douthat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-21T18:15:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ross Douthat', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ross', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Douthat', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/322b0c93-e32f-5678-9d17-9ba2b146386b', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/322b0c93-e32f-5678-9d17-9ba2b146386b'}, {'abstract': 'President Obama and Representative Paul D. Ryan each accused the other of posing a threat to the future of the Medicare program, with Mr. Obama getting a better reception.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/us/politics/medicare-takes-center-stage-on-the-campaign-trail.html', 'snippet': 'President Obama and Representative Paul D. Ryan each accused the other of posing a threat to the future of the Medicare program, with Mr. Obama getting a better reception.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WOODBRIDGE, Va. — President Obama traded charges with Representative Paul D. Ryan on Friday over the financing of Medicare, as each candidate sought to appeal to older voters by warning them, with arguments both familiar and new, that his opponent would threaten the future of this signature federal health care program.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '10', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/us/politics/921obama/921obama-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/21/us/politics/921obama/921obama-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/21/us/politics/921obama/921obama-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'su