het Muhammad. I try to look at why there’s such outrage at blasphemy in the Islamic world, and whether extremists are trying to hijack the Arab Spring. If I’d had a bit more space, I would have made one more point. The history of Islam is largely of being the dominant religion in its sphere, closely allied with rulers, while Christianity and Judaism have long experience as minority faiths. I think that one consequence is that Christianity has more expectation of sacrilege or mockery, while that’s more of a shock to many Muslims, particularly to Sunnis living in Sunni countries.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '‘Defenders’ of the Faith', 'kicker': 'Nicholas Kristof', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T17:44:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nicholas Kristof', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nicholas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kristof', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7df8597e-f4b5-5009-8b0f-5dc3a43e5ea1', 'word_count': 131, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7df8597e-f4b5-5009-8b0f-5dc3a43e5ea1'}, {'abstract': 'Aung San Suu Kyi hit the nail on the head last week when she said it is the fear of losing power that corrupts. It’s everywhere these days.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/opinion/sunday/friedman-hard-lines-red-lines-and-green-lines.html', 'snippet': 'Aung San Suu Kyi hit the nail on the head last week when she said it is the fear of losing power that corrupts. It’s everywhere these days.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Washington', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2018/04/02/opinion