 84, C.I.A. Operative, Later Tycoon and Ex-Convict, Is Dead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Espionage and Intelligence Services', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wilson, Edwin P', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Deaths (Obituaries)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Libya', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T17:52:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'National', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Douglas Martin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Douglas', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Martin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Obituary (Obit)', '_id': 'nyt://article/36e490ae-bc90-5f76-ae78-9bff917f548a', 'word_count': 1342, 'uri': 'nyt://article/36e490ae-bc90-5f76-ae78-9bff917f548a'}, {'abstract': 'As summer wanes, the evening air is alive with the symphonies of a cornucopia of insects.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/a-little-night-music/', 'snippet': 'As summer wanes, the evening air is alive with the symphonies of a cornucopia of insects.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This is the season of the night fiddlers, when you know summer is fraying away. The air brims with their eerie music, although we rarely see the musicians — katydids and crickets playing their marimbas, as they lift their wings high and rub a sharp edge of one wing over a ridge of pegs on the other. It’s as if they’re combing strands of song from their wings.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Little Night Music', 'kicker': 'Diane Ackerman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crickets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Insects', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Summer (Season)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T17:55:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Diane Ackerman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Diane', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Ackerman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/05e06930-09c3-5fe1-8e5f-e1f06c251203', 'word_count': 639, 'uri': 'nyt://article/05e06930-09c3-5fe1-8e5f-e1f06c251203'}, {'abstract': 'The argument that C.E.O.’s will leave if they aren’t compensated well, perhaps even lavishly, is bogus, according to a new study.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/business/ceos-and-the-pay-em-or-lose-em-myth-fair-game.html', 'snippet': 'The argument that C.E.O.’s will leave if they aren’t compensated well, perhaps even lavishly, is bogus, according to a new study.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CORPORATIONS are forever defending big executive paydays. If we don’t pay up, the argument goes, our sharpest minds will jump to our rivals. ', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 900, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 900}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1366, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2010/04/19/business/morgenson/morgenson-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'C.E.O.’s and the Pay-’Em-or-Lose-’Em Myth', 'kicker': 'Fair Game', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pamper ’Em or Lose ’Em? Not So Fast', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Boards of Directors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ferrere, Craig K', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Elson, Charles M', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executive Compensation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Executives and Management (Theory)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Delaware', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:05:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gretchen Morgenson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gretchen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morgenson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e209b67-cf91-5bf6-bbce-687d5889415e', 'word_count': 1137, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e209b67-cf91-5bf6-bbce-687d5889415e'}, {'abstract': 'Gov. Jerry Brown could put his state, and the nation, on a path to greater economic security if he signs a new retirement plan.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/opinion/sunday/california-takes-on-the-retirement-crisis.html', 'snippet': 'Gov. Jerry Brown could put his state, and the nation, on a path to greater economic security if he signs a new retirement plan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When you think of California as a trendsetter, retirement policy is probably not the first thing that comes to mind, but that may soon change. ', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'California Takes On the Retirement Crisis', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'California Takes on the Retirement Crisis', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'California', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pensions and Retirement Plans', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Law and Legislation', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Public Employees Retirement System', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brown, Edmund G Jr', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:10:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/19554022-0520-5586-8356-88a41909d9c3', 'word_count': 502, 'uri': 'nyt://article/19554022-0520-5586-8356-88a41909d9c3'}, {'abstract': 'It’s not the quantity of sleep that restores and refreshes, but the quality.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/opinion/sunday/rethinking-sleep.html', 'snippet': 'It’s not the quantity of sleep that restores and refreshes, but the quality.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SOMETIME in the dark stretch of the night it happens. Perhaps it’s the chime of an incoming text message. Or your iPhone screen lights up to alert you to a new e-mail. Or you find yourself staring at the ceiling, replaying the day in your head. Next thing you know, you’re out of bed and engaged with the world, once again ignoring the often quoted fact that eight straight hours of sleep is essential.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 429, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 429}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 731, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1463, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23COVER-SUB/23COVER-SUB-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rethinking Sleep', 'kicker': 'Opinion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rethinking Sleep', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fatigue', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Memory', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Google Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Research', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Productivity', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:11:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By David K. Randall', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': 'K.', 'lastname': 'Randall', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/e61fd87f-3da2-52da-b290-67dd46d14546', 'word_count': 1488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e61fd87f-3da2-52da-b290-67dd46d14546'}, {'abstract': 'The father of sleep medicine, Dr. William C. Dement, on playing jazz on a houseboat and a water pistol wake-up.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/opinion/sunday/catching-up-on-sleep-with-dr-william-c-dement.html', 'snippet': 'The father of sleep medicine, Dr. William C. Dement, on playing jazz on a houseboat and a water pistol wake-up.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Considered the father of sleep medicine, Dr. William C. Dement, 84, has studied slumbering since the 1950s. He has written countless scientific papers and books and is the founder of the Stanford Sleep Disorders Clinic, the world’s first sleep laboratory. Undergraduates still pack into lecture halls every semester to take his “Sleep and Dreams” course, and he has a cameo role in the film “Sleepwalk With Me,” which is currently in theaters.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23download/23download-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 374, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23download/23download-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1577, 'width': 576, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23download/23download-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23download/23download-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sunday-review/23download/23download-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Dr. William C. Dement', 'kicker': 'Download', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Dr. William C. Dement', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sleep', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:11:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kate Murphy', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Murphy', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/585a707f-833e-5033-aeec-5ed0390cd85b', 'word_count': 467, 'uri': 'nyt://article/585a707f-833e-5033-aeec-5ed0390cd85b'}, {'abstract': 'Maps showing possible new additions to the world’s list of independent nations.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/the-new-world/', 'snippet': 'Maps showing possible new additions to the world’s list of independent nations.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We appear to be on the brink of a nation-state baby boom. These new countries will not be the product of a single political change or conflict, as was the post-Soviet proliferation, nor will they be confined to a specific region. If anything, they are linked by a single, undeniable fact: history chews up borders with the same purposeless determination that geology does, as seaside villas slide off eroding coastal cliffs. Here is an interactive map of what could become the world’s newest international borders.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The New World', 'kicker': 'Borderlines', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'USSR (Former Soviet Union)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:15:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Frank Jacobs and Parag Khanna', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Frank', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Jacobs', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Parag', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Khanna', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6ca97d9-68c4-5d3d-9502-45178790513d', 'word_count': 86, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6ca97d9-68c4-5d3d-9502-45178790513d'}, {'abstract': 'To win manager of the year, a manager typically must win a lot more games than people expected, overcome an unforeseen obstacle or win in historic fashion.', 'web_url': 'https://bats.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/22/assessing-manager-of-the-year-races/', 'snippet': 'To win manager of the year, a manager typically must win a lot more games than people expected, overcome an unforeseen obstacle or win in historic fashion.', 'lead_paragraph': 'To win this award, a manager typically must do one of the following: win a lot more games than people expected (like the Padres’ Bud Black in 2010), overcome an unforeseen obstacle (like the Angels’ Mike Scioscia in 2009, after the death of pitcher Nick Adenhart) or win in historic fashion (like the Mariners’ Lou Piniella in 2001, when his team won 116 games).', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '3', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Assessing Manager of the Year Races', 'kicker': 'Bats', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Extra Bases: Managers of the Year', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Awards, Decorations and Honors', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Davey', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Melvin, Bob', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Scioscia, Mike', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Showalter, Buck', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ventura, Robin', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-22T18:20:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tyler Kepner', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tyler', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Kepner', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/913a028f-7fd8-559f-92ae-83e3b1bdcd61', 'word_count': 272, 'uri': 'nyt://article/913a028f-7fd8-559f-92ae-83e3b1bdcd61'}, {'abstract': 'Stephen Hill, the Jets’ rookie wide receiver, is seeing things he never did while playing three seasons in a run-oriented offense at Georgia Tech.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/sports/football/jets-rookie-wide-receiver-is-in-a-new-world-of-zigs-and-zags.html', 'snippet': 'Stephen Hill, the Jets’ rookie wide receiver, is seeing things he never did while playing three seasons in a run-oriented offense at Georgia Tech.', 'lead_paragraph': 'FLORHAM PARK, N.J. — Last Sunday in Pittsburgh, Stephen Hill grappled with the Steelers’ cornerbacks. He pushed to create separation on his routes. He shoved them as he blocked on running plays. They pushed and shoved him right back. By game’s end, Hill was exhausted. He had lined up at receiver for 40 of the Jets’ 51 offensive plays, and Mark Sanchez had thrown his way only twice — a deep ball that was broken up and a pass that bounced at his feet. ', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sports/subYJETS/subYJETS-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 816, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sports/subYJETS/subYJETS-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1633, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/sports/subYJETS/subYJETS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'heig