Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Brantley', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brantley', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c42c55b-ba56-50b7-8f15-01fd5a9857d5', 'word_count': 1087, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c42c55b-ba56-50b7-8f15-01fd5a9857d5'}, {'abstract': 'Dave Douglas’s new album, “Be Still,” is underpinned by a recommendation from his mother, who died last year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/arts/music/dave-douglass-new-album-be-still.html', 'snippet': 'Dave Douglas’s new album, “Be Still,” is underpinned by a recommendation from his mother, who died last year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Dave Douglas dealt sparingly with the emotional back story at 92YTriBeCa on Wednesday night, in the auspicious first outing by his new quintet. During a concert built around the Protestant hymns on his gorgeous and contemplative new album, “Be Still” — due out on Tuesday on Greenleaf, his independent label — Mr. Douglas spoke of his motivation only in passing. The album, he said simply, “came about because all these hymns and songs were songs that my mother recommended that I play.”', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/arts/24DOUGLAS/24DOUGLAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/24/arts/24DOUGLAS/24DOUGLAS-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/arts/24DOUGLAS/24DOUGLAS-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name':