'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Chris Perkins', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Chris', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Perkins', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3c44cc38-211f-53f6-9d0e-4c2f41e9385a', 'word_count': 595, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3c44cc38-211f-53f6-9d0e-4c2f41e9385a'}, {'abstract': 'A right-wing campaign to oust an able judge in Iowa raises worrisome questions.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/opinion/politics-principle-and-an-attack-on-the-courts.html', 'snippet': 'A right-wing campaign to oust an able judge in Iowa raises worrisome questions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Two years ago, in what should have been a quiet judicial retention election, Iowa voters threw out three State Supreme Court justices for taking seriously their duty to protect fundamental rights. The three had provoked right-wing wrath for joining a unanimous 2009 ruling overturning the state’s prohibition on same-sex marriage. Conservative forces succeeded in ousting them by tapping antigay sentiment and attacking “activist” judges. Those same forces — aided openly this time by the state’s Republican Party — are now calling for the removal of David Wiggins, another capable judge who joined in the marriage decision. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Politics, Principle and an Attack on the Courts', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Politics, Principle and an Attack on the Courts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Wiggins, David', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections, Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Same-Sex Marriage, Civil Unions and Domestic Partnerships', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Courts and the Judiciary', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iowa', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jindal, Bobby', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Santorum, Rick', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T01:12:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/e5c52348-d314-57a2-803f-bff78be44a68', 'word_count': 445, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e5c52348-d314-57a2-803f-bff78be44a68'}, {'abstract': 'Chicago’s fight may be over, but in Idaho, the debate over schools has morphed into a harsh discussion about whom the voters should trust.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/us/idaho-education-overhaul-is-subject-of-referendum.html', 'snippet': 'Chicago’s fight may be over, but in Idaho, the debate over schools has morphed into a harsh discussion about whom the voters should trust.', 'lead_paragraph': 'BOISE, Idaho — In the struggle to fix the nation’s public schools, the old red-state, blue-state idea is looking as dated as Dick and Jane. You can hear the change in the voice of Gov. C. L. Otter, a Republican here in one of the most deeply conservative corners of the country, when he expresses a brotherhood bond with Rahm Emanuel, the Democratic mayor of Chicago and former Obama administration chief of staff.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/us/CLASSROOM-1/CLASSROOM-1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'wi