'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T01:22:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Africa', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eric Schmitt, Helene Cooper and Michael S. Schmidt', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eric', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schmitt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Helene', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cooper', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}, {'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': 'S.', 'lastname': 'Schmidt', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 3}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6998539-c314-5c82-b648-66b0ca1c6e83', 'word_count': 1109, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6998539-c314-5c82-b648-66b0ca1c6e83'}, {'abstract': 'Iraq is engaging in a dangerous game by enabling the flow of arms from Iran to Syria.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/opinion/on-the-wrong-side.html', 'snippet': 'Iraq is engaging in a dangerous game by enabling the flow of arms from Iran to Syria.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Leaders of Iraq’s Shiite-led government have often advised the United States not to worry about their ties with Iran, where Shiites are also in the majority. But the assurances are getting harder to swallow. Baghdad is showing a distressing willingness to side with the region’s most repressive regimes, not just Iran but also Iran’s chief ally, the brutal Syrian president, Bashar al-Assad.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On the Wrong Side', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'On the Wrong Side', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iraq', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Iran', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Arms Trade', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Assad, Bashar al-', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T01:22:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c01bb8d3-cf3e-5a77-a715-09917f2b59e6', 'word_count': 424, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c01bb8d3-cf3e-5a77-a715-09917f2b59e6'}, {'abstract': 'Tiger Woods and Rory McIlroy, tournament co-favorites, shot 72 and 74 Sunday in the Tour Championship.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/sports/golf/tiger-woods-and-rory-mcilroy-struggle-in-final-round-but-have-some-reasons-to-celebrate.html', 'snippet': 'Tiger Woods and Rory McIlroy, tournament co-favorites, shot 72 and 74 Sunday in the Tour Championship.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — Let the record show that before this past weekend, Tiger Woods could not have owned the East Lake Golf Club course more if he had held the deed to the property.', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/sports/YTOUR/YTOUR-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/sports/YTOUR/YTOUR-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1474, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail',