, Thelonious', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kennedy, John F, Center for the Performing Arts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T03:26:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nate Chinen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nate', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chinen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6742cd0-8e15-593e-81fb-93fe02b61e78', 'word_count': 375, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6742cd0-8e15-593e-81fb-93fe02b61e78'}, {'abstract': 'What are aspiring U.S. diplomats saying about the murder of the U.S. ambassador to Libya and three other Americans? The attack might have reasonably led many of them to rethink their career paths. But graduate students now in the Foreign Service pipeline seem to have strengthened their plans to seek diplomatic postings abroad.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/23/aspiring-u-s-diplomats-stiffen-their-resolve-after-ambassadors-death/', 'snippet': 'What are aspiring U.S. diplomats saying about the murder of the U.S. ambassador to Libya and three other Americans? The attack might have reasonably led many of them to rethink their career paths. But graduate students now in the Foreign Service p...', 'lead_paragraph': 'The recent killing of the U.S. ambassador to Libya, J. Christopher Stevens, along with three other Americans at the mission in Benghazi, now appears to have been a premeditated assault, not merely collateral damage from yet another anti-American protest. The deadly ambush reinforced the idea that many diplomats — even those guarded by security personnel and working in fortified buildings — can quickly find themselves in harm’s way. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Aspiring U.S. Diplomats Stiffen Their Resolve After Ambassador’s Death', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Benghazi Attack (2012)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Diplomatic Service, Embassies and Consulates', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Stevens, J Christopher', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Peace Corps', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Benghazi (Libya)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T03:32:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mark McDonald', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mark', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McDonald', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0606cb67-d327-5011-aefe-f19f5d7bace9', 'word_count': 1182, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0606cb67-d327-5011-aefe-f19f5d7bace9'}, {'abstract': 'As Greenland’s massive ice cap recedes, vast new deposits of minerals and gems are being discovered, forming the basis of a potentially lucrative mining industry.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/slideshow/2012/09/23/world/europe/20120923GREENLAND.html', 'snippet': 'As Greenland’s massive ice cap recedes, vast new deposits of minerals and gems are being discovered, forming the basis of a potentially lucrative mining industry.', 'lead_paragraph': 'As Greenland’s massive ice cap recedes, vast new deposits of minerals and gems are being discovered, forming the basis of a potentially lucrative mining industry.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/world/europe/20120923GREENLAND-slide-1CDK/20