 Singer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Natasha', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Singer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9df8243e-17b2-512d-8828-95bff504011f', 'word_count': 1377, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9df8243e-17b2-512d-8828-95bff504011f'}, {'abstract': 'How Mathew Brady’s photographs changed the way Americans look at war.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/the-dead-of-antietam/', 'snippet': 'How Mathew Brady’s photographs changed the way Americans look at war.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 19, 1862, just two days after the Battle of Antietam, Alexander Gardner, an employee of the photographer Mathew Brady, began documenting the battle’s grim aftermath. One of Gardner’s photographs, titled “Dead Horse of Confederate Colonel; both killed at Battle of Antietam,” depicted a milky-white steed lying on the field in an eerily peaceful repose. Another showed a line of bloated Confederate bodies along the Hagerstown Pike. Titled “View in the Field, on the west side of Hagerstown road, after the Battle of Antietam,” it is one of the most reproduced photographs of Civil War dead. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Dead of Antietam', 'kicker': 'Disunion', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civil War (US) (1861-65)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Brady, Mathew', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T16:30:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Terry L. Jones', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Terry', 'middlename': 'L.', 'lastname': 'Jones', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank'