Fashion and Apparel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T16:37:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'TStyle', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Edward Barsamian', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Edward', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Barsamian', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dbe0833d-61f4-552a-a1d1-ebae994e8adc', 'word_count': 60, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dbe0833d-61f4-552a-a1d1-ebae994e8adc'}, {'abstract': 'Christine Lagarde, the managing director of the International Monetary Fund, warned that it would probably cut its estimates of global growth yet again this year.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/business/imf-sees-economic-deterioration-ahead.html', 'snippet': 'Christine Lagarde, the managing director of the International Monetary Fund, warned that it would probably cut its estimates of global growth yet again this year.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — Christine Lagarde, the managing director of the International Monetary Fund, warned on Monday that the institution would probably cut its estimates of global growth yet again this year because of the tepid recovery in the United States, a slowdown in emerging economies and continued troubles in the euro zone. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '9', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'I.M.F. Praises Central Banks, but Sees Slowing Ahead', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'I.M.F. Praises Central Banks, but Sees Slowing Ahead', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'International Monetary Fund', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lagarde, Christine', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Trade and World Market', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-24T16:43:14+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ec22778-ceb0-5cd6-b0d7-4da20fac9c93', 'word_count': 557, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ec22778-ceb0-5cd6-b0d7-4da20fac9c93'}, {'abstract': 'Despite an edict from the National Football League to respect the replacement officials, coaches and players have grown increasingly frustrated with them.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/sports/nfl-coaches-cant-hide-frustration-with-replacement-officials.html', 'snippet': 'Despite an edict from the National Football League to respect the replacement officials, coaches and players have grown increasingly frustrated with them.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It seems, in hindsight, a fitting end to a chaotic day of football. The most accomplished and highest-profile coach in the N.F.L. running after an official, seeking an explanation for what happened on the play that had just sent his team to defeat. And then that coach, the New England Patriots’ Bill Belichick, reached his right arm out to stop the line judge Esteban Garza as he headed for the tunnel. In that brief contact was crystallized the mounting frustration of coaches and players with replacement officials. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/football/25forward-pic/25forward-pic-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name':