-errors-send-japanese-to-homegrown-app.html', 'snippet': 'Buyers of the iPhone 5 have turned to Mapion, a map application for smartphones produced by a Japanese company.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — When Hikarie, Tokyo’s new must-visit shopping destination, opened in April, it was already old news on Mapion’s map application. An elite team at the company, which is based here, had marked the glass tower a year earlier, keeping the service a step ahead in this fast-changing city.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Apple Maps Errors Send Japanese to Homegrown App', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Apple Maps Errors Send Japanese to Homegrown App', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'iPhone', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Maps', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Mapion Co Ltd', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mobile Applications', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:16:13+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8ff1839c-d393-5580-bc69-a3a211033a5d', 'word_count': 678, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8ff1839c-d393-5580-bc69-a3a211033a5d'}, {'abstract': 'Pittsburgh was one of baseball’s feel-good stories for much of the season, moving as many as 16 games over .500 on Aug. 8, before losing 31 of its next 43 games, including Monday’s loss to the Mets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/sports/baseball/pirates-trying-to-salvage-season-mets-ike-davis-home-runs.html', 'snippet': 'Pittsburgh was one of baseball’s feel-good stories for much of the season, moving as many as 16 games over .500 on Aug. 8, before losing 31 of its next 43 games, including Monday’s loss to the Mets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When the Pirates took the field Monday, they could have taken solace knowing that the Mets understood their pain. Misery has always loved company.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/YPIRATES/YPIRATES-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 642, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/YPIRATES/YPIRATES-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1284, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/YPIRATES/YPIRATES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/YPIRATES/YPIRATES-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/sports/YPIRATES/YPIRATES-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Pirates Are Trying to Salvage a Promising Season Gone Bad', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Pirates Are Trying to Salvage a Promising Season Gone Bad', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Mets', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pittsburgh Pirates', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:18:07+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': 'By Zach Schonbrun', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Zach', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schonbrun', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/fcc13f24-af4d-5806-a9da-ce823fa34b03', 'word_count': 685, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fcc13f24-af4d-5806-a9da-ce823fa34b03'}, {'abstract': 'The State Assembly reprimanded Bill Eggler, who had been posting blog comments defending Speaker Sheldon Silver under cover of the name Sophia Walker.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/nyregion/assembly-worker-punished-for-using-pseudonym-to-praise-speaker-online.html', 'snippet': 'The State Assembly reprimanded Bill Eggler, who had been posting blog comments defending Speaker Sheldon Silver under cover of the name Sophia Walker.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York State Assembly on Monday reprimanded an employee who had used a pseudonym while posting comments on the Internet defending Speaker Sheldon Silver’s handling of a sexual harassment scandal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '19', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Assembly Punishes Worker for Online Posts', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Assembly Punishes Worker for Online Posts', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Blogs and Blogging (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Sexual Harassment', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Politics and Government', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Silver, Sheldon', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:18:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Danny Hakim', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Danny', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hakim', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c6d2816-91ad-5b97-b42f-efb24790b33c', 'word_count': 486, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c6d2816-91ad-5b97-b42f-efb24790b33c'}, {'abstract': 'Dan Meridor, the deputy prime minister, emphasized the need to return to the negotiating table, while Defense Minister Ehud Barak called for annexation of three large settlement blocs.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/world/middleeast/opposite-israeli-views-on-palestinian-stalemate.html', 'snippet': 'Dan Meridor, the deputy prime minister, emphasized the need to return to the negotiating table, while Defense Minister Ehud Barak called for annexation of three large settlement blocs.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — With the Palestinians planning to make their case this week to upgrade from organizational status to nonmember status at the United Nations General Assembly, one senior Israeli minister condemned the move as “the easy and wrong way out” on Monday, while another said the time had come for Israel to consider its own unilateral move toward a separate state: annexing parts of the West Bank and withdrawing from others.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/world/25mideast-map/25mideast-map-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/world/25mideast-map/25mideast-map-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 263, 'width': 190, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Before Palestinians Act at U.N., Israeli Officials Urge Other Steps', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Before Palestinians Act at U.N., Israeli Officials Urge Other Steps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Palestinian Authority', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Palestinians', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Israeli Settlements', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Barak, Ehud', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:22:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jodi Rudoren', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jodi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rudoren', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f01ad528-ab78-59c7-ae90-0800b2e09598', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f01ad528-ab78-59c7-ae90-0800b2e09598'}, {'abstract': 'A $12 million donation to expand health care for Special Olympics athletes is money well spent if it can draw more attention to the need.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/opinion/at-the-special-olympics-a-race-for-better-health.html', 'snippet': 'A $12 million donation to expand health care for Special Olympics athletes is money well spent if it can draw more attention to the need.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When Special Olympics holds its competitions in the United States and around the world, it often enlists volunteer doctors and nurses to give its athletes basic health screenings. What they routinely uncover is evidence of a persistent gap in health care for people with mental disabilities. In basic areas — like dental work, eye and foot care and hearing exams — their unmet needs are appalling. Of 76,900 athletes examined between 2007 and 2011, more than 37 percent had untreated tooth decay. More than half had gait abnormalities, often a sign of ill-fitting shoes. More than 20 percent had never had an eye exam.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At Special Olympics, a Race for Better Health', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'At Special Olympics, a Race for Better Health', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Olympics', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Disabilities', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicine and Health', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Golisano, Tom', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:26:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/5f8e24d9-bb57-5bce-a97b-04fd59a0b65b', 'word_count': 367, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5f8e24d9-bb57-5bce-a97b-04fd59a0b65b'}, {'abstract': 'Videotaping custodial interrogations in murder, serious sex crime and felony assault cases is a laudable breakthrough in law enforcement.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/opinion/smart-step-for-justice-in-new-york-city.html', 'snippet': 'Videotaping custodial interrogations in murder, serious sex crime and felony assault cases is a laudable breakthrough in law enforcement.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In a significant law enforcement breakthrough with likely national implications, the New York Police Department has adopted a broad policy of videotaping custodial interrogations in murder, serious sex crime and felony assault cases. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Smart Step for Justice', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Smart Step for Justice', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kelly, Raymond W', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Interrogations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Video Recordings and Downloads', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'False Arrests, Convictions and Imprisonments', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Crime and Criminals', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:26:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/b6e774a6-8259-5350-bc02-37a7ecb09579', 'word_count': 282, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b6e774a6-8259-5350-bc02-37a7ecb09579'}, {'abstract': 'A new proposal seeks a more efficient distribution system for patients on the waiting list for a transplant.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/opinion/discarded-kidneys.html', 'snippet': 'A new proposal seeks a more efficient distribution system for patients on the waiting list for a transplant.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than 93,000 patients are on the waiting list for a kidney transplant, and last year more than 4,700 patients died while waiting. That makes it especially tragic that more than 2,600 kidneys were recovered from deceased donors last year and then discarded. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Discarded Kidneys', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Discarded Kidneys', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Kidneys', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Transplants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Network for Organ Sharing', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Organ Donation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:26:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/a3c0bdec-ac10-56ea-af2f-88d883354ddc', 'word_count': 443, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a3c0bdec-ac10-56ea-af2f-88d883354ddc'}, {'abstract': 'By refusing to supply America with intelligent answers, all the reliable national polls now show that his campaign is driving voters away.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/opinion/why-mitt-romney-is-slipping.html', 'snippet': 'By refusing to supply America with intelligent answers, all the reliable national polls now show that his campaign is driving voters away.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Mitt Romney said in an interview aired on Sunday that his campaign “doesn’t need a turnaround.” He told CBS News’s “60 Minutes” that he is tied with President Obama; he has a “very effective campaign; it’s doing a very good job;” and all he needs to do to win is keep repeating his plan to restore economic freedom.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Why Romney Is Slipping', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Why Romney Is Slipping', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Polls and Public Opinion', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:27:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/48d57e46-f133-5d1b-a09d-74929efcd3a3', 'word_count': 540, 'uri': 'nyt://article/48d57e46-f133-5d1b-a09d-74929efcd3a3'}, {'abstract': 'Despite themselves, writers are often engaged in acts of unwitting self-contradiction.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/24/the-trouble-with-intentions/', 'snippet': 'Despite themselves, writers are often engaged in acts of unwitting self-contradiction.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On their own, sentences are implacably honest. They may be long, short, simple, complex, clear, ambiguous, even incoherent. But they don’t try to hide those qualities. They are what they are and they say what they say. It’s as plain as the words on their faces. The trouble is that most sentences have writers, a fact that readers are well aware of. That makes it hard to consider sentences entirely on their own. Other questions arise. What’s she saying? What did he mean?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Trouble With Intentions', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reading and Writing Skills', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T01:30:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Verlyn Klinkenborg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Verlyn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Klinkenborg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c6999e3d-9040-57b9-9bc3-5565d42490a1', 'word_count': 1040, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c6999e3d-9040-57b9-9bc3-5565d42490a1'}, {'abstract': 'In an address to a civic group on Monday, John B. Rhea, the chairman of the New York City Housing Authority, described a plan that he said could earn hundreds of millions of dollars for the agency.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/nyregion/housing-authority-chairman-offers-money-raising-plan.html', 'snippet': 'In an address to a civic group on Monday, John B. Rhea, the chairman of the New York City Housing Authority, described a plan that he said could earn hundreds of millions of dollars for the agency.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Acknowledging the “considerable challenges” bedeviling his agency, John B. Rhea, the beleaguered chairman of the New York City Housing Authority, unveiled a plan Monday to squeeze hundreds of millions of dollars out of parking lots, walkways, open spaces and other areas.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/NYCHA/NYCHA-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/NYCHA/NYCHA-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subt