b_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming.html', 'snippet': 'A toddler’s questions prompts a mother to recall her own mother’s love for music and the liberating power found in song.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Every morning these last few weeks, my 3-year-old son digs through a pile of old CDs on a bookshelf in our guest bedroom. He toddles between rooms, carrying one and then another of the plastic cases to the breakfast table, asking, “You like this one, Mama?”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 530, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 530}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 910, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1820, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/booming/25children-booming/25children-booming-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Retreating to Memory With a Song', 'kicker': 'From Our Children', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baby Boomers', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T09:00:35+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Booming', 'byline': {'original': 'By Kelly McMasters', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kelly', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'McMasters', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e0e66b75-4856-529c-bd12-a288c393a761', 'word_count': 1083, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e0e66b75-4856-529c-bd12-a288c393a761'}, {'abstract': 'Eighteen months after the Fukushima nuclear disaster in Japan, the photographer Kosuke Okahara has created a collection of haunting images of a land irreparably altered and the people who stayed behind.', 'web_url': 'https://lens.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/fragments-of-fukushima/', 'snippet': 'Eighteen months after the Fukushima nuclear disaster in Japan, the photographer Kosuke Okahara has created a collection of haunting images of a land irreparably altered and the people who stayed behind.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first time the Tokyo-based photographer Kosuke Okahara visited a town near the Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant in Japan, he grabbed his dosimeter, a device used to measure radiation levels, and looked at his watch to see how much time he could spend at the location.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fragments of Fukushima', 'kicker': 'Lens', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fukushima Daiichi Nuclear Power Plant (Japan)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Japan Earthquake and Tsunami (2011)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Photography', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Japan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tokyo (Japan)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T09:00:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Multimedia/Photos', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shreeya Sinha', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shreeya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sinha', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3323af0e-e82e-584d-b0ab-5ba1462c96af', 'word_count': 543, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3323af0e-e82e-584d-b0ab-5ba1462c96af'}, {'abstract': 'How important is happiness to you? Do you sometimes try so hard to be happy, or show friends that you’re happy, that it makes you anxious or unhappy?', 'web_url': 'https://learning.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/is-trying-too-hard-to-be-happy-making-you-sad/', 'snippet': 'How important is happiness to you? Do you sometimes try so hard to be happy, or show friends that you’re happy, that it makes you anxious or unhappy?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In her opinion piece “America the Anxious,” Ruth Whippman argues that Americans’ relentless pursuit of happiness is actually causing us much anxiety. She begins her piece with a quote by the author-philosopher Eric Hoffer: “The search for happiness is one of the chief sources of unhappiness.” ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Is Trying Too Hard to Be Happy Making You Sad?', 'kicker': 'The Learning Network', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T09:02:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Education', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Gonchar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gonchar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/17ef0856-931c-536f-aeaf-471f9f57697b', 'word_count': 426, 'uri': 'nyt://article/17ef0856-931c-536f-aeaf-471f9f57697b'}, {'abstract': 'Former lawyer speaks on his land rights advocacy group and their work in India.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/a-conversation-with-landesa-founder-roy-prosterman/', 'snippet': 'Former lawyer speaks on his land rights advocacy group and their work in India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'More than four decades ago, Roy Prosterman left a high-paying job at a white-shoe law firm in search of a different kind of job satisfaction. He ended up finding it in Landesa, a Seattle-based nonprofit that he founded, which works to secure land rights for the rural poor in countries around the world. Founded in 1966, the group is better known by its former name, Rural Development Institute.\xa0 Today, Landesa has 120 employees; it has operated in more than 50 countries, including China, Vietnam and Russia, and says 100 million families have benefited from its work. Thirty percent of the group’s $13 million annual budget is spent in India.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Conversation With: Landesa Founder Roy Prosterman', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Land Use Policies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Legal Profession', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rural Areas', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Third World and Developing Countries', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Prosterman, Roy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Landesa', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Rural Development Institute', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T09:16:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shivani Vora', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shivani', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Vora', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7b6a82b6-4988-508a-953e-9d0ec4420e51', 'word_count': 1481, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7b6a82b6-4988-508a-953e-9d0ec4420e51'}, {'abstract': 'Mortar shells launched from Syria landed in the Golan Heights near Israel’s northern border on Tuesday. No one was injured and no damage was caused by the shells.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/world/middleeast/syrian-shells-land-near-israeli-border.html', 'snippet': 'Mortar shells launched from Syria landed in the Golan Heights near Israel’s northern border on Tuesday. No one was injured and no damage was caused by the shells.', 'lead_paragraph': 'JERUSALEM — Several mortar shells launched from Syria landed in the Golan Heights near Israel’s northern border on Tuesday morning, prompting the Israeli military to file a complaint with United Nations forces operating in the area.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Syrian Shells Land Near Israeli Border', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Syrian Shells Land Near Israeli Border', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Golan Heights', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Israel', 'rank': 3, 'major':