/2012/09/25/turkeys-military-politics/', 'snippet': 'A Turkish court’s conviction of 325 senior officers for plotting to overthrow the state offers no catharsis.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ISTANBUL — A Turkish court’s conviction on Friday of 325 senior military officers for plotting to overthrow the state has left me with mixed feelings. Like many Turks, I’ve been pondering a host of questions: Was this justice or revenge? Is this the final curtain on a military that for decades believed it knew better than the elected politicians? Or was the 21-month courtroom drama the latest show trial, manipulated by a government that itself entertains delusions of grandeur?', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Turkey’s Military Politics', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': "Coups D'Etat and Attempted Coups D'Etat", 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Defense and Military Forces', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Turkey', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T12:51:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Finkel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Finkel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3119aba3-c1b9-56fe-b940-01028324d38e', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3119aba3-c1b9-56fe-b940-01028324d38e'}, {'abstract': 'Using Lily Kwong as his 3-D model, the photographer Simon Cave imagines a fashion world that is anything but flat.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/fashion-the-next-dimension/', 'snippet': 'Using Lily Kwong as his 3-D model, the photographer Simon Cave imagines a fashion world that is anything but flat.', 'lead_paragraph': 'With Lily Kwong as his 3-D model, the photographer Simon Cave imagines a fashion world that is anything but flat. How close is this to becoming a reality? In an exclusivevideo, Cave takes The Moment behind the scenes of his epic shoot to get the full 360 degree view. And to experience the final results, go to sc-3d.com.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Fashion, the Next Dimension', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:00:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alix Browne', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alix', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Browne', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/edce1417-d38b-52a6-81ef-b1d1b5f3f4fe', 'word_count': 58, 'uri': 'nyt://article/edce1417-d38b-52a6-81ef-b1d1b5f3f4fe'}, {'abstract': 'Analysts say worker unrest in China has grown more common because employees are more aware of their rights, and yet have few outlets to challenge or negotiate with their employers, David Barboza and Keith Bradsher report in Tuesday’s New York Times.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/daily-report-riot-at-foxconn-factory-highlights-labor-unrest/', 'snippet': 'Analysts say worker unrest in China has grown more common because employees are more aware of their rights, and yet have few outlets to challenge or negotiate with their employers, David Barboza and Keith Bradsher report in Tuesday’s New York Time...', 'lead_paragraph': 'A riot at a factory in Taiyuan, China, late Sunday was noteworthy because the site is managed by Foxconn Technology, one of the world’s biggest electronics manufacturers and an important supplier to major Western technology companies, David Barboza and Keith Bradsher report in Tuesday’s New York Times. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbStandard-v5.png', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbLarge-v5.png', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Daily Report: Riot at Foxconn Factory Highlights Labor Unrest', 'kicker': 'Bits', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Factories and Manufacturing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Apple Inc', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Foxconn Technology', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:03:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/69c12b15-42df-58e9-ab41-8cc26b5afe04', 'word_count': 319, 'uri': 'nyt://article/69c12b15-42df-58e9-ab41-8cc26b5afe04'}, {'abstract': 'The long line of people complaining about the replacement officials is now more like an angry mob. Have we reached the tipping point?', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/leading-off-a-fiasco-to-end-the-referee-fiasco/', 'snippet': 'The long line of people complaining about the replacement officials is now more like an angry mob. Have we reached the tipping point?', 'lead_paragraph': 'To be sure, the conga line of complaining about the replacement referee fiasco had started before Monday night’s N.F.L. game got even ESPN’s Jon “Everyone’s Doing a Great Job!” Gruden all riled up. The line was headed right to the gilded — and now probably fortified — door of Commissioner Roger Goodell, whose baronial arrogance makes him so easy to blame for just about anything. But the ending to that dreadful game Monday night may be his crowning achievement.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leading Off: A Fiasco to End the Referee Fiasco?', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Belichick, Bill', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Goodell, Roger', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hamilton, Josh', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:06:33+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Lynn Zinser', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Lynn', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Zinser', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c049683-c8ce-5196-9d5c-762049e1bc42', 'word_count': 516, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c049683-c8ce-5196-9d5c-762049e1bc42'}, {'abstract': 'The government faces years of legal bills to defend Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac executives accused of misleading investors about subprime mortgages.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/adding-up-the-governments-legal-bills-for-fannie-and-freddie/', 'snippet': 'The government faces years of legal bills to defend Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac executives accused of misleading investors about subprime mortgages.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The government’s takeover of the mortgage giants Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac in 2008 may turn out to be a very expensive proposition.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Adding Up the Government’s Legal Bills for Fannie and Freddie', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Subprime Mortgage Crisis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Raines, Franklin D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Syron, Richard F', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal Home Loan Mortgage Corp (Freddie Mac)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Federal National Mortgage Assn (Fannie Mae)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:07:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Peter J. Henning', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Peter', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Henning', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9ffc3a96-8694-5ba8-86ea-357a516367ac', 'word_count': 984, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9ffc3a96-8694-5ba8-86ea-357a516367ac'}, {'abstract': 'Women receive what they need to ply a trade, including basic education, before qualifying for a loan.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/in-west-bengal-a-cash-free-microfinance-program-opens-doors-for-women/', 'snippet': 'Women receive what they need to ply a trade, including basic education, before qualifying for a loan.', 'lead_paragraph': 'CANNING, West Bengal — A debt crisis in India’s microfinance sector in Andhra Pradesh in 2010 revived the question of how to help the hard-core poor without forcing them into a debt trap. Now it appears that microfinance institutions may have had an alternative all along.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In West Bengal, Cashless Microfinance Opens Doors for Women', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Microfinance', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'West Bengal (India)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:08:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sonia Faleiro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sonia', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Faleiro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e6892ddb-f2bf-5401-94dc-6d6773df062b', 'word_count': 989, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e6892ddb-f2bf-5401-94dc-6d6773df062b'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: Anthony Bologna gave Occupy Wall Street a mighty jump start last Sept. 24 when he blasted protesters with pepper spray. But have its people paid tribute to the inspector?', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/no-thank-you-note-but-several-lawsuits-for-a-pepper-spraying-inspector/', 'snippet': 'The Day: Anthony Bologna gave Occupy Wall Street a mighty jump start last Sept. 24 when he blasted protesters with pepper spray. But have its people paid tribute to the inspector?', 'lead_paragraph': ' Never mind the celebratory demonstrations last week. This Monday was the important anniversary for Occupy Wall Street. On that day a year ago, the movement truly kicked into gear. It owed an enormous debt to a police deputy inspector named Anthony Bologna, who has received not a word of thanks for having put it on the map.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'No Thank-You Note, but Several Lawsuits for a Pepper-Spraying Inspector', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests and Riots', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Suits and Litigation', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bologna, Anthony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Occupy Wall Street', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Police Department (NYC)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:08:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/4e92b5d4-88f0-5138-9da9-850c79b26965', 'word_count': 747, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4e92b5d4-88f0-5138-9da9-850c79b26965'}, {'abstract': 'The housing market is still gathering strength, new data showed, and the gains appear to be spreading even to the cheapest homes in many cities.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/business/home-prices-climb-again.html', 'snippet': 'The housing market is still gathering strength, new data showed, and the gains appear to be spreading even to the cheapest homes in many cities.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The housing market continues to gather strength, and the biggest gains in price now appear to be among the least expensive homes, whose values fell the most in the downturn and have weighed against any would-be recovery.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/business/HOUSING/HOUSING-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 370, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/26/business/HOUSING/HOUSING-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 370}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/business/HOUSING/HOUSING-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/business/HOUSING/HOUSING-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1366, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Prices Rise Again, This Time on the Low End', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Home Prices Rise Again, This Time on the Low End', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "Standard & Poor's Case-Shiller Home Price Index", 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Real Estate and Housing (Residential)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:11:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Shaila Dewan and Nelson D. Schwartz', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Shaila', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dewan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Nelson', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6e7210e3-ec22-5bc9-8d7c-8907c5579747', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6e7210e3-ec22-5bc9-8d7c-8907c5579747'}, {'abstract': 'Also, word of worker unrest at China was spread by social media; and the owner of Deadline, an online Hollywood news site, is the leading contender to buy Variety.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/the-breakfast-meeting-its-showtimes-time-and-n-f-l-s-monday-night-hangover/', 'snippet': 'Also, word of worker unrest at China was spread by social media; and the owner of Deadline, an online Hollywood news site, is the leading contender to buy Variety.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Emmy awards won by the tense, terrorism-centric Showtime series “Homeland” could not have come at a better time for the network, or the series, Brian Stelter writes. The awards — including best drama, best actress (Claire Danes), best actor (Damian Lewis) — represent a coming of age for the network, which had never received a series prize for comedy or drama and has long been in the shadow of the Emmy-award-laden HBO. With the second season of “Homeland” beginning on Sunday, the subscription network was immediately setting out to capitalize on the wins. “I think there might be some extra activity at the call centers this week,” said David Nevins, president for entertainment at Showtime.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: It’s Showtime’s Time, and N.F.L.’s Monday Night Hangover', 'kicker': 'Media Decoder', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:28:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Media', 'byline': {'original': 'By Noam Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Noam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6b339284-51e6-569d-82a4-e5da83622312', 'word_count': 436, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6b339284-51e6-569d-82a4-e5da83622312'}, {'abstract': 'Researchers had thought that five wild populations of greenback cutthroat trout were out there.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/rare-trout-survives-in-just-one-stream-dna-reveals/', 'snippet': 'Researchers had thought that five wild populations of greenback cutthroat trout were out there.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The rare greenback cutthroat trout, Colorado’s state fish, is even more imperiled than scientists thought, a new study suggests. By analyzing DNA sampled from cutthroat trout specimens pickled in ethanol for 150 years, comparing it with the genes of today’s cutthroat populations, and cross-referencing more than 40,000 historic stocking records, researchers in Colorado and Australia have revealed that the fish survives not in five wild populations, but just one.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Rare Trout Survives in Just One Stream, DNA Reveals', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Rogers, Kevin', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Bureau of Land Management', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'California Academy of Sciences', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Center for Biological Diversity', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Fish and Wildlife Service', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Park Service', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'University of Colorado', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Colorado', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:40:25+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By Josie Garthwaite', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Josie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Garthwaite', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/06f0d573-ad8d-5fc2-8733-5bca3c344845', 'word_count': 1006, 'uri': 'nyt://article/06f0d573-ad8d-5fc2-8733-5bca3c344845'}, {'abstract': 'The late calls by replacement officials in Monday night’s N.F.L. game, on the final play especially, marred an otherwise magical finish and struck at the very integrity of the sport.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/sports/football/absurd-ending-fuels-disgust-with-replacement-refs.html', 'snippet': 'The late calls by replacement officials in Monday night’s N.F.L. game, on the final play especially, marred an otherwise magical finish and struck at the very integrity of the sport.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SEATTLE — The play that best defines this N.F.L. season occurred at the end of another game in which replacement officials looked less like actual referees and more like the Keystone Kops. It was bizarre enough to almost defy description.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 345, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 345}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 759, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1517, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/sports/football/26onfootball/26onfootball-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Absurd Ending Fuels Disgust With Replacement Refs', 'kicker': 'On Pro Football', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Refs Turn Hail Mary Pass Into OMGs', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:46:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Greg Bishop', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Greg', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Bishop', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d683997-8a50-5d30-8755-51260e15d64c', 'word_count': 1032, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d683997-8a50-5d30-8755-51260e15d64c'}, {'abstract': 'The report urges the United States to “conduct a fundamental re-evaluation of current targeted killing practices” in Pakistan including “short- and long-term costs and benefits.”', 'web_url': 'https://atwar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/report-cites-high-civilian-toll-in-pakistan-drone-strikes/', 'snippet': 'The report urges the United States to “conduct a fundamental re-evaluation of current targeted killing practices” in Pakistan including “short- and long-term costs and benefits.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'A new report on targeted killing by C.I.A. drones in Pakistan’s tribal area concludes that the strikes have killed more civilians than American officials have acknowledged, alienated Pakistani public opinion and set a dangerous precedent under international law.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Report Cites High Civilian Toll in Pakistan Drone Strikes', 'kicker': 'At War', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Civilian Casualties', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Drones (Pilotless Planes)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Targeted Killings', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Pakistan', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:47:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Scott Shane', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Scott', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Shane', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6d56b142-444c-5326-a9db-be6cb414e952', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6d56b142-444c-5326-a9db-be6cb414e952'}, {'abstract': 'The musician recalls a time when the two camps sat at separate tables.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/can-vegans-and-carnivores-get-along-moby-thinks-so/', 'snippet': 'The musician recalls a time when the two camps sat at separate tables.', 'lead_paragraph': '“I’ve been a vegan for 24 years,” Moby, the musician, told me on the phone the other day. “With two lapses. I had yogurt in 1992, and I have to say, it was really good. And then I was at a friend’s restaurant in Portland, Oregon, a few years ago, and they made me some vegan sushi, but they accidentally brought me crab sushi. So I had a bite of crab sushi, and then my friend who ran the restaurant came running out yelling, like, ‘No! It’s got crab!’”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Can Vegans and Carnivores Get Along? Moby Thinks So', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veganism', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Moby', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T13:54:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jeff Gordinier', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jeff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gordinier', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ad7a0ad4-e364-536f-bfca-855f3a302033', 'word_count': 675, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ad7a0ad4-e364-536f-bfca-855f3a302033'}, {'abstract': 'A nonfiction project from Neil Patrick Harris, the Emmy-winning star of “How I Met Your Mother” and host of many an award show, will be published by Crown Archetype in spring 2014.', 'web_url': 'https://artsbeat.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/how-i-made-my-memoir-book-coming-from-neil-patrick-harris/', 'snippet': 'A nonfiction project from Neil Patrick Harris, the Emmy-winning star of “How I Met Your Mother” and host of many an award show, will be published by Crown Archetype in spring 2014.', 'lead_paragraph': 'By now Neil Patrick Harris’s bucket list must be short enough to fit on a Post-it note: host the Academy Awards; write a memoir; get the Criterion Collection to release a special cinephile edition of “Harold & Kumar Go to White Castle.” And he can cross one more item off that agenda: the Crown Archetype imprint of Random House’s Crown Publishing Group is expected to announce on Tuesday that it has acquired a new nonfiction book from Mr. Harris, the stage and screen performer; Emmy Award-winner; host of the Emmys, Tonys, TV Land Awards and Spike Video Game Awards; and star of CBS’s “How I Met Your Mother.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/arts/nph/nph-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 818, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/arts/nph/nph-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1636, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/arts/nph/nph-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/25/arts/nph/nph-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/arts/nph/nph-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'How I Made My Memoir: Book Coming From Neil Patrick Harris', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Harris, Neil Patrick', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Random House', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:00:15+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Dave Itzkoff', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Dave', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Itzkoff', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/159dc28a-6a9a-58a0-a42f-3f1509afde45', 'word_count': 266, 'uri': 'nyt://article/159dc28a-6a9a-58a0-a42f-3f1509afde45'}, {'abstract': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'web_url': 'https://dinersjournal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/what-were-reading-529/', 'snippet': 'A collection of links from the reporters and editors of the Dining section.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The New York Times: Spain, which has one of the world’s best cuisines, also has an unemployment rate of over 50 percent among young people, and many have resorted to foraging through the trash for food. —\xa0Emily Weinstein', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What We’re Reading', 'kicker': 'Diner&#039;s Journal', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/285bd525-da99-5f72-8ec2-3c7228faaa34', 'word_count': 470, 'uri': 'nyt://article/285bd525-da99-5f72-8ec2-3c7228faaa34'}, {'abstract': 'A few well-curated itineraries show off the more tasteful side of the holiday experience.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/in-munich-classing-up-oktoberfest/', 'snippet': 'A few well-curated itineraries show off the more tasteful side of the holiday experience.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Ah, autumn. That traditional time of pigskin, piping hot pretzels, copious quantities of beer and blitzed bonhomie. No, we’re not talking about college football season. We’re talking about Oktoberfest, another fall tradition that’s not exactly known for its high quotient of class. There you are, sitting cheek-by-jowl with a bunch of lederhosen-ed Bavarians — or, more likely, stag-partying Britons and study-abroad Americans — on a wooden bench in a frigid tent in some biergarten in the middle of Munich, slogging through steins and trying to hail the dirndl-clad beermaid (bierfrau?) to get another wurst. It’s the worst.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'In Munich, Classing Up Oktoberfest', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oktoberfest', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Munich (Germany)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:01:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Sessa', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sessa', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c228bdf-eb08-515f-8f43-268fb925e7d1', 'word_count': 387, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c228bdf-eb08-515f-8f43-268fb925e7d1'}, {'abstract': 'The secretary general, Ban Ki-moon, said conflicts roiling the Middle East were a major concern.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/world/un-leader-opens-general-assembly-on-somber-note.html', 'snippet': 'The secretary general, Ban Ki-moon, said conflicts roiling the Middle East were a major concern.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The leader of the United Nations formally opened its annual General Assembly on Tuesday with a speech conveying a broad sense of crisis because of pressing problems including armed conflict in the Middle East, disruptive climate change and religious intolerance.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/world/26nations-image1/26nations-image1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 688, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/world/26nations-image1/26nations-image1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1377, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/world/26nations-image1/26nations-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/26/world/26nations-image1/26nations-image1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/world/26nations-image1/26nations-image1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'U.N. Leader Opens General Assembly on Somber Note', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'International Relations', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Assembly (UN)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ban Ki-moon', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Middle East', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:03:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Neil MacFarquhar', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Neil', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'MacFarquhar', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/64d46f05-6d60-5e46-a61c-5db060c42a7a', 'word_count': 509, 'uri': 'nyt://article/64d46f05-6d60-5e46-a61c-5db060c42a7a'}, {'abstract': 'Should high schools provide access to emergency contraception to students?', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/new-york-city-schools-provide-morning-after-pills/', 'snippet': 'Should high schools provide access to emergency contraception to students?', 'lead_paragraph': 'In 13 New York City schools, the morning-after pill is available to students without parental permission, although parents are given the opportunity to opt their teenagers out of the health program (and the provision of any contraceptives) at the beginning of the school year.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'New York City Schools Provide Morning-After Pills', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Birth Control and Family Planning', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (K-12)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Plan B (Contraceptive)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenage Pregnancy', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Teenagers and Adolescence', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:05:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Style', 'byline': {'original': "By Kj Dell'Antonia", 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kj', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': "Dell'Antonia", 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/127151b0-f216-5f1c-9928-5198da358b9e', 'word_count': 372, 'uri': 'nyt://article/127151b0-f216-5f1c-9928-5198da358b9e'}, {'abstract': 'Trampoline injuries appear to be declining, but the American Academy of Pediatrics continues to advise against recreational trampoline use.', 'web_url': 'https://well.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/trampoline-injuries-down-but-caution-urged/', 'snippet': 'Trampoline injuries appear to be declining, but the American Academy of Pediatrics continues to advise against recreational trampoline use.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Trampoline injuries, which rose steadily through the middle of the last decade, appear to be on the downswing, according to a new study in the journal Pediatrics that looked at trampoline-related emergency room visits. But the drop in injuries appears to be a result of a decrease in trampoline sales rather than greater awareness of the dangers of trampolines and safer use, the authors said, and the American Academy of Pediatrics continues to advise against recreational trampoline use, a position it has taken since 1977.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/health/25well_trampoline/25well_trampoline-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Trampoline Injuries Down, but Caution Is Urged', 'kicker': 'Well', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Accidents and Safety', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Trampolines', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pediatrics (Journal)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:06:24+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Health', 'byline': {'original': 'By Laura Geggel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Laura', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Geggel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/1a4c4911-704c-546a-84d5-b5ad6b51bb59', 'word_count': 593, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1a4c4911-704c-546a-84d5-b5ad6b51bb59'}, {'abstract': 'The Rev. John C. Flynn, who was revered as an advocate for neighborhoods and people in South Bronx, died on Monday at 83.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/as-the-peoples-priest-he-fought-for-the-forgotten-of-the-bronx/', 'snippet': 'The Rev. John C. Flynn, who was revered as an advocate for neighborhoods and people in South Bronx, died on Monday at 83.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Rev. John C. Flynn could have been a monsignor, but as he told the story in later years, he refused the elevation because he already held a title more to his liking: the people’s priest.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '21', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/25flynn1-cityroom/25flynn1-cityroom-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/25flynn1-cityroom/25flynn1-cityroom-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/25flynn1-cityroom/25flynn1-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/25flynn1-cityroom/25flynn1-cityroom-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/25/nyregion/25flynn1-cityroom/25flynn1-cityroom-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'As the People’s Priest, He Fought for the Forgotten of the Bronx', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As the People’s Priest, He Fought for the Forgotten of the Bronx', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Affordable Housing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Flynn, John C', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'South Bronx (NYC)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:15:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Winnie Hu', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Winnie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hu', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a8da720d-149b-5304-9886-85864bffce9c', 'word_count': 902, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a8da720d-149b-5304-9886-85864bffce9c'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn Book Festival, which ended Sunday, was a showcase for the many small presses that have sprouted in Brooklyn.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/video/business/100000001803210/small-presses-take-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn Book Festival, which ended Sunday, was a showcase for the many small presses that have sprouted in Brooklyn.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn Book Festival, which ended Sunday, was a showcase for the many small presses that have sprouted in Brooklyn.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/business/video-smallpress/video-smallpress-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 338, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/24/business/video-smallpress/video-smallpress-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 338}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/business/video-smallpress/video-smallpress-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/24/business/video-smallpress/video-smallpress-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Small Presses Take Brooklyn', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Book Trade and Publishing', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:17:59+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'Erik Olsen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Erik', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Olsen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Video', '_id': 'nyt://video/c696f300-9111-5586-ba58-2c3e0dca42df', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://video/c696f300-9111-5586-ba58-2c3e0dca42df'}, {'abstract': 'One sports betting Web site was refunding wagers on the Packers, who appeared to be robbed of a win last night against the Seahawks by a ruling from replacement officials.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/sports/football/some-bettors-get-relief-on-game-decided-by-botched-call.html', 'snippet': 'One sports betting Web site was refunding wagers on the Packers, who appeared to be robbed of a win last night against the Seahawks by a ruling from replacement officials.', 'lead_paragraph': 'While it remains to be seen what, if anything, Commissioner Roger Goodell and the N.F.L. are going to do about the controversial ending to the game Monday night between the Seattle Seahawks and the Green Bay Packers, the gambling world — which was boosted by the replacement referees’ decision on the game’s final play, as significant money was bet on the Packers — appears to be showing at least a little compassion.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Some Bettors Get Relief on Game Decided by Botched Call', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Missed Call Lets Some Sports Books Show Pity', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Officiating (Sports)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gambling', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Seattle Seahawks', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Green Bay Packers', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'National Football League', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T14:23:47+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sam Borden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Borden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/300b1db9-cc66-5c04-bbfd-1d10d7a9dce3', 'word_count': 547, 'uri': 'nyt://article/300b1db9-cc66-5c04-bbfd-1d10d7a9dce3'}, {'abstract': 'When shown alongside the track records of other financial crises, the American job losses caused by the recent financial crisis don’t look so terrible.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/comparing-the-job-losses-in-financial-crises/', 'snippet': 'When shown alongside the track records of other financial crises, the American job losses caused by the recent financial crisis don’t look so terrible.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For a while I was regularly updating a chart each month showing how far employment plummeted in the latest recession and how little ground has been recovered since the recovery began in June 2009. Compared with other recent recessions and recoveries, the last few years have looked especially disastrous:', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Comparing the Job Losses in Financial Crises', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Recession and Depression', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'nam