pen councils’ accounts to auditors.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ROME — The conservative party of Italy’s former prime minister, Silvio Berlusconi, announced a series of housecleaning measures on Tuesday following a financing scandal that prompted the resignation of the party’s representative in the important Lazio region on Monday. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'As Scandal Grows, Party of Berlusconi Cleans House', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'As Scandal Grows, Party Of Berlusconi Cleans House', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Polverini, Renata', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'People of Liberty Party (Italy)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Berlusconi, Silvio', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Italy', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Embezzlement', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T20:02:58+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Elisabetta Povoledo', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Elisabetta', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Povoledo', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/72407305-b8db-54b2-afa8-22daed51a190', 'word_count': 577, 'uri': 'nyt://article/72407305-b8db-54b2-afa8-22daed51a190'}, {'abstract': 'A new party service employs servers with buckets of oysters on ice rather than a stationary raw bar.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/dining/oysters-party-like-its-the-40s.html', 'snippet': 'A new party service employs servers with buckets of oysters on ice rather than a stationary raw bar.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Rifko Meier, a Dutch art dealer and entrepreneur, created the Oyster Girls, a clever idea for parties that met with some success in his native Amsterdam. Now he’s brought them to New York. In place of a stationary raw bar, servers with buckets of oysters on ice hung around their waists circulate in the crowd and open shellfish on the spot for guests. They are also equipped with little containers of various sauces and a pail for shells. Each server can handle 100 oysters, Mr. Meier said. ', 'print_section': 'D', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 350, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 350}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/dining/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN/26STUFF_OYSTERSPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Oysters Party Like It’s the ’40s', 'kicker': 'Food Stuff ', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Oysters Party Like It’s the ’40s', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parties (Social)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Meier, Rifko', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Oysters', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T20:03:34+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Dining', 'section_name': 'Food', 'byline': {'original': 'By Florence Fabricant', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Florence', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Fabricant', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/28003c5e-8300-5ef6-99bc-84d1e5664e81', 'word_count': 153, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28003c5e-8300-5ef6-99bc-84d1e5664e81'}, {'abstract': 'Three very different new releases have captured our attention this fall.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/bookshelf-26/', 'snippet': 'Three very different new releases have captured our attention this fall.', 'lead_paragraph': '“The Phaidon Archive of Graphic Design” (Phaidon, $235) is the commercial art canon embodied: 500 topic sheets on everything from a 700-year-old Buddhist text to the album art of Beck’s “The Information.” “Thanks for the View, Mr. Mies” (Metropolis Books, $30) explores how Modernist architecture improves lives in the Lafayette Park section of Detroit, which has the world’s largest concentration of Mies van der Rohe buildings. (It’s also one of the most racially integrated neighborhoods in what might well be America’s most segregated city.) Peter Trachtenberg’s memoir “Another Insane Devotion” (Da Capo, $24) — about love, sex, marriage and especially cats — is a hallmark of brainy discursiveness, set into motion when the author loses Biscuit, a wan cat whose sniffles sound like “a whisk broom briskly sweeping.”', 'print_section': 'M2', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Bookshelf', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Bookshelf', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T20:04:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Heyman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Heyman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9b747baa-7bf3-5022-bac0-042f733b58d7', 'word_count': 128, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9b747baa-7bf3-5022-bac0-042f733b58d7'}, {'abstract': 'Thousands besieged Parliament as Spain’s two largest regions posed challenges to the country’s leadership.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/world/europe/protests-continue-in-spain.html', 'snippet': 'Thousands besieged Parliament as Spain’s two largest regions posed challenges to the country’s leadership.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MADRID — The pressures facing the government of Prime Minister Mariano Rajoy mounted on several fronts on Tuesday, as thousands of demonstrators besieged Parliament and Spain’s two largest regions took steps that underscored their deepening economic troubles and displeasure with his austerity plans. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '4', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/world/SPAIN-3/SPAIN-3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 643, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'j