,” follows a recent college graduate who finds that being a Caucasian in China can be a good thing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Over the centuries China has given the world many treasures: Confucianism, pandas, table tennis, a superhumanly synchronized Olympics opening ceremony, pretty much everything ever sold at Walmart. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/theater/26rent/26rent-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 576, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/theater/26rent/26rent-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1152, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Musical in Celebration of Saving Face in China, That Booming New Market', 'kicker': 'Theater Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Musical in Celebration of Saving Face in China, That Booming New Market', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T20:49:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Theater', 'subsection_name': 'Theater Reviews', 'byline': {'original': 'By Catherine Rampell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Catherine', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rampell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/6cb4df74-5e2f-5e02-aaa4-15b471db1249', 'word_count': 287, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6cb4df74-5e2f-5e02-aaa4-15b471db1249'}, {'abstract': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college, explains how the 2012 presidential election could affect college applicants.', 'web_url': 'https://thechoice.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/25/guidance-office-what-the-election-means-for-college-applicants/', 'snippet': 'Mark Kantrowitz, an expert on paying for college, explains how the 2012 presidential election could affect college applicants.', 'lead_paragraph': 'If you’re a college-bound student who has been (loosely) following the 2012 presidential campaign, this is an excellent time to tune in (and catch up).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/education/choice-kantrowitz/choice-kantrowitz-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/education/choice-kantrowitz/choice-kantrowitz-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/24/education/choice-kantrowitz/choice-kantrowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/24/education/choice-kantrowitz/choice-kantrowitz-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Presidential Candidate Is Better for College Applicants?', 'kicker': 'The Choice', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Colleges and Universities', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Financial Aid (Education)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Pell Grants', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Presidential Election of 2012', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Student Loans', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kantrowitz, Mark', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Romney, Mitt', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-25T20:57:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tanya Abrams', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tanya', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Abrams', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/0fcfb83e-3ee2-50cc-aeff-cff2c95f97a1', 'word_count': 1997, 'uri': 'nyt://article/0fcfb83e-3ee2-50cc-aeff-cff2c95f97a1'}, {'abstract': 'There have been plenty of calls by the N.F.L. replacement officials this season that puzzled fans, coaches and players, but five stand out.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/sports/football/five-other-head-scratching-calls-by-replacement-officials.html', 'snippet': 'There have been plenty of calls by the N.F.L. replacement officials this season that puzzled fans, coaches and players, but five stand out.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The first three weeks of the N.F.L. season have been defined not by gripping games or outstanding performances, but by the performance of the league’s replacement officials, who many say are turning the sport into a mockery. No calls brought more attention and embarrassment than the ones in the debacle Monday night in Seattle, where the officials awarded the Seahawks a game-winning touchdown as time ran out against Green Bay, despite Golden Tate’s interfering with a Packers defender and then another Packers defender seeming to have initial possession of the ball. But there are plenty of others that have prompted a head scratch — or seven. Here are five that stand out. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New