le': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/176cee9f-27cf-5bc2-82a6-98c00e207f23', 'word_count': 782, 'uri': 'nyt://article/176cee9f-27cf-5bc2-82a6-98c00e207f23'}, {'abstract': 'A regional court handed down the first guilty verdicts in a case that exposed the systematic torture of suspects in a police department in the capital of the Tatarstan Republic.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/world/europe/russia-2-police-officers-found-guilty-in-kazan-brutality-case.html', 'snippet': 'A regional court handed down the first guilty verdicts in a case that exposed the systematic torture of suspects in a police department in the capital of the Tatarstan Republic.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A regional court handed down the first guilty verdicts on Tuesday in a police brutality case that exposed the systematic torture of suspects in a police department in Kazan, the capital of the Tatarstan Republic. A judge said two officers, Ilshat Garifullin and Ramil Nigshatzyanov, illegally arrested Sergei Nazarov of Kazan, accused of stealing a cellphone. Mr. Nazarov later died. Four other officers are accused of sodomizing him with a Champagne bottle in an interrogation. Mr. Garifullin was sentenced to two and a half years in prison, and Mr. Nigshatzyanov to two years. The four officers charged in the assault have yet to stand trial. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Russia: 2 Police Officers Found Guilty in Police Brutality Case in Kazan', 'kicker': 'World Briefing | Europe', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Russia: 2 Police Officers Found Guilty In Police Brutality Case in Kazan', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Russia', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Police Brutality and Misconduct', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Decisions and Verdicts', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tatarstan (Russia)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T00:30:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Europe', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andrew Roth', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andrew', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Roth', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Brief', '_id': 'nyt://article/51fed8ec-b952-5031-a961-fadb650533e6', 'word_count': 105, 'uri': 'nyt://article/51fed8ec-b952-5031-a961-fadb650533e6'}, {'abstract': 'The replacement of the company’s chief finance officer, Tim Morse, who had a history of cost-cutting, with Ken Goldman of Fortinet could indicate that Yahoo is ready to expand.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/technology/yahoos-choice-of-new-chief-financial-officer-suggests-a-plan-for-deals.html', 'snippet': 'The replacement of the company’s chief finance officer, Tim Morse, who had a history of cost-cutting, with Ken Goldman of Fortinet could indicate that Yahoo is ready to expand.', 'lead_paragraph': 'SAN FRANCISCO — As Yahoo’s newly installed chief executive, Marissa Mayer has given employees free cafeteria food, replaced their BlackBerry phones with iPhone and Android smartphones and closed a long-awaited deal with Alibaba that gives Yahoo $625 million. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/business/YAHOO/YAHOO-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 682, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/business/YAHOO/YAHOO-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1768, 'width': 1178, '