ub_date': '2012-09-26T10:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Andy Benoit', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Andy', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Benoit', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a240831-72e1-5d9e-a878-10352d773d67', 'word_count': 963, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a240831-72e1-5d9e-a878-10352d773d67'}, {'abstract': 'Redistributive public policy not only depressed the labor market but changed the composition of the work force so that it includes more people who are married and more skilled, an economist writes.', 'web_url': 'https://economix.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/labor-market-scars-left-by-redistributive-public-policy/', 'snippet': 'Redistributive public policy not only depressed the labor market but changed the composition of the work force so that it includes more people who are married and more skilled, an economist writes.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Casey B. Mulligan is an economics professor at the University of Chicago.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Labor-Market Scars Left by Redistributive Public Policy', 'kicker': 'Economix', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Keynes, John Maynard', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T10:00:44+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Casey B. Mulligan', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Casey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b12eb853-d2eb-5b91-807e-f038add0a3eb', 'word_count': 889, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b12eb853-d2eb-5b91-807e-f038add0a3eb'}, {'abstract': 'What the director of ‘Looper’ learned from ‘Macbeth.’', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/rian-johnson-builds-a-better-time-machine.html', 'snippet': 'What the director of ‘Looper’ learned from ‘Macbeth.’', 'lead_paragraph': '“Looper” is a film about fate, regret, decisions, revenge and the old maxim that the child is the father of the man, but mostly it’s about time travel, so let’s travel back in time: to 10 years ago, before the director Rian Johnson’s celebrated 2005 debut film, “Brick” (or his follow-up, “The Brothers Bloom”), to the moment he first had the idea for “Looper.” Back then, it was conceived as a short film, about 10 minutes long, which he never actually shot. But the script would eventually provide the opening voice-over for “Looper”: “Time travel has not yet been invented. But 30 years from now, it will have been.”', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '40', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 784, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 784}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 767, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1124, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30looper/30looper-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rian Johnson Builds a Better Time Machine', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rian Johnson Builds a Better Time Machine', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willis, Bruce', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Looper (Movie)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Rian', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon-Levitt, Joseph', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:09+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Adam Sternbergh', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Adam', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sternbergh', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f0a080b0-a555-5f72-8d94-0f6f53344bca', 'word_count': 1108, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f0a080b0-a555-5f72-8d94-0f6f53344bca'}, {'abstract': 'Howard Gordon and Alex Gansa discuss the influence of Chicken Little.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/the-creators-of-homeland-exorcise-the-ghost-of-24.html', 'snippet': 'Howard Gordon and Alex Gansa discuss the influence of Chicken Little.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Homeland,” the geopolitically astute spy thriller on Showtime, features three bedeviled protagonists: Carrie Mathison (Claire Danes), a brilliant and bipolar C.I.A. agent; Saul Berenson (Mandy Patinkin), her mentor; and Nicholas Brody (Damian Lewis), a former prisoner of war who tried (and failed) to assassinate the vice president. Much of what made “Homeland” successful in its first season was the artful way in which the show plucked from real-time history, a trick that the show’s creators, Howard Gordon and Alex Gansa, honed while working together on “24.” We talked to them about the challenges of writing a show that so closely mirrors the real-world shadow wars. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '37', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 371, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 371}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 883, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 568, 'width': 883, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30homeland/30homeland-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Creators of ‘Homeland’ Exorcise the Ghost of ‘24’', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Creators of ‘Homeland’: Exorcise the Ghost of ‘24’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Homeland (TV Program)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Showtime', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gansa, Alex', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon, Howard', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:10+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'Interview by Willa Paskin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Interview', 'middlename': 'Willa', 'lastname': 'Paskin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/852f59a5-dd06-556a-a50e-e31502f97bfb', 'word_count': 769, 'uri': 'nyt://article/852f59a5-dd06-556a-a50e-e31502f97bfb'}, {'abstract': 'The ‘Last Resort’ star shares his sea-captain reading list.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/andre-braugher-last-resort.html', 'snippet': 'The ‘Last Resort’ star shares his sea-captain reading list.', 'lead_paragraph': 'How exactly do you prepare to play the defiant captain of a nuclear submarine who goes rogue? If you’re Andre Braugher, you read. “I got a stack of books and tried to figure out who sea captains are, what life on the sea is like and what goes into the making of a captain who can endure this kind of exile,” he says of preparing for “Last Resort,” an ABC series about a sub captain who commandeers a tropical island. “It’s an apprenticeship by proxy.” Should you ever find yourself in a similar position, here is Braugher’s recommended reading list:', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '44', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 683}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 797, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1167, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30braugher/30braugher-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Andre Braugher Has a Homework Assignment for You', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Andre Braugher Has a Homework Assignment for You', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Submarines and Submersibles', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Braugher, Andre', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Last Resort (TV Program)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/091d23e0-0a18-5dc6-be0e-a39ba18d779a', 'word_count': 228, 'uri': 'nyt://article/091d23e0-0a18-5dc6-be0e-a39ba18d779a'}, {'abstract': 'The French art film that’s spawned more blockbusters than any comic book.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/la-jetee.html', 'snippet': 'The French art film that’s spawned more blockbusters than any comic book.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Chris Marker, who died earlier this year at 91, was an enigmatic figure, reluctant to be photographed and prone to biographical embellishment. A creature of the European postwar avant-garde, Marker would seem an unlikely influence on modern Hollywood. But his 28-minute “photo-novel,” “La Jetée” — a shuffle of mostly still black-and-white images now marking its 50th birthday — has spawned more would-be blockbusters than any comic book or series of fantasy novels.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '41', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 427, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 427}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 729, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 823, 'width': 1156, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo1/30argo1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'What ‘Back to the Future’ and ‘Terminator’ Owe to ‘La Jetée’', 'kicker': 'The Inspiration Issue', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '‘La Jetée’', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Marker, Chris', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9a18929f-c4d0-50ef-a15c-c15347e13230', 'word_count': 296, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9a18929f-c4d0-50ef-a15c-c15347e13230'}, {'abstract': 'The military has responded to the mental health crisis among veterans with more treatment, including alternative therapies and mindfulness training.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/for-veterans-a-surge-of-new-treatments-for-trauma/', 'snippet': 'The military has responded to the mental health crisis among veterans with more treatment, including alternative therapies and mindfulness training.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Suicide is now the leading cause of death in the army. More soldiers die by suicide than in combat or vehicle accidents, and rates are rising: July, with 38 suicides among active duty and reserve soldiers, was the worst month since the Army began counting. General Lloyd Austin III, the army’s second in command, called suicide “the worst enemy I have faced in my 37 years in the army.” This Thursday, the Army is calling a “Suicide Stand-Down.” All units will devote the day to suicide prevention.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Veterans, a Surge of New Treatments for Trauma', 'kicker': 'Fixes', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mental Health and Disorders', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Traumatic Brain Injury', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Veterans', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Veterans Affairs Department', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Tina Rosenberg', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Tina', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rosenberg', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5d519052-1021-5754-aa95-d053ea2a06cd', 'word_count': 2225, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5d519052-1021-5754-aa95-d053ea2a06cd'}, {'abstract': 'Based on sheer loyalty, most entrepreneurs are inclined to try to find a way to make things work. That’s almost always a mistake.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/what-do-you-do-with-the-brilliant-jerk/', 'snippet': 'Based on sheer loyalty, most entrepreneurs are inclined to try to find a way to make things work. That’s almost always a mistake.', 'lead_paragraph': 'My most recent experience with a Brilliant Jerk occurred when I was helping a group of 25 doctors thrash out their shared values as they tried to become a real company.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'What Do You Do With the Brilliant Jerk?', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Entrepreneurship', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Small Business', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Start-ups', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T11:00:55+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Cliff Oxford', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Cliff', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Oxford', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/00d7be7a-59ee-53f3-8933-17f28929eb66', 'word_count': 1158, 'uri': 'nyt://article/00d7be7a-59ee-53f3-8933-17f28929eb66'}, {'abstract': 'Chicago’s River North’s transformation is extending to one of the neighborhood’s most storied — and decidedly low-tech — commercial addresses. The Merchandise Mart, a Depression-era behemoth of limestone, concrete and steel that has long been synonymous with fabric bolts and furniture, is becoming a destination for the city’s digital set.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/daily-report-relic-of-an-era-revitalized-for-tech-start-ups/', 'snippet': 'Chicago’s River North’s transformation is extending to one of the neighborhood’s most storied — and decidedly low-tech — commercial addresses. The Merchandise Mart, a Depression-era behemoth of limestone, concrete and steel that has long been syno...', 'lead_paragraph': 'Once a dormant area of empty warehouses, the River North section of Chicago has evolved into a nexus of dining, nightlife and, most recently, an aspiring rival to Silicon Valley. Its 45 square blocks are home to the headquarters of Groupon, the Chicago offices of Google and several hundred technology start-ups.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-articleLarge-v4.png', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 400}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-jumbo-v6.jpg', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-superJumbo-v3.png', 'height': 400, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/11/30/technology/bits-daily-report/bits-daily-report-thumbSt