f_material': 'Slideshow', '_id': 'nyt://slideshow/31eff0f4-d604-54a9-87ab-9a69c2588205', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://slideshow/31eff0f4-d604-54a9-87ab-9a69c2588205'}, {'abstract': 'When states fail to expand Medicaid for parents, there’s an unintended impact on children.', 'web_url': 'https://parenting.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/when-parents-cant-enroll-in-medicaid-children-stay-uninsured/', 'snippet': 'When states fail to expand Medicaid for parents, there’s an unintended impact on children.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Census Bureau just released new data on poverty, income and health insurance for last year, and the numbers aren’t pretty. Overall poverty remained at the highs we’ve seen for the past three years, with the picture still particularly bleak for children: 15 percent of Americans and 1 in 5 children live in poverty. Lack of insurance is closely tied to that number. Nearly 14 percent of children living in poverty lacked health insurance last year, compared with under 10 percent for children overall, a number that stayed flat despite the decrease in the rate for everyone under 65.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'When Parents Can’t Enroll in Medicaid, Children Stay Uninsured', 'kicker': 'Motherlode', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Children and Childhood', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Health Insurance and Managed Care', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Medicaid', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Parenting', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poverty', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': "State Children's Health Insurance Program (S-CHIP)", 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Urban Institute', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T15:41:10+0000', 'document_type': 'arti