ork Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'It’s Finally Pink on Top of Music Charts', 'kicker': 'ArtsBeat', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'It’s Finally Pink on Top of Chart', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Pink', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T21:36:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ben Sisario', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ben', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Sisario', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2a93c95a-40a2-539f-8d99-297d5112fa93', 'word_count': 318, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2a93c95a-40a2-539f-8d99-297d5112fa93'}, {'abstract': 'While some of the Internet-famous have moved to the movie or TV screen, others have found a niche — and sometimes a comfortable living — on Twitter, Tumblr or Instagram.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/technology/personaltech/internet-stars-find-an-audience-in-social-media-tool-kit.html', 'snippet': 'While some of the Internet-famous have moved to the movie or TV screen, others have found a niche — and sometimes a comfortable living — on Twitter, Tumblr or Instagram.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Social media have made it easier than ever to catapult from obscurity to prominence. In recent years, the Web has blossomed into a more egalitarian version of “American Idol,” where nobodies compete for attention alongside somebodies. ', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/business/27-TOOLKIT1/27-TOOLKIT1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/business/27-TOOLKIT1/27-TOOLKIT1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Finding Fame, and Sometimes Fortune, in Social Media', 'kicker': 'Tool Kit', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Finding Fame, and Sometimes Fortune, in Social Media', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Tumblr', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Computers and the Internet', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Twitter', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Pinterest', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Instagram Inc', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-26T21:43:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Technology', 'subsection_name': 'Personal Tech', 'byline': {'original': 'By Steven Leckart', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Steven', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Leckart', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a5f5bd13-a35c-5d3d-81ac-afbbc5a0ce53', 'word_count': 1307, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a5f5bd13-a35c-5d3d-81ac-afbbc5a0ce53'}, {'abstract': 'Snapshots of an old theater in Cuba: “a pastiche of railway cars, faded murals, cast iron and fantasy.”', 'web_url': 'https://6thfloor.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/26/the-reguero-theater-gets-its-close-up/', 'snippet': 'Snapshots of an old theater in Cuba: “a pastiche of railway cars, faded murals, cast iron and fantasy.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'We’ve received many comments from readers about the pictures by Andrew Moore\xa0that accompanied John Jeremiah Sullivan’s cover story\xa0about Cuba. One in particular, from Virgina Schofield, piqued our interest. Schofield, who is 73, grew up in Cuba and has a special connection to the Reguero Theater that Moore photographed in Morón (above). Her maternal grandfather, Miguel Reguero, spent his small inheritance to build that theater, and her mother occasionally played the piano in productions there. When the enterprise bankrupted Schofield’s grandfather, he moved the family to La Gloria, where her mother met Walton Schofield, whose parents were from England. “I always say my last name is Schofield because of that theater,” Schofield says, referring to the circumstances that led her parents to meet.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/magazine/26-moore-cuba-2/26-moore-cuba-2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 803, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/magazine/26-moore-cuba-2/26-moore-cuba-2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 941, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/magazine/26-moore-cuba-2/26-moore-cuba-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/26/magazine/26-moore-cuba-2/26-moore-cuba-2-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/26/magazine/26-moore-cuba-2/26-moore-cuba-2-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Reguero Theater Gets Its Close-Up', 'kicker': 'The 6th