', 'value': 'Black People', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Race and Ethnicity', 'rank': 8, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Los Angeles (Calif)', 'rank': 9, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T09:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Michael Chabon', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Michael', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Chabon', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/5c47a395-4230-5c30-9d45-a80f9bc06123', 'word_count': 2218, 'uri': 'nyt://article/5c47a395-4230-5c30-9d45-a80f9bc06123'}, {'abstract': 'The author of ‘Cloud Atlas’ celebrates the strange process of seeing his novel turned into a film.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/cloud-atlas.html', 'snippet': 'The author of ‘Cloud Atlas’ celebrates the strange process of seeing his novel turned into a film.', 'lead_paragraph': '“So how does it feel?” is the question you hear when your book completes the long ascent from production purgatory to movieplex. Well, first there’s a primal kick: actors speak dialogue you wrote years ago, and all those nonexistent people are now real. They find flashes of humor or menace you never spotted, and soon all memory of how you imagined the character before the actor muscled in is gone. ', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '58', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30lives/mag-30Lives-t_CA0-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30lives/mag-30Lives-t_CA0-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 600, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30lives/30lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30lives/30lives-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30lives/30lives-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Putting Words in Halle Berry’s Mouth', 'kicker': 'Lives', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Putting Words in Halle Berry’s Mouth', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Cloud Atlas (Movie)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T09:00:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By David Mitchell', 'person': [{'firstname': 'David', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Mitchell', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/d0e5ad3d-0d74-53e2-8a99-37f45c3fd0d4', 'word_count': 753, 'uri': 'nyt://article/d0e5ad3d-0d74-53e2-8a99-37f45c3fd0d4'}, {'abstract': '‘Call Me Maybe’ and its many interpreters, from Katy Perry to Cookie Monster', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/carly-rae-jepsen-call-me-maybe.html', 'snippet': '‘Call Me Maybe’ and its many interpreters, from Katy Perry to Cookie Monster', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before February, few people outside Canada had ever heard of a “Canadian Idol” runner-up named Carly Rae Jepsen. Then Justin Bieber filmed a homemade video to her song “Call Me Maybe,” which went viral and turned the song into the biggest hit of the summer. (Jepsen’s own rendition has received 260 million YouTube hits.) Here, the 26-year-old reflects on how it started.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '53', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae1-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 713}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 819, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1500, 'width': 1200, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30carlyrae1/30carlyrae1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Carly Rae Jepsen Writes a Song You Can’t Escape', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Carly Rae Jepsen Writes a Song You Can’t Escape', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jepsen, Carly Rae', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T09:00:36+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gaby Dunn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gaby', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dunn', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2164f544-fa81-5029-92ae-f4947502dcea', 'word_count': 335, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2164f544-fa81-5029-92ae-f4947502dcea'}, {'abstract': 'The Brooklyn-based artist faced a personal tragedy and found beauty.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/mickalene-thomas-rediscovers-her-mother-and-her-muse.html', 'snippet': 'The Brooklyn-based artist faced a personal tragedy and found beauty.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Brooklyn-based artist Mickalene Thomas has often used her mother, Sandra Bush, a former fashion model, as a muse going all the way back to the work she made as a Yale M.F.A. student a decade ago. Mama Bush, as her mother is known, has stood in for everything from a 1970s diva to a nude odalisque in some of Thomas’s exuberant, rhinestone-encrusted collage-paintings; in the 2009 video piece “Ain’t I a Woman (Sandra),” Bush vamps to Eartha Kitt in a bold red-and-black sweater with big shoulder pads.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '50', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30thomas1/30thomas1-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 720, 'width': 600, 'subType': '