aragraph': '“The idea came from desperation,” says Tony Mendez, a retired C.I.A. officer and the real-life inspiration for the character played by Ben Affleck in the coming movie “Argo.” As the head of the C.I.A.’s deceptively boring-sounding Graphics and Authentication Division, Mendez was responsible for foreign agents and their cover stories — which is why he was called upon in 1979 to construct a plan to evacuate six U.S. diplomats hiding at the Tehran homes of two Canadian diplomats.', 'print_section': 'MM', 'print_page': '41', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/magazine/30argo/30argo-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'The Spy Behind ‘Argo’ Out-Hollywoods Hollywood', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Spy Behind ‘Argo’ Out-Hollywoods Hollywood', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Argo (Movie)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mendez, Tony', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Central Intelligence Agency', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T09:00:42+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Magazine', 'section_name': 'Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mickey Rapkin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mickey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Rapkin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8c294493-7d95-506c-8275-7351cd36141a', 'word_count': 343, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8c294493-7d95-506c-8275-7351cd36141a'}, {'abstract': 'Bringing bleak spaghetti westerns to a drama of the antebellum South.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/magazine/quentin-tarantino-django.html', 'snippet': 'Bringing bleak spaghetti westerns to a drama of the antebellum South.', 'lead_paragraph': 'This year, Quentin Tarantino’s Christmas present to the world is “Django Unchained,” the violent story of a slave (Jamie Foxx) on a mission to free his wife (Kerry Washington) from the plantation of the man who owns her (Leonardo DiCaprio). Tarantino’s biggest influences for the film, he says, were not movies about American slavery but the spaghetti westerns of the Italian director Sergio Corbucci. Here Tarantino explains how Corbucci’s movies — including “Django,” which lent its name to Tarantino’s title character — became the inspiration for his own spaghetti southern. (Interview by Gavin Edwards.)', 'print_sect