percent in the spring, down from its previous estimate of 1.7 percent.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/business/economy/last-quarters-growth-is-revised-down-sharply.html', 'snippet': 'The United States government said the gross domestic product grew at an annual rate of 1.3 percent in the spring, down from its previous estimate of 1.7 percent.', 'lead_paragraph': 'WASHINGTON — The Commerce Department said Thursday that the United States economy grew at an annual pace of just 1.3 percent in the second quarter of the year, showing that the recovery came close to stalling in the spring. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 657, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1315, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/business/economy/28econ/28econ-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Last Quarter’s Growth Is Revised Down Sharply', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Last Quarter’s Growth Is Revised Down Sharply', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'United States Economy', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Gross Domestic Product', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Durable Goods (Economic Indicator)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T13:00:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Business', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Economy', 'byline': {'original': 'By Annie Lowrey', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Annie', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Lowrey', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/24daf2f3-d249-5015-a306-2ee858c29677', 'word_count': 691, 'uri': 'nyt://article/24daf2f3-d249-5015-a306-2ee858c29677'}, {'abstract': 'What a Keynesian perspective really says.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/notes-on-internal-devaluation-wonkish/', 'snippet': 'What a Keynesian perspective really says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Still in jet-lag city. Talking to people, and also reading what comes across the threshold, it occurs to me that there’s widespread misunderstanding of what a more or less Keynesian view of Europe’s problems actually implies. People seem to think that it means that (a) internal devaluation can never work (b) any sign of recovery, even a partial rebound, proves Keynes/Krugman wrong (this is a critical part of Baltic boosterism, where the partial recovery of Latvia and Estonia is supposed to be some kind of incredible triumph).', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Notes On Internal Devaluation (Wonkish)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T13:03:38+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/dd18023a-ed14-58fc-ba13-e55e42cc33c4', 'word_count': 267, 'uri': 'nyt://article/dd18023a-ed14-58fc-ba13-e55e42cc33c4'}, {'abstract': 'Britain and France are second-ranking powers that “punch above their weight,” in part because they have nuclear weapons. But some would like to cut the cost of this ticket to the top table without jeopardizing their world power status at the United Nations.', 'web_url': 'https://rendezvous.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/france-and-britain-weigh-the-price-of-nuclear-deterrence/', 'snippet': 'Britain and France are second-ranking powers that “punch above their weight,” in part because they have nuclear weapons. But some would like to cut the cost of this ticket to the top table without jeopardizing their world power status at the Unite...', 'lead_paragraph': 'LONDON — There were reports on Thursday of stirrings within the British military about the need to scale back the country’s nuclear deterrent in order to spend the money on confronting more conventional threats.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/blogs/rdv-unga/rdv-unga-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/blogs/rdv-unga/rdv-unga-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'France and Britain Weigh the Price of Nuclear Deterrence', 'kicker': 'IHT Rendezvous', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ARMS CONTROL AND LIMITATION AND DISARMAMENT', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Security Council (UN)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'France', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Great Britain', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T13:05:27+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'byline': {'original': 'By Harvey Morris', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Harvey', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Morris', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/39adabeb-c4ef-51e3-a393-7f2b8b7cdd6c', 'word_count': 687, 'uri': 'nyt://article/39adabeb-c4ef-51e3-a393-7f2b8b7cdd6c'}, {'abstract': 'The number of Syrians fleeing to neighboring countries for safety and aid is likely to exceed 700,000 by the end of the year, humanitarian agencies said.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/world/middleeast/un-says-syrian-refugee-numbers-are-surging.html', 'snippet': 'The number of Syrians fleeing to neighboring countries for safety and aid is likely to exceed 700,000 by the end of the year, humanitarian agencies said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GENEVA — The number of Syrians fleeing to neighboring countries for safety and aid is likely to exceed 700,000 by the end of the year, according to the United Nations and humanitarian agencies that are struggling to keep pace with the surge in numbers in recent weeks.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'U.N. Says Syrian Refugee Numbers Are Surging', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'U.N. Says Syrian Refugee Numbers Are Surging', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Syria', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Refugees and Displaced Persons', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T13:05:46+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Foreign', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Middle East', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nick Cumming-Bruce', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nick', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cumming-Bruce', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7ac4c040-dedb-5ace-9b8c-87e6523c72a8', 'word_count': 503, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7ac4c040-dedb-5ace-9b8c-87e6523c72a8'}, {'abstract': 'Two weeks out, polls show Henrique Capriles is in a dead-heat with President Hugo Chávez of Venezuela. Can Capriles pull off an upset?', 'web_url': 'https://latitude.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/capriles-in-dead-heat-with-chavez/', 'snippet': 'Two weeks out, polls show Henrique Capriles is in a dead-heat with President Hugo Chávez of Venezuela. Can Capriles pull off an upset?', 'lead_paragraph': 'MONTREAL — Even on TV, the energy of the crowd comes through. The opposition candidate Henrique Capriles Radonski is ripping into his stump speech, and the townspeople in the city of Maturin, in Eastern Venezuela, are eating it up: “Today,” he bellows, “I want you to think about your problems, and think: Has the government brought solutions to your problems?!”\xa0 The crowd shouts back: “Nooooo!”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Challenger', 'kicker': 'Latitude', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Capriles Radonski, Henrique', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Chavez, Hugo', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Venezuela', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T13:07:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Francisco Toro', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Francisco', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Toro', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/9fced62b-8282-5457-ac9d-4f0ee481c864', 'word_count': 643, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9fced62b-8282-5457-ac9d-4f0ee481c864'}, {'abstract': 'Also, David Pogue gets lost as he test drives the new Apple maps for the iPhone 5, and J.K. Rowling’s novel for adults is reviewed by Michiko Kakutani.', 'web_url': 'https://mediadecoder.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/the-breakfast-meeting-n-f-l-quickly-reverses-on-referees-and-satire-gone-wild/', 'snippet': 'Also, David Pogue gets lost as he test drives the new Apple maps for the iPhone 5, and J.K. Rowling’s novel for adults is reviewed by Michiko Kakutani.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The debacle of an ending to “Monday Night Football” on ESPN apparently was enough to propel a settlement Wednesday night to the lockout of N.F.L. referees, Judy Battista reported. Under the eight-year labor deal, pensions will remain in place for current officials through the 2016 season; new officials will get a 401(k) instead. The average official’s salary will rise to $173,000 in 2013 from $149,000 in 2011.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Breakfast Meeting: N.F.L. Quickly Reverses on Referees, and Satire 