 arundo, a high-energy crop, for biofuels production.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A biofuels center is promoting mass cultivation of arundo, a fast-growing field grass that yields three times as much ethanol per acre as corn, for a proposed biofuel refinery in eastern North Carolina. But some scientists and environmentalists argue that arundo is a noxious weed that could prove as invasive as kudzu. [The News and Observer]', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'On Our Radar: A Miracle Crop, or a Pest?', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T15:10:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Science', 'subsection_name': 'Environment', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e86cda4a-b688-5708-af1a-d614bf1736cd', 'word_count': 186, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e86cda4a-b688-5708-af1a-d614bf1736cd'}, {'abstract': 'Goldman Sachs settled allegations on Thursday that one of its investment bankers curried favor with a public official to win lucrative government contracts in Massachusetts.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/goldman-to-pay-12-million-to-settle-s-e-c-pay-to-play-case/', 'snippet': 'Goldman Sachs settled allegations on Thursday that one of its investment bankers curried favor with a public official to win lucrative government contracts in Massachusetts.', 'lead_paragraph': '8:34 p.m. | Updated Goldman Sachs on Thursday settled federal allegations that one of its investment bankers curried favor with a public official to win lucrative government contracts in Massachusetts.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Goldman to Pay $12 Million to Settle S.E.C. ‘Pay to Play’ Case', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_k