graph': 'PHILADELPHIA — The British artist Jeremy Deller, the subject of a thought-provoking exhibition at the Institute of Contemporary Art here, is the anti-Damien Hirst. Born in 1966, he came of age during the surge of the Young British Artists, when Mr. Hirst, Chris Ofili, the Chapman brothers, Tracey Emin and like-minded artists were producing extravagantly provocative works that the mega-collector Charles Saatchi acquired in bulk and packaged in the exhibition “Sensation,” which greatly offended Mayor Rudolph W. Giuliani when it came to the Brooklyn Museum in 1999. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '30', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/28DELLER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/28DELLER1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/SUBDELLER1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 682, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/SUBDELLER1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1364, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/28DELLER1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DELLER1_SPAN/28DELLER1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit