d.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28FRINGE/28FRINGE-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Taking a Time-Bending Leap Into a Final Season', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Taking a Time-Bending Leap Into a Final Season', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Television', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:48:05+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Television', 'byline': {'original': 'By Mike Hale', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Mike', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hale', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b874464f-5a1d-55d0-9199-27c97594708f', 'word_count': 796, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b874464f-5a1d-55d0-9199-27c97594708f'}, {'abstract': 'The filmmaker David Fisher and his three siblings retrace the steps of their Hungarian Jewish father, Joseph, a Holocaust survivor.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/movies/six-million-and-one-from-the-documentarian-david-fisher.html', 'snippet': 'The filmmaker David Fisher and his three siblings retrace the steps of their Hungarian Jewish father, Joseph, a Holocaust survivor.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In “Six Million and One,” the Israeli documentarian David Fisher’s third film in a family trilogy that includes “Love Inventory” (2000) and “Mostar Round-Trip” (2011), he and his three siblings retrace the steps of their Hungarian Jewish father, Joseph, a Holocaust survivor. Joining Mr. Fisher are his sister, Estee Fisher Heim, and his brothers, Gideon and Ronel. Their father was interned in Gusen and Gunskirchen, Austria, after a brief time at Auschwitz. While imprisoned he kept a memoir that was discovered 12 years after his death. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '12', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/SIXMILLION1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/SIXMILLION1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN/28SIXMILLION1_SPAN-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Facing Pain in a Father’s Footsteps', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Facing Pain In a Father’s Footsteps', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Documentary Films and Programs', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Holocaust and the Nazi Era', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Fisher, David', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:48:19+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Stephen Holden', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Stephen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Holden', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/4bf6b8ae-16f9-5bc5-a79a-effb742e2aa2', 'word_count': 393, 'uri': 'nyt://article/4bf6b8ae-16f9-5bc5-a79a-effb742e2aa2'}, {'abstract': 'What if our English teachers were wrong?', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/which-language-rules-to-flout-or-flaunt/', 'snippet': 'What if our English teachers were wrong?', 'lead_paragraph': 'Here’s a chilling thought: What if our English teachers were wrong? Maybe not about everything, but about a few memorable lessons. So many millions of writers have needlessly contorted their prose to avoid ending a sentence with a preposition. So many well-intentioned editors have fought to change “a historic” to “an historic.” If it turns out that the guidelines we cling to (“to which we cling”?) are nonsense, maybe the texters have the right idea when they throw out the old rules and start fresh.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Which Language Rules to Flout. Or Flaunt?', 'kicker': 'Draft', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Grammar', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Writing and Writers', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:50:26+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By The Editors', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The Editors'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/f362014d-c312-5ff3-9423-6cf9207407e2', 'word_count': 113, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f362014d-c312-5ff3-9423-6cf9207407e2'}, {'abstract': 'Franco Zeffirelli’s flamboyant production of Puccini’s “Turandot” returns to the Metropolitan Opera.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/arts/music/turandot-opens-at-metropolitan-opera.html', 'snippet': 'Franco Zeffirelli’s flamboyant production of Puccini’s “Turandot” returns to the Metropolitan Opera.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One of the enigmas of Puccini’s “Turandot” is why anyone would seek to marry a man-hating princess with a track record of decapitating her suitors. But the unattainable beauty proves irresistible, luring dozens of admirers to their deaths. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/28TURANDOT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 390, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/28TURANDOT_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 390}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/TURANDOT-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 713, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/TURANDOT-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 817, 'width': 1174, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/28TURANDOT_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/28TURANDOT_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28TURANDOT_SPAN/28TURANDOT_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Where an Incorrect Answer Can Extinguish Everything', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Where an Incorrect Answer Can Extinguish Everything', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Opera', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Zeffirelli, Franco', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Metropolitan Opera', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Guleghina, Maria', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Puccini, Giacomo', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:51:32+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Vivien Schweitzer', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Vivien', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Schweitzer', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/28982286-77e6-54c5-997f-8d959bf13537', 'word_count': 412, 'uri': 'nyt://article/28982286-77e6-54c5-997f-8d959bf13537'}, {'abstract': 'The Americans’ prowess at table tennis has become a major line of questioning in the pre-Ryder Cup interview sessions.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/waiting-to-play-golf-u-s-team-excels-at-table-tennis/', 'snippet': 'The Americans’ prowess at table tennis has become a major line of questioning in the pre-Ryder Cup interview sessions.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEDINAH, Ill. – Don’t be fooled, Bubba Watson said Thursday with a twinkle in his eye. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Waiting to Play Golf, U.S. Team Excels at Table Tennis', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Ryder Cup (Golf)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuchar, Matt', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Mickelson, Phil', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simpson, Webb', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Bubba', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:55:08+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3465b651-3585-5e09-950e-9550056d94c6', 'word_count': 320, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3465b651-3585-5e09-950e-9550056d94c6'}, {'abstract': 'Pen-ek Ratanaruang’s “Headshot” is a familiar story of good and evil with some contemporary touches.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/movies/headshot-directed-by-pen-ek-ratanaruang.html', 'snippet': 'Pen-ek Ratanaruang’s “Headshot” is a familiar story of good and evil with some contemporary touches.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The world turns literally upside down in “Headshot,” a dreamy, elliptical neo-noir about a cop turned killer turned something else altogether. This 180 shift in perspective occurs early in the movie, which the Thai writer and director Pen-ek Ratanaruang adapted from a novel by his compatriot Win Lyovarin. It’s a contemporary if shopworn story of good and evil, decency versus corruption and one man fighting the machine when topsy-turvy and not. Mr. Pen-ek has said it’s also a political tale, if not transparently so for those unfamiliar with Thai history. For the uninitiated, the kick won’t be the story or its meanings but rather its silky fusion of pulp fiction material and art cinema style.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Thai Tale Wrapped in a Smoky Noir', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Thai Tale Wrapped in a Smoky Noir', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Ratanaruang, Pen-ek', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:56:12+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/458f3bbd-f936-5010-92e8-b63771afed40', 'word_count': 492, 'uri': 'nyt://article/458f3bbd-f936-5010-92e8-b63771afed40'}, {'abstract': 'A historian’s collaborative efforts have helped to restore the reputation of the jewelry maker Gustav Manz.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/arts/design/giving-a-craftsman-his-due.html', 'snippet': 'A historian’s collaborative efforts have helped to restore the reputation of the jewelry maker Gustav Manz.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The jewelry maker Gustav Manz produced thousands of works in gold, silver and platinum, operating a Manhattan workshop from around 1901 to 1944. But his name shows up hardly anywhere.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 683, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28ANTIQUES1/ANTIQUES1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1365, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28ANTIQUES1/28ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28ANTIQUES1/28ANTIQUES1-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28ANTIQUES1/28ANTIQUES1-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Determined to Give a Craftsman His Due', 'kicker': 'Antiques', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Determined to Give A Craftsman His Due', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Jewels and Jewelry', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'ANTIQUES', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:56:31+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Art & Design', 'byline': {'original': 'By Eve M. Kahn', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Eve', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': '', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/bc290277-555c-5ecf-a7f4-ffea121bed4b', 'word_count': 1069, 'uri': 'nyt://article/bc290277-555c-5ecf-a7f4-ffea121bed4b'}, {'abstract': 'The “Flow” exhibition on Randalls Island features outdoor works by five up-and-coming artists.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/nyregion/an-artsy-blast-before-winter.html', 'snippet': 'The “Flow” exhibition on Randalls Island features outdoor works by five up-and-coming artists.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It should be no big secret that the Miser is a huge fan of Governors Island; more than a few recent columns have pointed readers there. But to be fair, it’s not the city’s only culturally rich island park. Before winter’s bluster starts making outdoor adventures less palatable, it’s time to consider other, equally distinctive destinations. ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '31', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'An Artsy Blast Before Winter', 'kicker': 'Weekend Miser', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Randalls Island (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T21:56:56+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By A. C. Lee', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'C.', 'lastname': 'Lee', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/430a8e29-abdc-545c-953b-5452169748b8', 'word_count': 451, 'uri': 'nyt://article/430a8e29-abdc-545c-953b-5452169748b8'}, {'abstract': 'Chris Johnson was one of the top running back draft picks in fantasy football this season, but he ranks as one of the worst performers and is among our unfavorable matchup picks for Week 4.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthd