rce': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Romney Adds Note of Inevitability to Stump Speech', 'kicker': 'The Caucus', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Romney Is Sounding Note of Inevitability', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:03:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Ashley Parker', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Ashley', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Parker', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7e3318e6-d2fd-5765-bcb3-b4c9b1d89d7d', 'word_count': 488, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7e3318e6-d2fd-5765-bcb3-b4c9b1d89d7d'}, {'abstract': 'Three other men in New York City have been sickened, one critically, by the infection in the last month, the city health department said.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/one-dead-in-meningitis-outbreak-involving-men-who-have-sex-with-men/', 'snippet': 'Three other men in New York City have been sickened, one critically, by the infection in the last month, the city health department said.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One man has died of meningitis and three others have fallen ill, one critically, over the last month in an outbreak of the infection among men who have sex with men, the New York City health department said on Thursday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'One Dead in Meningitis Outbreak Among Men Who Have Sex With Men', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Meningitis', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:07:29+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Anemona Hartocollis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Anemona', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hartocollis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/ceacb704-b6be-5cbc-b07a-a8cb1307db3e', 'word_count': 256, 'uri': 'nyt://article/ceacb704-b6be-5cbc-b07a-a8cb1307db3e'}, {'abstract': 'Answers to questions about dealing with teenagers, a friend, a boyfriend and strangers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/fashion/girls-will-be-girls.html', 'snippet': 'Answers to questions about dealing with teenagers, a friend, a boyfriend and strangers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'I am in my early 30s, and was on a flight home after a brutal workweek abroad. I fell asleep quickly but was awakened by two teenage girls, who were laughing and pointing at me, as well as taking my picture and uploading it onto social media. I asked them to stop, but they didn’t. So I reported them to the flight attendant, who kindly changed my seat. But the mocking began all over again when I saw them at customs, where they had gathered with several more teenagers and a teacher chaperon. Any thoughts on dealing with girls like these? Elizabeth', 'print_section': 'ST', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 599, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-articleLarge-v2.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 599}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-jumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-superJumbo-v2.jpg', 'height': 923, 'width': 925, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbStandard-v3.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2011/07/28/fashion/social_inline/social_inline-thumbLarge-v3.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Girls Will Be Girls', 'kicker': 'Social Q’s', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Girls Will Be Girls', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Women and Girls', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Customs, Etiquette and Manners', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:08:01+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Styles', 'section_name': 'Fashion & Style', 'byline': {'original': 'By Philip Galanes', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Philip', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Galanes', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Question', '_id': 'nyt://article/f6e1c62c-99b3-5cb4-ab81-414758971634', 'word_count': 784, 'uri': 'nyt://article/f6e1c62c-99b3-5cb4-ab81-414758971634'}, {'abstract': 'In Rian Johnson’s “Looper,” a young hit man (Joseph Gordon-Levitt) confronts his older self thanks to time travel.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/movies/looper-with-bruce-willis-and-joseph-gordon-levitt.html', 'snippet': 'In Rian Johnson’s “Looper,” a young hit man (Joseph Gordon-Levitt) confronts his older self thanks to time travel.', 'lead_paragraph': '“Looper,” an obstreperously entertaining, bullet- and attitude-ridden science-fiction pastiche, opens in a futuristic world that looks and sounds paradoxically out of the past. Set in 2044, in a dystopia with Turner Classic Movies flavor, it puts a spin on the familiar figure of the existentially troubled gun for hire while leaving a layer of dust on many of the other genre fundamentals it plays with. Some of it you’ve seen and heard before, intentionally so, though it adds a weird wrinkle: an attempt to transform Joseph Gordon-Levitt, his nose prosthetically bobbed and a smirk surfing his thinned upper lip, into the wisecracking Bruce Willis of the “Moonlighting” years.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/movies/looper-1/looper-1-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 681, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/movies/looper-1/looper-1-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1363, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/movies/looper-1/looper-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/27/movies/looper-1/looper-1-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/27/movies/looper-1/looper-1-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'In What Universe Does This Guy Turn Into Bruce Willis?', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In What Universe Does This Guy Turn Into Bruce Willis?', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Rian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Gordon-Levitt, Joseph', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Willis, Bruce', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Blunt, Emily', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'creative_works', 'value': 'Looper (Movie)', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:09:00+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/415176b8-1ab5-550c-bbf7-204aa0dbf8fe', 'word_count': 659, 'uri': 'nyt://article/415176b8-1ab5-550c-bbf7-204aa0dbf8fe'}, {'abstract': 'The choreographer D D Dorvillier, drawing on one of Beethoven’s late string quartets, has called “Danza Permanente” her attempt to make music visible in silence.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/arts/dance/danza-permanente-at-the-kitchen.html', 'snippet': 'The choreographer D D Dorvillier, drawing on one of Beethoven’s late string quartets, has called “Danza Permanente” her attempt to make music visible in silence.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The systematic dreariness of D D Dorvillier’s “Danza Permanente” is depressing — schematic and soporific. In the first place, the ambience onstage is disagreeable. Four dancers metronomically plod their way through long routines, occasionally counting out loud. Usually they avert their eyes from one another and the audience — but sometimes they connect conspiratorially, like prisoners who are hoping the guards will not notice their escape plan. The escape, of course, came when they stopped.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DANZA/28DANZA-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1022, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DANZA/28DANZA-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 2044, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DANZA/28DANZA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DANZA/28DANZA-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28DANZA/28DANZA-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Beethoven Quartet, Better Heard Than Seen', 'kicker': 'Dance Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Beethoven Quartet, Better Heard Than Seen', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Kitchen, The (Manhattan, NY, Performance Space)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Dancing', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Dorvillier, D D', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:09:51+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Dance', 'byline': {'original': 'By Alastair Macaulay', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Alastair', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Macaulay', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/8a3680d5-b51a-5630-9a91-87cc37da8b16', 'word_count': 630, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8a3680d5-b51a-5630-9a91-87cc37da8b16'}, {'abstract': 'The 50th New York Film Festival is as eclectic as ever, with Hollywood products like “Life of Pi,” foreign award winners like “Amour” and independent offerings like “Frances Ha.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/movies/new-york-film-festival-at-lincoln-center.html', 'snippet': 'The 50th New York Film Festival is as eclectic as ever, with Hollywood products like “Life of Pi,” foreign award winners like “Amour” and independent offerings like “Frances Ha.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'There is talk going around these days —  again — about the death of movies, and the 50th New York Film Festival, which starts Friday night, might have offered an occasion for fond, gloomy retrospect. This festival was born in what is now invoked as a heroic era of local and global film culture — a feverish atmosphere perhaps best recalled by the writer Phillip Lopate in his 1998 book, “Totally Tenderly Tragically” — and its home at Lincoln Center frequently seems haunted by the ghosts of golden ages past. Remember the first festival in 1963, when we were dazzled and perplexed by Luis Buñuel’s “Exterminating Angel,” Roman Polanski’s “Knife in the Water” and Alain Resnais’s “Muriel”?', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '1', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 380, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 380}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF_SPAN-thumbStandard-v2.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28NYFF_SPAN/28NYFF_SPAN-thumbLarge-v2.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'A Festival Whose Age Is Just a Number', 'kicker': 'Critic’s Notebook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Festival Whose Age Is Just A Number', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'New York Film Festival', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Film Society of Lincoln Center', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-27T22:18:03+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Weekend', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By A.O. Scott', 'person': [{'firstname': 'A.', 'middlename': 'O.', 'lastname': 'Scott', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/02f16a15-73c6-59a4-ae33-ad3091dbea54', 'word_count': 1223, 'uri': 'nyt://article/02f16a15-73c6-59a4-ae33-ad3091dbea54'}, {'abstract': 'The Met’s exhibition of the Rinpa artists of Japan is expansive, while the Japan Society’s is more narrowly focused.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/arts/design/rimpa-painters-at-japan-society-and-the-met.html', 'snippet': 'The Met’s exhibition of the Rinpa artists of Japan is expansive, while the Japan Society’s is more narrowly focused.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Have any artists ever, anywhere, caught the hello-ness of spring and the farewell-ness of autumn more sweetly and sharply than the Rimpa painters of Japan? Two shimmering fall exhibitions, one at Japan Society and the other at the Metropolitan Museum of Art, argue persuasively that no, no artists ever have.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '25', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SILVER_SPAN/JPSILVER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 273, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SILVER_SPAN/JPSILVER2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 273}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SILVER_SPAN/JPSILVER2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 466, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/28SILVER_SPAN/JPSILVER2-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 932, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': N