, 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/b4ab3689-46aa-5bf2-8209-eb3896a5c068', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/b4ab3689-46aa-5bf2-8209-eb3896a5c068'}, {'abstract': 'The Arab revolutions have not turned anti-Western. They are still about social justice and democracy.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/opinion/the-arab-spring-still-blooms.html', 'snippet': 'The Arab revolutions have not turned anti-Western. They are still about social justice and democracy.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE violent demonstrations that have spread across the Muslim world in recent weeks have convinced many in the United States and Europe that the Arab revolutions that began in late 2010 are now over and that the democratic project has failed. Bitterness and a sense of impending catastrophe are replacing the enthusiasm that followed the toppling of dictators in Tunisia and Egypt last year.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'The Arab Spring Still Blooms', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'The Arab Spring Still Blooms', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Muslims and Islam', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Demonstrations, Protests, and Riots', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Middle East and North Africa Unrest (2010- )', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Fringe Groups and Movements', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Tunisia', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T00:48:23+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Moncef Marzouki', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Moncef', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Marzouki', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/9c0b388c-1f7e-5e84-8fc7-442f7ff5ad62', 'word_count': 795, 'uri': 'nyt://article/9c0b388c-1f7e-5e84-8fc7-442f7ff5ad62'}, {'abstract': 'An army of government workers and government contractors live in suburban Maryland and favor Democrats by significant margins.', 'web_url': 'https://fivethirtyeight.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/27/growth-of-suburban-d-c-is-felt-politically-in-maryland/', 'snippet': 'An army of government workers and government contractors live in suburban Maryland and favor Democrats by significant margins.', 'lead_paragraph': 'We continue our Presidential Geography series, a one-by-one examination of the peculiarities that drive the politics in all 50 states and the District of Columbia. Here is a look at Maryland, the Old Line State. FiveThirtyEight spoke with Donald F. Kettl, the dean of the School of Public Policy at the University of Maryland; and Theodore F. Sheckels, a professor of communications and English at Randolph-Macon College in Ashland, Va.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Growth of Suburban D.C. Is Felt Politically in Maryland', 'kicker': 'FiveThirtyEight', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T00:48:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Micah Cohen', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Micah', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Cohen', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/41e0b6bc-2bb5-59ea-b2cd-c1447ecdee40', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/41e0b6bc-2bb5-59ea-b2cd-c1447ecdee40'}, {'abstract': 'It is startling to find that Chinese people’s feelings of well-being have declined in a period of such momentous improvement in their economic lives.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/opinion/in-china-growth-outpaces-happiness.html', 'snippet': 'It is startling to find that Chinese people’s feelings of well-being have declined in a period of such momentous improvement in their economic lives.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Los Angeles', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '35', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 392, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 392}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 669, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1338, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/opinion/0928OPEDwaszul/0928OPEDwaszul-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'When Growth Outpaces Happiness', 'kicker': 'Op-Ed Contributor', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'When Growth Outpaces Happiness', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Happiness', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Reform and Reorganization', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Labor and Jobs', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Communist Party of China', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'China', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Privatization', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T00:48:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'OpEd', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Richard A. Easterlin', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Richard', 'middlename': 'A.', 'lastname': 'Easterlin', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Op-Ed', '_id': 'nyt://article/80414873-7fbc-58d8-8fc6-273952286497', 'word_count': 854, 'uri': 'nyt://article/80414873-7fbc-58d8-8fc6-273952286497'}, {'abstract': 'Darren Star has put his three-bedroom condo with walls of windows facing Central Park and the Hudson River on the market for $15 million.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/realestate/exclusive-sex-and-the-city-creator-lists-apartment.html', 'snippet': 'Darren Star has put his three-bedroom condo with walls of windows facing Central Park and the Hudson River on the market for $15 million.', 'lead_paragraph': 'THE sophisticated glass-sheathed condominium at One Central Park West exudes sensuality, and why not? Since 2005, it has been the East Coast lair of the producer Darren Star, the creator of a string of frothy television series headlined by “Sex and the City,” the cult classic that zeroed in on the high-wire exploits of four romance-addicted New York City career women. He also produced the film version of “Sex and the City” and its sequel.', 'print_section': 'RE', 'print_page': '2', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 683, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1365, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/realestate/30EXCLU1_SPAN/EXCLU-A-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': '‘Sex and the City’ Creator Lists Apartment', 'kicker': 'Exclusive | One Central Park West', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Carrie Bradshaw Muse Slept Here'