r': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T00:53:43+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joseph Berger', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joseph', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Berger', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/83c6cd03-4a96-592b-a69e-69c448d88a66', 'word_count': 760, 'uri': 'nyt://article/83c6cd03-4a96-592b-a69e-69c448d88a66'}, {'abstract': 'The island off the southern tip of Manhattan is a wasteland no more. These days it’s a very busy place.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/opinion/governors-island-takes-its-new-shape.html', 'snippet': 'The island off the southern tip of Manhattan is a wasteland no more. These days it’s a very busy place.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Governors Island was, for many years, little more than a tantalizing wasteland — a 172-acre chunk of rock and mostly abandoned military structures. Politicians and urban planners dreamed of ways to turn this underused space into a compelling daytime destination for New Yorkers, but nothing really happened until the state, in 2010, ceded most of the island to New York City. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '34', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Governors Island and Its Future', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Governors Island and Its Future', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Area Planning and Renewal', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Governors Island (NYC)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Bloomberg, Michael R', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T00:54:18+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/8e67d188-02d2-597a-a8ad-08540c2ac206', 'word_count': 334, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8e67d188-02d2-597a-a8ad-08540c2ac206'}, {'abstract': 'The Atlanta Braves have won each of Kris Medlen’s last 22 starts, dating to 2010, tied for the longest streak in major league history.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/sports/baseball/braves-medlen-has-a-little-man-on-his-shoulder-and-fire-in-his-right-arm.html', 'snippet': 'The Atlanta Braves have won each of Kris Medlen’s last 22 starts, dating to 2010, tied for the longest streak in major league history.', 'lead_paragraph': 'ATLANTA — The hottest starting pitcher in baseball is a 5-foot-10 right-hander with spiked hair, a flat-brim cap, a rebuilt elbow and a little man on his shoulder giving him all the answers.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '15', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/sports/yPITCHER/yPITCHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/sports/yPITCHER/yPITCHER-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/sports/yPITCHER/yPITCHER-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 598, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/sports/yPITCHER/yPITCHER-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1196, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': '