ine': {'original': 'By Prateek Pradhan and Heather Timmons', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Prateek', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Pradhan', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}, {'firstname': 'Heather', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Timmons', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 2}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/af01b6ca-601e-50b5-8c20-b340d39348fe', 'word_count': 286, 'uri': 'nyt://article/af01b6ca-601e-50b5-8c20-b340d39348fe'}, {'abstract': 'The perils of generational forgetting.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/debt-is-a-drug-and-so-is-austerity/', 'snippet': 'The perils of generational forgetting.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A commenter tells me that there is a term of art in the substance use literature, “generational forgetting”, that seems relevant to our economic troubles. And it’s true, e.g. here (pdf):', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Debt Is a Drug (And So Is Austerity)', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T10:50:57+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/e54e18e7-5597-51c0-b7d3-2d4ee2ea9120', 'word_count': 312, 'uri': 'nyt://article/e54e18e7-5597-51c0-b7d3-2d4ee2ea9120'}, {'abstract': 'Servers have been trained to offer to take a group picture if they see a smartphone on the table. This is a terrific way to generate Instagram and Facebook photos.', 'web_url': 'https://boss.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/how-a-las-vegas-diner-gets-the-most-out-of-social-media/', 'snippet': 'Servers have been trained to offer to take a group picture if they see a smartphone on the table. This is a terrific way to generate Instagram and Facebook photos.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Squeeze In is a modest little breakfast-and-lunch diner with four locations in the greater Reno, Nev. area. The restaurants are open seven days a week, 7 a.m. to 2 p.m. While online commenters rave about the food, I can’t get over how creatively this little chain has managed its social media. It has a Web site and a custom mobile site. It promotes the business through Foursquare, Twitter, Facebook, Pinterest and Instagram.', 'print_section': 'B', 'print_page': '8', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'How a Diner Gets the Most Out of Social Media', 'kicker': 'You&#039;re the Boss', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'A Diner Taps Social Media', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Restaurants', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Social Networking (Internet)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Reno (Nev)', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T11:00:04+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'subsection_name': 'Entrepreneurship', 'byline': {'original': 'By Melinda F. Emerson', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Melinda', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Emerson', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/13554039-b3f6-5b73-a211-f904f4b8382c', 'word_count': 930, 'uri': 'nyt://article/13554039-b3f6-5b73-a211-f904f4b8382c'}, {'abstract': 'What Churchill wrought.', 'web_url': 'https://krugman.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/internal-devaluation-and-the-road-to-wigan-pier/', 'snippet': 'What Churchill wrought.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Greg Ip, in correspondence, directs me to Chapter 3 of the latest IMF World Economic Outlook, which among other things contains an analysis of a case that bears directly on the attempts of euro area countries to restore economic health through fiscal austerity and internal devaluation: Britain’s return to the gold standard after World War I.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Internal Devaluation and the Road to Wigan Pier', 'kicker': 'Paul Krugman', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T11:02:37+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Paul Krugman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Paul', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Krugman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/60d854e9-def3-5946-9eef-772585827a01', 'word_count': 199, 'uri': 'nyt://article/60d854e9-def3-5946-9eef-772585827a01'}, {'abstract': 'In a deal worth $640 million, Sony said it would hold an 11.46 percent stake after buying new shares that would be issued by Olympus.', 'web_url': 'https://dealbook.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/sony-agrees-to-acquire-stake-in-olympus/', 'snippet': 'In a deal worth $640 million, Sony said it would hold an 11.46 percent stake after buying new shares that would be issued by Olympus.', 'lead_paragraph': 'TOKYO — Sony is set to become the biggest shareholder in Olympus with an investment of 50 billion yen, or $645 million, investment, the two companies announced Friday.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Sony Agrees to Acquire Stake in Olympus', 'kicker': 'DealBook', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Mergers, Acquisitions and Divestitures', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Hirai, Kazuo', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Olympus Corp', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Sony Corporation', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T11:06:59+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': 'By Hiroko Tabuchi', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Hiroko', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Tabuchi', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/2f8575c4-fd4a-51ad-bdcb-35999950a9d8', 'word_count': 473, 'uri': 'nyt://article/2f8575c4-fd4a-51ad-bdcb-35999950a9d8'}, {'abstract': 'A demand for coats and other luxury leopard items in Asia has caused poaching of the animals to soar to more than 200 leopards a year in India alone, a report says.', 'web_url': 'https://green.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/leopard-poaching-on-the-rise/', 'snippet': 'A demand for coats and other luxury leopard items in Asia has caused poaching of the animals to soar to more than 200 leopards a year in India alone, a report says.', 'lead_paragraph': 'When it comes to the poaching of endangered species, elephants, tigers and rhinos tend to be in the limelight. But a new report sets out to plug the information gap on a different species that is imperiled by a tide of demand related to rising affluence in Asia: leopards.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Leopard Poaching on the Rise, Group Warns', 'kicker': 'Green', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Endangered and Extinct Species', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Leopards', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Poaching (Wildlife)', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank'