:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nacho Alegre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nacho', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alegre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/791d0532-c3a3-5645-9ebf-c05e95f8ddcc', 'word_count': 29, 'uri': 'nyt://article/791d0532-c3a3-5645-9ebf-c05e95f8ddcc'}, {'abstract': 'The Day: New Yorkers love few things more than to hate the United Nations, and they really love to hate it in a week like this one, when world leaders convened for the General Assembly.', 'web_url': 'https://cityroom.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/at-the-u-n-agitation-outrage-and-business-as-usual/', 'snippet': 'The Day: New Yorkers love few things more than to hate the United Nations, and they really love to hate it in a week like this one, when world leaders convened for the General Assembly.', 'lead_paragraph': ' Here in the “capital of the world,” which is New York’s characteristically modest description of itself, people seem to have survived their annual immersion in foreign affairs with but the faintest disruption to their lives. We knew all along that they could do it, despite the overblown forecasts of impending disaster, including the menace they were instructed by television to dread most of all: traffic jams. ', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'At the U.N., Agitation, Outrage and Business as Usual', 'kicker': 'City Room', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Yom Kippur', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'AHMADINEJAD, MAHMOUD', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Netanyahu, Benjamin', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Obama, Barack', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'General Assembly (UN)', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'United Nations', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York City', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T13:16:45+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'New York', 'byline': {'original': 'By Clyde Haberman', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Clyde', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Haberman', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/45798cf5-eee3-5d70-a765-dd87e756d97a', 'word_count': 758, 'uri': 'nyt://article/45798cf5-eee3-5d70-a765-dd87e756d97a'}, {'abstract': 'Complaints and jokes about Apple’s new maps have not stopped since the company released iOS 6 last week, a software update that replaced Google’s maps with its own. Now the company’s chief executive, Tim Cook, is apologizing.', 'web_url': 'https://bits.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/tim-cook-maps/', 'snippet': 'Complaints and jokes about Apple’s new maps have not stopped since the company released iOS 6 last week, a software update that replaced Google’s maps with its own. Now the company’s chief executive, Tim Cook, is apologizing.', 'lead_paragraph': 'After more than a week of complaints and jokes about Apple’s new mapping service, the company’s chief executive apologized to customers on Friday for the frustration it has caused.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/23/technology/23bits-disrupt/23bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 340, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/23/technology/23bits-disrupt/23bits-disrupt-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 340}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'typ