ely short time frame that shows substan...', 'lead_paragraph': 'With the economy an issue in the presidential campaign, the answer to the question of how the economy is doing may depend on one’s point of reference. The charts show four economic indicators, using a relatively short time frame that shows substantial improvement, and a much longer one, showing that current conditions are well below prerecession levels.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Off the Charts: Looking Better, or Looking Bad?', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Economic Conditions and Trends', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Consumer Confidence (Economic Indicator)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Unemployment', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T16:05:36+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Business Day', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/61bc1e30-ca89-5284-b83d-526cc984d059', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/61bc1e30-ca89-5284-b83d-526cc984d059'}, {'abstract': 'The Lincoln administration’s battleground regulations have reached far beyond American shores.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/books/review/lincolns-code-by-john-fabian-witt.html', 'snippet': 'The Lincoln administration’s battleground regulations have reached far beyond American shores.', 'lead_paragraph': 'In recent years, the American debate about war — about torture, drone strikes, the killing of civilians in Afghanistan and Pakistan — has also been a debate about the international laws of war. But much of this law, as John Fabian Witt shows in his magnificent new book, was originally made in America.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '20', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 294, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 294}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 501, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1003, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/books/bkr-bass/bkr-bass-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Rules of War', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Rules of War', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Books and Literature', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Witt, John Fabian', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-28T16:05:52+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'BookReview', 'section_name': 'Books', 'subsection_name': 'Book Review', 'byline': {'original': 'By Gary J. Bass', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Gary', 'middlename': 'J.', 'lastname': 'Bass', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/561f1833-692e-5b15-b237-35738fc20135', 'word_count': 1191, 'uri': 'nyt://article/561f1833-692e-5b15-b237-35738fc20135'}, {'abstract': 'Ranking the presidents is a favorite parlor game of the politics-obsessed.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/books/review/where-they-stand-by-robert-w-merry.html', 'snippet': 'Ranking the presidents is a favorite parlor game of the politics-obsessed.', 'lead_paragraph': 'One night a few months ago, my 7-year-old son — who during the Republican primaries developed a mildly obsessional interest in presidential history — asked me to rank the American chief executives in order of greatness. With bedtime looming, I resolved to make it quick. Rattling off the successes (George Washington, Abraham Lincoln, Franklin D. Roosevelt) proved easy, as did listing the failures (James Buchanan, Andrew Johnson, Herbert Hoover, Richard Nixon). But filling in the middle drove me crazy. Should William McKinley place higher or lower than Gerald Ford? Martin Van Buren above or below Rutherford B. Hayes? Yawning, and under pressure from my eager son, I randomly plugged in Millard Fillmore, Chester A. Arthur and the Harrisons and called it a night.', 'print_section': 'BR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/bkr-greenberg/bkr-greenberg-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/28/arts/bkr-greenberg/bkr-greenberg-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1980, 'width': 1980, 'subType': 'superJumbo', '