 'By Kristin Hohenadel', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Kristin', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Hohenadel', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/a792332a-56b4-55db-8c39-3c5b370ea641', 'word_count': 1072, 'uri': 'nyt://article/a792332a-56b4-55db-8c39-3c5b370ea641'}, {'abstract': 'Gotham Chopra, who made a film about his father, says Deepak Chopra is seen most clearly not in isolation but in public.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/movies/deepak-chopras-son-makes-him-a-documentary-subject.html', 'snippet': 'Gotham Chopra, who made a film about his father, says Deepak Chopra is seen most clearly not in isolation but in public.', 'lead_paragraph': 'UNDERSTANDING one’s parents might be as difficult as fathoming the cosmos, but it’s something most of us try to do, in one way or another. Doing this through the lens of a camera for a documentary film, as a number of children of famous parents have in works like “My Architect,” by the son of Louis Kahn; “William Kunstler: Disturbing the Universe,” by the radical lawyer’s two daughters; and “The Man Nobody Knew: In Search of My Father, CIA Spymaster William Colby,” by Carl Colby, can be an effective, if grandiose method. It allows the filmmaker to use the tried-and-true tools of the profession while also making a very public statement.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '13', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/arts/30CHOPRA2_SPAN/30CHOPRA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/arts/30CHOPRA2_SPAN/30CHOPRA2-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/