1074, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b2130db9-4ca5-5ac1-ade9-50f10691e831'}, {'abstract': 'John Wayne stars in four “Three Mesquiteers” installments from the 1930s: “Overland Stage Raiders,” “Red River Range,” “The Night Riders” and “Three Texas Steers.”', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/movies/homevideo/new-on-dvd-three-mesquiteers-films-with-john-wayne.html', 'snippet': 'John Wayne stars in four “Three Mesquiteers” installments from the 1930s: “Overland Stage Raiders,” “Red River Range,” “The Night Riders” and “Three Texas Steers.”', 'lead_paragraph': 'LOUISE BROOKS is barely recognizable in “Overland Stage Raiders,” the 1938 Republic western that would prove to be her last film: the magnetizing star who went to Germany to make “Pandora’s Box” and “Diary of a Lost Girl” (both released in 1929) has let her daringly close-cropped hair grow out into conventional waves, her nose and chin seem sharper, and the fire in her eyes has dimmed. But there she is, providing the perfunctory love interest in a low-budget feature meant for Saturday morning children’s matinees, playing against a good-looking but gawky young man who a few years later would become one of the biggest stars in the business, John Wayne.', 'print_section': 'AR', 'print_page': '14', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/arts/30KEHR2_SPAN/30KEHR2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 360, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/arts/30KEHR2_SPAN/30KEHR2_SPAN-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 360}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/arts/30KEHR2_SPAN/30KEHR2-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 766, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 