: 'A look at three techniques that Russell Martin of the Yankees and other Major League catchers use on plays at the plate.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/interactive/2012/09/29/sports/baseball/29catcher-graphic.html', 'snippet': 'A look at three techniques that Russell Martin of the Yankees and other Major League catchers use on plays at the plate.', 'lead_paragraph': 'A look at three techniques that Russell Martin of the Yankees and other Major League catchers use on plays at the plate.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Home Protection', 'kicker': None, 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Martin, Russell', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Yankees', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T00:52:56+0000', 'document_type': 'multimedia', 'news_desk': 'Sports', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Baseball', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Interactive Feature', '_id': 'nyt://interactive/eea18a9b-b321-5d47-aa2a-895bd524dbb0', 'word_count': 0, 'uri': 'nyt://interactive/eea18a9b-b321-5d47-aa2a-895bd524dbb0'}, {'abstract': 'Your teammates are your in-season family, and your instinct is to support your brother even if you do not like his politics, his swagger or his batting average.', 'web_url': 'https://opinionator.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/a-father-to-baseball/', 'snippet': 'Your teammates are your in-season family, and your instinct is to support your brother even if you do not like his politics, his swagger or his batting average.', 'lead_paragraph': 'It is the end of the regular season, and time for goodbyes. I remember walking off the field in 2002, after we played our last game. One of my Phillies teammates said it was “a sad time” because you will never play with this same group again. So we gave out big hugs, knowing this nuclear family would forever be dispersed.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'A Father to Baseball', 'kicker': 'Doug Glanville', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Baseball', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Families and Family Life', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Philadelphia Phillies', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T01:00:06+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': 'By Doug Glanville', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Doug', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Glanville', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3a13596a-35b2-52f6-b6a7-7e9bc5fee81c', 'word_count': 1173, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3a13596a-35b2-52f6-b6a7-7e9bc5fee81c'}, {'abstract': 'New York State lawmakers need to restructure a costly, fraud-riddled program.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/opinion/overhaul-special-education-for-preschool.html', 'snippet': 'New York State lawmakers need to restructure a costly, fraud-riddled program.', 'lead_paragraph': 'New York State clearly needs to strengthen oversight of the fraud-riddled program that spends $2 billion a year on special education services for preschool children with learning or other disabilities. The State Education Department got off to a good start this month when it issued a preliminary reform proposal. ', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '22', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Overhaul Special Education for Preschool', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Overhaul Special Education for Preschool', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'New York State', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Education Department (NYS)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Special Education', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Frauds and Swindling', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'State Legislatures', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Education (Pre-School)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T01:01:41+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/85e561e2-c339-508c-a317-81d839b5da9f', 'word_count': 499, 'uri': 'nyt://article/85e561e2-c339-508c-a317-81d839b5da9f'}, {'abstract': 'Bubba Watson, Webb Simpson, Matt Kuchar and Dustin Johnson watched the morning pairings on television. In the afternoon, they showed what they can do.', 'web_url': 'https://onpar.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/28/for-some-u-s-players-waiting-pays-off/', 'snippet': 'Bubba Watson, Webb Simpson, Matt Kuchar and Dustin Johnson watched the morning pairings on television. In the afternoon, they showed what they can do.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MEDINAH, Ill. — They were plenty rested after sitting out the morning matches, and when their turn finally came to show what they could do, Bubba Watson, Webb Simpson, Matt Kuchar and Dustin Johnson broke fast out of the gate at Medinah Country Club.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'For Some U.S. Players, Waiting Pays Off', 'kicker': 'On Par', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Golf', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Johnson, Dustin', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Kuchar, Matt', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Simpson, Webb', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Watson, Bubba', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Medinah Country Club', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T01:05:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Golf', 'byline': {'original': 'By Karen Crouse', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Karen', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Crouse', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/8507708a-929a-555e-a118-ce8bcdf84f42', 'word_count': 351, 'uri': 'nyt://article/8507708a-929a-555e-a118-ce8bcdf84f42'}, {'abstract': 'Four new breweries are changing Connecticut’s reputation as something of a laggard in the production of craft beers.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/nyregion/craft-beers-emerge-in-connecticut.html', 'snippet': 'Four new breweries are changing Connecticut’s reputation as something of a laggard in the production of craft beers.', 'lead_paragraph': 'MENTION craft beer and places like California, Colorado and Oregon, where start-up breweries have thrived for decades, come to mind; not so Connecticut, where small-batch brewing has lagged behind. But with four new breweries — and two more preparing to release beers by year’s end — the way we think about the state’s locally made beer is finally poised to change.', 'print_section': 'CT', 'print_page': '7', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/nyregion/30DINECT3/30DINECT3-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 768, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None,