 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'U.S.', 'subsection_name': 'Politics', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jada F. Smith', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jada', 'middlename': 'F.', 'lastname': 'Smith', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/b05eb270-9f39-5916-be39-f7bcc05b7066', 'word_count': 444, 'uri': 'nyt://article/b05eb270-9f39-5916-be39-f7bcc05b7066'}, {'abstract': 'Bollywood actress roped in as sanitation and hygiene ambassador in government’s campaign to make India open-defecation-free.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/vidya-balan-and-jairam-ramesh-team-up-for-toilets/', 'snippet': 'Bollywood actress roped in as sanitation and hygiene ambassador in government’s campaign to make India open-defecation-free.', 'lead_paragraph': 'NEW DELHI– A traveling village fair is scheduled to kick off next week in India, but instead of cotton candy and tchotchkes, it will sell an important message: Use soap to wash your hands, and don’t defecate in the open.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Vidya Balan and Jairam Ramesh Team Up For Toilets', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Bathrooms and Toilets', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hygiene and Cleanliness', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Balan, Vidya', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T10:45:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By Sruthi Gottipati', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Sruthi', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Gottipati', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/7cd965c1-981d-50b0-bf8f-8aaedd83b53e', 'word_count': 606, 'uri': 'nyt://article/7cd965c1-981d-50b0-bf8f-8aaedd83b53e'}, {'abstract': 'A round-up of news from across India.', 'web_url': 'https://india.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/newswallah-bharat-edition-58/', 'snippet': 'A round-up of news from across India.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Jammu and Kashmir: Chief Minister Omar Abdullah dismissed reports of a brewing “battle” between himself and Rahul Gandhi of the Congress party over the issue of providing security to sarpanches, the elected heads of village councils, Kashmir Live reported. A group of\xa0 sarpanches from Kashmir met with Mr. Gandhi in New Delhi on Thursday to discuss security concerns after the killing of three village council members in the Kashmir valley earlier this week.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Newswallah: Bharat Edition', 'kicker': 'India Ink', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'India', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T11:03:28+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'World', 'subsection_name': 'Asia Pacific', 'byline': {'original': 'By The New York Times', 'person': [], 'organization': 'The New York Times'}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/27360fb2-7245-58ad-a9e5-b88769c13fe9', 'word_count': 352, 'uri': 'nyt://article/27360fb2-7245-58ad-a9e5-b88769c13fe9'}, {'abstract': 'The allegations against leaders of a youth football team in California sound so outlandish that it seems as if they might be from a “Saturday Night Live” skit.', 'web_url': 'https://fifthdown.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/accusations-of-football-bounties-for-10-year-olds/', 'snippet': 'The allegations against leaders of a youth football team in California sound so outlandish that it seems as if they might be from a “Saturday Night Live” skit.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The allegations against leaders of a youth football team in California sound so outlandish that they seem to be from a “Saturday Night Live” skit. Imagine the former Saints defensive coordinator Gregg Williams telling a group of 10-year-olds that there may be a trip to Dairy Queen after the game, “but only after you affect the head, kill the head.”', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Accusations of Football Bounties for 10-Year-Olds', 'kicker': 'The Fifth Down', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Football', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T11:39:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Pro Football', 'byline': {'original': 'By Toni Monkovic', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Toni', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Monkovic', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/96f629ae-0ad7-564a-a3df-bf8850bfe38b', 'word_count': 248, 'uri': 'nyt://article/96f629ae-0ad7-564a-a3df-bf8850bfe38b'}, {'abstract': 'The artist was at the Barclays Center in Brooklyn for the first show of a sold-out eight night run in the new rusty bunker that will house the Brooklyn Nets.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/arts/music/jay-z-comes-home-to-open-an-arena-in-brooklyn.html', 'snippet': 'The artist was at the Barclays Center in Brooklyn for the first show of a sold-out eight night run in the new rusty bunker that will house the Brooklyn Nets.', 'lead_paragraph': 'For many years, Jay-Z closed out his concerts with “Encore,” a soothing, triumphant number from “The Black Album,” which at the time of its release in 2003 was billed as something of a retirement. “From Marcy to Madison Square,” he rapped, sketching an arc that had taken him from a Brooklyn housing project to headlining the most symbolically important arena in the country.', 'print_section': 'A', 'print_page': '28', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'xlarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-articleLarge.jpg', 'height': 330, 'width': 600, 'subType': 'xlarge', 'crop_name': 'articleLarge', 'legacy': {'xlarge': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-articleLarge.jpg', 'xlargewidth': 600, 'xlargeheight': 330}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 674, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1349, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/us/JAY-Z/JAY-Z-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Jay-Z Comes Home to Repay a Debt to Brooklyn', 'kicker': 'Music Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'In Sold-Out Concert, Jay-Z Comes Home to Repay Debt to Brooklyn', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Rap and Hip-Hop', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Atlantic Yards (Brooklyn)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Brooklyn Nets', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Music', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Stadiums and Arenas', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Jay-Z', 'rank': 6, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'Barclays Center (NYC)', 'rank': 7, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T12:09:54+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Metro', 'section_name': 'Arts', 'subsection_name': 'Music', 'byline': {'original': 'By Jon Caramanica', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Jon', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Caramanica', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/fe8bae69-f90c-5861-a156-a02830a53295', 'word_count': 943, 'uri': 'nyt://article/fe8bae69-f90c-5861-a156-a02830a53295'}, {'abstract': 'A round-up of the newest, more ambitious coffee shops springing up across the city.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/ristretto-paris-is-brewing/', 'snippet': 'A round-up of the newest, more ambitious coffee shops springing up across the city.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Finally, Paris has a coffee scene. The city has always been the paradox of the industry, a great cafe town where an otherwise discerning audience happily throws back watery shots of ashy swill. I first wrote about Paris coffee in 2010, and after returning to the topic last year I decided to sit on my hands until somebody in the city figured out what it was missing.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Ristretto | Paris is Brewing', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T13:00:53+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Oliver Strand', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Oliver', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Strand', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/c3d51f5b-bc1d-5ee5-aa40-ad013c832795', 'word_count': 551, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c3d51f5b-bc1d-5ee5-aa40-ad013c832795'}, {'abstract': 'A UEFA Women’s Champions League game against F.K. Spartak took the writer to a new place: Serbia.', 'web_url': 'https://goal.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/a-champions-league-sojourn/', 'snippet': 'A UEFA Women’s Champions League game against F.K. Spartak took the writer to a new place: Serbia.', 'lead_paragraph': 'GOTHENBURG, Sweden — I have to admit, I looked up Serbia on the map when I found out we would be going there for Champions League. This was a business trip, like any time I’ve traveled to play a game, but it turned out to be a fascinating experience, leaving me wishing I could extend my stay and be a tourist for a couple days.', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/29/sports/soccer/yael22/yael22-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 768, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/29/sports/soccer/yael22/yael22-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1536, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/29/sports/soccer/yael22/yael22-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/09/29/sports/soccer/yael22/yael22-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'captio