g doubts about his commitment to democratic governance.', 'lead_paragraph': 'No leader of the former Soviet republics has been more pro-Western than President Mikheil Saakashvili of Georgia. He moves easily in Washington and dreams of bringing his country into full NATO membership. But his government’s handling of prisons and its approach to parliamentary elections set for Monday are raising doubts about his commitment to democracy and reform.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Democracy in the Former Soviet Republics', 'kicker': 'Editorial', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Democracy in the Former Soviet Republics', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Saakashvili, Mikheil', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'glocations', 'value': 'Georgia (Georgian Republic)', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Human Rights and Human Rights Violations', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Elections', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Editorials', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T18:55:49+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Editorial', 'section_name': 'Opinion', 'subsection_name': 'Sunday Review', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Editorial', '_id': 'nyt://article/c18a13fd-c2a8-5eff-bedf-dbee60b1d4b2', 'word_count': 401, 'uri': 'nyt://article/c18a13fd-c2a8-5eff-bedf-dbee60b1d4b2'}, {'abstract': 'The Rangers veteran Brad Richards is skating and training and hoping the N.H.L. lockout ends soon.', 'web_url': 'https://slapshot.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/30-seconds-with-brad-richards-locked-out-but-dialed-in/', 'snippet': 'The Rangers veteran Brad Richards is skating and training and hoping the N.H.L. lockout ends soon.', 'lead_paragraph': 'Before the N.H.L. lockout, the Rangers’ Brad Richards was hoping to build on his performance in last season’s playoffs. The alternate captain in his first season with the team, he had six goals and nine assists in 20 postseason games. During the regular season, Richards compiled 25 goals and 41 assists. He won the Stanley Cup with the Tampa Bay Lightning in 2004, when he was awarded the Conn Smythe Trophy as the most valuable player during the playoffs.', 'print_section': 'SP', 'print_page': '11', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': '30 Seconds With Brad Richards: Locked Out but Dialed In', 'kicker': 'Slap Shot', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Locked Out But Dialed In', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Hockey, Ice', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Richards, Brad', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'organizations', 'value': 'New York Rangers', 'rank': 3, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T18:58:39+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'Sports', 'subsection_name': 'Hockey', 'byline': {'original': 'By Joe Brescia', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Joe', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Brescia', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/3781c403-8d3b-5ddf-b48d-42fbe5c71630', 'word_count': 542, 'uri': 'nyt://article/3781c403-8d3b-5ddf-b48d-42fbe5c71630'}, {'abstract': 'Scenes from the Paris fashion week photo diary of Nacho Alegre.', 'web_url': 'https://tmagazine.blogs.nytimes.com/2012/09/29/photos-of-the-moment-haider-ackerman/', 'snippet': 'Scenes from the Paris fashion week photo diary of Nacho Alegre.', 'lead_paragraph': 'var data;function jsonSlideshowCallback(response){data = response;}NYTD.options = {source: data,cropType: \'blog480\',appendTo: \'fullScreenSlideShow\',showHeadLine: \'false\', renderstyle: "embeddedSlideShowTmag"}', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [], 'headline': {'main': 'Photos of The Moment | Haider Ackermann', 'kicker': 'T Magazine', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': '', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T19:00:50+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': '', 'section_name': 'T Magazine', 'byline': {'original': 'By Nacho Alegre', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Nacho', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Alegre', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'News', '_id': 'nyt://article/6376e7c7-69a3-5a54-b2c0-850fd0729306', 'word_count': 36, 'uri': 'nyt://article/6376e7c7-69a3-5a54-b2c0-850fd0729306'}, {'abstract': 'A new biography discusses John Keats’s opium use, but there is no need to find a chemical source for his extraordinary creativity.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/opinion/sunday/can-opium-or-illness-explain-a-keats-poem.html', 'snippet': 'A new biography discusses John Keats’s opium use, but there is no need to find a chemical source for his extraordinary creativity.', 'lead_paragraph': 'On Sept. 21, 1819, John Keats wrote to his brother George that he was “scarcely content to write the best verses for the fever they leave behind. I want to compose without this fever.” The fever is no metaphor. In another letter dated that same day, he had sent a friend a copy of his poem “To Autumn,” written just then, one of the greatest poems in the English language and the last of Keats’s great odes. For Keats, poetry was never emotion recollected in tranquillity, as it was for Wordsworth. It was often tranquillity refigured by his fevered mind.', 'print_section': 'SR', 'print_page': '16', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/opinion/30editorial/30editorial-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 985, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_