': 'Letters', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Repair the Infrastructure, Or Pay Much More Later', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Infrastructure (Public Works)', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-29T20:48:48+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'SundayBusiness', 'section_name': 'Business Day', 'byline': {'original': '', 'person': [], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Letter', '_id': 'nyt://article/57a4daf5-170b-53f8-b17f-962ce0691fb0', 'word_count': 214, 'uri': 'nyt://article/57a4daf5-170b-53f8-b17f-962ce0691fb0'}, {'abstract': 'One way to overhaul the tax code, Richard H. Thaler says, is to eliminate deductions for income above $1 million, and to treat all sources of that income equally.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/09/30/business/for-the-wealthy-a-28-percent-tax-solution.html', 'snippet': 'One way to overhaul the tax code, Richard H. Thaler says, is to eliminate deductions for income above $1 million, and to treat all sources of that income equally.', 'lead_paragraph': 'EVERYONE knows that America’s tax code is a mess, yet despite considerable talk when elections come around, we’ve done nothing substantial to fix it. What is odd is that there is broad agreement on what should be done. We should strive to get the “lowest possible tax rates on the broadest base,” as five well-known conservative economists put it recently.', 'print_section': 'BU', 'print_page': '6', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/business/30-VIEW/30-VIEW-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 1024, 'width': 737, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/09/30/business/30-VIEW/30-VIEW-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 2048, 'width': 1475, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name