isaki-lacuestas-double-steps-recalls-francois-augieras.html', 'snippet': '“The Double Steps” from Isaki Lacuesta weaves a fable around an American-born artist who painted in the Sahara.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The Spanish director Isaki Lacuesta clearly set out to create an art movie with “The Double Steps,” a self-conscious exercise set in Africa, largely by swapping one set of cinematic conventions for another. The movie won top honors at 2011 San Sebastián Film Festival (and feels very much as if it was made for the festival circuit). It hinges on an American-born artist and writer, François Augiéras (1925-71), who lived and worked in the Sahara and who covered an abandoned army bunker in frescoes, hoping, as some introductory text puts it, to make it “the Sistine Chapel of the desert.” “Today,” the on-screen text continues, “some travelers are preparing to find, finally, Augiéras’s lost frescoes.” ', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'jumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/01/arts/double/double-jumbo.jpg', 'height': 765, 'width': 1024, 'subType': 'jumbo', 'crop_name': 'jumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'superJumbo', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/01/arts/double/double-superJumbo.jpg', 'height': 1531, 'width': 2048, 'subType': 'superJumbo', 'crop_name': 'superJumbo', 'legacy': {}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbnail', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/01/arts/double/double-thumbStandard.jpg', 'height': 75, 'width': 75, 'subType': 'thumbnail', 'crop_name': 'thumbStandard', 'legacy': {'thumbnail': 'images/2012/10/01/arts/double/double-thumbStandard.jpg', 'thumbnailwidth': 75, 'thumbnailheight': 75}}, {'rank': 0, 'subtype': 'thumbLarge', 'caption': None, 'credit': None, 'type': 'image', 'url': 'images/2012/10/01/arts/double/double-thumbLarge.jpg', 'height': 150, 'width': 150, 'subType': 'thumbLarge', 'crop_name': 'thumbLarge', 'legacy': {}}], 'headline': {'main': 'Following an Artist’s Footsteps in the Sand', 'kicker': 'Movie Review', 'content_kicker': None, 'print_headline': 'Following an Artist’s Footsteps in the Sand', 'name': None, 'seo': None, 'sub': None}, 'keywords': [{'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Art', 'rank': 1, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Theater', 'rank': 2, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'subject', 'value': 'Movies', 'rank': 4, 'major': 'N'}, {'name': 'persons', 'value': 'Lacuesta, Isaki', 'rank': 5, 'major': 'N'}], 'pub_date': '2012-09-30T21:11:02+0000', 'document_type': 'article', 'news_desk': 'Culture', 'section_name': 'Movies', 'byline': {'original': 'By Manohla Dargis', 'person': [{'firstname': 'Manohla', 'middlename': None, 'lastname': 'Dargis', 'qualifier': None, 'title': None, 'role': 'reported', 'organization': '', 'rank': 1}], 'organization': None}, 'type_of_material': 'Review', '_id': 'nyt://article/1fcc10c5-1ea9-52ed-a3ab-36bc135fc05a', 'word_count': 371, 'uri': 'nyt://article/1fcc10c5-1ea9-52ed-a3ab-36bc135fc05a'}, {'abstract': '“Give Up Tomorrow” details the doubts surrounding a late-1990s rape and murder case in the Philippines.', 'web_url': 'https://www.nytimes.com/2012/10/01/movies/give-up-tomorrow-directed-by-michael-collins.html', 'snippet': '“Give Up Tomorrow” details the doubts surrounding a late-1990s rape and murder case in the Philippines.', 'lead_paragraph': 'The miscarriage of justice recounted in “Give Up Tomorrow,” filmed over six years, is so comprehensive as to be disorienting. Paco Larrañaga, a Filipino-Spanish student from a privileged family who was convicted in 1999 as the ringleader of a 1997 double murder and rape of two sisters in the Philippines, was, for starters, miles away on a different island when the crimes took place, according to 35 witnesses.', 'print_section': 'C', 'print_page': '5', 'source': 'The New York Times', 'multimedia': [{'rank': 0, 'subtype': '